const TP_MODULES=[{"t":"The Munger latticework: a mind stocked with models","kick":"Start here","mins":"1.5 hrs","obj":["Use cross-disciplinary models as explicit, revisable hypotheses and distinguish independent evidence from several labels for the same observation.","Identify incentive/selection and interaction risks without diagnosing motives from an outcome, or equating a revised written talk with a historical video."],"vids":[{"id":"pqzcCfUglws","t":"The Psychology of Human Misjudgement - Charlie Munger Full Speech","by":"BuffettMungerWisdom","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Written source editions are labeled separately."},{"id":"FlWL1Qz23co","t":"Charlie Munger's Full 1995 Speech at Harvard","by":"State of MedTech","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Written source editions are labeled separately."}],"reads":[{"t":"Psychology of Human Misjudgment (transcript)","u":"https://fs.blog/great-talks/psychology-human-misjudgment/","n":"Revised 2005 written talk discussing twenty-five tendencies. Treat it as a historical synthesis, not a verbatim transcript of the 1995 recording or an exhaustive modern taxonomy."},{"t":"Mental Models: The Best Way to Make Intelligent Decisions","u":"https://fs.blog/mental-models/","n":"Use simplified models from several disciplines, distinguish the map from the territory and stay within an evidence-based circle of competence. A list of analogies is not proof of a conclusion."},{"t":"Munger: written edition revised in 2005","u":"https://assets.ctfassets.net/fzn2n1nzq965/0RUnI35jpt78x10nvlO2Y/b66a46dba182182a2a0082213eafc634/SP_PCA-ZINE_2023_11_27.pdf","n":"Written Munger edition revised in 2005. Treat it as a separate historical text, not a verbatim transcript of the 1995 recording."}],"ex":"Decision worksheet using a synthetic case, not private employer/customer information. Define the decision, objective, constraints, evidence and what would change your mind. Select two or three relevant models from different fields, state where each applies and where it may fail, and write competing predictions. More model names do not necessarily mean more independent evidence. A model is a simplified representation, not a substitute for observations or domain expertise. Cross-disciplinary breadth complements depth rather than proves experts or specialization useless.\nEdition check: the assigned Farnam Street Psychology page explicitly describes an extensive 2005 revision. A separate written edition says selections from three talks combined into one talk never made after 2005 revisions. Neither establishes a word-for-word transcript of an assigned 1995 video. Its 25 tendencies are Munger's historical synthesis, not an exhaustive modern diagnostic taxonomy or independently replicated proof of each anecdote. The FS mental-models page's map/territory and competence discussion supports revisable lenses; do not silently turn metaphorical physics/biology analogies into quantitative laws about companies. The man-with-a-hammer idea warns against forcing every case into one favored lens. It does not imply every single-model calculation is invalid.\nSynthetic sales-team case: paying only for signed contracts may reward volume while masking returns or delivery quality. Compare retained revenue, returns, customer outcomes and controls before attributing a worker's motive; a commission is not proof of dishonesty. A crowd of positive reviews may share one source or reflect selection, so ten copies are not ten independent confirmations. Look for disconfirming evidence, incentive structure, base rates and plausible alternative causes, rather than dismissing everyone who disagrees as biased.\nStipulated 2 x 2 experiment measures conversion in matched populations: baseline 10%,incentive A 12%,social cue B 13%,both 20%. The additive prediction from separate lifts is 15%; observed joint rate exceeds it by 5 percentage points:20-12-13+10=5. This is a toy interaction on an additive probability scale, not a theorem that all biases multiply or proof these are the causal psychological mechanisms. In real data, assignment, sample size, uncertainty and alternative explanations matter. Munger's lollapalooza label describes converging forces with large combined effects, not a numeric formula or guaranteed extreme result. Choose an action/stop condition and review date from the evidence; no real message, purchase or team change is required. Evaluate a concrete decision using evidence rather than memorized quotations.","quiz":[{"q":"Several model names explain the same sales rise. Stronger evidence automatically?","o":["Yes; every additional model label is an independent measurement.","No; they may relabel one observation rather than add independent evidence.","Yes; cross-disciplinary language removes the need for data.","No; models from different fields can never inform the same decision."],"a":1},{"q":"Favorite incentive model is applied to every failure without checking data. Concern?","o":["Man-with-a-hammer overuse; test other causes and the model's scope.","All incentive explanations must be rejected because they are economic.","One favored model is always sufficient if its name is familiar.","The failure proves every domain expert is biased in the same way."],"a":0},{"q":"FS assigned psychology text describes a 2005 revision. Match to 1995 video?","o":["Treat every revised written sentence as spoken in the 1995 recording.","Do not assume a verbatim match; label editions and verify any compared passage.","Treat all historical video assignments as automatically watched.","Discard all pedagogical value because the written edition is revised."],"a":1},{"q":"Commission-paid seller recommends a product. What follows?","o":["The recommendation is necessarily objective because compensation is disclosed.","The recommendation is necessarily dishonest regardless of fit evidence.","Check incentives and independent fit evidence; commission alone does not prove deceit.","Incentives cannot affect judgment if the seller believes the advice."],"a":2},{"q":"Ten reviews copied from one original source. Independent confirmations?","o":["Ten, because each copy appears at a different URL.","Potentially one source, not ten independent observations.","Zero useful information because copying always falsifies a claim.","Twenty, counting reviewers and webpages separately."],"a":1},{"q":"Baseline 10%,A 12%,B 13%. Additive-lift prediction for both?","o":["35%, adding baseline,A and B.","25%, adding A and B without subtracting shared baseline.","15%.","20%, using the observed joint rate as the prediction."],"a":2},{"q":"Observed both 20% against that 15% additive prediction. Interaction?","o":["+50 percentage points, mixing relative and absolute changes.","+20 percentage points, treating the entire outcome as interaction.","-5 percentage points, reversing observed minus predicted.","+5 percentage points on this scale."],"a":3},{"q":"Lollapalooza label establishes?","o":["A hypothesis of converging forces, not a universal multiplication rule.","A fixed equation that multiplies all psychological biases in every case.","A guarantee every decision has an extreme outcome.","An exhaustive list of every empirically proven mental disorder."],"a":0},{"q":"Physical inertia analogy used to predict company response. Need?","o":["Only the word inertia; all organizations obey the same measured mass equation.","Relevant mechanism/data; mass laws do not transfer quantitatively by metaphor alone.","No check because physics analogies are always more accurate than business data.","Reject every analogy because cross-domain reasoning is never informative."],"a":1},{"q":"Evidence contradicts the preferred mental model. Better response?","o":["Recheck evidence and assumptions, then revise or narrow the model.","Preserve the model by redefining every contradictory observation as bias.","Add more model names without changing predictions.","Infer the evidence collector's motive and stop checking the facts."],"a":0}]},{"t":"First principles: reason from the ground up","kick":"Toolkit","mins":"15 mins","obj":["Separate evidence, physical constraints, institutional constraints and assumptions; rebuild a testable estimate without declaring all convention avoidable.","Compare analogy, parametric and bottom-up estimates with explicit scope/yield/time bases; treat historical cost anecdotes as prompts to investigate, not current benchmarks."],"vids":[{"id":"NV3sBlRgzTI","t":"The First Principles Method Explained by Elon Musk","by":"Innomind","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Written source editions are labeled separately."}],"reads":[{"t":"What is First Principles Thinking?","u":"https://fs.blog/first-principles/","n":"Context-dependent foundations, questioning/evidence. Treat the rocket-cost story as an illustration, not a current material-cost estimate."},{"t":"NASA Cost Estimating Handbook: scope","u":"https://www.nasa.gov/ocfo/ppc-corner/nasa-cost-estimating-handbook-ceh/","n":"Summary or description; use the written exercise for the worked case."},{"t":"NASA AppendixC: cost-estimating methods","u":"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/ceh_appc.pdf","n":"Compare analogy, parametric and work-breakdown estimating methods. Document adjustments and assumptions; the 2.3-to-3.4 kW example is conditional, not a current aerospace quote."}],"ex":"Synthetic cost teardown, not an actual rocket/battery investment or quotation. FS explains first principles as separating knowledge from assumptions and admits foundations are context-dependent/revisable. Asking why can expose assumptions but is not proof; look for measurements, sources and feasible tests. Analogy is not necessarily mindless copying: NASA's cost-methods appendix explicitly uses analogy, parametric and engineering build-up, selected by available data, definition and time constraints. A matched analogy with documented adjustments may be more useful than an invented detailed spreadsheet. Physical limits, legal/contract requirements, safety/testing needs and organizational conventions are different kinds of constraints; social origin does not mean they can simply be ignored.\nHistorical FS rocket/battery examples quote raw-material price comparisons. Treat the rocket-cost anecdote as an illustration, not as evidence of the original percentage or current operating costs. Even if raw materials are a small share of a quoted product price, that does not establish the remainder is waste or removable profit. Manufacturing yield, labor, tooling, engineering, qualification, integration, logistics, overhead and risk may matter. A commodity bill is not a flight-ready system or current supplier quote. Do not score a memorized 2% as a physical/current economic law.\nToy per-delivered-unit breakdown: materials 20,processing 30,assembly 25,testing 15,allocated overhead 10=100 cost units. Halving materials alone saves 10 and gives total 90,not 50; hold every other category/volume/allocation fixed. If materials cost 20 per attempt and yield is 80%, material cost per good unit is 20/.8=25 before other costs, assuming failed attempts consume the same input and no salvage. Track scope so per-attempt inputs are not compared directly with per-good outputs. A chosen toy electrical load 100 W for 2 hours uses 200 Wh=.2 kWh, but energy alone does not specify a bill or all system power losses.\nNASA's illustrative analogy scales a 10 M solar-array cost at 2.3 kW to 3.4 kW under an explicitly assumed linear cost-power relationship:10*3.4/2.3=14.7826 M. The assumption is part of the example, not proof all hardware scales linearly. Inflation, technical maturity and testing/compliance may need documented adjustments. Record estimate range/sensitivity and test the largest uncertain drivers; recomputing the same unmeasured assumptions more precisely does not make them true. First-principles reasoning may reveal a constraint or no cheaper feasible design, not a guaranteed breakthrough. No purchase, construction, unsafe experiment or operational policy change is part of this worksheet.","quiz":[{"q":"A raw-material bill is 2% of quoted finished-system price. Inference?","o":["The supplier's margin must equal 98% under every accounting basis.","Exactly 98% can be removed with no effect on manufacturing/testing.","The finished price violates a physical law because materials are cheaper.","It is a partial cost comparison, not proof 98% is avoidable waste."],"a":3},{"q":"Toy 20 materials+30 processing+25 assembly+15 testing+10 overhead. Total?","o":["90 cost units, omitting overhead.","100 cost units.","85 cost units, omitting testing.","120 cost units, counting materials twice."],"a":1},{"q":"Halve only materials 20 to 10, all other categories fixed. New total?","o":["50, because one category was halved.","90, a 10% reduction from 100.","80, subtracting the whole original material cost.","110, adding the savings to the original total."],"a":1},{"q":"20 material units per attempt,80% yield,no salvage. Cost per good unit?","o":["25 before other costs.","16 before other costs, multiplying by yield.","20 including all manufacturing/testing costs.","100 before other costs, dividing by scrap fraction."],"a":0},{"q":"100 W for 2 hours at stated constant draw. Energy?","o":["50 Wh, dividing by time.","200 Wh, or 0.2 kWh.","200 kWh, omitting watt-to-kilowatt conversion.","0.2 W, treating energy as power."],"a":1},{"q":"NASA illustration:10 M cost at 2.3 kW, target 3.4 kW, assumed linear. Estimate?","o":["10 M exactly, because all analogy adjustments must be zero.","About 6.7647 M, reversing the scale ratio.","34 M, ignoring baseline 2.3 kW.","About 14.7826 M, conditional on the linear assumption and scope."],"a":3},{"q":"First-principles estimate uses unmeasured yield but many decimal places. Strong?","o":["No; precision does not establish the uncertain input's accuracy.","Yes; additional decimals automatically validate the physical assumption.","Yes; a bottom-up label removes all estimate uncertainty.","No; arithmetic precision can never be useful after data are measured."],"a":0},{"q":"Early project has a relevant historical analog but little design detail. Approach?","o":["A documented adjusted analogy may be appropriate; compare methods/data limits.","Reject analogies even though the bottom-up inputs would be invented.","Assume the new cost equals the old without checking scope/date.","Use a celebrity quote as a complete current project estimate."],"a":0},{"q":"A testing requirement is institutional rather than a physical law. Can omit?","o":["Not by that fact alone; check safety, legal/contract and operating constraints.","Yes; all nonphysical requirements are costless to remove.","Yes; calling a rule convention overrides contract obligations.","No; no institutional requirement can ever be revised by authorized parties."],"a":0},{"q":"A first-principles analysis finds no feasible cheaper design. Failed thinking?","o":["Yes; every conclusion must contradict existing practice.","Yes; the method guarantees every product becomes cheaper.","Not necessarily; identifying a real constraint is a valid result.","No; this proves all future designs are impossible forever."],"a":2}]},{"t":"Second-order thinking: and then what?","kick":"Deceptively hard","mins":"25 mins","obj":["Map immediate and downstream effects as conditional mechanisms with explicit time horizons, evidence and alternative scenarios.","Compare contribution/profit and expected-value toys coherently; avoid guaranteed competitor reactions, unbounded chains or double-counted consequences."],"vids":[{"id":"WBz7VB4vA1k","t":"Decision Making Framework | Shane Parrish","by":"Farnam Street","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Written source editions are labeled separately."},{"id":"gOETwvNUwf0","t":"Second Order Thinking","by":"Moonshots Podcast","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Written source editions are labeled separately."}],"reads":[{"t":"Second-Order Thinking: What Smart People Use to Outperform","u":"https://fs.blog/second-order-thinking/","n":"Consequences/time/stakeholder response may have little impact; scenarios are not guaranteed outcomes."}],"ex":"Use a synthetic decision, not a real manager's motives or medical advice. FS second-order explainer asks what happens next, how stakeholders may respond and how effects change over time. It explicitly notes the response may be little or no impact. A plausible causal chain is a scenario, not a prediction proved by naming second-order thinking. More distant effects can be beneficial, harmful or negligible; longer/more contrarian reasoning is not automatically better. Identify mechanism, delay, affected people, evidence, counterfactual and signals that would change the plan. Avoid inventing certainty about a fired employee's effect on morale or competitor price matching.\nOne-period business toy: baseline price 100,cost 60 per unit,fixed 1000,volume 100 gives revenue 10000,contribution 4000,profit 3000. A cut to 90 and forecast 120 units gives revenue 10800,contribution 3600,profit 2600. Sales/revenue rise but profit falls 400 under the stipulated constant cost/fixed-cost assumptions. To preserve baseline 3000 profit at 90 price, solve 30*q-1000=3000:q=133.333...,so at least 134 whole units. This is required volume, not evidence demand will reach it; capacity, working capital, quality and competition could change the assumptions. Count outcomes once: lost contribution is already reflected in profit, so do not subtract it again as a second-order loss.\nAlternative scenario toy for the same stated horizon/cost basis: resulting profit 3500 with probability 0.4,or 2000 with probability 0.6 gives expected 2600 versus baseline 3000. These probabilities/profits are invented inputs, not calibrated market forecasts or added to the earlier 2600 scenario. The high-case probability needed for expected 3000 is(3000-2000)/(3500-2000)=2/3. Expected value is not a guarantee or complete decision rule; downside, liquidity, rights, irreversibility and nonfinancial effects can override an attractive mean. Changing the horizon/baseline or omitting future costs makes the comparison incoherent.\nSketch a feedback loop such as promotion->demand->service backlog->quality->retention, marking which links are unverified and how delay changes interpretation. A short pilot and stop/review threshold may test uncertain links when authorized and safe; no actual price change or personnel decision is made here. 'And then what?' should lead to bounded, decision-relevant investigation rather than endless speculative branches. The parade/antibiotic examples are not scored as universal geometry or medical advice in this worksheet. Base decisions on supported causal evidence and explicit values, not a memorable story alone.","quiz":[{"q":"Competitors matching a price cut is described. Evidence status without case data?","o":["An established fact because second-order thinking predicts every reaction.","A possible response scenario, not a guaranteed industry-wide outcome.","Impossible because competitor behavior cannot affect contribution margins.","A physical law independent of market structure or incentives."],"a":1},{"q":"Baseline P 100,V 100,variable cost 60,fixed 1000. Profit?","o":["10000, reporting revenue as profit.","4000, omitting fixed cost.","3000.","-3000, subtracting the variable cost twice."],"a":2},{"q":"Cut P 90,V 120,same cost 60/fixed 1000. Profit?","o":["3000, assuming greater unit sales preserve profit automatically.","10800, treating revenue as profit.","3600, omitting the fixed cost.","2600, despite revenue 10800."],"a":3},{"q":"At P 90,what whole volume preserves at least baseline 3000 profit?","o":["133 units, rounding below the exact 133.333 threshold.","120 units, the proposed forecast.","100 units, because fixed costs are unchanged.","134 units."],"a":3},{"q":"Required volume 134 units means demand will reach 134?","o":["Yes; all price reductions increase volume enough to preserve profit.","Yes; algebra proves customers will buy that many.","No; it is a threshold under assumptions, not an empirical demand forecast.","No; required-volume calculations have no planning use at all."],"a":2},{"q":"Alternative profits 3500 with p=0.4 and 2000 with p=0.6. Expected profit?","o":["2750, using equal weights instead of stated probabilities.","3000, averaging baseline and high case.","2600 on that horizon/cost basis.","5500, adding scenario profits without probability weights."],"a":2},{"q":"High 3500/low 2000. p needed for mean 3000?","o":["0.5, assuming the baseline is exactly halfway.","1/3, reversing the required improvement.","2/3, about 66.67%.","0.4, copying the original scenario probability."],"a":2},{"q":"Price-cut lost contribution already included in projected profit. Subtract again?","o":["No; profit forecasts can never include any downstream effects.","Yes; every second-order label is a new independent cash outflow.","Yes; revenue and profit changes must always be summed as losses.","No; that double-counts the same effect unless a separate cost is identified."],"a":3},{"q":"A long causal story has many uncertain links. Better next step?","o":["Wait for perfect knowledge of every possible future before any decision.","Treat chain length as proof the final outcome is likely.","Assume each link has probability 1 because it is narratively plausible.","Test decision-relevant links, alternatives and review thresholds rather than add speculation."],"a":3},{"q":"An option has higher expected profit but severe rights/downside risks. Choose automatically?","o":["No; calculating scenario means is never useful in decisions.","Yes; any positive mean overrides all other constraints.","Yes; expected value guarantees the realized high outcome.","No; expected value is one input, not permission or a complete decision rule."],"a":3}]},{"t":"Inversion: avoid stupidity before seeking brilliance","kick":"The judo move","mins":"10 mins","obj":["Use inversion/premortem to generate testable failure hypotheses and safeguards while distinguishing necessary from sufficient conditions.","Prioritize concrete mitigations with evidence, owner, review and residual risks instead of assuming a stop-doing list guarantees success."],"vids":[{"id":"L0Kp7lBVM30","t":"Charlie Munger: Invert, Always Invert","by":"Stock Compounder","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"reads":[{"t":"Inversion and The Power of Avoiding Stupidity","u":"https://fs.blog/inversion/","n":"Secondary attribution and heuristic, not guaranteed success theorem."},{"t":"Project premortem: Gary Klein2007 summary","u":"https://hbr.org/2007/09/performing-a-project-premortem","n":"Summary of Gary Klein's 2007 premortem: imagine failure to surface plausible concerns. It is a prompt for investigation, not proof those events will occur or that the method guarantees better outcomes."}],"ex":"Synthetic project premortem, not a real employment, investment or health decision. State the goal, deadline, success criteria and constraints. Imagine it failed, let participants independently list plausible causes, then compare evidence and alternatives. A failure story is a hypothesis, not a confirmed cause or probability. The bounded Gary Klein 2007 HBR summary describes assuming failure to surface reservations, not full-method validation or guaranteed improvement. FS inversion supports examining forward and backward perspectives; quote attributions are secondary-source context, not a scored memory gate or proof of a theorem.\nLogic toy: acceptance requires BOTH timely submission A and quality criterion B. Not A is sufficient for failure under this rule; A is necessary but not sufficient for acceptance. Submitting on time does not prove B. Avoiding a listed cause of failure does not eliminate unlisted causes. A plan can need positive actions as well as stopping harmful ones. Simply doing the opposite of the crowd, refusing every risk or removing all controls is not inversion as a complete decision strategy.\nStipulated risk toy: three independent failure modes with probabilities .10, .20, .05 and any one causes failure. Success=.9*.8*.95=.684; failure=.316, not the sum .35 because events can overlap. A hypothetical mitigation changes first risk from .10 to .02 with everything else fixed: success=.98*.8*.95=.7448, an increase .0608 or 6.08 percentage points; residual failure=.2552. These are assumed probabilities/independence, not measured project forecasts. If causes share dependencies or mitigation creates new risks, recompute from a suitable joint model rather than multiply blindly. A long list of scary outcomes does not tell their likelihood or optimal priority.\nConvert a selected risk into a testable safeguard: for example, synthetic backup recovery test before a practice deadline, with owner/date, pass condition and fallback. A file named backup is not evidence it restores. Prioritize impact, probability, confidence, feasibility and unintended effects rather than only ease/count. A stop-doing item should name an observable behavior and why changing it helps; do not stop essential testing just to reduce activity. Review whether mitigations work and whether success criteria changed. No actual deadline reminder, account backup, purchase, financial bet or external message is created by this worksheet.","quiz":[{"q":"Premortem story lists a plausible cause. Evidence status?","o":["A guaranteed future outcome because failure was imagined vividly.","A failure hypothesis to check, not proof that it occurred or will occur.","An empirical probability estimate without any data or model.","A confirmed root cause even when alternative mechanisms fit."],"a":1},{"q":"Accept iff timely A and quality B. Timely submission A alone?","o":["Necessary, not sufficient for acceptance under this stated rule.","Sufficient even if quality B fails.","Sufficient but not necessary if quality alone defines acceptance.","Both necessary and sufficient regardless of the quality result."],"a":0},{"q":"Same rule: not A means?","o":["Only a probability of failure can be inferred from a deterministic rule.","Acceptance is assured if submission quality is high.","Failure is assured under this rule, regardless of B.","The quality criterion must also necessarily be false."],"a":2},{"q":"One listed failure mode has been eliminated. Success guaranteed?","o":["No; mitigating any failure mode can never improve success chances.","Yes; eliminating any cause eliminates every possible failure.","Yes; a stop-doing list is logically equivalent to a complete plan.","No; other listed/unlisted causes and changed conditions may remain."],"a":3},{"q":"Independent failure risks .10, .20, .05; any causes failure. Success?","o":["0.684.","0.65, subtracting their sum from 1.","0.35, reporting summed failure risks as success.","0.001, multiplying failure probabilities instead of complements."],"a":0},{"q":"Same toy. Probability of at least one failure?","o":["0.684, returning success probability.","0.35, adding overlapping failure events without correction.","0.316.","0.001, requiring all failures instead of any."],"a":2},{"q":"First risk .10->.02, others unchanged. New success?","o":["0.98, ignoring the other two risks.","0.764, adding the whole .08 risk reduction to success.","0.7448.","0.2552, returning residual failure."],"a":2},{"q":"Failure modes share a common dependency. Multiply complements automatically?","o":["No; dependent events cannot be modeled probabilistically at all.","Yes; naming three separate risks proves statistical independence.","Yes; multiplication is always valid for every list of probabilities.","No; justify independence or use their joint/conditional probabilities."],"a":3},{"q":"A proposed safeguard says create a backup. Better completion check?","o":["A successful recovery test under the required scope, not just file existence.","The backup filename looks reassuring, so no restore evidence is needed.","The file is larger than yesterday, so every recovery case is proven.","The plan mentions backups, so the safeguard already happened."],"a":0},{"q":"Project has a stop-doing list but no success actions or review. Sufficient?","o":["Yes; removing activity always guarantees a good result.","No; pair mitigations with positive requirements, owners, tests and residual-risk review.","Yes; a longer list is automatically a complete strategy.","No; inversion and forward planning can never be combined."],"a":1}]},{"t":"Systems thinking: stocks, flows and feedback","kick":"See the machine","mins":"20 mins","obj":["Distinguish stocks from rate flows and compute a dimensionally consistent balance with nonnegative/capacity constraints.","Trace feedback polarity, gain, delays and boundaries without treating loop names, diagrams or leverage rankings as guarantees of behavior."],"vids":[{"id":"RUhy9q08LbY","t":"Operational Systems Thinking | Stocks, Flows & Feedback Loops","by":"Learn With Dr. Hakeem-Ur-Rehman","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"reads":[{"t":"Systems Thinking: What, Why, When, Where, and How?","u":"https://thesystemsthinker.com/systems-thinking-what-why-when-where-and-how/","n":"Study boundaries, inquiry and simple diagrams. A shared system story is a hypothesis to test, not proof of causation."},{"t":"Meadowsleverage-point list: historical repost","u":"https://nautilus.org/gps/solving/places-to-intervene-in-a-system/","n":"Historical twelve-item leverage-point list attributed to Meadows in 1999. Use it to generate hypotheses, not as a universal ranking guaranteed to work in every system."},{"t":"Thinking in Systems: leverage-point chapter excerpt","u":"https://www.gc.soton.ac.uk/files/2015/01/Thinking-in-Systems-145-165.pdf","n":"Chapter excerpt on delays, feedback, gains and buffers. The leverage-point list evolved and is not a quick recipe; historical policy illustrations are not current recommendations."}],"ex":"Paper/CPU systems model using invented task counts. Define boundary, units, time step and assumptions before drawing arrows. Backlog stock 50 tasks; arrivals 10 tasks/day; completions 8 tasks/day for 3 days gives 56 tasks if rates are constant and there is work available. Flow changes the stock through accumulation over time; a high inflow is not itself the stock. Net flow zero can leave a positive stock unchanged. With 5 initial tasks+2 arrivals, requested 10 completions cannot actually remove 10: at most 7 available can depart and backlog ends 0, unless borrowing/negative stock has been explicitly modeled. Avoid impossible arithmetic by limiting the outflow to availability/capacity and declaring within-step timing.\nLink signs indicate direction of a change holding other factors fixed, not whether the effect is good/bad. A closed loop with an even number of negative links is reinforcing; an odd number is balancing. Reinforcing can amplify growth or decline, while balancing tends to counter deviation but does not guarantee stability or an ethically desirable goal. Check actual causal mechanisms; a diagram agreeing with participants' story is not independent validation of the story. The assigned Systems Thinker primer emphasizes inquiry, boundaries and simplicity, not measured proof of a proposed real-world intervention.\nToy discrete controller, no explicit measurement delay: xnext=x+k*(10-x), start 0. Withk=.5:0, 5, 7.5, 8.75 approaches target; with k=1.5:0, 15, 7.5, 11.25 overshoots and oscillates with shrinking amplitude. This is a stated recurrence, not a general stability theorem or fitted model of a company. It illustrates that balancing polarity alone does not ensure monotonic response; gain matters. Delays relative to response rate can contribute to overshoot/oscillation, but not every delayed system oscillates and delay is not the sole cause of surprising behavior. Saturation, competing loops, nonlinearity and boundary errors may matter. Hiring more people may initially reduce throughput under onboarding/coordination conditions, but need not always do so.\nMeadows' leverage-point chapter explicitly says no quick/easy formula and that its list is evolving, not a recipe. Buffers, information, rules/goals and feedback can matter, but changing a lever in the wrong direction can worsen outcomes. A small intervention is not automatically cheap, authorized, safe or high-impact. Mark uncertain links, decide what observations could refute them, choose a reversible test only when authorized, and monitor unintended outcomes/delays. This is not an actual staffing, financial, environmental or organizational intervention; historical policy examples are not current policy recommendations.","quiz":[{"q":"Backlog 50, arrival 10/day, completion 8/day, 3 days. End stock?","o":["52 tasks, applying the net rate for only one day.","56 tasks under constant-rate/availability assumptions.","74 tasks, adding completions rather than subtracting them.","30 tasks, subtracting completion flow but omitting arrivals."],"a":1},{"q":"Which pair has stock and matching flow units?","o":["Tasks and tasks/day.","Tasks/day and tasks, reversing the roles.","Tasks and tasks squared/day, adding an extra stock factor.","Tasks/day and tasks/day, both rates."],"a":0},{"q":"Net flow zero. Stock must be zero?","o":["Yes; zero rate necessarily erases every prior accumulation.","No; a positive stock can remain unchanged.","Yes; stocks and rates are the same quantity.","No; zero net flow means stock must grow forever."],"a":1},{"q":"Stock 5, arrivals 2, requested completions 10, all available in step. Actual departures max?","o":["7, leaving 0 backlog without negative/borrowed stock.","10, leaving-3 without any model constraint needed.","5, ignoring the within-step 2 arrivals.","12, adding requested departures to arrivals."],"a":0},{"q":"Two negative causal links in a closed loop. Polarity?","o":["Beneficial, because reinforcing always means good outcomes.","Balancing, because any negative link makes the loop negative.","No feedback, because negative signs cancel every causal relation.","Reinforcing, since the sign product is positive."],"a":3},{"q":"One positive/one negative link in a closed loop. Polarity?","o":["Necessarily unstable regardless of gain and delays.","Reinforcing, because one link is positive.","Balancing, with negative sign product.","Necessarily harmless because balancing always means safe."],"a":2},{"q":"xnext=x+.5*(10-x), start 0. First three updates?","o":["10, 10, 10, ignoring the gain.","5, 7.5, 8.75.","5, 10, 15, adding a fixed 5 each time.","0.5, 1, 1.5, treating gain as constant inflow."],"a":1},{"q":"Same recurrence with k 1.5:0->15->7.5->11.25. What follows?","o":["All systems with no explicit delay must be stable and nonoscillating.","Every balancing loop approaches monotonically by definition.","Balancing can overshoot/oscillate here even without an explicit measurement delay.","Oscillation proves the sign product must be positive."],"a":2},{"q":"A causal loop diagram matches the team's story. Validated model?","o":["No; diagrams can never help clarify assumptions.","Yes; consensus makes every arrow causal.","Yes; drawing more arrows guarantees predictive accuracy.","Not yet; test links, units, boundary and behavior against evidence."],"a":3},{"q":"Meadows leverage list used to choose a tiny change. Guaranteed large benefit?","o":["No; context, direction, constraints and unintended effects still need checks.","Yes; any small change at a named lever is automatically beneficial.","Yes; ranking replaces system-specific diagnosis.","No; information/rules/goals can never affect system behavior."],"a":0}]},{"t":"Probabilistic thinking: bet on the odds, not the outcome","kick":"Bet well","mins":"45 mins","obj":["Update explicit base rates with likelihood evidence without reversing conditionals or double-counting dependent observations.","Score defined forecasts with a stated binary loss convention and distinguish sample performance/calibration from guarantees, tail assumptions or permission to bet."],"vids":[{"id":"uYNsSeYjkp4","t":"Thinking in Bets | Annie Duke | Talks at Google","by":"Talks at Google","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"reads":[{"t":"The Value of Probabilistic Thinking","u":"https://fs.blog/probabilistic-thinking/","n":"Its hard-cap normal-tail simplification is corrected with NIST; historical risk/return anecdotes not current personalized facts."},{"t":"Bayes theorem: reverse conditionals and partitions","u":"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat414/Lesson06","n":"Distinguish priors and posteriors, use mutually exclusive and exhaustive partitions, and match likelihoods to the question being asked."},{"t":"Normal distribution: density and unbounded support","u":"https://itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/eda/section3/eda3661.htm","n":"A normal distribution has support over the whole real line, not a finite hard cap. A tail probability can be tiny without being exactly zero."},{"t":"Forecast scoring and calibration, Berkeley2023","u":"https://www.stat.berkeley.edu/~ryantibs/statlearn-s23/lectures/calibration.pdf","n":"Binary-event squared-loss convention stated explicitly, not conflated with multi-category scaling."}],"ex":"Use invented nonmedical quality-control data, not a diagnosis, real wager or investment recommendation. Define an event, reference class, resolution rule, deadline and prior before forecasting. A base rate can inform a prior when population/selection/time are relevant; it is not an unquestionable universal frequency. Probabilities may change with evidence, remain unchanged under uninformative evidence, or require a revised model if assumptions fail. Do not replace likelihoods with a vague claim that all new facts must weaken the prior. Outcomes inform review but a single success/failure cannot prove the original process or estimated odds were sound.\nSynthetic batch 1000 items:10 defective and 990 good. Aflag catches 90% of defective items and falsely flags 10% of good items:9 true flags+99 false flags=108 flags. P(defective|flag)=9/108=1/12=8.33%, not 90%; reverse conditional P(flag|defective)differs. Prior odds 1/99; likelihood ratio .9/.1=9; posterior odds 1/11 and probability 1/12. If an additional independent flag has the same likelihood ratio conditional on status, posterior odds multiply by 9 again to 9/11, probability 9/20=.45. Repeating the same report is not independent evidence and must not automatically cause that update. LR 1 leaves odds unchanged. These values are stipulated, not measured factory/test performance.\nDefine binary Brier loss as mean((p-y)^2), y 0 or 1, lower better. Forecasts(.8, .6, .2), outcomes(1, 0, 0)give losses(.04, .36, .04), average .1466667; constant .5 baseline loss .25. This binary-event convention differs by scaling/constant from some multi-category quadratic-score formulas, so compare like with like. Three observations are a learning log, not strong calibration evidence or proof of skill. For a bin of 100 forecasts each .8 with 80 events, observed frequency .8 is consistent in that sample/bin, not proof of calibration at every level or no drift. Preserve dated forecasts before outcomes and avoid selecting only wins; define scoring/rules in advance. No real reminder or future monitor is created by the course exercise itself.\nCorrect the assigned explainer's bounded-tail simplification: a mathematical normal distribution has support from negative infinity to positive infinity, as NIST shows. Thin tails do not mean a hard cap; heavier-tail models differ in tail decay/probabilities and must be named/validated for the domain. Heavy tails do not imply every extreme is likely or every mean is useful/finite; a chosen distribution's moments/constraints matter. Historical crime/health/market-return anecdotes are not current personalized risks or expected-return promises. Expected value requires a coherent outcome distribution and finite relevant expectation; even a favorable mean does not settle ruin risk, constraints or affordability. This worksheet teaches arithmetic and forecast review, not guaranteed gains or a right to spend.","quiz":[{"q":"10 defective/990 good; 90% true-flag, 10% false-flag. Flagged counts?","o":["10 defective+990 good=1000, flagging every item.","9 defective+1 good=10, using false flags over defective count.","90 defective+99 good=189, using wrong defective denominator.","9 defective+99 good=108."],"a":3},{"q":"Same batch. P(defective|flag)?","o":["1%, ignoring the flag evidence.","90%, returning P(flag|defective).","1/12, about 8.33%.","10%, returning false-positive rate."],"a":2},{"q":"Prior odds 1/99, LR 9. Posterior odds?","o":["9/100, confusing odds with joint probability.","9/11, applying the same evidence twice.","1/891, dividing by LR instead of multiplying.","1/11."],"a":3},{"q":"Second conditionally independent flag with LR 9 after odds 1/11. Probability?","o":["9/11, reporting odds as probability.","0.45, from odds 9/11.","1/12, ignoring the stipulated additional independent evidence.","0.90, reporting sensitivity rather than posterior."],"a":1},{"q":"Same report copied twice. Count as two independent observations?","o":["Yes; every new URL is a new independent observation.","No; preserve dependence rather than square its likelihood ratio automatically.","Yes; reading it later makes the original evidence independent.","No; copied reports can never provide any information at all."],"a":1},{"q":"New evidence has likelihood ratio 1. Change in odds?","o":["Always increase to certainty because evidence is new.","None under that model.","Always decrease because every new fact challenges a prior.","Invert prior odds regardless of the observed event."],"a":1},{"q":"p(.8, .6, .2), y(1, 0, 0). Mean binary Brier loss(p-y)^2?","o":["0.333333, using event frequency instead of squared loss.","0.44, returning the sum rather than mean.","0.25, the constant .5 baseline instead.","About 0.146667."],"a":3},{"q":"100 forecasts at .8, 80 events. Claim?","o":["All future events will occur with exactly the same frequency.","Every individual .8 forecast must be correct exactly 80% of itself.","Observed frequency matches this bin/sample, not proof of universal calibration.","The model has 80% accuracy on every unrelated task."],"a":2},{"q":"Mathematical normal distribution support?","o":["All real numbers; thin tails are not a hard finite cap.","A strict finite interval around the mean for every normal model.","Only nonnegative numbers because probabilities are nonnegative.","Only values within one standard deviation."],"a":0},{"q":"A positive expected-value option loses once. Appropriate review?","o":["Conclude probabilistic reasoning always fails after any loss.","Double the stake automatically to recover the loss.","Conclude the odds were certainly correct because the mean was positive.","Recheck odds/model/process and constraints; one outcome alone is not conclusive."],"a":3}]},{"t":"Circle of competence: know its edge","kick":"Stay in bounds","mins":"15 mins","obj":["Define competence by a scoped task, evidence and known failure modes rather than confidence, status or a few selected wins.","Choose a bounded learning, independent review or abstention path without equating expertise with authority or guaranteed performance."],"vids":[{"id":"eIUFGSbaxg8","t":"Warren Buffett: Here's How To Determine Your Circle of Competence","by":"Adam J. Mead","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"reads":[{"t":"Mastering Success: Navigating Within Your Circle of Competence","u":"https://fs.blog/circle-of-competence/","n":"Lightly edited interview attribution; no universal loss/advantage claim."},{"t":"Berkshire1996letter: original circle passage","u":"https://berkshirehathaway.com/letters/1996.html","n":"Berkshire's 1996 shareholder letter, signed February 28, 1997. Focus on the circle-of-competence paragraph and its context; it is a historical management view, not an automatic investment rule."}],"ex":"Synthetic capability review, not a recommendation to trade, hire, diagnose or change careers. Berkshire 1996 letter, dated 28 February 1997, says investors need to evaluate selected businesses and know the boundaries of their circle. This is historical investing commentary, not proof that every person outside a circle loses money or every person inside makes money. Do not turn its surrounding portfolio advice into a current personalized instruction. FS uses examples and lightly edited interview quotations; those are secondary quotations, not the original interviews or a transcript of the assigned video.\nDefine a task at a useful resolution: forecasting delays on one repeated local workflow is not competence in every company or market. List relevant mechanisms, assumptions, observable predictions, decision horizon, failure modes and what would refute the current view. A plain-language explanation helps expose gaps but is not by itself proof of accuracy. A credential, interest, job title or years of exposure can be evidence to investigate, not automatic mastery. Competence is context-dependent, may degrade as conditions change, and need not transfer to adjacent fields. A person can know a business well yet pay too much, face an unmodeled shock or lack permission to act.\nSelection toy:40 prospectively recorded binary forecasts, 24 correct. A public highlight reel shows 8 wins and no losses selected from those 40. Highlight accuracy 8/8=100%, but full-log accuracy 24/40=60%. An always-no baseline is correct 30/40=75% on that same set if only 10 events occurred. Raw 60% does not beat that baseline and cannot establish useful skill; use appropriate probability scores, calibration, costs and representative samples rather than comparing selective anecdotes. This does not prove the forecaster has no knowledge in every domain. Review uncertainty and drift; don't equate a small sample of wins with a stable perimeter.\nOutside the current evidence boundary, options include passing, gathering evidence through low-stakes practice, narrowing the task or seeking qualified independent help. A costly/irreversible deadline decision may require escalation or abstention, not indefinite delay or pretending competence. Delegation does not erase verification: check the expert's relevant record, incentives, scope, conflicts and explanation; weigh independent evidence, not just confidence. Expertise is not consent to spend or act for anyone. Deliberate practice with feedback can widen capability, but no course, amount of effort or elapsed time guarantees mastery. Record what was learned and what remains uncertain; no actual investment, message, appointment or external intervention is created.","quiz":[{"q":"A strong explanation of how a business works is enough to prove investing competence?","o":["Yes; fluent language establishes valuation and future returns.","No; compare scoped predictions, failure modes and independent evidence too.","Yes; confidence replaces relevant track-record checks.","No; explanation can never reveal a knowledge gap."],"a":1},{"q":"Expert at one repeated workflow. Claim about all adjacent fields?","o":["A job title alone establishes competence across every task.","Every adjacent field is automatically inside the same boundary.","One unrelated mistake disproves all original expertise.","Transfer must be checked against mechanisms, context and evidence."],"a":3},{"q":"40 forecasts/24 correct. Selected 8 wins shown. Full-log accuracy?","o":["60%.","100%, using only the selected winners.","20%, dividing highlighted wins by the whole log.","75%, using the separate baseline as the forecaster score."],"a":0},{"q":"Same 40 events, 10 yes/30 no. Always-no accuracy?","o":["50%, assuming balanced classes without evidence.","25%, counting yes events rather than no correct predictions.","60%, copying forecaster performance.","75%."],"a":3},{"q":"Forecaster 60%, always-no 75%. Which follows?","o":["The forecaster beats the baseline because 24 is more than 10.","Raw accuracy does not beat that baseline on this set; assess proper task-specific evidence.","Selected wins prove a 100% reliable circle of competence.","One sample proves the person is incompetent in every possible task."],"a":1},{"q":"Inside a scoped competence boundary. Guaranteed profitable result?","o":["No; price, uncertainty, constraints and shocks can still matter.","Yes; understanding eliminates all uncertainty.","Yes; knowing boundaries is an insurance against losses.","No; competence and decision quality can never be related."],"a":0},{"q":"Outside competence, high stakes and irreversible. Better response?","o":["Guess confidently because the deadline grants expertise.","Escalate, seek qualified help or abstain within the decision constraints.","Pass the whole choice to the loudest expert without checking scope.","Delay forever even when escalation/fallback is available."],"a":1},{"q":"Named expert offers a view. Sound review?","o":["Count ten copied endorsements as ten independent validations.","Treat every confident statement as verified authority.","Check relevant evidence, scope, conflicts and independent support.","Ignore all expertise because nobody has complete knowledge."],"a":2},{"q":"A new tool or market regime changes the task. Existing competence?","o":["Recheck transfer, drift and known failure modes.","Unchanged by definition because experience never expires.","Entirely erased in every domain without any assessment.","Guaranteed to expand automatically with the new terminology."],"a":0},{"q":"Deliberate practice and feedback imply?","o":["Certain expertise after any preset number of hours.","An opportunity to learn, not a fixed-time mastery guarantee or permission to act.","Automatic authority over other people's accounts.","No value unless every initial attempt succeeds."],"a":1}]},{"t":"The map is not the territory: models are tools, not truth","kick":"The capstone","mins":"20 mins","obj":["Treat models, metrics, interviews and observations as scoped representations/evidence with selection, timing and measurement limits.","Diagnose proxy-target failure without turning Goodhart's warning into a universal theorem or abandoning useful measurement."],"vids":[{"id":"EUE__gO-2hE","t":"The Map is Not the Territory","by":"Vishal Khandelwal","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."},{"id":"cuEJb__ZUnY","t":"The Map is not the Territory (Mental Model Monday)","by":"Zach Cresswell","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"reads":[{"t":"The Map and the Territory","u":"https://fs.blog/map-and-territory/","n":"Interview/log mismatch demands checking both, not anecdote supremacy."},{"t":"Goodhart2013retrospective on his law","u":"https://lelibellio.polytechnique.fr/uploads/volume9_num4/articles/goodharts_law.pdf","n":"Goodhart's 2013 retrospective explains the limited empirical 1975 origin and the broader warning about targets; distinguish Strathern's 1997 formulation. It is not a theorem that every target destroys all useful information."}],"ex":"Synthetic support-service measurement case, not a change to a real company's dashboard. FS page is an edited book excerpt, not the original Bezos interview or physics/history sources. It illustrates maps as reductions and the need to inspect how data is collected. Anecdotes are not automatically right when they conflict with a dashboard: interviews have recall, framing and selection limits; instruments/logs also have scope, timing and missing-data limits. Reconcile event definitions, source population, collection process, timestamps, units and exceptions before choosing a story. Observations are evidence about the world, not perfect unmediated access to every fact. A model can be useful within its tested domain without being exact or universally applicable.\nGoodhart 2013 retrospective describes his original 1975 line as limited empirical monetary-policy observation, a general warning about changed behavior/unintended consequences, not a universal mathematical theorem. The familiar target-measure wording is attributed there to Strathern 1997. No quote-memory gate or claim that every target inevitably destroys all information. Pressure on a proxy can change agents' and authorities' behavior; degradation can also arise from selection, definitions, errors or changing conditions rather than deliberate misconduct. Do not infer bad intent from a metric mismatch alone. Adding a second metric is not a guaranteed cure if it is also gameable or misses the objective.\nTicket toy, two separate weeks with equal completed reopen windows: before 100 tickets closed, 20 reopened; after 150 closed, 60 reopened. Close count rises 50%, but reopened share rises 20% to 40%. If a ticket is counted as durable precisely when it is closed and not reopened within the defined window, durable counts 80 before and 90 after:12.5% increase, not 50%. This denominator/window definition is stipulated; unclosed backlog, severity, customer outcomes, staffing/cost and later reopens are not provided. Neither gross closure growth nor a worse reopen share alone proves total welfare improved/worsened. A gross closure target can reward premature closure, a plausible mechanism to test, not proof it happened.\nSurvey toy:20 of 100 customers respond, 16 report satisfaction. Respondent satisfaction 80%, not observed satisfaction 80% for all 100. With binary satisfied/unsatisfied and no information on 80 nonrespondents, total satisfied could be 16 to 96, so population share 16%-96%. These bounds illustrate missing information, not an estimate that endpoints are equally likely. Don't call interviews inherently closer to truth than any dashboard. Select a proportionate evidence plan, protect sources, use outcome/quality checks and revisit model limitations; no actual customer contact, monitoring setup or metric change is authorized by this worksheet.","quiz":[{"q":"Dashboard and customer anecdotes disagree. First sound step?","o":["Average incompatible numbers before checking their meaning.","Assume anecdotes always outrank every quantitative source.","Assume a dashboard cannot have missing or mislabeled data.","Check definitions, sampling, collection timing and errors across both sources."],"a":3},{"q":"Goodhart 2013 retrospective supports which reading?","o":["Measurement itself must always be abandoned.","Every target makes every metric entirely useless in every context.","Every mismatch proves deliberate fraud by the measured person.","A policy warning about altered behavior/statistical relationships, not a universal theorem."],"a":3},{"q":"Closed 100->150 tickets. Count growth?","o":["33.33%, using the new count as denominator.","50%.","150%, confusing new level with percentage increase.","12.5%, using durable closure growth instead."],"a":1},{"q":"Reopened 20/100 before, 60/150 after. Reopen shares?","o":["80% and 60%, reporting nonreopened shares instead.","20% and 60%, keeping the old denominator after growth.","20% and 40%.","20% and 20%, ignoring increased reopen count."],"a":2},{"q":"Durable means closed/not reopened in window. Durable growth?","o":["20->60, or 200%, reporting reopens rather than durable closure.","100->150, or 50%, ignoring reopens.","80->90, or 11.11%, using the new count as base.","80->90, or 12.5%."],"a":3},{"q":"Same ticket toy. Total customer welfare improved?","o":["Proven by gross close count alone.","Unresolved; severity, backlog, cost and actual customer outcomes are missing.","Disproven by reopen share alone regardless of all other outcomes.","Exactly 12.5% better because durable count equals welfare."],"a":1},{"q":"16 satisfied among 20 respondents from 100 customers. Observed respondent share?","o":["20%, returning response rate rather than satisfaction.","16%, the lower possible whole-population share instead.","96%, assuming every nonrespondent satisfied.","80%, not an established population satisfaction rate."],"a":3},{"q":"Same survey, 80 unknown binary statuses. Whole-population satisfaction bounds?","o":["16% to 96%.","80% to 80%, assuming respondents represent everyone.","0% to 100%, discarding the 16 known satisfied/4 known unsatisfied.","16% to 80%, incorrectly capping at respondent share."],"a":0},{"q":"Proxy mismatch suggests premature closure gaming. Confirmed cause?","o":["Yes; a plausible story establishes motives without evidence.","Yes; metric disagreement always proves gaming.","Not yet; check mechanism and alternative explanations before attributing intent.","No; incentive effects can never change behavior."],"a":2},{"q":"Models are incomplete. Therefore?","o":["Treat interview recollections as perfect unmediated reality.","Discard every model because only exact descriptions have value.","Keep a detailed model unchanged regardless of new observations.","Use them within tested scope and revise when evidence/conditions warrant."],"a":3}]}];

const EC_MODULES=[{"t":"The Pyramid Principle: answer first, then prove it","kick":"Start here","mins":"30 mins","obj":["Write a clear provisional answer/ask with logically grouped evidence and visible assumptions for a defined audience/question.","Distinguish inductive grouping from deductive chain, scope MECE partitions and preserve caveats without treating communication structure as proof or permission to send."],"vids":[{"id":"elMPIxLYtoc","t":"Consultant Explains the Pyramid Principle","by":"Analyst Academy","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."},{"id":"j4Y3TdVVBCA","t":"The Minto Pyramid Principle Explained with Examples","by":"EPM","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"reads":[{"t":"The Pyramid Principle: Book Summary and Review (StrategyU)","u":"https://strategyu.co/pyramid-principle-partone/","n":"Secondary book summary, not the original book or an effectiveness guarantee."},{"t":"Book Summary: The Pyramid Principle (To Summarise)","u":"https://www.tosummarise.com/book-summary-the-pyramid-principle-by-barbara-minto/","n":"2025 secondary summary identifying a 2009 revised edition. Distinguish it from Minto's 1996 textbook; adapt MECE and answer-first structure to the audience rather than treating them as universal persuasion guarantees."},{"t":"Minto own concept/edition description","u":"https://barbaraminto.com/","n":"Read Minto's own explanation of a single main point and SCQ. Distinguish the textbook editions from later courses, summaries and commercial services."},{"t":"Barbara Minto interview: grouping rules and editions","u":"https://www.mckinsey.com/alumni/news-and-events/global-news/alumni-news/barbara-minto-mece-i-invented-it-so-i-get-to-say-how-to-pronounce-it","n":"Summary/same-kind/logical order and edition distinction, not randomized evidence or universal rules for every audience."}],"ex":"Drafting exercise only: use a fictional memo, not private messages from real contacts or a send action. Minto's own site presents a single governing point with grouped support and SCQ to clarify the reader's question. Her 1996 textbook is distinguished there from the 1987 book/unchanged 2002 reissue. StrategyU reviews a 2010 republication; To Summarise describes a 2009 revised edition. Do not merge these summaries into proof that they cover an identical revised text or the assigned videos. The McKinsey interview states summary, same-kind and logical-order rules. These sources support a writing framework, not a universal experimentally established law that every brain naturally has exactly seven slots or that every communication needs exactly three bullets.\nState audience, question and intended decision. Think through the evidence, perhaps bottom-up, before presenting a top-down answer. A governing thought should say something substantive, not merely list topics. The support should answer why/how in the relevant logic, be at comparable abstraction and state its connection to the conclusion. A clear outline does not make weak assumptions true or provide missing evidence. Use an honest provisional recommendation if uncertainty remains; do not hide caveats in an appendix just to sound decisive. Answer-first is a useful business default, not a ban on necessary context, indirect openings for a resistant audience, safety warnings, chronological procedures or other appropriate forms. The supplied summary explicitly notes audience exceptions; no measured time-saving/effectiveness guarantee is claimed.\nSynthetic proposal: a practice workflow takes 10 minutes per case for 100 cases/week. An offline trial on 20 cases averages 8 minutes per case, assumed same quality for illustration. Point: test a bounded pilot, not declare rollout complete. Conditional weekly time saving if 8 minutes generalizes to all 100 cases=(10-8)*100=200 minutes, or 3 hours 20 minutes. That is not cash savings or proven future throughput; setup, exceptions, error/rework and sample selection may alter the result. Reasons could be time potential and a bounded learning opportunity, with a separate quality/rollback gate. Owner/deadline/cost/authorization remain explicit unresolved action requirements; this worksheet sends no memo or approval.\nMECE toy partition over ticket ages solely:0<=age<24 hours and age>=24 hours covers nonnegative ages without overlap. Using 0<=age<=24 and age>=24 double-counts age 24; changing second group to age>48 leaves a gap. State the universe and boundary, not just the label MECE. A structural partition differs from a deductive chain (premise, related premise, implication); do not force every deductive step into mutually exclusive interchangeable categories or count supportive reasons as statistically independent merely because written separately. In a real multi-cause case, allow explicit shared causes/labels rather than inventing tidy disjointness. SCQ frames context/complication/question without manufacturing agreement or suppressing dissent. Revise the answer if a necessary premise fails; polished pyramid shape is not validation.","quiz":[{"q":"Governing thought versus topic heading?","o":["A list of nouns with no claim for the reader to evaluate.","A substantive answer/ask that the supporting points explain or defend.","The longest paragraph regardless of its relationship to evidence.","A confident conclusion that need not follow from the grouped support."],"a":1},{"q":"Good writing process must always start with final answer before analysis?","o":["Yes; evidence must be chosen only after the conclusion is fixed.","No; thinking can be bottom-up/iterative even when presentation is top-down.","Yes; editing structure removes the need to reason through premises.","No; clear writing must always reveal every discovery in chronological order."],"a":1},{"q":"Deductive premise chain compared with inductive reason group?","o":["Statistically independent evidence merely because each is a bullet.","Identical because all adjacent sentences must be mutually exclusive.","Different relationships; do not force dependent deductive steps into disjoint peer categories.","Unusable because deductions cannot appear anywhere in a memo."],"a":2},{"q":"Partition nonnegative ages:[0, 24), [24, infinity). At age 24?","o":["Neither group because 24 is always excluded.","Both groups because the first includes its open endpoint.","Only the second group.","It depends on ticket severity despite the stated age-only rule."],"a":2},{"q":"Instead:[0, 24] and [24, infinity). MECE?","o":["Not exhaustive because all age 24 tickets are omitted.","MECE because both labels contain the word age.","Not mutually exclusive:age 24 appears in both.","MECE regardless of overlap if totals look plausible."],"a":2},{"q":"Use [0, 24) and (48, infinity). Coverage?","o":["Only age 24 is uncovered; every age 25 to 48 belongs to the second group.","MECE over all nonnegative ages because the groups do not overlap.","Not exhaustive; ages 24 through 48 inclusive are uncovered.","Overlapping at every age above 48."],"a":2},{"q":"Time 10->8 minutes/case, 100 cases/week. Conditional weekly saving?","o":["200 minutes, or 3 hours 20 minutes.","2 minutes, total, ignoring the 100 cases.","800 minutes, returning the new total time.","20 minutes, applying the reduction to 10 rather than 100 cases."],"a":0},{"q":"20-case offline trial is faster. What can memo honestly claim?","o":["A limited trial result; weekly saving is conditional on generalization and quality/cost checks.","Verified permanent cash saving and approval for immediate rollout.","Guaranteed benefit for all future cases from the mean alone.","No learning possible because a small trial is never informative."],"a":0},{"q":"A reader needs context before a controversial recommendation. Answer-first?","o":["Adapt opening to audience/question while retaining a clear answer and evidence.","Always omit context even if the action cannot be understood.","Always hide the conclusion until the final sentence in every business memo.","Use ambiguity so no one can challenge assumptions."],"a":0},{"q":"All reasons are neatly grouped but necessary premise is false. Recommendation?","o":["Add more headings instead of addressing the premise.","Keep it because pyramid formatting validates the evidence.","Send it as approved because a draft is polished.","Recheck or revise it; structure cannot make a false premise true."],"a":3}]},{"t":"MECE: split the problem without gaps or overlaps","kick":"The consultant's sieve","mins":"20 mins","obj":["Check disjointness and coverage separately against a stated universe, units, boundaries and allocation rules.","Distinguish a valid identity or useful issue tree from a proven causal explanation or universally complete framework."],"vids":[{"id":"6kReKGrcoSc","t":"How To Be MECE","by":"Management Consulted","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."},{"id":"Vl7b_vpuLxE","t":"The MECE Principle","by":"PrepLounge","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"reads":[{"t":"The 5 Ways to be MECE in Case Interviews (Crafting Cases)","u":"https://www.craftingcases.com/the-5-ways-to-be-mece/","n":"Introductory framework for structuring a case. Five techniques are one publisher's taxonomy, not a guarantee of interview success."},{"t":"MECE Framework: Case Interview Example (Management Consulted)","u":"https://managementconsulted.com/mece-framework-case-interview-example/","n":"The television/geography example and the deodorant transcript are different cases. A useful approximation can help structure a case; it does not guarantee an offer."},{"t":"Five MECE structuring techniques: Part 2","u":"https://www.craftingcases.com/the-5-ways-to-be-mece-part-2/","n":"Read Part 2 for the five techniques, then test which structure fits the case and where its assumptions fail."},{"t":"Algebraic structures: definitions and limits","u":"https://www.craftingcases.com/the-5-ways-to-be-mece-part-3/","n":"Define the variables and units before using a formula. Dimensional consistency alone does not prove the formula is valid or guarantee an interview offer."},{"t":"Process structure and coverage assumptions","u":"https://www.craftingcases.com/the-5-ways-to-be-mece-part-4/","n":"Check rework loops and shared allocations before treating a process structure as complete and non-overlapping. It is not a universal MECE guarantee or an interview-frequency statistic."},{"t":"Segmentation and mix effects","u":"https://www.craftingcases.com/the-5-ways-to-be-mece-part-6/","n":"Separate changes in component prices from changes in the mix. A segmentation framework does not by itself establish case frequency or a root cause."}],"ex":"Synthetic issue-tree exercise only, not a real organizational restructure or data export. Mutually exclusive means at most one bucket for each item; collectively exhaustive means at least one bucket for every item in the stated universe. Together they give exactly one. Exclusivity alone does not establish coverage. A useful tree names its question, scope, time period, unit of analysis and assignment rules before testing gaps and overlaps. Causes can be shared; do not erase real interactions merely to make boxes neat. Separate a clean accounting allocation from a causal explanation.\nThe assigned CraftingCases Part 1 is largely promotional. Its recovered Part 2 names algebra, process, conceptual frameworks, segmentations and opposite words as its five approaches. This is a coaching taxonomy, not an exhaustive theorem or a job-offer guarantee. A named framework does not automatically meet MECE for any given problem. An equation needs valid definitions and data: matching dimensions is necessary for a physically meaningful equation but does not prove an identity. A process can include rework loops, parallel tasks and shared overhead; listing stages does not by itself make costs disjoint or cover every pathway. Opposite labels such as internal/external need a defined boundary and treatment of shared/unknown cases. A plus/minus profit decomposition is a signed identity, not a set of two positive shares or proof of underlying causes.\nSet toy: universe 100 orders; 60 mobile and 50 promotional, 20 both. Their union 60+50-20=90, so 10 neither. Mobile/promotional is overlapping and incomplete, not MECE. Four buckets mobile-only 40, promo-only 30, both 20, neither 10 form a partition of this universe with these definitions. Retain the both bucket rather than count it twice. Orders cannot be assigned by customer demographics without a specified allocation rule. Geography may require destination/billing/operating-country rules; mixed locations/unknowns cannot be silently dropped. A residual other bucket can preserve coverage if defined as the complement, but a large residual may hide important heterogeneity; its existence does not mean substantive analysis is complete.\nMix toy: channel A price 100, channel B price 50. Units 80 A+20 B gives average 90; changing only mix to 20 A+80 B gives average 60, with no within-channel price reduction. That is a 30 unit fall or 33.33% relative decline from 90, not a pricing-policy change. Segment data by a relevant hypothesis; no arbitrary segmentation guarantees a root cause. ManagementConsulted's page has a TV/geography prose example and a different deodorant publisher transcript; keep them distinct and historical. Its own conclusion allows useful approximations under time limits, not absolute completion. Disclose assumptions/overlaps/gaps, prioritize missing information and revise; no three-branch rule or guaranteed interview success.","quiz":[{"q":"Mutually exclusive alone means each universe item belongs to?","o":["Exactly one bucket even if coverage was never checked.","At most one bucket; coverage is a separate requirement.","At least one bucket, with any number of overlaps allowed.","Equal-sized buckets regardless of membership."],"a":1},{"q":"100 orders:60 mobile, 50 promo, 20 both. Union size?","o":["80, subtracting overlap twice.","110, adding both totals without removing overlap.","90.","20, returning intersection rather than union."],"a":2},{"q":"Same universe. Neither mobile nor promotional?","o":["0, assuming the two labels cover everyone.","10.","20, copying the overlap count.","30, subtracting mobile-only rather than union."],"a":1},{"q":"Which partition matches those counts?","o":["Mobile-only 40, promo-only 30, with both/neither discarded.","Mobile 60, promo 50, neither 10, with shared 20 counted twice.","Mobile-only 60, promo-only 50, both 20, neither 10.","Mobile-only 40, promo-only 30, both 20, neither 10."],"a":3},{"q":"Equation has matching units. Identity proven?","o":["Yes; a plausible label replaces testing the equation.","Yes; dimensions alone prove every numerical relationship.","No; valid definitions, relationships and data still need checks.","No; dimensional checks are never useful."],"a":2},{"q":"Process stages include rework and shared overhead. Sum costs blindly?","o":["No; define scope, repeat paths and allocation to avoid omissions/double counts.","Yes; stage labels automatically prove disjoint complete costs.","Yes; shared costs must be charged in full to every stage.","No; every process model is useless if any task repeats."],"a":0},{"q":"Channel A 100, B 50; 80 A/20 B units. Average price?","o":["90.","75, the unweighted mean ignoring mix.","100, using only the largest channel price.","50, using only the cheaper channel."],"a":0},{"q":"Same prices; 20 A/80 B units. New average and interpretation?","o":["75; use an unweighted mean regardless of volumes.","60; both channel prices must have fallen.","90; averages cannot change if prices are constant.","60; mix alone changed the average, not within-channel prices."],"a":3},{"q":"The five approaches from CraftingCases imply?","o":["An exhaustive mathematical classification of every possible problem.","A source-specific structuring toolkit whose fit/assumptions need checks.","A guarantee of a consulting offer when memorized.","A proof that any conceptual framework is automatically MECE."],"a":1},{"q":"A defined other bucket covers every unassigned item. Analysis complete?","o":["Yes; the larger other is, the stronger the root-cause proof.","Yes; residual membership proves every cause is understood.","No; complement buckets can never provide coverage.","Coverage may hold, but residual heterogeneity and causal questions remain."],"a":3}]},{"t":"SCQA: storylining that lands","kick":"Narrative","mins":"20 mins","obj":["Write SCQA with factual context, a supported complication, the decision question and an answer that actually addresses it.","Adapt ordering to the audience while preserving uncertainty, evidence and genuine action authority instead of manufacturing urgency."],"vids":[{"id":"0JVV1H_uD_M","t":"SCQA: Situation, Complication, Question, Answer - A Consulting Problem Solving Framework","by":"StrategyU","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."},{"id":"HnlWiVs9P5o","t":"PowerPoint Storytelling: How McKinsey, Bain and BCG create compelling presentations","by":"Analyst Academy","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"reads":[{"t":"SCQA - Overview and Practical Examples (Corporate Finance Institute)","u":"https://corporatefinanceinstitute.com/resources/career/scqa/","n":"Use SCQA to organize context, complication, question and answer. Answer-first is a variant, not a proven persuasion rule for every audience."},{"t":"SCQA: What is it and how does it work? (Analytic Storytelling)","u":"https://analytic-storytelling.com/scqa-what-is-it-how-does-it-work-and-how-can-it-help-me/","n":"The therapy and gamification story illustrates narrative structure; it is not clinical evidence or a clinical quiz fact."}],"ex":"Fictional writing practice, not a real request to a professor, manager, client or patient. SCQA names Situation, Complication, Question, Answer. The situation supplies relevant context and common ground, but a writer must not invent agreement or label disputed facts as settled. The complication identifies a change, threat, opportunity or unresolved hurdle; it need not mean the status quo is impossible or that a crisis exists. The question should follow from that context, and the answer must address it with reasons and limitations. The question can be explicit or clear from context; narrative order need not be four literal sentences. Distinguish the question from a testable hypothesis and distinguish both from evidence that a proposed cause is true.\nThe assigned CFI guide describes a steel-capacity example and an answer-first variant; its before/after letter examples are images; use the written explanation for this exercise. Analytic Storytelling offers a physical-therapy gamification narrative. That is an illustrative communication example, not clinical evidence for its 70% statistic, motivation diagnosis, causal effects or full-recovery promise. These are not scored medical facts. A coherent causal story still needs independent evidence; an appealing format does not guarantee understanding, agreement or ethical persuasion. Avoid panic language, selective facts and invented precision.\nSynthetic example: one practice queue receives 50 requests/day and completes 40/day, starts with 100 pending. Under constant rates/no other flows for 5 days, pending 150. Situation: the queue has 100 pending and handles this type of request. Complication: under current stipulated rates it adds 10 pending/day. Question: which feasible authorized option would stop growth without hiding quality/cost risk? Answer: evaluate an additional 10 completed/day or a 10/day reduction in accepted inflow, with quality, cost, staffing, rights and approval checks. Reaching 50 completions/day only stabilizes backlog at 100 if changed immediately; it does not clear it. At 60 completions/day with inflow 50, backlog falls 10/day and clears 100 in 10 days if rates/capacity/availability persist. These are arithmetic scenarios, not evidence staffing will achieve them or an actual staffing decision. A forecast should carry its assumptions, not present projected counts as observed.\nAn impatient audience may benefit from answer-first with the situation/complication supplied next; a reader lacking context or resisting a premise may need context earlier. A slide's action title can state a takeaway as a full sentence but must not claim an observed result when it is a recommendation or conditional forecast. Neither visual hierarchy nor SCQA authorizes sending a message. Draft a suitable answer-first and context-first version, test whether the same question/evidence survives both, and remove repetition without deleting necessary caveats. No external message, reminder, clinical advice, task allocation or spending is created.","quiz":[{"q":"A complication must mean the current state is completely untenable?","o":["Frame the current situation as a crisis whenever that makes the recommendation easier to accept.","No; it may be a change, opportunity or hurdle supported by evidence.","Escalate the urgency of the complication beyond the evidence if the audience already accepts the current state.","Put the final recommendation inside every complication so the narrative cannot leave an open question."],"a":1},{"q":"Situation includes a disputed assumption. Best handling?","o":["Label it and its evidence/uncertainty instead of pretending common agreement.","Present the assumption as shared background first, leaving its disputed status to supporting notes.","Remove contrary evidence from the situation so the reader can follow one coherent starting point.","Use the story's internal consistency to treat the disputed assumption as established for the recommendation."],"a":0},{"q":"Queue 100; in 50/day, out 40/day, 5 days. End backlog?","o":["50, subtracting the net inflow rather than adding.","150.","110, applying one day instead of five.","200, adding arrivals but ignoring completions."],"a":1},{"q":"Same start 100; immediately out 50/day, in 50/day. After 5 days?","o":["150; ignore increased output.","0; balanced flow automatically clears stock.","100; growth stops but existing backlog remains.","50; balanced flow drains ten per day."],"a":2},{"q":"Start 100, in 50/day, out 60/day. Time to clear under fixed assumptions?","o":["Never, because inflow is nonzero.","2 days, dividing by inflow rather than net reduction.","5 days, assuming twice the actual net outflow.","10 days."],"a":3},{"q":"Question asks how to stop queue growth. Answer only praises team morale. Fit?","o":["Keep the answer unchanged because the SCQA outline was already approved for the draft.","Treat morale improvements as a sufficient answer without showing how arrival or completion rates change.","Use the morale sentence as the answer because it is concise and positive, and leave feasibility unstated.","Insufficient unless it links supported feasible actions to the actual question."],"a":3},{"q":"Gamification story claims full recovery. Clinical conclusion?","o":["Not established by a writing illustration; need appropriate clinical evidence.","Established because the story follows SCQA.","Guaranteed for every patient who finds games entertaining.","Unrelated to evidence because narrative claims cannot be checked."],"a":0},{"q":"Impatient audience already knows context. Ordering option?","o":["Delete the answer to build suspense.","Lead with a clear answer, then concise supporting context and limits.","Always add four paragraphs of background first.","Hide uncertainty because an executive is reading."],"a":1},{"q":"Slide title for untested queue pilot should say?","o":["A limited pilot could test whether capacity rises without quality loss.","The pilot has eliminated backlog, although no trial ran.","The proposed target is already verified performance.","Every team will clear queues in 10 days regardless of starting stock."],"a":0},{"q":"A story is coherent and engaging. What follows?","o":["The audience must agree if it understood the text.","Its causal explanation is proven by narrative coherence.","It may help presentation, but facts, causality and agreement still need checks.","The writer may send it on anyone's behalf without review."],"a":2}]},{"t":"Write like an executive: memos that get read","kick":"Capstone","mins":"25 mins","obj":["Use BLUF and a clear decision/ask with known owners, timing, sources and material caveats without false precision.","Edit for brevity and completeness while separating historical memo practices from current policy or guaranteed reader behavior."],"vids":[{"id":"e47wAgIhZ7o","t":"Jeff Bezos on banning PowerPoint in meetings at Amazon","by":"Lex Clips","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."},{"id":"NE8DX8_Xg4E","t":"Jeff Bezos on Writing Memos at Amazon","by":"PodiumVC","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"reads":[{"t":"How to write an Amazon-style narrative memo (SEO MBA)","u":"https://newsletter.seomba.com/p/how-to-write-an-amazon-style-narrative","n":"Support specific numbers with evidence and keep material uncertainty visible."},{"t":"Why Jeff Bezos makes Amazon execs read 6-page memos (CNBC)","u":"https://www.cnbc.com/2018/04/23/what-jeff-bezos-learned-from-requiring-6-page-memos-at-amazon.html","n":"Use the 2017 shareholder letter as historical primary evidence for Amazon's memo and revision practices, not current policy for every company."},{"t":"Amazon2017letter: historical six-page memo practice","u":"https://www.aboutamazon.com/news/company-news/2017-letter-to-shareholders","n":"Study the letter's discussion of high standards, domain knowledge, scope and revision. A silent reading period does not guarantee understanding."},{"t":"Army writing lesson: accuracy,BLUF and completeness","u":"https://rdl.train.army.mil/catalog-ws/view/100.ATSC/5D2BDA88-E8E5-4119-89D1-3FC243778F94-1308929329079/is1460/ch3.htm","n":"Distinguish a purpose statement from a thesis and put the main point first. Brevity must not sacrifice clarity or completeness; a historical Army writing lesson is not civilian workplace policy."},{"t":"2015Army email article: editing context","u":"https://www.army.mil/article/151267/mastering_the_art_of_email","n":"Proofread, read aloud and put the main point first. The historical article's 300-word tip, signature limits and traffic statistics are not universal current workplace rules."}],"ex":"Fictional executive-writing worksheet, not permission to inspect/send private messages. Amazon's 2017 shareholder letter, published 18 April 2018, describes six-page narrative memos, silent study-hall reading and shared revision. It explicitly says memo quality varies and discusses realistic effort/team skill. This is historical primary evidence, not a verified current universal company ban on every slide or a rule that every organization needs six pages. Time made available to read is not proof everyone read, understood or agreed. A week-or-more illustration about high-quality memos is not an ETA for a short status email or a guarantee of quality after elapsed time. SEO MBA 2023 advice and CNBC 2018 report are distinct secondary sources; their 2004 email quotation is not independently checked against its original here. The quoted email concerns a particular senior-team context, not an automatic policy instruction for the learner.\nBLUF means bottom line up front. Army training distinguishes a purpose statement('this memo discusses options')from a thesis/conclusion/recommendation('test option A under these conditions'). Put the relevant bottom line and actual ask/decision near the opening, along with a real deadline if one is established, not an invented deadline or commitment. Relevant context, evidence, assumptions, alternatives, downside and next step follow. A one-idea paragraph should support a coherent thought; not every paragraph must contain one sentence or exactly three. A concise source-backed number is useful only when its definition, unit, time and uncertainty are honest. If evidence is qualitative or a number unknown, say that rather than manufacture precision. Do not convert every adjective into an invented measurement or delete a material hedge merely to sound confident.\nSynthetic revision: draft 300 words to 200 words is 100 words removed, one-third reduction. 300 to 210 is 90 removed, 30% reduction, not one-third. The point is clearer reasoning without losing necessary meaning, not a quota. Preserve assumptions, exclusions, risks and the exact scope of an approval. The Army writing lesson explicitly says never sacrifice clarity for brevity. Proofread facts, recipient references, attachments, units and source links as well as spelling; a grammar checker cannot establish truth. Read aloud/review/iterate when useful, but no ritual ensures approval or correctness.\nToy business case: save 2 minutes per case on 600 cases/month =>1200 minutes=20 hours/month if generalizable. At a stated capacity valuation 500 per hour, capacity value 10000/month. With setup cost 15000 and three-month recurring running cost 2000/month, three-month net modeled value=3*(10000-2000)-15000=9000. This is an assumed capacity valuation, not verified cash saving; fixed salaries need not fall and freed time must be usable. Price/quality/volume/measurement and rollout permission remain unresolved. A memo should distinguish observed sample, conditional projection and proposed action, not say savings are realized just because a spreadsheet adds up. A draft is not sent, an ask is not approved, and no actual purchase, meeting, employment or external commitment is created.","quiz":[{"q":"BLUF purpose-only line:this memo discusses options. Sufficient bottom line?","o":["No; state the relevant conclusion/recommendation or actual decision/ask too.","Yes; a topic label always gives the reader the outcome.","Yes; the reader must infer the requested decision from attachments.","No; BLUF requires a universal six-page narrative every time."],"a":0},{"q":"Amazon 2017 letter describes study-hall reading. Safest claim?","o":["Silent time guarantees comprehension and agreement.","Every meeting today worldwide necessarily bans all slides.","A historical described practice, not proof all readers understood or current universal policy.","Exactly six pages always produce a good decision."],"a":2},{"q":"No reliable quantitative estimate exists. Replace vague adjective with?","o":["No caveat, because confidence is more important than uncertainty.","A precise-looking percentage chosen to make the memo persuasive.","A rounded number from an unrelated population without attribution.","A clear qualitative statement plus evidence limits, not an invented number."],"a":3},{"q":"300 words->200 words. Fraction removed?","o":["30%, using 90 words rather than 100.","Two-thirds, the fraction remaining instead.","One-third, about 33.33%.","50%, using final 200 as denominator."],"a":2},{"q":"300 words->210 words. Reduction?","o":["42.86%, using the final 210 as denominator.","One-third, because any roughly shorter draft qualifies.","70%, returning fraction remaining.","30%."],"a":3},{"q":"A mandatory caveat prevents a neat one-third cut. Better choice?","o":["Keep necessary meaning and shorten elsewhere; the quota is not the objective.","Delete the caveat so the percentage target is achieved.","Turn unknown assumptions into facts to save words.","Keep only the ask and remove all material risks."],"a":0},{"q":"2 minutes saved/case, 600 cases/month. Conditional time value?","o":["1200 hours/month, forgetting minute conversion.","1200 minutes=20 hours/month.","300 minutes/month, dividing cases by minutes.","2 hours/month, ignoring volume and units."],"a":1},{"q":"Capacity value 10000/month, running 2000/month, setup 15000. Three-month net modeled value?","o":["15000, omitting running cost.","24000, omitting one-time setup.","9000.","-7000, using only one month of net benefit."],"a":2},{"q":"Capacity value equals guaranteed cash saved?","o":["No; time can never have any economic value.","Yes; valuing time automatically reduces paid salaries.","Yes; spreadsheet arithmetic proves funds reached the bank.","No; usable freed time, actual expense changes and assumptions need checks."],"a":3},{"q":"Polished memo asks for a purchase and contains a deadline. Approval?","o":["Established by professional formatting and a clear ask.","Not established; an ask/draft and invented schedule do not authorize a commitment.","Established if a reader was given silent reading time.","Established by cutting exactly one-third of words."],"a":1}]}];

const IB_MODULES=[{"t":"What a bank actually sells","kick":"Start here","mins":"1.5 hrs","obj":["Distinguish transaction advisory/underwriting mandates from the broader banking group and map ECM/DCM/M&A to a specified financing plan.","Calculate stipulated engagement fees without inventing actual advisers, universal fee rates, client identities or underwriting guarantees."],"vids":[{"id":"iCQ1g_u25bE","t":"How Do Investment Banks Make Money?","by":"Primerli","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."},{"id":"nd5OA02kbq0","t":"WTF Do Investment Bankers Actually Do?","by":"How Money Works","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."},{"id":"E3UUpIArHek","t":"Day in the life of an Investment Banking Analyst","by":"Corporate Finance Institute","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"reads":[{"t":"Investment banking: the industry map","u":"https://mergersandinquisitions.com/investment-banking/","n":"Roles vary across firms and groups. Treat salary and career claims as dated source opinions, not promises."},{"t":"Investment banking overview","u":"https://corporatefinanceinstitute.com/resources/careers/investment-banking-overview/","n":"Use this reference to study Investment banking overview. Apply the source's date and jurisdiction; it is not a current Indian legal or accounting opinion."},{"t":"Fee form variation: FSG","u":"https://mergersandinquisitions.com/financial-sponsors-group-fsg/","n":"Retainers and completed-deal fees vary by group and engagement. Read the actual client, capacity, fee base and trigger before calculating an adviser's revenue."},{"t":"Restructuring fee forms,not live quote","u":"https://mergersandinquisitions.com/restructuring-investment-banking-group/","n":"Fee examples are historical, not current quotes or universal contract terms."}],"ex":"Synthetic mandate map, not actual financial advice, fundraising or a bank engagement. Investment banking division commonly provides M&A advice and capital-raising execution; a full-service group may also contain trading, research, asset management, commercial and retail banking. Do not infer that every bank has every desk or that the same legal entity has every activity/permission. Product and industry teams can collaborate; labels and responsibilities vary by institution. ECM concerns equity issuance, DCM debt issuance, M&A acquisition/divestiture advice, but an all-cash acquisition need not require new equity or bonds if already funded. A mandate is not a trade, and transaction advice is not a guarantee of closing, financing or investment returns.\nRead the engagement and verified company/deal disclosures to identify who retained the adviser, in which capacity and who owes the fee. Sell-side clients can be a company, selling shareholders or another specified party; a buyer appearing in the news does not authenticate either side's adviser. Do not guess names or fee payers from brand familiarity. An announcement is not a completed deal. A success fee can create incentives, but incentives alone do not prove misconduct; check scope/conflicts and disclosures. Advisory retainers, completion fees and financing/underwriting fees vary with contract and activity. Career-source examples establish possible fee forms, not current fee quotes or universal proportions.\nToy contract explicitly states: nonrefundable retainer 2 units, credited against a 3%-of-100 deal value completion fee; no other fees, expenses, taxes or interest. If it closes on that basis, total fee 3 and additional closing payment 1; if it fails with no completion fee, total paid 2. Without the credit provision, adding retainer 2 and completion fee 3 would give 5, but that's a different contract. Do not add both under the credited contract or assume an actual mandate has this wording. Fees paid to an adviser do not all become profit; staffing and other costs remain. A financing fee is separate only if the specified mandate says so.\nUnderwriting terms also matter. A firm commitment involves the underwriter purchasing agreed securities and bearing unsold-inventory risk under the contract; best efforts is not a guaranteed full purchase; all-or-none makes completion conditional on the specified full placement condition. These are educational distinctions, not a jurisdiction-specific legal opinion or proof every issue uses one simple form. Toy issuance 100 units gross with 5 units stipulated fees/expenses gives 95 net issuer proceeds, not 100; this is neither a live price nor an approval. For a fictional acquisition funded by existing cash plus new bonds, M&A and DCM may be relevant; no automatic ECM work. No bank contact, security offering, fee payment, investment, account change or deal action is performed.","quiz":[{"q":"IB division compared with full-service banking group?","o":["Trading, research and retail accounts are interchangeable with a specific M&A mandate.","Every bank only sells M&A advice and cannot lend or take deposits anywhere.","Every bank has exactly the same desks and legal permissions worldwide.","The group may contain other businesses; division mandates do not define every group activity."],"a":3},{"q":"Fictional acquisition uses existing cash plus new bonds. Likely relevant desks?","o":["Only ECM because every acquisition requires new shares.","M&A advice and DCM; ECM is not implied without an equity issue.","Only retail deposit operations because cash is involved.","All desks must participate regardless of financing or mandate."],"a":1},{"q":"To identify sell-side adviser client/fee payer, first check?","o":["The buyer's identity, which automatically determines the seller's adviser.","The bank with the best-known logo in the target country.","Engagement/disclosures identifying party, capacity and fee obligations.","A guess based on banks used in unrelated past deals."],"a":2},{"q":"Retainer 2 credited against 3% of 100 completion fee. Total if closed?","o":["2 units, ignoring the completion fee.","5 units, adding the credited retainer twice.","3 units.","1 unit, returning only incremental closing payment."],"a":2},{"q":"Same contract. Additional payment due at closing?","o":["0 units, because retainer 2 exceeds the full 3 fee.","3 units, ignoring retainer credit.","5 units, adding retainer and full completion fee.","1 unit."],"a":3},{"q":"Same contract, fails, no completion fee. Total paid?","o":["2 units under the stipulated nonrefundable retainer.","0 units, because every advisory fee is always contingent.","3 units, charging completion fee despite its condition failing.","5 units, charging both despite no completion."],"a":0},{"q":"Best-efforts underwriting means?","o":["The bank guarantees full issue purchase in every case.","No automatic obligation to purchase every unsold security under that educational form.","The issuer necessarily receives full gross proceeds even if nothing sells.","The bank is paid only if the share price rises after listing."],"a":1},{"q":"Firm commitment distinction?","o":["The underwriter purchases agreed securities and bears specified unsold inventory risk.","The underwriter merely provides nonbinding demand estimates with no purchase obligation.","All investors are guaranteed profitable exits.","The issuer becomes exempt from disclosures or deal conditions."],"a":0},{"q":"Issuance gross 100, stipulated fees/expenses 5. Net issuer proceeds?","o":["95 units.","100 units, ignoring fees/expenses.","105 units, adding issuance costs as proceeds.","5 units, returning only fees/expenses."],"a":0},{"q":"A success fee incentive is disclosed. What follows?","o":["The fee becomes pure profit with no operating costs.","Misconduct is proven by the fee condition alone.","The bank must close the deal whatever regulators or parties decide.","Review conflicts/evidence; the incentive alone does not prove misconduct or guaranteed closing."],"a":3}]},{"t":"Accounting for bankers: three statements, one story","kick":"The language","mins":"3 hrs","obj":["Link accrual profit, operating balances, cash and equity under explicit transaction/tax assumptions without plug entries.","Distinguish the common three-statement model from full financial reporting and define operating/free cash flow metrics consistently."],"vids":[{"id":"x0nejWua7vA","t":"3 Financial Statements Explained","by":"Brian Feroldi","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."},{"id":"qaDFkAh3J4k","t":"Session 4: Financial Statement Analysis","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."},{"id":"1AUBZbbk53g","t":"Build a 3-Statement Financial Model From Scratch","by":"Josh Aharonoff","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"reads":[{"t":"The 3-statement model guide","u":"https://mergersandinquisitions.com/3-statement-model/","n":"Trace the links between the three statements. Cash-flow sign conventions and simplified links depend on definitions; they are not universal formulas for every account movement."},{"t":"The three financial statements","u":"https://corporatefinanceinstitute.com/resources/accounting/three-financial-statements/","n":"Link the income statement, balance sheet and cash-flow statement. These are the core modeling statements, not every reporting requirement; cash-flow statements can use different presentation methods."},{"t":"SEC financial-statement educational guide","u":"https://www.sec.gov/about/reports-publications/beginners-guide-financial-statements","n":"The SEC guide describes four main financial statements. Net income and cash are related but different. This is educational guidance, not current Indian reporting law."}],"ex":"All amounts below are synthetic lakh-unit changes from a previously balanced opening balance sheet. No tax, VAT, interest, other expenses, dividends, FX or other transactions unless explicitly stated; not current Indian accounting/tax advice. Three-statement modeling links income statement, balance sheet and cash flow statement; it does not mean a complete report has only three statements. SEC's basic guide also lists statement of equity and emphasizes notes/MD&A. Balance sheet is a point-in-time stock; income/cash flow statements cover a period. Assets=liabilities+equity must reconcile without unexplained cash/debt plugs. Two-sided postings alone are not evidence transactions were measured/classified correctly; omissions can still balance.\nInventory toy:buy 10 inventory on credit:inventory+10, accounts payable+10, no cash movement or immediate cost of sales for unsold inventory. Then sell half for 8 cash, with 5 inventory cost:revenue+8, cost of sales+5, net income+3, cash+8, inventory net+5, payables+10, retained earnings/equity+3. Total assets+13=liabilities+10 plus equity+3. Indirect operating cash flow=NI 3-change inventory 5+change payables 10=8. Paying the 10 supplier balance later reduces cash 10 and payable 10, no second inventory expense; cash movement can be a 10 outflow even though that payment itself creates no new loss. These are changes, not impossible negative opening cash assumptions. Recognizing a profit is not collecting every sale's cash.\nSeparate depreciation toy 5 expense, no tax effects:net income-5, net PP&E-5, equity-5; indirect CFO=-5+5=0 if no other changes. The addback reverses this noncash expense in cash flow reconciliation; it doesn't create 5 cash or erase original capital expenditure. With tax effects, actual tax payment timing and deductibility need explicit modeling; do not impose a fictional universal current rate. Cash capex generally affects investing cash flow in this basic model; depreciation affects allocation of asset cost across periods. Cash flow statement direct and indirect presentation differ; starting every cash flow statement with NI is not a universal rule.\nDefined FCFF toy=EBIT*(1-t)+D&A-CapEx-increase in operating NWC, assuming ordinary operating business, consistent cash-tax model and no other adjustments. Stipulate EBIT 20, t 25%, D&A 4, CapEx 6, NWC increase 3=>10. Borrowing 5 is financing cash flow, not operating performance or an addition to this defined FCFF. CFO minus CapEx can be a useful defined metric but is not automatically unlevered FCFF if CFO includes interest/other financing-related items or classification differs. Working capital definitions/sign conventions must be stated. Earnings and cash both require scrutiny; cash can be affected by timing, classifications, one-off asset sales or borrowing. Rising profit/falling cash calls for reconciliation, not a universal single diagnosis or blanket claim cash is unmanipulable. No actual financial report, loan, trade, tax filing or external action is made.","quiz":[{"q":"Buy 10 inventory on credit, no other effects. Initial changes?","o":["Equity+10 and cash+10 without an obligation.","Inventory+10, cash-10, payables unchanged.","NI-10 immediately even though inventory remains unsold.","Inventory+10, payables+10, cash/NI unchanged."],"a":3},{"q":"Then sell half of that inventory for 8 cash, COGS 5. NI change?","o":["+8, ignoring cost of sales.","+3.","-2, expensing all 10 instead of half 5.","+5, reporting remaining inventory rather than profit."],"a":1},{"q":"After both transactions. Asset/liability/equity changes?","o":["Assets+3=liabilities+10-equity 7.","Assets+8=liabilities 0+equity+8, ignoring inventory/payables.","Assets+15=liabilities+10+equity+5.","Assets+13=liabilities+10+equity+3."],"a":3},{"q":"Indirect CFO from combined toy NI 3, inventory increase 5, payables increase 10?","o":["8.","18, adding inventory increase instead of subtracting.","-12, subtracting both operating balances.","3, ignoring working capital changes."],"a":0},{"q":"Later pay supplier 10, no new purchase/tax. Transaction itself?","o":["Cash+10 and payables-10, turning payment into receipt.","NI-10 again and payable unchanged.","Cash-10, payables-10, no second expense for already recognized inventory.","Inventory-10 even though the payment does not move goods."],"a":2},{"q":"Separate depreciation 5, no tax or other changes. Indirect CFO change?","o":["-5, because every expense is a current cash payment.","+5, because addback creates new cash.","0, as NI-5 plus noncash addback 5.","-10, subtracting depreciation twice."],"a":2},{"q":"A three-statement model means only three financial statements exist?","o":["Yes; all other statements are invalid by definition.","No; full reporting can include equity statement, notes and other disclosures.","Yes; the SEC guide excludes any equity statement.","No; income and cash flow are actually one identical statement."],"a":1},{"q":"EBIT 20, t 25%, D&A 4, CapEx 6, NWC increase 3. Defined FCFF?","o":["22, adding CapEx instead of subtracting.","15, stopping after operating tax adjustment.","16, omitting CapEx.","10."],"a":3},{"q":"Borrow 5. Effect on that defined FCFF?","o":["None directly; borrowing is financing, not this operating cash flow metric.","Adds 5 to FCFF because all cash receipts are operating.","Subtracts 5 from FCFF because debt proceeds are an expense.","Always eliminates taxes and makes FCFF unlevered."],"a":0},{"q":"Balance sheet balances and cash increased. Financial quality proven?","o":["Yes; balanced totals prove all transactions are correct.","No; check notes, classification, timing, omissions and financing/nonrecurring sources.","Yes; cash is impossible to affect through timing or classification.","No; balanced accounts imply fraud automatically."],"a":1}]},{"t":"Valuation I: comps and precedents - pricing by analogy","kick":"Market truth","mins":"2.5 hrs","obj":["Build matched value/metric multiples with a stated date, period, currency, consolidation and EV-equity bridge.","Use comparable and precedent ranges as conditional market evidence, not price truth, universal controlpremiums or investment recommendations."],"vids":[{"id":"DrdqhwzRlJQ","t":"Financial Modeling Quick Lessons: Trading Comps (Part 1)","by":"Wall Street Prep","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."},{"id":"dpKvb7D9ek4","t":"Trading Comps Valuation Tutorial (Ex J.P. Morgan)","by":"rareliquid","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."},{"id":"g1R52gCsRuU","t":"Session 26: Acquisition Valuation","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"reads":[{"t":"Comparable company analysis","u":"https://www.wallstreetprep.com/knowledge/comparable-company-analysis-comps/","n":"Use matched enterprise-value and revenue periods. The source's TEV/revenue bullet contains an inconsistent Revenue/EBITDA label; follow explicit units and denominators, not the typo."},{"t":"Precedent transaction analysis","u":"https://breakingintowallstreet.com/kb/valuation/precedent-transaction-analysis/","n":"Match transaction multiples, enterprise-to-equity bridges and disclosure quality. Precedents may span a wider range without necessarily being higher; a takeover premium is not universal."},{"t":"NYU value/EBITDA derivation","u":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/vebitda.pdf","n":"Value-to-EBITDA multiples depend on taxes, reinvestment and operating economics. Historical sector examples are not current-market facts."}],"ex":"Invented valuation worksheet only, not a buy/sell recommendation for a real security. Peer selection needs similar business mix, growth, margins, risk, geography/accounting and capital intensity; exchange or founding year alone is insufficient. Align valuation date, currency/units, LTM/NTM periods, consolidation and adjustment definitions. Do not combine a current EV with an arbitrary stale earnings period without explaining it. Scrub recurring/nonrecurring items consistently; calling an expense one-time does not prove it won't recur. Record inclusions/exclusions; don't silently delete an inconvenient outlier or imply the median is objective intrinsic value.\nEV toy:common equity 100+debt 30+preferred 5+noncontrolling interest 10-nonoperating cash 20=125 under the stated bridge; no leases, other assets/claims, pension adjustments or dilution. EV/EBITDA 25=5 x; EV/revenue 100=1.25 x; common equity/net income 10=10 x P/E. Numerator claimholders must match denominator:EV with consolidated pre-financing operating metric, equity with relevant equity earnings. Market cap+net debt is only a simplified case when other claims/assets absent and cash treatment consistent. NCI adjustment matters if earnings consolidate 100% of a subsidiary; don't mix parent-only numerator with all subsidiary EBITDA. Lease/debt-like items and cash needed for operations need a consistent policy in real comps.\nAssigned WSP page's TEV/Revenue bullet incorrectly prints Revenue/EBITDA. Correct multiple is enterprise value divided by revenue, as corroborated by BIWS page's 2500 EV/1000 revenue=2.5 x example and the matching rule. Don't propagate the typo. EV/EBITDA uses a pre-interest/pre-tax denominator, but value/multiple isn't tax neutral or immune to leverage, capex, NWC, growth/risk. NYU's derivation explicitly includes taxes, reinvestment and working capital. EBITDA is not free cash flow; negative/zero EBITDA can make a usual positive-business multiple meaningless or undefined, not automatically a bargain. For financial institutions, sector-specific metrics may be more useful.\nPeer toy 8 x, 10 x, 12 x gives median 10 x. Applying to target 15 EBITDA implies 150 EV, not 150 common equity. If target debt 30, preferred 5, NCI 10, nonoperating cash 20, same bridge =>equity 125. This range/model is conditional, not a current market price or evidence target deserves exact median. Precedents reflect acquisition contexts, time, control/synergies, competition/distress and incomplete disclosures; not invariably higher multiples than trading comps. BIWS expressly warns of wider/more variable results. Offer 120 versus stated unaffected price 100 =>20% premium, not 20% synergy value or universal fair price. Define reference price/date; rumors or changed markets can alter interpretation. Past purchase prices are data to check, not immutable valuation truth. No live peer data, premium quote, share purchase, mandate or adviser claim made.","quiz":[{"q":"Equity 100, debt 30, preferred 5, NCI 10, nonoperating cash 20, stated bridge. EV?","o":["165, adding cash rather than subtracting.","110, using only equity+net debt and omitting preferred/NCI.","125.","65, subtracting debt instead of adding it."],"a":2},{"q":"Same EV 125, EBITDA 25. EV/EBITDA?","o":["4 x, using equity 100 instead of EV.","5 x.","0.2 x, inverting the multiple.","1.25 x, using revenue 100 instead of EBITDA 25."],"a":1},{"q":"Same EV 125, revenue 100. EV/Revenue?","o":["4 x, using Revenue/EBITDA 25.","1.25 x, not Revenue/EBITDA.","0.8 x, inverting EV/revenue.","5 x, using EBITDA instead of revenue."],"a":1},{"q":"EV/EBITDA is before interest/tax in denominator. Tax neutral?","o":["Yes; EV/EBITDA always equals cash flow margin.","Yes; tax rates can never change operating business value.","No; tax, reinvestment, risk and other differences can affect value and multiples.","No; it can never compare different-leverage companies at all."],"a":2},{"q":"Peer 8 x, 10 x, 12 x; target EBITDA 15. Median-implied EV?","o":["150.","125, already subtracting the separate bridge.","450, using the sum rather than median of multiples.","10, returning median multiple instead of value."],"a":0},{"q":"EV 150, target debt 30, preferred 5, NCI 10, cash 20. Equity value?","o":["125.","150, confusing EV with common equity.","175, adding claims/subtracting cash in reverse.","100, subtracting debt and cash without the other claims."],"a":0},{"q":"Positive EV/negative EBITDA gives negative multiple. Cheapest peer?","o":["Yes; negative EBITDA creates negative enterprise value automatically.","Yes; any negative multiple is cheaper than a positive one.","Not inferable; usual positive-multiple ranking is not meaningful without further analysis.","No; company value must be exactly zero regardless of assets/prospects."],"a":2},{"q":"Precedents must always exceed trading comps?","o":["No; precedents never contain any information.","Yes; control always causes the same fixed premium.","Yes; all past deals are automatically more recent than current prices.","No; deal date, conditions, distress, synergies and data differences may widen or lower ranges."],"a":3},{"q":"Offer 120, stated unaffected price 100. Equity price premium?","o":["20% of EV in every possible capital structure.","16.67%, using offer 120 as denominator.","120%, confusing offer level with growth.","20%."],"a":3},{"q":"Peer 25 x versus median 12 x. Next step?","o":["Delete quietly because all outliers are wrong.","Check growth, risk, accounting/period and data comparability; document any exclusion.","Use 25 x for target because higher gives a better story.","Average blindly even if denominator periods differ."],"a":1}]},{"t":"Valuation II: the DCF, from first principles","kick":"The core skill","mins":"3.5 hrs","obj":["Forecast unlevered free cash flow: revenue to EBIT, tax, reinvestment and working capital","Defend a WACC and a terminal value - the two numbers that run the whole model","Run sensitivities and present a DCF as a range with honest assumptions, never a point estimate"],"vids":[{"id":"veSXjIzTa3c","t":"Session 3: Intrinsic and DCF Valuation - Laying the Groundwork","by":"Aswath Damodaran"},{"id":"QARLbZyvRuU","t":"Session 16 (Val Undergrad): DCF Valuations","by":"Aswath Damodaran"},{"id":"fmoE3H8RjP4","t":"Advanced DCF Tutorial (Ex J.P. Morgan Analyst)","by":"rareliquid"}],"reads":[{"t":"DCF model: the full guide","u":"https://mergersandinquisitions.com/dcf-model/","n":"Study DCF model: the full guide alongside the written exercise."},{"t":"Damodaran's data library","u":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/New_Home_Page/data.html","n":"Study Damodaran's data library alongside the written exercise."}],"ex":"Synthetic DCF in currency units, not a valuation of a real investment. Match FCFF to WACC and FCFE to cost of equity; currency, nominal/real basis, timing and risk assumptions must agree. Defined FCFF=EBIT*(1-t)+D&A-CapEx-increase in operating NWC; debt proceeds are not operating FCFF. A two-source, constant-weight WACC toy uses market-value equity 80%, debt 20%, cost of equity 12%, pre-tax debt 8%, and a fully usable stipulated 25% interest tax shield: .8*.12+.2*.08*(1-.25)=10.8%. These are invented inputs, not current Indian rates or proof every interest deduction is available. Preferred capital, changing leverage, tax limits and distress can require different treatment. Do not infer cost of equity from one historical share-price return or turn beta into a guaranteed movement.\nExplicit timing toy: end-of-year 1 FCFF 10, end-of-year 2 FCFF 10.4, then FCFF grows 4% forever, constant WACC 12%>g. Year 1 terminal value=10.4/(.12-.04)=130; time 0 operating value=(10+130)/1.12=125. At WACC 14%, holding the cash-flow path fixed, TV 104 and time 0 value 100. Do not add undiscounted TV to discounted FCFF, use year 1 FCFF as next-period numerator, or call operating value common equity without cash/debt/other-claim adjustments. Separate bridge: operating value 125, nonoperating cash 20, debt 30, no other claims=>equity 115. Terminal growth needs a sustainable economic story and reinvestment; a revenue forecast needs an explicit terminal reinvestment convention, not just a growth-rate assumption. This toy instead specifies the cash-flow path directly.\nHigher terminal g does not always raise value. Holding next-period NOPAT 20, WACC 10% and stable ROC 10%, reinvestment rate=g/ROC: g 2%=>FCFF 16/TV 200; g 4%=>FCFF 12/TV 200. Changing g changes reinvestment, not just the denominator. Stable ROC above/below WACC changes this conclusion; holding cash flows fixed is a different sensitivity. g>=discount rate invalidates this positive growing-perpetuity convergence model; finite life/liquidation needs a different closure. Terminal value can be a large share, but no fixed majority is guaranteed; explicit-period assumptions also shape its base. State scenario and sensitivity assumptions, not artificial precision or a guaranteed market price. Damodaran data landing page says January 9, 2026 major update, some datasets more frequent; it is a gateway, not verification of any chosen beta/ERP/industry input. Record actual dataset/date/currency/method before use. No trade, bank contact or investment action.","quiz":[{"q":"FCFF discounted to operating value should be paired with?","o":["Revenue growth used as the discount rate.","Cost of equity regardless of whose cash flows are modeled.","WACC on a consistent currency, timing and risk basis.","A historical share-price increase treated as a guaranteed rate."],"a":2},{"q":"Two-source toy: E 80%, D 20%, Ke 12%, Kd 8%, fully usable 25% shield. WACC?","o":["8.4%, applying the shield to both costs.","11.2%, omitting the stipulated debt tax shield.","9.6%, discarding debt without renormalizing equity.","10.8%."],"a":3},{"q":"Year 1 FCFF 10; Year 2 FCFF 10.4 then g 4%; WACC 12%. TV at year 1?","o":["65, adding rates rather than subtracting.","125, confusing time 0 total operating value with terminal value.","104, using the 14% sensitivity discount rate.","130."],"a":3},{"q":"With year 1 FCFF 10 and TV 130 at year 1, WACC 12%. Time 0 operating value?","o":["116.07, omitting explicit FCFF 10.","140, leaving both amounts undiscounted.","126.07, discounting only terminal value.","125."],"a":3},{"q":"Same fixed cash-flow path at WACC 14%, g 4%. Time 0 value?","o":["100.","104, reporting terminal value rather than time 0 value.","125, leaving the 12% result unchanged.","122.81, reusing TV 130 despite changing WACC."],"a":0},{"q":"NOPAT next period 20, WACC=ROC 10%. g 2% vs 4%, reinvestment=g/ROC. TV?","o":["160 then 120, multiplying cash flow by 10 without the growth denominator.","250 then 333.33, ignoring reinvestment.","200 in both cases; FCFF changes from 16 to 12.","100 then 200, treating growth as the entire operating return."],"a":2},{"q":"g equals WACC in a positive growing-perpetuity model. Response?","o":["A negative terminal value proves the company is insolvent.","Terminal value is exactly zero.","Use the division-by-zero output as a valid price.","The convergence condition fails; revisit closure and assumptions."],"a":3},{"q":"Operating value 125, nonoperating cash 20, debt 30, no other claims. Equity?","o":["125, omitting the bridge entirely.","135, adding debt and subtracting cash.","115.","175, adding both cash and debt."],"a":2},{"q":"Terminal value must exceed half of every DCF?","o":["Yes; explicit-period cash flows are irrelevant.","Yes; it is exactly 80% by definition.","No; size depends on cash-flow path, horizon and closure; explicit assumptions still matter.","No; terminal value is always zero when forecasts exist."],"a":2},{"q":"A dated data-library landing page establishes a current company beta?","o":["No; historical data may never be used for any assumption.","Yes; every number is refreshed simultaneously every day.","Yes; an industry average is the exact beta of every company.","No; inspect the specific dataset, observation date, scope and method."],"a":3}]},{"t":"M&A mechanics: the merger model and accretion/dilution","kick":"Deal math","mins":"3 hrs","obj":["Structure an offer: purchase price, cash-stock mix, and the sources-and-uses table","Run the accretion/dilution test: does the buyer's EPS rise or fall, and exactly why","Judge synergies - how much is real, how much is pitch-book poetry"],"vids":[{"id":"dYArdL40wj4","t":"Financial Modeling Quick Lessons: Accretion / Dilution (Part 1)","by":"Wall Street Prep"},{"id":"rqjhxrWTlU0","t":"Accretion and dilution","by":"The Finance Storyteller"}],"reads":[{"t":"The merger model","u":"https://www.wallstreetprep.com/knowledge/merger-model/","n":"Study The merger model alongside the written exercise."}],"ex":"Fictional full-year merger model, same accounting/earnings period, positive earnings, unchanged buyer share price for issuance, no fees, synergies, tax changes, purchase-accounting charges, integration effects, fractional-share restrictions or other changes unless stated. Buyer NI 100, shares 100, price 20=>EPS 1, equity 2000/P-E 20 x. Target NI 20, pre-offer equity 200=>P-E 10 x. All stock at 200 means issue 10 shares; combined NI 120/shares 110=>EPS 1.090909, accretion 9.0909%. A 30% premium makes offer 260 and purchase P-E 13 x, not 10 x; issue 13 shares=>120/113=1.061947 EPS, 6.1947% accretion. Two P/E ratios establish direction under these restrictions, not a unique accretion percentage without relative size. A sufficiently large premium or other costs can overturn it.\nSeparate debt-funded 260 toy: stipulated after-tax interest cost 6% of 260=15.6 per year, no new shares/other changes, combined NI 100+20-15.6=104.4, EPS 1.044, 4.4% accretion. Offer earnings yield 20/260=7.6923% exceeds 6%, so earnings cover modeled financing cost, not risk-adjusted value creation. Do not compare pre-premium 10% yield against actual offer price, or assume deductions are usable in a real jurisdiction. Cash funding has forgone earnings/opportunity cost; it is not free. All-stock high-P/E/low-P/E rule is restricted to consistent positive earnings and no other adjustments, not a promise about goodwill, growth or risk.\nSources and uses must reconcile consideration, fees, refinanced obligations and required cash with debt, cash and equity funding under the actual transaction definition. Synthetic cash-funded equity purchase 260+fees 10+cash injection 5=uses 275; debt 200+buyer cash 75=sources 275. A stock component is not cash available to pay an unrelated cash bill; enterprise purchase price is not automatically cash to sellers. Debt balances, interest timing, fee amortization, purchase accounting, tax and full/partial-year effects require explicit modeling outside these toys. Separate stipulated pre-tax gross synergy 8 less recurring implementation cost 3=>net 5; do not book revenue as profit or double-count savings in both baseline and synergy. Owners, milestones, cash timing, customer/employee effects and downside determine feasibility; no unsupported ranking of why all synergies fail.\nEPS accretion is not economic value creation or a guaranteed share-price rise. Price paid, risk, growth, reinvestment, financing obligations and cash flows can make an accretive deal value-destroying or a dilutive one value-creating. Damodaran 2012 explains the mechanism; no current market/outcome statistic or linked study is endorsed here. Assigned WSP prose prints target$2*200 million shares=$4 million; correct$400 million, consistent with its later line. Its exact EBT 3500-108-100+200-35=3457 million, tax 691.4/NI 2765.6, EPS 4.254769/6.36923% accretion; rounded display numbers should not be chained as exact. Do not copy its broad market-reward claim or apply US purchase-accounting/tax details as current Indian rules. No offer, contact, mandate, financing or share transaction.","quiz":[{"q":"Buyer NI 100, shares 100, price 20. Standalone EPS and P/E?","o":["EPS 100 and P/E 20 x, ignoring shares in EPS.","EPS 20 and P/E 1 x, exchanging price and earnings.","EPS 1 and P/E 20 x.","EPS 1 and P/E 10 x, using the target multiple."],"a":2},{"q":"Target consideration 200, paid in buyer shares at 20. New shares?","o":["100, using the buyer existing share count.","10.","20, confusing target NI with share issuance.","200, treating currency units as shares."],"a":1},{"q":"No-cost all-stock 200 toy: combined NI 120/shares 110. Accretion?","o":["0%, assuming no synergies means no EPS change.","20%, ignoring the 10 newly issued shares.","10%, reporting the share-count increase instead.","9.09% approximately."],"a":3},{"q":"30% premium on 200; target NI 20. Purchase P/E and new shares at 20?","o":["30 x and 30 shares, mistaking premium percentage for quantities.","10 x and 10 shares, ignoring the premium.","13 x and 13 shares.","6.5 x and 26 shares, doubling the issuance."],"a":2},{"q":"At offer 260 all stock, NI 120, shares 113. Accretion?","o":["6.19% approximately.","9.09%, leaving share issuance at the no-premium level.","20%, using NI growth without dilution.","30%, treating price premium as EPS growth."],"a":0},{"q":"Debt-funded 260 at stipulated 6% after-tax cost, target NI 20. EPS change from 1?","o":["+4.4%, with no new shares or other effects.","+20%, ignoring the financing cost.","+15.6%, confusing interest expense with earnings gain.","-6%, assuming interest rate itself is the EPS decline."],"a":0},{"q":"Cash uses 260 purchase+10 fees+5 injection. Debt 200 plus buyer cash needed?","o":["75, so total sources 275 match uses 275.","60, ignoring fees and injection.","70, including fees but excluding required cash.","275, ignoring the new debt source."],"a":0},{"q":"Gross pre-tax synergy 8, recurring implementation cost 3. Net contribution?","o":["5 before any further effects/tax.","8, ignoring the cost.","11, adding the cost as a saving.","3, reporting implementation cost as the benefit."],"a":0},{"q":"An accretive deal guarantees value creation/share-price rise?","o":["Yes; the combined P/E can never change.","No; price, risk and future cash flows matter beyond current EPS.","Yes; debt-financed earnings are risk-free.","No; every accretive transaction necessarily destroys value."],"a":1},{"q":"Assigned target EPS$2 times 200 million shares is printed$4 million. Correct treatment?","o":["$4 million because the publisher is authoritative on arithmetic.","$400 million; label the source error rather than propagate it.","$400 billion because EPS is multiplied by market capitalization.","$2 million because only one share should count."],"a":1}]},{"t":"The LBO: how private equity prices a company","kick":"Buy-side lens","mins":"3 hrs","obj":["Explain the LBO engine: buy with debt, pay it down from the company's own cash, sell the delevered equity","Separate EBITDA growth, multiple change and net debt/cash effects, testing each assumption rather than ranking them universally.","Spot a good LBO candidate: stable cash, modest capex, assets to borrow against, a fixable story"],"vids":[{"id":"Q7qNxmxkm8o","t":"Simple Leveraged Buyout (LBO) Tutorial","by":"rareliquid"},{"id":"zeCfu5g0Uis","t":"How to Build an LBO Model in 5 Steps","by":"rareliquid"},{"id":"YEmF3jItGxY","t":"Leveraged Buyout Fundamentals","by":"Financial Edge Training"}],"reads":[{"t":"The LBO model guide","u":"https://mergersandinquisitions.com/guides/lbo-model/","n":"Study The LBO model guide alongside the written exercise."},{"t":"Simple LBO model - Excel and video","u":"https://breakingintowallstreet.com/kb/leveraged-buyouts-and-lbo-models/simple-lbo-model-excel/","n":"Study Simple LBO model - Excel and video alongside the written exercise."}],"ex":"Paper LBO with stated cash/debt assumptions, not a deal recommendation or permission to borrow. Normalize entry EBITDA 100, enterprise purchase price 8 x=800, new debt 5 x=500 and sponsor equity 300. No entry cash, refinanced legacy debt, fees, rollover, other claims or ownership dilution. Source debt 500+equity 300=uses 800. Exit after exactly 5 years EBITDA 125 (25% total growth, not 25% each year), multiple 8 x=>EV 1000. Stipulate remaining exit debt 300, zero exit cash and fees, no interim sponsor distributions/contributions: exit equity 700, MOIC 700/300=2.333333, annual IRR=(700/300)^(1/5)-1=18.47% approximately. The 200 cumulative debt reduction must come from cash remaining after modeled interest, tax, capex, NWC and other obligations; EBITDA growth alone does not prove cash available or repayment. A full model needs its annual debt/interest/liquidity schedule.\nWithout any debt repayment, same exit EV 1000/debt 500/zero cash=>equity 500, MOIC 1.666667, annual IRR 10.76%; entry multiple/growth/exit multiple alone do not specify either return. If excess cash instead accumulates, add verified unrestricted exit cash, not both cash and the repayment it funded. For exactly one entry and one exit, no interim flows, IRR=MOIC^(1/years)-1; multiple alone does not state an annual return or handle dated interim distributions. Separate downside exit EBITDA 80 at 6 x=>EV 480 below debt 500; simple common-equity residual is zero, not a sponsor receipt of negative 20. This is a limited-liability residual toy, not a legal ruling about guarantees, capital calls, fees, seniorities, restructuring or lender recoveries.\nDebt is not free or automatic value creation: interest, covenants, liquidity/minimum cash, refinancing and distress risk matter. Stable cash/modest capex can help debt service, but no universal ideal candidate and no honest ranking of growth/deleveraging/multiple change without evidence. Multiple expansion is a market assumption, not impossible and not manager-controlled. EBITDA growth may consume capex/NWC and can be manufactured by cutting useful investment, so test durability and cash quality. A lender claim is contractual but repayment still at risk.\nSeparate annual cash-sweep toy, no other transactions: debt beginning 100, cash beginning 20, cash flow 50 already AFTER interest/tax/capex/NWC but before principal, minimum cash 15, mandatory principal 10, optional sweep 50% of remaining available cash. Availability=max(0, 20+50-10-15)=45; optional=min(45*.5, 100-10)=22.5. Ending debt 67.5 and cash 37.5; cannot repay more than outstanding principal or use restricted minimum cash. PIK interest can grow debt without current cash payment; independent balance 100 plus 10% PIK with no repayment=>110, not 90 or 100. Do not treat PIK addback as debt forgiveness.\nThe assigned M&I guide presents a timed modeling test and flags errors in video ending cash and management options/workbook components. Use the explicit written case assumptions for calculations. BIWS simple LBO subtracts EBITDA 250-depreciation 35-interest 75=140 but calls it EBIT; correct EBIT 215/EBT 140, tax 56/NI 84 at stipulated 40%, cash 84+35+6-35=90; its no-paydown assumption differs from this toy. No copied workbook/video is certified. No live term sheet, debt covenant, tax deduction, borrowing, investment or outside action.","quiz":[{"q":"Entry EBITDA 100, 8 x EV, 5 x new debt, no other uses. Sponsor equity?","o":["800, ignoring debt funding.","300.","500, confusing debt with sponsor equity.","1300, adding debt to the enterprise purchase price."],"a":1},{"q":"EBITDA 100 grows 25% total over 5 years; exit multiple 8 x. Exit EV?","o":["125, returning EBITDA rather than EV.","800, ignoring earnings growth.","2441.41, treating 25% as annual growth.","1000."],"a":3},{"q":"Exit EV 1000, debt 300, zero cash/fees/other claims. Sponsor proceeds?","o":["700.","1000, leaving debt outstanding without deducting it.","1300, adding the lender claim.","500, using entry debt rather than stated exit debt."],"a":0},{"q":"Entry 300, only exit 700 exactly 5 years later. MOIC and annual IRR?","o":["1.333 x and 18.47%, confusing profit multiple with proceeds multiple.","2.333 x and 133.33%, treating total gain as annual.","2.333 x and 18.47% approximately.","2.333 x and 26.67%, annualizing total gain linearly."],"a":2},{"q":"Instead exit debt 500, EV 1000, zero cash/fees. MOIC?","o":["1.667 x approximately.","2.333 x, assuming unspecified debt repayment.","3.333 x, ignoring debt at exit.","1.000 x, ignoring enterprise value growth."],"a":0},{"q":"Same return multiple paid only at exit. Longer holding period?","o":["Same annual IRR because the multiple is unchanged.","Lower annual IRR for a positive-gain multiple, all else equal.","Higher annual IRR solely because more time passes.","IRR equals MOIC in every holding period."],"a":1},{"q":"Downside EV 480, debt 500, no cash/other claims, limited-liability residual toy. Common equity?","o":["-20 sponsor proceeds, as a negative sale receipt.","Zero residual; debt shortfall needs separate analysis.","480, ignoring debt priority.","500, treating lender principal as equity value."],"a":1},{"q":"Sweep toy cash 20+FCF 50-mandatory 10-minimum cash 15, 50% sweep. Optional repayment?","o":["45, ignoring the 50% sweep condition.","25, using half FCF without cash/minimum/mandatory reconciliation.","22.5, capped by remaining principal.","35, using half all cash before obligations."],"a":2},{"q":"Debt 100 plus 10% PIK, no repayment/fees. Ending debt?","o":["100, treating noncash interest as no liability.","110.","90, subtracting accrued interest.","10, reporting only the interest addition."],"a":1},{"q":"BIWS example 250 EBITDA-35 depreciation-75 interest=140. Label?","o":["EBT 140; EBIT 215 before the interest deduction.","EBIT 140, because any earnings subtotal is EBIT.","Revenue 140, because all expenses have been deducted.","Net income 140 even with the stated 40% tax."],"a":0}]},{"t":"Running a deal: the sell-side process, end to end","kick":"Process craft","mins":"2 hrs","obj":["Walk the sell-side: pitch, teaser, NDA, CIM, management presentations, data room, bids, negotiation, signing","Separate truthful process coordination from control of bidder behavior, approval, confidentiality and outcomes.","Describe the buy-side mirror: screening, indication of interest, diligence, final bid"],"vids":[{"id":"zl7456UA3Ak","t":"The M&A Deal Process Explained by a Former Investment Banker","by":"Market Maker"},{"id":"yM1lDDYdhqg","t":"Sell-Side M&A (Rosenbaum and Pearl, Ch 6)","by":"FinanceKid"},{"id":"niUl6YM4XGQ","t":"M&A Sell-Side Process: Overview","by":"Waypoint Private Capital"}],"reads":[{"t":"The investment banking analyst","u":"https://mergersandinquisitions.com/investment-banking-analyst/","n":"Optional companion materials; this exercise does not require a paid add-in, deck or workbook. Apply the source's date and jurisdiction; it is not a current Indian legal or accounting opinion."},{"t":"M&A model overview","u":"https://www.nobledesktop.com/learn/financial-modeling/m-and-a-model-overview","n":"Optional companion materials; this exercise does not require a paid add-in, deck or workbook. Apply the source's date and jurisdiction; it is not a current Indian legal or accounting opinion."}],"ex":"Fictional sell-side process map, not an instruction to contact buyers or disclose a real company. A common sequence is preparation/engagement, approved buyer list, anonymized teaser, confidentiality arrangements, controlled CIM release, initial indications, management meetings and diligence, revised bids/LOI, confirmatory diligence/document negotiation, signing, conditions and closing. Stages overlap or change in negotiated sales, auctions, distressed or regulated deals; no fixed universal calendar or mandatory buyer count. Signing is not automatically closing, and an announcement is not proof funds or control transferred. Advisers organize materials, communication and negotiation; they do not control regulators, bidder decisions, financing or outcomes.\nDraft a ten-row synthetic plan with stage, owner, material, authorized audience, dependency, decision and output. Set seller permission and confidentiality controls before sharing. A teaser may omit the name but distinctive metrics/customers can identify a company. NDA signature alone is not permission for every dataset or onward recipient; actual scope, exclusions, privacy, competitive sensitivity and seller authority matter. CIM is a marketing/information package, not an audit certificate or guarantee; permissions and release conditions must be checked. Data room access is staged and logged; do not upload personal records, credentials or competitively sensitive data without appropriate authority/safeguards. Clean-team/redaction arrangements are deal-specific, not a claim of legal compliance. Buyer questions and management forecasts need traceable answers and consistent approved updates, not a fictional answer to create tension.\nAn IOI commonly communicates an indicative value/range, structure, financing assumptions and conditions. It is not automatic binding funding or a signed sale; legal effects of any IOI/LOI/exclusivity/confidentiality clause depend on actual wording and law. An LOI may contain binding provisions even if core purchase terms are nonbinding. Do not erase that distinction by labeling a whole document always binding or never binding. Compare price on the same enterprise/equity/cash/debt/NWC basis, conditional versus cash-at-close amounts, funding and approvals, timing, diligence risk and execution certainty. Toy: bid A headline 120 includes cash 90 plus maximum conditional earnout 30; bid Bcash 110 has no earnout. Guaranteed-at-close amounts on those stipulated terms differ 90 versus 110; neither headline nor a probability-free expected earnout establishes best overall bid. Earnout 20 at 50% probability yields 10 modeled expected units before time/risk/collection costs, not guaranteed cash.\nCredible competitive tension means real qualified alternatives and disciplined timing, not invented bidders, false deadlines, leaking confidential bids or ignoring exclusivity. Faster isn't always better than diligence or proper approval. Closing needs the specified agreements, consents, conditions and funding evidence; no blanket current legal checklist. Houlihan Capital's 2024 sequence is an illustrative adviser account, not universal 45-90 day closing evidence. Noble's assigned page mainly explains merger EPS modeling; it doesn't verify an end-to-end sell-side playbook or establish accretion as a sufficient deal-merit test. No bank/buyer contact, NDA signature, data upload, mandate or commitment.","quiz":[{"q":"An anonymous teaser omits the name. Confidentiality assured?","o":["No; distinctive facts may identify the business; review approved content and audience.","Removing the company name is sufficient if the buyer has not previously met the business.","Treat the teaser metrics as public once several prospective buyers have received the same document.","Withhold all anonymized summaries, including content approved for the intended buyer audience."],"a":0},{"q":"A buyer signed an NDA. May every employee record be uploaded?","o":["Upload records into the restricted room first, then review necessity after the buyer asks a question.","Use the signed NDA as sufficient authority for any employee dataset requested during diligence.","No; verify scope, seller authority, necessity and safeguards before release.","Decline all diligence uploads because an NDA can never support controlled access to employee data."],"a":2},{"q":"CIM and data room establish what?","o":["A controlled information/diligence channel, not automatic audit certification or truth.","Seller information is complete enough to treat missing evidence as confirmation of the stated claim.","The transaction has cleared regulatory review because its advisers opened a controlled data room.","Projections in the CIM can be used as guaranteed outcomes once the buyer has acknowledged receipt."],"a":0},{"q":"An IOI is normally enough to prove acquisition funding and closing?","o":["No; it has no informational use in any process.","Yes; an expression of interest transfers control.","Yes; the buyer has automatically paid cash.","No; indicative terms/assumptions require further evidence and definitive process."],"a":3},{"q":"An LOI says core price is nonbinding. Are all clauses necessarily nonbinding?","o":["No; confidentiality/exclusivity or other provisions may have legal effect under wording/law.","Treat every clause as nonbinding because the title and nonbinding price paragraph control the whole document.","Treat the LOI as transferring ownership now because some confidentiality or exclusivity terms can bind.","Defer review of confidentiality and exclusivity until the definitive agreement, because price is nonbinding."],"a":0},{"q":"Bid A 120 headline=90 cash+maximum 30 earnout; bid B 110 cash. Stated cash at close?","o":["A 150 and B 110, adding earnout twice.","A 120 and B 110, treating maximum earnout as cash at close.","A 90 and B 80, subtracting A earnout from B.","A 90 and B 110; conditional maximum is not guaranteed cash."],"a":3},{"q":"Earnout 20 with 50% probability, before time/risk/collection adjustments. Modeled expectation?","o":["40, dividing by probability rather than multiplying.","20, equating maximum with expectation.","10, not a guaranteed receipt.","0, assuming uncertain means impossible."],"a":2},{"q":"Credible process tension permits?","o":["Forwarding all confidential bids to every prospect.","Inventing an extra bidder to raise price.","Real qualified alternatives and truthful authorized communication within commitments.","Ignoring a signed exclusivity condition for convenience."],"a":2},{"q":"Signed agreement and public announcement mean closing is complete?","o":["Yes; signing always settles cash simultaneously.","Not necessarily; check specified conditions, consents, funding and transfer evidence.","Yes; an announcement is the final bank receipt.","No; signing can never occur on closing day."],"a":1},{"q":"What can the banker directly organize versus guarantee?","o":["Regulator votes and every bidder financing decision.","Information flow, schedule and advice; not bidder/regulator outcomes or guaranteed highest price.","A fixed 45-day closing for every sale.","A legally binding bid merely by writing a longer CIM."],"a":1}]},{"t":"Capital markets: ECM, DCM and the IPO machine","kick":"Going public","mins":"2 hrs","obj":["Explain what ECM and DCM desks do: price and distribute new shares and new bonds","Walk an IPO: mandate, draft prospectus, roadshow, book-building, pricing, listing day","Distinguish offer price, allocation and aftermarket return; explain incentives without promising a listing pop."],"vids":[{"id":"l4HMCr5roAM","t":"What is an IPO?","by":"CNBC International"},{"id":"14KNcxe4nqc","t":"Inside the IPO Process","by":"Fidelity Investments"},{"id":"RTvjn9wGv60","t":"Debt Capital Markets (DCM) Explained by a Former Goldman Sachs Banker","by":"Peter Su"}],"reads":[{"t":"The IPO process","u":"https://mergersandinquisitions.com/ipo-process/","n":"Optional companion materials; this exercise does not require a paid add-in, deck or workbook. Apply the source's date and jurisdiction; it is not a current Indian legal or accounting opinion."},{"t":"Equity capital markets","u":"https://mergersandinquisitions.com/equity-capital-markets/","n":"Optional companion materials; this exercise does not require a paid add-in, deck or workbook. Apply the source's date and jurisdiction; it is not a current Indian legal or accounting opinion."},{"t":"SEBI IPO educational presentation, dated February2025","u":"https://investor.sebi.gov.in/pdf/reference-material/ppt/PPT-3%20How%20to%20invest%20in%20Intial%20Public%20Offer_%20Feb%202025.pdf","n":"Optional companion materials; this exercise does not require a paid add-in, deck or workbook. Apply the source's date and jurisdiction; it is not a current Indian legal or accounting opinion."}],"ex":"Use an invented IPO rather than treat incomplete news as a verified recent Indian prospectus. ECM commonly supports equity/equity-linked origination and distribution; DCM supports debt issuance. Team labels, instruments and roles vary; equity-linked debt is not identical to ordinary equity, and issuance desks aren't the same as secondary trading. A possible IPO sequence includes mandate/preparation, diligence, jurisdiction-specific draft/registration documents and revisions, permitted marketing/roadshow, demand assessment, pricing/allocation, issuance/listing and continuing obligations. Sequence and rules vary by market/transaction. US Form S-1 and Indian DRHP are different documents within different systems, not interchangeable filings or proof regulator merits approval. No current filing rule, quiet-period length, lock-up period or tax rate is supplied here.\nBook-building collects indications/bids and price/volume information to inform offer pricing/allocation under the applicable framework. Demand can change; subscription multiple isn't a forecast of return, guaranteed allocation or mathematical clearing-price rule in every market. Roadshow communicates business/risk information within applicable disclosure rules, not a regulator vote. Offer price and first-trade/open/close prices are different observations. There can be gains or losses; underpricing explanations involve incentives, uncertainty and demand, not a guaranteed/deliberate pop or universal required discount. Do not call a gain realized without a sale or infer a causal counterfactual merely from an observed quote.\nToy currency/share units, no fees/taxes unless stated: pre-offer shares 100; issuer creates 20 new primary shares at 10; existing holders sell 10 old shares at 10. Gross total offer 300, primary issuer proceeds 200, selling holders 100, post-offer outstanding 120 not 130. Existing shares 100 remain 100 whether transferred or retained; primary 20/120=16.6667% of post-issue share count. No option dilution, greenshoe, repurchase, change in voting classes or other transaction. Separately stipulate issuer-borne fees 12=>issuer net 188, not 288 or 300. First-day closing quote 12=>20% close-to-offer price change, not primary proceeds 240. An allocated investor buying 100 shares at 10 has 200 unrealized mark-to-close gain before costs if still holding, not automatic cash. A later open-market buyer paid 11 and sees 12 close=>9.0909% mark-to-close, not 20% on their cost.\nAt 12 reference, primary 20*price gap 2=40 and secondary 10*2=20 are arithmetic proceeds-difference illustrations, not proof a higher price was achievable for the whole offering or that any specific party caused a loss. Company doesn't receive secondary resale proceeds; opportunity-cost language must name which shares and a defensible counterfactual. For an actual issue, verify final prospectus, fresh versus sale shares, dated category subscription, offer price and exact opening/closing quote/exchange, fees and allocation constraints before conclusions. The SEC 2013 educational bulletin supports generic offer/trading distinction and secondary proceeds allocation, not its old thresholds as current law or any Indian approval. SEC effectiveness is not investment merit endorsement; no investment, application, order or signup performed.","quiz":[{"q":"Book-building subscription multiple proves listing gain and allocation?","o":["No; investor price/volume information is never relevant.","Yes; twice-subscribed means exactly 100% profit.","Yes; every bid receives the full requested quantity.","No; it describes demand on its stated basis, not guaranteed price performance or allotment."],"a":3},{"q":"US Form S-1 and Indian DRHP?","o":["Both guarantee regulator approval of investment quality.","One universal form with the same legal effect worldwide.","Both are bank loan contracts.","Different jurisdiction-specific documents; neither should be substituted for the other."],"a":3},{"q":"Toy 20 new+10 existing shares at 10. Gross issuer versus selling-holder proceeds?","o":["200 issuer and 100 holders, before stipulated fees.","300 issuer and 0 holders, allocating every sale to the company.","100 issuer and 200 holders, reversing primary/secondary.","200 issuer and 300 holders, double-counting the offering."],"a":0},{"q":"Pre-offer 100 shares, issue 20 new, transfer 10 old. Post-offer outstanding?","o":["110, subtracting sold shares from the total.","130, treating transferred old shares as new.","120.","90, treating all existing sales as cancelled shares."],"a":2},{"q":"Issuer gross 200, issuer-borne fees 12. Net issuer cash?","o":["288, including selling-holder proceeds before fees.","188.","200, ignoring fees.","312, adding fees to total gross offer proceeds."],"a":1},{"q":"Offer 10, first-day close 12. Close-to-offer price change?","o":["20%.","16.67%, using close as the denominator.","120%, confusing price level with gain.","2%, treating currency-unit difference as percentage."],"a":0},{"q":"Allocated 100 shares at 10, still held at 12 close. Before costs, mark-to-close gain?","o":["200 realized automatically without a sale.","1200 gain, confusing gross value with profit.","1000 gain, confusing cost with profit.","200 unrealized, not 200 cash paid out."],"a":3},{"q":"Open-market buyer paid 11, closes 12. Mark-to-close return on cost?","o":["8.33%, dividing by close rather than purchase cost.","20%, using the IPO offer price instead of their cost.","9.09% approximately.","100%, treating one-unit change as doubling."],"a":2},{"q":"Observed first-day pop proves the issuer deliberately lost exactly the gap on all shares?","o":["No; separate primary/secondary, actual proceeds and an uncertain counterfactual.","Yes; every post-issue share was sold by the issuer in the IPO.","Yes; closing quote was guaranteed achievable at any offering size.","No; price differences can never inform any analysis."],"a":0},{"q":"SEC declares a registration statement effective. Investment merits approved?","o":["Yes; the regulator guarantees the issuer cannot fail.","No; effectiveness is not SEC endorsement or guaranteed accuracy/returns.","Yes; shares cannot trade below offer price.","No; registration documents contain no useful investor information."],"a":1}]},{"t":"The analyst's craft: pitch books, Excel and zero mistakes","kick":"Daily reality","mins":"2 hrs","obj":["Describe what goes into a pitch book - situation, credentials, valuation pages, process plan - and why it is a sales document","Use supported efficient spreadsheet methods with consistent units, traceable inputs and independent checks.","Run the attention-to-detail protocol: version names, page numbers, the late-night re-check before anything leaves"],"vids":[{"id":"Th9RaCypK_I","t":"Investment Banking Pitchbook Template","by":"Corporate Finance Institute"},{"id":"F5pradjEIBM","t":"Investment Banking Pitch Deck - Full Breakdown","by":"Chief Analyst"}],"reads":[{"t":"The investment banking pitchbook","u":"https://www.wallstreetprep.com/knowledge/investment-banking-pitchbook/","n":"Optional companion materials; this exercise does not require a paid add-in, deck or workbook. Apply the source's date and jurisdiction; it is not a current Indian legal or accounting opinion."},{"t":"Investment banking pitch books","u":"https://mergersandinquisitions.com/investment-banking-pitch-books/","n":"Optional companion materials; this exercise does not require a paid add-in, deck or workbook. Apply the source's date and jurisdiction; it is not a current Indian legal or accounting opinion."},{"t":"Macabacus Excel shortcuts","u":"https://www.macabacus.com/learn/excel-shortcuts","n":"Optional companion materials; this exercise does not require a paid add-in, deck or workbook. Apply the source's date and jurisdiction; it is not a current Indian legal or accounting opinion."}],"ex":"Build a fictional one-page pitch profile for learning, not a real client-ready or approved communication. A pitchbook is often a sales/advisory presentation with situation, team credentials, transaction alternatives, analysis, risks and a proposed process. The term is also used for other client presentations; content depends on mandate/audience, not one universal page order or mandatory three valuation methods. A football field only compares properly labeled compatible enterprise/equity/per-share ranges, currency, dates and periods. Comps/precedents/DCF outputs aren't interchangeable without bridges and assumptions. Never invent transactions, credentials, clients, projections or approval merely to make the page persuasive.\nSynthetic worksheet: revenue 120/COGS 72=>gross profit 48/margin 40%; EBITDA 24=>20% margin; debt 30/cash 5=>net debt 25; enterprise value 100=>equity 75 under a no-other-claims bridge; diluted shares 15=>per-share value 5. Keep monetary units and share units consistent. If revenue stored in crore units 120 and another source 1200 lakh, convert before comparison:1 crore=100 lakh, so 1200 lakh=12 crore, not 120 crore. Arithmetic and balance checks necessary but don't prove correct definitions, omitted obligations, period alignment or source truth. A balanced model can still contain two offsetting errors.\nSeparate inputs/calculations/outputs; label source URL/document/page, date/as-of period, units and definitions. Trace formulas and external links, test sign/dimension/rounding conventions, reconcile net debt, share count and presentation against model. Handle missing data explicitly; IFERROR(formula, 0) can hide a broken source, not fix it. Toy hardcoded debt 30 but actual intended debt 40 and cash 15 instead of 5 leaves net debt 25 unchanged; passing one net-debt output check doesn't validate each input. Use independent sources and input-level checks, not only a green total.\nOne release owner, clear dated/version identifier and changelog, a frozen reviewed export and recovery copy. File names alone don't establish review status; verify the actual content, all slide/model links, numbers, names, page order/footnotes and intended recipients/attachments. Before any real external release, obtain the needed content/recipient authority and apply the organization's confidentiality/review process; don't assume a filename FINAL is approval. Preserve underlying evidence/comments internally; remove only information not approved for the recipient in a separate release copy after inspection. Blanket note-stripping/deleting backups isn't a quality protocol. If timebox 45 minutes expires with a material unresolved source, report the limitation and hold release, not label zero mistakes or send anyway.\nAssigned Macabacus page currently markets configurable add-in shortcuts, tracing and formatting; not a verified native Excel shortcut table or a universal fixed keyboard mapping. Keys can differ by version/layout and remapping. Keyboard efficiency is useful practice, not proof quality or requirement to install/buy anything; choose the appropriate accessible supported method. No add-in installation, subscription, login, macro execution, real pitchbook share or external message. An example pitchbook alone does not prove a bank's mandate; distinguish the proposed role from the signed engagement. Review effort reduces error risk but cannot certify a universal zero-error outcome.","quiz":[{"q":"A polished pitchbook establishes independent audit assurance?","o":["Yes; good formatting proves every forecast.","No; a sales/advisory presentation needs evidence and review, not assumed certification.","Yes; the phrase pitchbook means regulator-approved filing.","No; presentations can never contain useful financial analysis."],"a":1},{"q":"Toy revenue 120, COGS 72. Gross profit and margin?","o":["192 and 160%, adding costs to revenue.","72 and 60%, confusing costs with gross profit.","48 and 66.67%, using COGS as the margin denominator.","48 and 40%."],"a":3},{"q":"Toy EBITDA 24/revenue 120. EBITDA margin?","o":["20%.","40%, using gross profit 48 instead of EBITDA 24.","500%, inverting revenue/EBITDA.","24%, treating currency units as a percentage."],"a":0},{"q":"EV 100, debt 30, cash 5, no other claims. Equity/per-share with 15 shares?","o":["100 and 6.667, omitting debt and cash.","125 and 8.333, reversing the net-debt bridge.","75 and 5.","65 and 4.333, subtracting cash as if it were debt."],"a":2},{"q":"A source 1200 lakh converted to crore units?","o":["12 crore.","120 crore, dividing by 10 instead of 100.","1200 crore, changing only the unit label.","120000 crore, multiplying by 100."],"a":0},{"q":"Intended debt 40/cash 15; mistakenly use 30/5. Net debt 25 passes. Inputs proven?","o":["Yes; gross debt and cash are irrelevant in every model.","Yes; a green net-debt output proves every source.","No; offsetting input errors can leave a total unchanged.","No; matching totals imply deliberate fraud."],"a":2},{"q":"IFERROR(broken link, 0) displays a plausible zero. Ready?","o":["Yes; no error text means the input is correct.","No; investigate missing source/error rather than silently equate it to zero.","Yes; missing data always has economic value zero.","No; formulas may never use any error handling."],"a":1},{"q":"A file called FINAL is enough release approval?","o":["No; a final file can never be revised.","Yes; a filename grants recipient permission.","Yes; every link updates itself to the correct export.","No; verify reviewed content, version and authorized audience/attachments."],"a":3},{"q":"Before sending, strip every note and delete all backups?","o":["Yes; deleted backups guarantee confidentiality and correctness.","Yes; provenance is never useful after presentation.","No; preserve evidence internally and inspect an authorized release copy.","No; every private reviewer note must go to every recipient."],"a":2},{"q":"Macabacus shortcut landing page means all keys work identically without add-in?","o":["Yes; every key is native Excel and fixed worldwide.","No; page describes configurable product shortcuts; verify version/layout/capability.","Yes; fast typing alone proves the model is right.","No; keyboard practice has no possible benefit."],"a":1}]},{"t":"Breaking in: interviews, technicals and the first 90 days","kick":"The offer","mins":"2.5 hrs","obj":["Answer the big technicals cold: walk me through a DCF, how the statements link, what WACC is, accretion/dilution intuition","Structure 'why banking' and your story so it survives a skeptical MD","Build trust with truthful status, early blockers, clear review checkpoints and verified work, not a zero-error promise."],"vids":[{"id":"wSjk7j9rN_M","t":"Walk Me Through a DCF - Interview Question","by":"Financeable Training"},{"id":"lsF9AFIa3zU","t":"Hardest Investment Banking Technical Questions","by":"Aced"},{"id":"uoES-70o-Ls","t":"Watch Me Nail Morgan Stanley's DCF Interview Question","by":"Wall Street Mastermind"}],"reads":[{"t":"Investment banking interview questions","u":"https://mergersandinquisitions.com/investment-banking-interview-questions/","n":"Study Investment banking interview questions alongside the written exercise."},{"t":"Walk me through a DCF","u":"https://www.wallstreetprep.com/knowledge/walk-me-through-dcf/","n":"Study Walk me through a DCF alongside the written exercise."}],"ex":"Practice a synthetic 90-second DCF explanation, not a script guaranteed to win an interview: forecast defined FCFF using operating earnings, cash taxes and reinvestment; discount at matching WACC; estimate an economically supported terminal value at the forecast end and discount it too; bridge operating value to common equity with consistent cash/debt/other claims; divide by relevant diluted shares; explain scenarios, sensitivities and limits. FCFE instead uses cost of equity and already reflects financing claims. Match timing, currency and nominal/real basis. No mandatory 5-10 year horizon, universal 1-3% terminal growth, universal majority terminal value or one dilution method for every instrument. A model-implied value isn't a guaranteed market price. Explain assumptions rather than memorizing a formula or bluffing.\nDepreciation toy in currency units, changes from balanced opening, no other effects: book AND tax depreciation expense increases 10, with a fully usable stipulated 25% deduction and current cash-tax saving this period. Pretax income-10, tax expense-2.5, NI-7.5. Indirect CFO=-7.5+10=+2.5. Balance sheet cash+2.5, net PP&E-10=>assets-7.5; retained earnings/equity-7.5. No new capex or cash received from depreciation itself. The cash-tax assumption drives+2.5, not the addback alone. Separate no-tax-effect scenario=>NI-10, CFO 0, net PP&E/equity-10. If book expense has no current deduction, loss carryforwards or deferred/payment timing differ, work out tax assets/liabilities and actual cash timing instead of assuming instant savings. No current Indian tax rate/deductibility claim.\nToy numerical DCF: FCFF 11 at the end of year 1 and 11 at the end of year 2, no later cash flows/liquidation proceeds, WACC 10%; value 11/1.1+11/1.1^2=19.0909. This is a deliberately finite asset, not a general going-concern terminal assumption. Bridge separate operating value 100+nonoperating cash 10-debt 30-preferred 5-NCI 5=equity 70; 10 diluted shares=>7 per share. Calculations and units must reconcile; a confident answer isn't evidence of correct assumptions.\nFor a truthful career story, link actual role interest to specific work you did, what you learned and relevant limitations; label hypothetical practice as hypothetical. Do not invent employers, mandates, deal ownership or confidential client details. If you don't know, state the gap, clarify assumptions and reason from known principles; do not promise the interviewer will reward it. For first 90 days, agree scope, deadlines/review checkpoints and quality expectations; signal blockers early, ask focused questions and verify deliverables. Fast acknowledgments aren't completion, silence isn't reliability and unlimited availability/zero mistakes isn't a sustainable guarantee. A 90 second target is practice timing, not certified interview performance. No recording upload, job application or outreach performed.","quiz":[{"q":"A clear FCFF DCF explanation includes?","o":["Quote an exact market price without limitations.","Discount only terminal value and ignore explicit cash flows.","Use revenue growth as WACC because both are percentages.","Forecast/discount FCFF and terminal value consistently, bridge to equity and test assumptions."],"a":3},{"q":"Extra book/tax depreciation 10, fully usable current 25% cash-tax saving. NI change?","o":["-10, ignoring the stipulated tax effect.","-7.5.","+2.5, confusing tax saving with NI change.","-12.5, adding the tax saving as expense."],"a":1},{"q":"Same stipulated depreciation/tax toy. Indirect CFO change?","o":["+2.5.","+10, treating the addback as new cash.","-7.5, omitting the noncash addback.","+7.5, reversing NI change rather than reconciling cash."],"a":0},{"q":"Same toy. Balance-sheet changes?","o":["Cash-7.5/net PP&E-10/equity-7.5, failing the reconciliation.","Cash+10/net PP&E 0/equity+10, treating depreciation as cash revenue.","Cash+2.5/net PP&E-10/equity-7.5, both sides-7.5.","Cash 0/net PP&E-10/equity-7.5, omitting tax cash effect."],"a":2},{"q":"Separate depreciation 10 with no tax effects and no other changes. CFO?","o":["-10, treating expense as current payment.","+2.5, importing a tax benefit excluded by the toy.","+10, making noncash addback into cash.","0; NI-10+addback 10."],"a":3},{"q":"Finite FCFF 11 year 1/11 year 2/no later flows; WACC 10%. Time 0 value?","o":["22, failing to discount either flow.","19.0909 approximately.","20, discounting both flows only one year.","110, using a perpetuity despite finite life."],"a":1},{"q":"Operating value 100+cash 10-debt 30-preferred 5-NCI 5; 10 shares. Equity/per-share?","o":["100 and 10, omitting every bridge adjustment.","80 and 8, omitting preferred/NCI.","70 and 7.","150 and 15, adding all claims and cash."],"a":2},{"q":"Interview question has an unknown tax assumption. Response?","o":["State the gap, clarify or label an assumption, then reason without bluffing.","Claim the latest tax rate from memory without checking.","Invent a client example to sound experienced.","Treat unknown as evidence the rate is zero."],"a":0},{"q":"A persuasive career story requires?","o":["Confidential client documents supplied without authority.","Invented deal ownership because examples sound more convincing.","Truthful specific experience/interest with limits; hypothetical work labeled.","An unsupported claim of guaranteed zero-error work."],"a":2},{"q":"A blocker threatens a promised draft deadline in first 90 days. Best action?","o":["Mark complete because the request was acknowledged.","Stay silent until asked, so responsiveness looks high.","Flag it early with impact, what was checked and a realistic next checkpoint.","Send unchecked numbers to preserve an appearance of speed."],"a":2}]}];

const FO_MODULES=[{"t":"The most misunderstood job in the company","kick":"A chief of staff can be a force multiplier or an expensive bottleneck. The difference starts with the mandate: whose decisions get better, whose time gets returned, and what this role must never own.","mins":50,"obj":["Compare actual CoS, EA and operating mandates without assuming universal title boundaries.","Explain why the role exists: the leverage math of a principal's scarcest resource, attention","Explain how a mandate can evolve while decision rights and boundaries remain explicit"],"vids":[{"id":"Mz45OETX8f0","t":"What is a Chief of Staff (said 6 ways)","by":"Nova Chief of Staff"}],"reads":[{"t":"What does a chief of staff do? A guide to the most versatile role in business","u":"https://www.chiefofstaff.network/blog/what-does-a-chief-of-staff-do-a-guide-to-the-most-versatile-role-in-business","n":"Study What does a chief of staff do? A guide to the most versatile role in business alongside the written exercise."},{"t":"The Case for a Chief of Staff (Harvard Business Review)","u":"https://www.danciampa.com/images/The%20Case%20for%20a%20Chief%20of%20Staff.pdf","n":"Study The Case for a Chief of Staff (Harvard Business Review) alongside the written exercise."},{"t":"How to be an Exceptional Chief of Staff","u":"https://review.firstround.com/how-to-be-an-exceptional-chief-of-staff-advice-for-scaling-impact-at-startups/","n":"Study How to be an Exceptional Chief of Staff alongside the written exercise."}],"ex":"Fictional role-design exercise, not approval to hire or delegate company authority. A chief of staff can improve a principal's information, decisions, priorities, follow-through and cross-team coordination, but title alone doesn't define scope. Administrative scheduling/travel/correspondence may indicate EA capacity; that is valuable work, not inherently inferior or proof a CoS can never do it. COO often has operating accountability, but P&L, reports and boundaries depend on the actual structure; CoS can manage projects/teams or oversee administrative systems. Founder associate/VP operations titles can overlap. A universal title definition may contradict an explicit mandate. Access to a principal isn't permission to approve budgets, staff changes, contracts or messages on their behalf.\nWrite a proposed one-page charter for hypothetical Series A CEO: mission, context/problem, principal/function-owner relationships, specific decision rights, forbidden actions, escalation path, authorized information/audiences, review date and success measures. Example proposed scope, not real authority: maintain internal decision-log format and agreed review cadence; recommend prioritization; coordinate named project milestones. Budget/hiring/strategy/customer promises and representation remain with designated owners unless explicitly delegated. No exclusive ownership of every cross-functional issue. Principal and affected function owners must agree and communicate boundaries; revise when stage or needs change, rather than silently expand rights because trust or workload grows. A role charter describes a proposal until adopted.\nDiagnose the bottleneck first: administrative load may call for EA support; slow decisions may stem from unclear rights, missing evidence, overload, conflict or the principal retaining decisions. Hiring a CoS is not the sole cure or proof of positive ROI. Separate attention returned from organizational work added. Toy weekly principal saves 6 hours but spends 2 hours reviewing new material=>net 4 principal hours. CoS spends 12 hours and other team members add 3 hours; organization-wide hours don't magically fall 4: added 15 minus principal 4 saved=>net 11 additional hours on this specified task basis. That could still be worthwhile if decision quality/capacity improves; it isn't cash saving or a measured 4 x return. Stipulate principal review part included only once, no double counting.\nToy decision cycle median 10 days to 7 days=>30% reduction on a comparable defined population, not a 3 percentage-point drop or proof CoS caused it. Monitor rework, errors, escalation speed, owner clarity and team trust along with time; a faster dashboard can hide bad decisions or displaced work. Use baseline, period, denominator and qualitative feedback; no single 12 month metric guarantees success. First Round is one practitioner's account of evolving scope/systems and trust, not experimental evidence or blanket consent to record coworkers. Ciampa 2020 levels and CoS Network frameworks differ and can be used as lenses, not universal taxonomies or current salary/role counts. No hire, org-chart change, meeting invite, recording, tool purchase or external communication.","quiz":[{"q":"Role titles alone settle CoS/EA/COO boundaries?","o":["Yes; a CoS may never help administrative work under any charter.","Yes; every COO owns a P&L and every CoS has no direct reports.","No; inspect actual context, mandate, accountabilities and decision rights.","No; boundaries should stay permanently undefined."],"a":2},{"q":"Principal needs travel/calendar support, no other stated gap. Next step?","o":["Conclude administrative work has no organizational value.","Hire a CoS automatically because all busy executives need one.","Assign COO ownership of every flight booking.","Assess administrative/EA capacity before assuming a strategic CoS hire is needed."],"a":3},{"q":"Proposed charter says coordinate project deadlines. May CoS approve a new budget?","o":["Yes; coordination implies all financial decisions.","Not from that scope alone; obtain the designated budget authority.","Yes; closeness to the CEO overrides function ownership.","No; no CoS can ever receive any specific budget delegation."],"a":1},{"q":"Principal gains 6 hours, spends 2 hours on new review. Net principal time returned?","o":["4 hours on the stated weekly basis.","6 hours, ignoring review time.","8 hours, adding review burden as savings.","12 hours, substituting CoS workload for principal benefit."],"a":0},{"q":"Net principal 4 hours saved; CoS 12 and other team 3 hours added. Net organizational hours?","o":["4 hours saved across the entire company.","11 additional hours on that specified task basis.","15 hours saved, treating new work as avoided work.","19 hours saved, adding all figures without direction."],"a":1},{"q":"Comparable median decision cycle 10 days to 7 days. Relative reduction?","o":["3%, confusing days with a percent.","30%.","42.86%, dividing by final rather than baseline.","70%, reporting remaining share instead of reduction."],"a":1},{"q":"Cycle time fell after hiring. CoS impact proven?","o":["No; time metrics can never be useful.","Yes; sequence in time establishes cause.","Yes; no other metric matters.","No; check comparable population, other changes, quality and displaced work."],"a":3},{"q":"Mandate expands as principal trusts CoS. Best boundary practice?","o":["Refuse all scope change even after explicit agreement.","Silently absorb all unresolved decisions.","Assume prior coordination covers every new audience and commitment.","Agree, document and communicate changed rights with affected owners."],"a":3},{"q":"A CoS should maximize how much work passes through them?","o":["No; a CoS can never own any named project.","Yes; queue length is the best proof of usefulness.","Yes; every function decision should go through the principal.","No; build effective ownership and systems, not dependence or extra approvals."],"a":3},{"q":"First Round describes async recordings at one company. Permission to record this team?","o":["No; authorized recordings can never have a useful purpose.","Yes; all coworkers agreed by holding startup jobs.","Yes; best-practice articles override privacy restrictions.","No; a source example is not consent or a universal workflow."],"a":3}]},{"t":"Gatekeepers: power lessons from the White House","kick":"One well-documented version of this job runs the West Wing. Chris Whipple studies how presidents and their chiefs shape each other. Use those cases as a lens for access and candour, not a claim that a startup is the White House.","mins":55,"obj":["Distinguish source-grounded historical role principles from unverified book/video cases and startup interpretation.","Explain access and information influence with fair brokerage, explicit decision rights and urgent-risk safeguards.","Apply the honest-broker-versus-gatekeeper balance to a startup context"],"vids":[{"id":"pow0clfIiMU","t":"The Gatekeepers: How the White House Chiefs of Staff Define Every Presidency","by":"Chris Whipple at Roosevelt House"}],"reads":[{"t":"The Gatekeepers: publisher description and context","u":"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/231946/the-gatekeepers-by-chris-whipple/","n":"Study The Gatekeepers: publisher description and context alongside the written exercise."},{"t":"The Chief of Staff: White House role, gatekeeping and honest brokerage","u":"https://www.whitehousetransitionproject.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/03/WHTP-2009-21-Chief-of-Staff.pdf","n":"Study The Chief of Staff: White House role, gatekeeping and honest brokerage alongside the written exercise."}],"ex":"Use historical evidence as a lens, not a claim a startup is a presidency. Publisher description establishes Whipple's book/edition topic and reported interview-based approach, not verification of every case, talk or causal claim. A summary is not a substitute for the original book or recording. WHTP Report 2009-21, copyright 2008, by Cohen/Walcott/Warshaw/Wayne is a university-authored interview-based institutional study. Its printed 1-4 and 11-14 distinguish managerial/advisory roles, principal-dependent office design, information routing, dangers of excessive access filtering and honest brokerage. Historical interview recollection/author interpretation is evidence with perspective/selection limits, not current-officeholder list, randomized evidence or universal startup prescription.\nStudy says no single best operating style across principals/circumstances. Printed 13 says honest brokerage doesn't mean no opinions or refusal to advise: all relevant perspectives should be fairly represented, without the chief's agenda distorting options. Printed 14 discusses deputies' extra access and the danger of undermining colleagues in private moments. Printed 11 warns discretion can be abused or perceived as abuse; printed 12 contrasts access problems and warns against spur-of-the-moment decisions without staff input. Printed 13 also notes scheduling can be delegated; owning every calendar action isn't the definition of effective CoS. Keep source claim, quoted recollection and your startup interpretation separate. Do not convert anecdotes into current legal authority, a ranking of people or a claim access control alone determines success.\nSynthetic startup translation: create a principal-approved routing policy with topics, evidence, who decides, relevant function reviewers, ordinary cadence and exception route for urgent material risks. Example: routine informational update goes to agreed digest; a proposed spend beyond delegated rights goes to budget owner; credible security/safety/customer-harm risk takes the designated urgent route without waiting for weekly triage. An allegation requires safe, confidential handling and qualified review, not public disclosure or accusation as fact. Report material dissent/uncertainty fairly, distinguish evidence from recommendation, show alternatives and consequences, preserve source access for authorized reviewers. Honesty doesn't require sharing every private detail with everyone.\nAbsorb an agreed scheduling refusal or protect focus time without pretending a principal's decision is your unilateral authority; don't invent agreement or conceal who owns the decision. Gatekeeping must not suppress whistleblowing or material bad news, create retaliation, or become personal-visibility optimization. Restrict needless interruptions while ensuring authorized escalation and fair access, not either total unrestricted access or a single opaque veto. Review missed signals, delays, decision rework and whether people trust the route; meeting count/visibility alone are not success. No actual calendar/access change, private disclosure, refusal message, contact or political action is made.","quiz":[{"q":"Whipple publisher description proves all cases and assigned talk verified?","o":["No; it supports book/edition context, not full source or causal verification.","Treat the publisher synopsis as sufficient verification of the assigned talk's specific claims.","Apply the historical office arrangements directly because the cases involve executive decision making.","Exclude the publisher synopsis from the bibliography because it is not the full primary account."],"a":0},{"q":"WHTP 2009-21 copyright 2008 should be read as?","o":["A historical interview-based institutional study with context and limits.","A baseline description of today's White House office, unless a newer official list is found.","Evidence of the most effective office structure because several experienced chiefs were interviewed.","A practical mandate that a startup CoS can adopt without first agreeing local decision rights."],"a":0},{"q":"Honest brokerage requires the chief have no opinions?","o":["Give the principal the chief's favored option, summarizing dissent only when the principal requests it.","Keep recommendations out of the brief even when asked, so that brokerage remains visibly neutral.","No; advice is compatible with fair representation of relevant perspectives.","Present competing views without their evidence so that the chief cannot appear to prefer a view."],"a":2},{"q":"Deputy has extra private time with principal. Appropriate use?","o":["Use private access to resolve cross-functional disputes before the designated owners see the issue.","Restate colleagues' views in the deputy's preferred framing, while keeping the original notes private.","Ensure relevant information reaches the principal without covertly undermining colleagues.","Prioritize issues personally observed by the deputy, treating colleagues' reports as a later follow-up."],"a":2},{"q":"Control of calendar guarantees effective decision making?","o":["No; decision quality, fair information and explicit rights matter; scheduling can be delegated.","Yes; controlling a door confers every substantive decision right.","Yes; fewer meetings proves no risk is being missed.","No; agenda design can never affect decisions."],"a":0},{"q":"Principal dislikes bad news. What should routing do?","o":["Publish every private allegation as established fact.","Remove uncomfortable evidence from every brief.","Delay urgent harm reports until the weekly digest.","Keep an authorized route for material risks and fairly represent dissent."],"a":3},{"q":"A principal-approved refusal boundary can be communicated how?","o":["Truthfully within scope, preserving ownership of the agreed decision.","Pretend the principal approved when they did not.","Hide the boundary and blame an uninvolved colleague.","Treat a historical anecdote as permission to send any refusal."],"a":0},{"q":"Which claim is justified by source comparison?","o":["The White House operating model transfers unchanged to every company.","Office design depends on principal/context; startup adaptation is an interpretation to test.","One book proves every principal needs the same calendar restrictions.","Historical chiefs had no differences in responsibilities or style."],"a":1},{"q":"Routine digest policy and credible urgent harm signal. Response?","o":["Wait for digest because routine policy has no exceptions.","Use the designated urgent escalation route, with appropriate confidentiality and evidence.","Suppress it because it may hurt the chief's visibility.","Broadcast all details to every employee without authorization."],"a":1},{"q":"CoS makes personal visibility/access the goal. Main organizational risk?","o":["It automatically guarantees better decisions because visibility is measurable.","Information routing starts serving the chief rather than decision quality and the mandate.","It permanently grants authority over every budget.","It proves every visible chief acted dishonestly."],"a":1}]},{"t":"The first 90 days: trust before agenda","kick":"New chiefs of staff arrive with a mandate and a plan. The ones who last arrive with questions instead. Trust is the only currency this role spends, and you earn it before you spend it.","mins":50,"obj":["Design a context-dependent onboarding plan with early authority boundaries and urgent-risk exceptions.","Use a deliberate listening sample, distinguish perspectives from evidence and protect confidentiality.","Choose owner-supported measurable early improvements without claiming guaranteed wins."],"vids":[{"id":"0PjfkFEVE3o","t":"Your First 90 Days as a Chief of Staff","by":"Ask a Chief of Staff"}],"reads":[{"t":"The Chief of Staff onboarding playbook: your first 90 days","u":"https://www.chiefofstaff.network/blog/onboarding-playbook-first-90-days-chief-of-staff","n":"Heuristics/examples do not grant authority or prove outcomes."},{"t":"The first 90 days as a Chief of Staff: an operating plan","u":"https://www.execopsbrief.com/library/first-90-days-chief-of-staff-operating-plan","n":"Heuristics/examples do not grant authority or prove outcomes."}],"ex":"Synthetic onboarding plan, not a request to interview real coworkers or change company operations. A 30-60-90 plan is a planning lens, not a validated universal sequence or guaranteed day 45 win. Diagnose stage, inherited mandate, urgent risks and ownership first. Learn principal preferences AND business/stakeholder context; neither proximity nor trust creates authority. Agree an initial working boundary early, then revise with evidence. CoS Network's 2026 playbook favors learning/visible win/later written mandate; ExecOps operating plan says learning is not a rule against action and calls for an early lightweight agreement. Use that distinction: no need to defer urgent safety, financial or customer harm for 30 days, and no urgent request grants unlimited power. Escalate promptly through authorized routes and keep any permitted intervention narrow, documented and reviewed.\nDraft a hypothetical plan with purpose, stakeholder sample, questions, notes policy, initial rights/exclusions, dependencies and review checkpoints. For the listening tour ask what works, what slows delivery, where decisions recur, where ownership is unclear and what this role should make easier. Deliberately include affected functions and people close to the work, not only loud voices or principal allies. Interview reports are perspectives/hypotheses, not verified wrongdoing or statistical representativeness. Test conflicting accounts against authorized records/observed workflow; don't promise absolute secrecy you cannot keep, secretly record, solicit unrelated secrets or attribute private comments broadly. Return themes with proper audience/access boundaries and consent where required.\nProposed stages: first 30 days build map/working agreement; days 31-60 pilot one or two bounded improvements with owners; days 61-90 keep only useful maintainable routines and agree next-period mandate. Overlap as situation requires. A quick win needs owner support, explicit authority, feasible scope and observable benefit, not merely visibility/low budget. A repeated complaint alone doesn't license changing a meeting, reporting line or investor update. Baseline and guardrails matter. Toy weekly meeting 12 people*60 minutes=12 person-hours; revised 12 people*40 minutes=8 person-hours=>4 meeting-person-hours saved. If extra preparation adds 2 person-hours, net 2 person-hours saved on this defined basis; unchanged decision/rework quality must be checked. Not cash savings or a causal success measure by itself.\nToy commitments 10 due this period, 8 completed and 2 still blocked=>80% completed, not 100% because someone acknowledged all 10. State what changed, blocker/owner and next checkpoint before deadline, not silently remove blocked items from denominator. A promised draft versus approved final are different states. By 90 days seek a useful evidence/ownership map, a few appropriately verified improvements and an agreed ongoing scope; no universal win count or complete discovery of every informal power/grievance. Trust includes candor and boundary discipline, not saying yes to everything or avoiding disagreement. No invitations, interviews, recordings, disclosures, changes or commitments performed.","quiz":[{"q":"First 30 days reveal a credible urgent customer-harm risk. Must learning plan block action?","o":["No; promptly use the authorized escalation route and keep any permitted intervention narrow.","Follow the original sequence and put the risk into the day 31 improvement backlog.","Ask the reporting manager for routine weekly review without mentioning urgency.","Make the broad operational change first and seek retroactive rights at the 90 day review."],"a":0},{"q":"When to clarify initial decision rights?","o":["Early enough to work safely, then revise and communicate as evidence develops.","Wait for the 60 day quick win review so rights reflect the first full month of delivery.","Rely on the principal's introduction to convey whatever authority stakeholders infer.","Use the previous CoS job description unchanged until the 90 day mandate refresh."],"a":0},{"q":"Five interviews repeat a complaint. Conclusion?","o":["A useful hypothesis to test with context/evidence, not proof or a representative census.","Treat five matching interviews as sufficient confirmation and draft the fix now.","Use the most senior interviewee's account as the tie-breaker for conflicting details.","Report the common theme without marking which supporting facts remain unverified."],"a":0},{"q":"Listening-tour sampling should?","o":["Let the principal choose only people who understand the proposed agenda already.","Focus on senior leaders first and treat the resulting themes as company-wide coverage.","Include affected functions and people close to work, with deliberate coverage and limits stated.","Interview convenient respondents and omit the sampling limits from the final map."],"a":2},{"q":"A visible recurring problem qualifies as a quick win automatically?","o":["Prefer the smallest budget change, without considering confidentiality or disruption.","Start where the problem is most visible and collect owner support after the launch.","No; check owner support, authority, feasible scope, baseline and quality guardrails.","Choose the loudest repeated request and use its frequency as the only success measure."],"a":2},{"q":"Meeting 12 people*60 minutes weekly, then 40 minutes with same 12. Meeting-person-hours saved?","o":["4 hours.","20 hours, treating minute reduction as hours.","8 hours, returning revised meeting cost rather than savings.","12 hours, returning baseline meeting cost."],"a":0},{"q":"That meeting saves 4 person-hours but adds 2 person-hours preparation. Net on stated basis?","o":["4 person-hours, ignoring preparation.","2 person-hours saved, before checking quality/displaced work.","6 person-hours, adding new preparation as saving.","0 because all meetings are equally useful."],"a":1},{"q":"10 commitments due; 8 complete/2 blocked. Completion rate?","o":["20%; report only the blocked share as completion.","100%; all 10 were acknowledged.","100%; remove blocked items from the denominator.","80%; blocked work remains in denominator with status explained."],"a":3},{"q":"Interviewee asks for absolute secrecy while discussing possible harm. Best stance?","o":["Retain identifiable notes for broad internal distribution because findings need context.","Offer confidentiality without describing escalation limits until an issue actually arises.","Explain actual confidentiality/escalation limits; handle only necessary details safely.","Let the principal read every verbatim comment before deciding if attribution matters."],"a":2},{"q":"A missed deadline is likely. Trust-building status?","o":["Send the draft as if final so the original deadline is met in the status report.","Wait for a revised delivery estimate before sharing any risk to the current deadline.","Tell owner early, explain impact/blocker and set a grounded next checkpoint.","Update the internal tracker quietly and assume affected owners will notice."],"a":2}]},{"t":"The principal's operating system","kick":"A CEO's calendar is not a schedule. It is the strategy, printed. Whoever shapes those 168 hours a week shapes the company, and that is the chief of staff's first machine to build.","mins":50,"obj":["Use actual time allocation as partial evidence of attention, not literal whole strategy.","Design and test a delegation ladder; measure net principal capacity after review and rework.","Design an owned review cadence with evidence of completion and urgent exceptions, not a guarantee."],"vids":[{"id":"MtrkDoQFArU","t":"Ultimate Guide to Managing Executive Assistants and Delegating Like a Pro","by":"Sam Corcos with Tim Ferriss"}],"reads":[{"t":"The CEO's Weekly Checklist","u":"https://a16z.com/the-ceos-weekly-checklist/","n":"Heuristics/examples do not grant authority or prove outcomes."},{"t":"A tactical guide to working with EAs: make delegation your superpower","u":"https://review.firstround.com/a-tactical-guide-to-working-with-eas-how-to-make-delegation-your-superpower/","n":"Heuristics/examples do not grant authority or prove outcomes."},{"t":"Sam Corcos on delegation and leverage","u":"https://tim.blog/2023/09/20/sam-corcos/","n":"Heuristics/examples do not grant authority or prove outcomes."}],"ex":"Synthetic time/delegation design, no access to another person's calendar or messages implied. Calendar allocation can reveal attention and constraints, but isn't the whole strategy, actual work record or proof values/causality. Include unscheduled work, context switching, outcomes and recovery/personal obligations; don't label all nonmeeting time waste. 168 calendar hours aren't 168 available work hours. Audit your own or an explicitly authorized principal's work with agreed scope/privacy; a two-week window is this exercise convention, not a universal minimum or evidence all weeks are comparable. Missing entries aren't zero work and planned meetings aren't actual attendance.\nBuild a hypothetical working-week time log with mutually exclusive primary categories and secondary tags where useful, duration and output, plus an explicit unknown category. Toy 40 observed work hours:10 decision, 12 build, 8 meet, 6 react, 4 unknown=>40. Meeting share 8/40=20%; don't drop unknown 4 and silently report 8/36 as if full coverage. Decide/build/meet/react are context labels, not universal value rankings. Meetings can contain decisions/building; avoid double counting the same hour under primary categories. Compare week type and role priorities before prescribing change.\nDelegation brief: desired outcome/output, context/source access, owner, decision/representation/spending limits, dependencies, quality check, due time/timezone, exception triggers and feedback/closeout. Ask for playback and a small review sample; a private practice draft is not permission to send to customers/investors. Appropriate low-risk/reversible recurring work may be a candidate, but scheduling can still create social costs, confidential disclosures or cancellation fees. Evaluate permissions, capability, sensitivity, actual reversibility and supervision. Eliminate/automate/delegate is a diagnostic lens, not \"delegate everything automatically\" or proof an EA cannot exercise judgment. Automated does not mean unattended correctness or erased owner accountability.\nToy delegated work previously 3 principal hours weekly, new principal review 0.5 and corrections 0.5=>net 2 hours capacity returned. EA work 4 hours remains real work; not a company-wide 2 hour reduction or cash saving. If setup 5 principal hours paid once and net 2 per week stays unchanged, cumulative principal time break-even 2.5 weeks, first whole week at 3; horizon/maintenance/rework matter. This is time accounting, not current wage/cost quote or predicted results. If no current capability/authority, keep prep below the execution boundary, not repeat an unsafe send to learn.\nWeekly review checklist can support recurring checks of priorities, customers, people, decisions/risks and follow-through but cannot guarantee completion or replace judgment. Adapt owner/cadence to company needs, distinguish reviewed from merely checked, and keep urgent-risk escalation outside weekly delay. a16z 2012 CEO account's suggested customer-time share/MBWA/meeting habits are personal advice, not universal target. First Round 2022 and Ferriss 2023 notes describe one practitioner's delegation/recording workflow, not permission to capture private screens, share accounts or spend. No recording/tool signup/calendar edit/mail/contact or hiring action.","quiz":[{"q":"Calendar planned hours alone prove actual strategy and all work?","o":["Treat calendar hours as the primary outcome measure, regardless of actual attendance.","No; include actual unscheduled work, constraints, outcomes and missingness.","Compare meeting counts across principals without adjusting for scope or week type.","Use only scheduled work and exclude unscheduled demands as measurement noise."],"a":1},{"q":"A week contains 168 calendar hours. Available work hours?","o":["Copy a 40 hour assumption without checking role, recovery and other obligations.","Use the principal's previous busiest week as the standard capacity for all future weeks.","Subtract existing meetings from 168 and call the remainder available work capacity.","Not inferable; sleep, recovery and other obligations must be respected."],"a":3},{"q":"40 observed work hours with 8 meeting hours and 4 unknown. Meeting share of observed total?","o":["80%, reporting the complement.","22.22%, silently discarding unknown hours.","10%, using unknown rather than meeting hours.","20%."],"a":3},{"q":"One hour meeting also makes a decision. Primary category totals?","o":["Exclude all mixed-purpose meetings and treat the missing hours as free capacity.","Count it in both primary categories and compare total against 40 without deduplication.","Count the hour once in the agreed primary scheme; optional secondary tags separate.","Choose the highest-value category each week without keeping classification rules stable."],"a":2},{"q":"Delegation brief includes output/deadline but no decision limits. Ready for external execution?","o":["Proceed with the output deadline and assume the normal tools cover external authority.","Not yet; clarify authority, audience, downside and exceptions before affected action.","Use a prior similar workflow as permission despite the new recipient and spending scope.","Let the delegate choose the audience and effects without a defined escalation boundary."],"a":1},{"q":"Private parallel draft has two successful rehearsals. May it be sent to all investors?","o":["Send to a small investor subset first and ask for formal authority after feedback.","Treat successful internal rehearsals as the final approval for the external audience.","Reuse last quarter recipient list without checking current content or distribution scope.","Not without grounded recipient/content authority and applicable review."],"a":3},{"q":"Prior 3 principal hours, now review 0.5 plus correction 0.5 weekly. Net returned?","o":["3 hours, ignoring review/corrections.","2 hours of principal capacity, not automatic organizational/cash saving.","4 hours, adding review burden to benefit.","0.5 hours, reporting only the review duration."],"a":1},{"q":"Setup 5 principal hours, net 2 hours weekly afterwards, no other changes. Break-even?","o":["2.5 weeks; first whole week 3.","5 weeks, treating setup hours as weeks.","2 weeks, rounding before cumulative time turns positive.","10 weeks, multiplying setup by weekly benefit."],"a":0},{"q":"Recurring scheduling is always harmless/reversible?","o":["Use the principal's available slot as implicit approval for any resulting commitment.","Treat invite editing as sufficient reversal without checking notifications or penalties.","Screen only technical task complexity and ignore recipient and confidentiality risk.","No; check recipients, confidentiality, commitments, fees and real cancellation/reversal route."],"a":3},{"q":"A weekly checklist guarantees critical reviews occur?","o":["Keep the standard checklist fixed even when urgent risks arise between reviews.","Use the completed template as evidence each underlying review actually occurred.","No; verify actual review, owner and follow-through; keep urgent escalation separate.","Measure checklist item count rather than whether owners acted on material findings."],"a":2}]},{"t":"Decision infrastructure","kick":"Companies do not suffer from too few decisions. They suffer from decisions made in hallways, forgotten by Friday and relitigated every quarter. The chief of staff builds the machinery that makes decisions stick.","mins":50,"obj":["Assess reversibility, downside and required authority before choosing decision process.","Write a concise decision brief with linked evidence, uncertainty and explicit approvals.","Keep a traceable decision log that supports review on material new evidence, not immunity to revision."],"vids":[{"id":"KQ4S0pn9tYA","t":"The Chief of Staff Role Through the Eyes of Principals","by":"Emily Sander"}],"reads":[{"t":"High-velocity decision making: the original 2016 shareholder letter","u":"https://www.aboutamazon.com/news/company-news/2016-letter-to-shareholders","n":"Heuristics/examples do not grant authority or prove outcomes."},{"t":"Decision brief memo template","u":"https://hogonext.com/templates/decision-brief-memo","n":"Heuristics/examples do not grant authority or prove outcomes."},{"t":"Decision log template","u":"https://pairnorth.com/templates/decision-log","n":"Heuristics/examples do not grant authority or prove outcomes."}],"ex":"Synthetic decision-infrastructure exercise, not a real decision approval or spend. Amazon 2016 letter, published 2017, distinguishes lightweight process for reversible two-way-door decisions from one-size-fits-all bureaucracy, suggests most decisions \"probably\" around 70% of wished-for information, stresses course correction, reciprocal disagree-and-commit and prompt escalation of deep misalignment. It is a management viewpoint, not a measured 70% confidence threshold or permission to bypass safety, legal, money or disclosure checks. Reversibility must be established in consequences, not assumed because a setting is editable. Reversal time/cost, exposed people, obligations, reputation and unrecoverable data matter; no binary label replaces an actual downside assessment.\nPropose a one-page brief plus linked evidence where needed: exact decision and accountable decider, deadline/timezone/reason, context, alternatives including do-nothing, comparable cost/risk/benefit/horizon, uncertainties, recommendation, needed approvals, implementation owner, monitoring and stop/review/reversal triggers. A one-page/five-minute target is communication practice, not proof all technical/legal evidence fits or approval exists. Don't pad strawman options, force yes/no when choices are genuinely multiway, or omit uncertainty to hit length. Keep confidential source detail within authorized audience; commercial templates don't supply agreement, spending or tool authority.\nToy one-month internal pilot, amounts synthetic and no residual benefit/other costs: setup 300+running 100+expected observed-period benefit 500=>modeled net 100. Benefit 350 instead=>net-50. Expected estimate isn't realized cash saving; identify owner, assumptions and downside. Pilot capped at 400 cost on this toy basis, but a cap isn't authorization. Stipulated guardrail: pause new exposure if complaints exceed 5 in 100 pilot users; 6/100=>6% and exceeds 5% threshold. Equal 5/100 doesn't exceed but still review under actual policy; count/scope/time and qualitative harm matter. No actual user experiment or rollout occurs. A reversible software toggle can still cause irreversible disclosure or charging, so stop limits/rollback aren't proof safety.\nDecision log records status as proposed, approved, in progress, complete or superseded with actual evidence, not \"approved\" because memo exists. Capture decider / authority / date, consulted perspectives/dissent, options/rationale/assumptions, owner/next step, review date and what would change our mind. Preserve prior entries or link superseding decisions rather than silently rewrite history. Revisit when material new evidence, assumptions or conditions change, not freeze a decision to prevent relitigation; avoid repetitive debate with no new basis. Decision owner may need board/regulatory/functional concurrence; DRI doesn't mean unilateral dictatorship, sole expertise or blame for every outcome.\nDisagree-and-commit can include leader supporting a team's view after genuine disagreement; it isn't compelled silence, false consensus or abandonment of risk escalation. Deep goal misalignment may need the designated decider instead of endless meetings/exhaustion. Pair North template explicitly includes change-our-mind/review date; HogoNext brief supplies optional fields/examples, not universal most important line or actual launch approval. No template-product login/upload/installation, real decision log mutation, pilot spend, message or outside action.","quiz":[{"q":"Amazon 2016\"around 70% information\" means?","o":["An instruction to proceed once 70% of required controls are complete.","A numerical stop rule that supplies enough evidence for every decision category.","A useful default that also substitutes for missing spend approval in a reversible pilot.","A qualified management heuristic, not a numerical confidence rule or waiver of required checks."],"a":3},{"q":"Editable setting exports private customer data. Two-way door solely because toggle can be reset?","o":["Yes; defer the confidentiality review until after testing whether rollback works.","Yes; record the toggle undo steps and classify the entire downstream effect reversible.","Yes; use the small size of the exported group instead of checking disclosure authority.","No; disclosure may be irreversible despite reversible UI."],"a":3},{"q":"Decision brief exists with a recommendation. Approval state?","o":["Approved if the decider received the memo before the stated decision deadline.","Proposed unless the proper decider actually approved within authority.","In progress once the memo author has selected a recommended option.","Approved if no consulted stakeholder objects within the next business day."],"a":1},{"q":"One-month pilot setup 300+running 100/benefit 500, no other items. Modeled net?","o":["400, confusing cost with net benefit.","100.","200, omitting running cost.","900, adding costs as benefits."],"a":1},{"q":"Same pilot benefit 350 instead. Modeled net?","o":["+250, subtracting only running cost.","+50, reversing the sign.","-50.","+350, ignoring all cost."],"a":2},{"q":"Pause if complaints exceed 5 in 100 users. 6 complaints/100 users?","o":["5%, because the threshold fixes the observed rate.","6%, exceeds the stated 5% guardrail; use the specified pause/escalation.","0.6%, dividing by 100 twice.","No pause because a pilot is reversible by definition."],"a":1},{"q":"Decision log says old assumption X; material evidence now contradicts X. Response?","o":["Reopen the choice informally but leave decision owner and implementation status unchanged.","Continue unchanged until the scheduled review even if the key assumption has failed.","Update the current rationale without retaining a link to the superseded basis.","Reassess through owner/review route and preserve superseded rationale."],"a":3},{"q":"Disagree-and-commit requires boss always wins?","o":["No; leader may sincerely support team choice while preserving authorized escalation.","The team commits after the leader decides; reciprocal leader commitment is out of scope.","Record consensus instead of disagreement so execution can start without visible friction.","Hold implementation indefinitely whenever a consulted person still prefers an alternative."],"a":0},{"q":"A DRI means?","o":["The memo author has the final call unless someone more senior explicitly objects.","Clear accountable ownership with defined rights and needed consultation/approvals.","The implementation lead may approve dependencies outside their documented rights.","Consulted teams share all decision ownership, so no single owner needs follow-through."],"a":1},{"q":"One-page/five-minute brief target permits omitting material uncertainty?","o":["No; concise summary can link necessary evidence and identify unresolved decisions.","Remove material unresolved assumptions first because a short memo must look decisive.","Collapse multiple viable options to yes/no even when the tradeoffs are different.","Replace supporting technical evidence with the memo author's confidence rating."],"a":0}]},{"t":"The operating rhythm","kick":"A useful rhythm makes information, decisions and follow-through reliable. Keep the cadence stable enough to compare, and flexible enough to fit changing risks and needs.","mins":55,"obj":["Design a proportionate cadence with clear purpose, rights, inputs and ownership.","Compare trends and investigate material anomalies without inventing cause or closure.","Review exceptions, meeting cost and retirement criteria without blocking urgent escalation."],"vids":[{"id":"FmpIMt95ndU","t":"Listen, learn... then lead (leadership context; use the readings for WBR design)","by":"Stanley McChrystal at TED"}],"reads":[{"t":"Chief of Staff Playbook","u":"https://www.mcchrystalgroup.com/docs/default-source/playbooks/chief-of-staff-playbook.pdf","n":"Use this reference to study Chief of Staff Playbook."},{"t":"Weekly business review template","u":"https://orgtp.com/templates/weekly-business-review","n":"Use this reference to study Weekly business review template."},{"t":"The operating rhythm pack","u":"https://peakroad.com/blog/operating-rhythm-pack/","n":"Use this reference to study The operating rhythm pack."}],"ex":"Synthetic operating-rhythm design for a 40 person startup, not permission to cancel meetings or send email. Build only needed daily/weekly/monthly/quarterly forums: purpose, decision rights, participants, frequency/timezone, duration, metric owner/source/definition/cutoff, prework, exceptions, output owner, access limits and review/retirement trigger. There is no universal meeting count, ideal day, attendee range or fixed schedule that guarantees delivery. A reliable cadence supports coordination but must change when context, purpose or material risks change. An authorized urgent risk shouldn't wait for next WBR. Unexplained repeated exceptions can erode reliability; justified communicated exceptions aren't inherently failure. Distinguish review/decision/learning/information purposes; not every meeting must decide something.\nMcChrystal Group playbook selected sections link formal/informal networks, priorities, CIRs and explicit decision space to an operating rhythm. Shared consciousness is a common operating picture, not sharing every secret or suppressing uncertainty. Coordination and influence aren't automatic authority to decide for principal/function owners. This written framework isn't proof the assigned TED talk teaches a WBR procedure. OrgTP WBR reads consistent charts against trend/target/prior-year and drills anomalies; Peak Road favors scorecard/reds and persistent yellows/owners/dates/metric dictionary and adapting cadence. These are different practices to test, not a universal mandate to read every chart aloud or discuss only colored exceptions. A normal aggregate can conceal segment/safety risk; an anomaly may need investigation before a known root cause/action exists. Don't invent certainty, metric owner approval or a causal diagnosis.\nToy four weekly WBRs each 8 attendees*45 minutes:24 person-hours/month. Four weekly preps each 8 people*15 minutes:8 additional person-hours=>32 total. A proposed 30 minute WBR saves 8 meeting-person-hours/month, but if prep now 30 minutes per person weekly, prep becomes 16 and total still 32. Quality/decision latency/rework and actual basis matter, not declaring 8 net saved. All-time is labor capacity, not cash/causal attribution. Keep a metric dictionary and denominator/missingness:12 late from 200 shipments=6%; later 12 late from 300=4%, down 2 percentage points or 33.33% relative, same count. No causal link to WBR without further evidence, and definitions/windows/composition must match.\nDraft one sample agenda: review prior commitments and data caveats, scan comparable trends, choose material anomalies/risks, agree investigation/decision/action ownership and checkpoint. Artifacts show what was actually approved versus proposed, next owner/date/status and unresolved evidence. Same-day recap is a useful target, not universal statutory rule or permission to circulate confidential material widely. Confirm closure evidence rather than assume a promised action was completed. Draft a private retirement note for one hypothetical redundant meeting with reason, replacement/escalation/access route and effective date only after owner approval. A meeting with no current decision may still serve useful learning/coordination; don't delete solely by that criterion. No invites, calendar changes, meeting cancellation, private data pulls, recordings, messages or products purchased.","quiz":[{"q":"A schedule stays identical while the business and risks change. Good rhythm by itself?","o":["No; replace every agenda weekly even when comparison needs stability.","Yes; keep the original deck even when definitions no longer fit.","Yes; attendance and fixed day are enough to demonstrate decision quality.","No; preserve useful consistency while reviewing purpose, data, participants and exceptions."],"a":3},{"q":"An urgent credible risk appears between WBRs. Route?","o":["Change all decision rights immediately without the appropriate approval.","Save it for the next scorecard because cadence must not be interrupted.","Keep it only in the pre-read without flagging its urgency.","Use the agreed urgent escalation path now; don't wait for the weekly forum."],"a":3},{"q":"Scorecard green overall but one segment shows material customer harm. Handling?","o":["Change the segment definition so it matches the aggregate.","Exclude it because only red metrics may ever be discussed.","Investigate/escalate the material issue despite aggregate color.","Wait for the overall average to cross a threshold first."],"a":2},{"q":"Cause of an anomaly isn't known yet. Record?","o":["Unknown cause, investigation owner/evidence needs and next checkpoint.","A confident cause chosen from the most plausible narrative.","Closed action because somebody promised to investigate.","Remove the anomaly until a fully verified root cause exists."],"a":0},{"q":"Four weekly meetings 8 people*45 minutes each. Monthly meeting-person-hours?","o":["24.","6, ignoring participant count.","32, adding prep not yet specified.","180, confusing elapsed minutes with person-hours."],"a":0},{"q":"Plus four weekly prep rounds 8 people*15 minutes. Total meeting+prep?","o":["40 person-hours, counting prep twice.","24 person-hours, omitting prep.","8 person-hours, returning prep alone.","32 person-hours."],"a":3},{"q":"Proposed 30 min meetings but prep 30 min per person weekly, same 4 weeks/8 people. Net time saved vs 32?","o":["16; count only new meeting time.","8; count only meeting reduction and ignore added prep.","0; meetings 16+prep 16=32.","32; assume prework costless."],"a":2},{"q":"Late 12 of 200 then 12 of 300, matched definitions. Rate change?","o":["6% to 4%:down 2% relative, count halved.","6% to 4%:down 2 percentage points,33.33% relative; count unchanged.","4% to 6%:up 2 percentage points.","12% both periods because late count is 12."],"a":1},{"q":"Does a WBR recap imply every proposed action was approved?","o":["Yes; documenting a recommendation is enough authorization.","No; separate proposed/approved/under investigation and verify rights/closure.","Yes; metric owner attendance approves all cross-functional commitments.","No; no operating review may ever make a decision."],"a":1},{"q":"Retire a hypothetical redundant meeting. Appropriate draft?","o":["Delete without replacement so people discover the new process themselves.","Announce cancellation immediately because low meeting count is the success metric.","Reason, replacement/escalation route, affected owners and approval before real cancellation.","Keep forever because a fixed cadence must never change."],"a":2}]},{"t":"Influence without authority","kick":"Influence depends on trust, context and a fair process. Formal authority is a separate, explicit agreement, not a side effect of proximity to the principal.","mins":50,"obj":["Distinguish credibility and influence from formal authority.","Use prewire conversations to learn, test options and surface dissent without predetermining approval.","Build a useful intake channel with access and urgent-risk safeguards."],"vids":[{"id":"RP4vJeIb7WU","t":"The art of influence: the skill AI cannot replace","by":"Jessica Fain on Lenny's Podcast"}],"reads":[{"t":"The art of influence","u":"https://www.lennysnewsletter.com/p/the-art-of-influence-jessica-fain","n":"Use this reference to study The art of influence."},{"t":"Role clarity: what the chief of staff owns and does not own","u":"https://www.antoinebuteau.com/the-chief-of-staff-operating-model-series-2-role-clarity-what-the-chief-of-staff-owns-and-does-not-own/","n":"Use this reference to study Role clarity: what the chief of staff owns and does not own."},{"t":"Ask a Chief of Staff newsletter","u":"https://askachiefofstaff.substack.com/","n":"Use this reference to study Ask a Chief of Staff newsletter."}],"ex":"Synthetic influence/prewire plan, not a request to negotiate with actual stakeholders or trade real concessions. Use a fictional decision and three skeptical stakeholder roles: concerns, incentives, evidence needed, affected people, formal rights and next question. Influence can come from competence, reliability, fair process and shared context; formal authority needs an explicit communicated mandate. Neither title nor proximity/credibility grants automatic power to approve spend, speak as CEO, override owners, bypass confidential channels or promise resources. Some CoS roles own substantial delivery/people work, so \"owns almost nothing\" isn't a universal definition. Antoine Buteau role-clarity piece distinguishes owns/drives temporarily/supports/does not own, explicit rights/representation norms and review/handoff. This is practitioner advice, not law or a mandatory organizational template.\nPrewire is authorized advance learning/context sharing: test assumptions, understand constraints, present real options and surface tradeoffs early. Go to learn, not merely persuade. The Fain publisher summary recommends asking what led a puzzling decision and notes global versus local optimization/why one option can fail. Do not conclude every executive is right or has superior information, or quote an unverified full episode. Update recommendation if evidence changes. Private conversations don't replace a legitimate collective board/team decision, eliminate dissent, allow deceptive selective facts or guarantee that the meeting only ratifies. Meeting exploration can be necessary; don't pressure private commitments or trade away an absent team's resources. Mark proposed concessions as not offered until within real authority and approval. Offer a conditional alternative, not a manufactured commitment.\nWrite a hypothetical stakeholder map with agreed privacy limits: function, current objection, evidence source/uncertainty, permission to share, actual decision role and escalation route. Start sequence based on dependencies/knowledge/risks and include affected voices, not automatically senior first, ally first or opponent first. Return material objections fairly to the authorized group with attribution bounded by consent/confidentiality, not verbatim private gossip. A useful intake channel has clear owner, stated purpose, response expectations and urgent exceptions. Reducing friction may increase voluntary use; don't block direct access, create artificial switching costs, hoard info or treat every alternate route as personal insubordination. Whistleblowing/safety/conflict channels may need to bypass routine routing.\nToy three stakeholder weights 50/30/20 specified only for a synthetic prioritization model. Evidence-readiness scores 80%/50%/100%=>weighted 75%; this is an internal readiness index, not 75% probability of approval, consent or legitimate authority. If one required approver declines, weighted score cannot override them. Three consultations completed of five planned=>60% outreach done, not 60% agreement/representative evidence. A stakeholder may acknowledge the brief yet not approve the proposal. Capture agreed/disagreed/unknown, actual next step/owner/date; silence isn't consent. The Ask-a-CoS homepage is a publication overview; use the specific written exercise rather than treating the homepage as a lesson. No recording, actual stakeholder interview, promise, budget change, access restriction, message, publication subscription or other execution.","quiz":[{"q":"Credibility and principal proximity grant formal decision rights?","o":["Yes; the principal's introduction approves every later cross-functional tradeoff.","Yes; strong relationships supersede the functional owner's charter.","No; formal rights need an explicit communicated scope; credibility supports influence.","No; CoS roles never possess any delegated rights."],"a":2},{"q":"Advance stakeholder conversation should aim to?","o":["Seek private agreement on the recommendation first and leave contrary views outside the decision record.","Learn constraints/evidence and surface options and objections before the proper decision.","Discuss only the preferred option so the final meeting can focus on implementation rather than alternatives.","Adjust cost assumptions separately for each stakeholder so the recommendation fits their stated priorities."],"a":1},{"q":"Puzzling executive decision, applying Fain publisher summary?","o":["Ask what led to the view and examine constraints before arguing for your option.","Assume the executive view is automatically correct because it is global.","Send more facts without checking whether you understand the decision problem.","Escalate immediately to the principal without exploring the relevant concern."],"a":0},{"q":"Prewire discussion finds material contradictory evidence. Next?","o":["Continue with the earlier assessment so the principal can compare a stable recommendation at the planned meeting.","Update assessment and surface relevant uncertainty through the authorized decision route.","Keep the contradiction in private notes, then address it only if someone challenges the recommendation.","Share the contradiction after the meeting with the implementation plan, avoiding disruption of the decision."],"a":1},{"q":"Offer another team's engineer-week to win stakeholder buy-in?","o":["Yes; cross-functional coordination inherently includes staff allocation rights.","Only as a proposal until the proper owner has authorized that resource commitment.","Yes; if it improves weighted stakeholder support even without owner agreement.","No; resource options should never appear in a private planning draft."],"a":1},{"q":"Stakeholder routes directly to CEO. Durable response?","o":["Clarify useful routing/rights and preserve legitimate direct or urgent channels.","Make your route compulsory in every case so bypassing is expensive.","Add reporting steps that discourage alternate access regardless of purpose.","Ask CEO to publicly enforce personal control on every interaction."],"a":0},{"q":"Weights 50/30/20; readiness 80%/50%/100%. Weighted index?","o":["75%, not approval probability or consent.","76.67%, using an unweighted mean.","230%, adding raw readiness percentages.","50%, reporting only the largest weight."],"a":0},{"q":"One required approver declines but readiness index is 75%. Approved?","o":["Yes; if the principal's closest ally has the largest weight.","Yes; any weighted score above 50% overrides individual required rights.","No; model score cannot substitute for required actual approval.","No; the whole project must end permanently without revised options."],"a":2},{"q":"Three of five planned consultations complete. Conclusion?","o":["100% approval if the three consulted were senior leaders.","60% approval established because meetings occurred.","60% of planned outreach complete; not 60% agreement or representativeness.","40% complete, reporting remaining consultations instead."],"a":2},{"q":"Sensitive objection needs group consideration. Share?","o":["Share the verbatim private objection with the full group so neutrality can be independently checked.","Share only authorized substance/audiences, within consent, attribution and escalation limits.","Keep the objection entirely private until everyone supports the proposed recommendation.","Give each stakeholder only the part of the evidence most likely to secure their support."],"a":1}]},{"t":"Board prep and the executive narrative","kick":"Prepare an accurate board narrative and explicit asks, while preserving real deliberation and the board's decision rights.","mins":50,"obj":["Plan source-owned board preparation with reconciled numbers and controlled revisions.","Explain the miss, alternatives, uncertainties and bounded asks honestly.","Preserve board deliberation, disclosure boundaries and traceable follow-up states."],"vids":[{"id":"4OJ7Dll0Mvs","t":"A Startup CEO's Guide to Board Meetings, with Dave Kellogg","by":"Run the Numbers with CJ Gustafson"},{"id":"VP6FATP84lU","t":"How to Build the Perfect Board Deck","by":"LeanScale"}],"reads":[{"t":"Executive narrative and board prep","u":"https://resources.rework.com/guides/chief-of-staff-playbooks/exec-narrative-board-prep","n":"Use this reference to study Executive narrative and board prep."},{"t":"The secret to a great planning process","u":"https://www.lennysnewsletter.com/p/the-secret-to-a-great-planning-process","n":"Use this reference to study The secret to a great planning process."}],"ex":"Fictional Series A board deck for a revenue miss, not real disclosure, director outreach, financial statement or board approval. Board preparation needs accurate context and genuine deliberation, not a staged ratification or winning narrative. Use a concise story anchored in comparable plan/actual/source period/definitions, material changes/risks, assumptions/uncertainties, options and actual asks. Some boards have no major approval item that meeting; governance/oversight and other duties still matter. No universal single decision,8-12 slide max,7 metric max, exact 3 asks,2 week production line or 24/48 hour deadline applies to all companies. Rework uses those author conventions and anecdotal 80 to 25 hours, not independently measured causal result or guaranteed timing. Timeframe and distribution must follow applicable governance/docs/approved owner process, not a template. No current legal rule asserted.\nProposed owner-reviewed cadence: agree narrative/data asks and board needs early; financial/functional owners supply traceable inputs; reconcile baseline/period/units/cohorts and cross-slide consistency; keep unknown/provisional numbers visibly labeled. Draft/review/confidential dry run and distribute with appropriate notice/access, not automatically the night before. Changes after data freeze need controlled versions and material correction notice, not silent replacement or hiding late bad news. Compare revenue actual 2.1 million/plan 2.4 million=>-0.3 million/-12.5% plan variance, not Rework printed 12%. Source comment can be illustrative but all exact calculations independently check. A deal slipping 0.4 million is an explanation hypothesis; don't invent its probability/closing date or falsely assign all 0.3 million net miss to it without positive offsets/coherent bridge. Toy bridge 2.4 plan-.4 slip+.1 other favorable=2.1 actual. Gross profit and revenue definitions/cash timing differ.\nToy starting cohort 100 customers/10 lost/no acquisitions=>90% gross logo retention. Starting cohort ARR 1 million, churn 0.1 million+contraction 0.05 million+expansion 0.2 million=>ending 1.05 million/NRR 105%; GRR 85% excluding expansion. New customers outside the opening cohort aren't added to NRR. Synthetic no FX/acquisitions/reclassifications/window effects. Revenue target miss isn't automatically ARR miss, NRR decline or liquidity runway; keep measures/cohorts distinct. Definitions, dates and source ownership before color rules. A favorable aggregate doesn't justify omitting material risk or an adverse cohort, and uncertainty isn't a sign of incompetence to remove from slides.\nWrite an outline: agenda/context, honest miss/variance bridge, customer/cohort/operational evidence suited to this business, cash/risk/uncertainty, options/tradeoffs, bounded asks and next steps. Use appendix for supporting depth, not burying material miss/risks. Potential prewire roles: CEO/CFO for source/variance; relevant functional owner for diagnosis/alternatives; designated board chair/appropriate owner for agenda/process. These are private proposed roles, not approved contacts or a blanket instruction to tell each director separately. Need-to-know, conflict handling, fair access, disclosure rules and board decision rights apply. Communicate material news promptly through the authorized route, don't wait solely for polished deck; neither private prewire nor management consensus approves board-reserved decisions. Confidentiality does not justify hiding material information from its rightful audience.\nAfter meeting record actually decided/proposed/open with owner/date and appropriate review/distribution. A CoS recap isn't formal approved minutes or evidence legal quorum/consent/vote occurred. Reconcile divergent recollection with designated governance owner while preserving record, not retroactively mark a discussed hire approved. Protect privileged/personnel detail with qualified review; don't independently classify legal privilege. Lenny 2019 planning excerpt gives W framework context/plans/integration/buy-in as process advice, not board approval or proof universal outcomes. Kellogg videos aren't verified from Rework prose; source-attributed video quiz claims held until publisher transcript/AV checked. No real investor call, board email, deck share, confidential upload, hiring commitment or securities action.","quiz":[{"q":"Actual 2.1 million vs plan 2.4 million. Revenue variance?","o":["-0.3 million and-12% exactly, copying printed example.","-0.3 million and-12.5% of plan.","-0.3 million and-14.29% of plan, dividing by actual.","+0.3 million and+12.5%, reversing actual minus plan."],"a":1},{"q":"Plan 2.4, one deal-.4, other favorable+.1. Actual?","o":["2.9 million, treating the slipped deal as a gain.","2.0 million, omitting the favorable offset.","2.3 million, subtracting only the favorable change.","2.1 million; bridge reconciles the net-.3 miss."],"a":3},{"q":"Opening cohort 100 customers,10 lost/no acquisitions. Gross logo retention?","o":["110%, adding lost customers.","10%, reporting churn rate.","100%, treating all opening logos as retained.","90%."],"a":3},{"q":"Opening cohort ARR 1 m, churn 0.1 m/contraction 0.05 m/expansion 0.2 m. NRR?","o":["120%, counting expansion but ignoring all losses.","85%, omitting expansion.","115%, ignoring churn.","105%."],"a":3},{"q":"Same opening cohort, GRR excluding expansion?","o":["90%, omitting contraction.","105%, including expansion.","95%, omitting churn.","85%."],"a":3},{"q":"Material miss and deck not polished. Route?","o":["Timely authorized material update with uncertainty/context, not delay solely for presentation.","Wait for the polished deck regardless of timing/duty.","Tell only the most supportive director to secure private backing.","Place it in appendix and assume every director will notice."],"a":0},{"q":"Does 8-12 slides/exact 3 asks guarantee a proper board meeting?","o":["No; author conventions adapt to board needs, evidence and applicable process.","Yes; short deck substitutes for needed financial reconciliation.","Yes; any fourth material issue must be omitted to preserve prioritization.","No; every board always requires 47 slides."],"a":0},{"q":"Management prewires all participants to a recommendation. Board approval?","o":["Approved if no director responded before the deck deadline.","Approved because private alignment removes the need for the meeting.","Not established; actual rightful board deliberation/approval still required.","Completed when the CEO signs off the slide wording."],"a":2},{"q":"New material evidence after data freeze. Handling?","o":["Keep old number to preserve a stable deck even if misleading.","Verify/reconcile, label revisions and notify through authorized correction route.","Silently replace distributed slides without version/correction notice.","Move the changed number to appendix so headline stays consistent."],"a":1},{"q":"Postmeeting CoS recap says hire discussed. Hire approved?","o":["No; all discussed items are permanently rejected.","Yes; discussion in recap is the same as formal approval.","Yes; the role appears on a draft ask slide.","Not proven; verify actual decision and required approval record with appropriate owner."],"a":3}]},{"t":"How the role fails","kick":"Use failure patterns as pre-mortem prompts, not labels that blame someone before checking expectations and context.","mins":50,"obj":["Use eight source-named patterns as context-dependent diagnostics.","Clarify expectations, rights and capacity before blaming a roleholder.","Respect administrative work and diagnose actual support needs."],"vids":[{"id":"L19TrFMw8k4","t":"WTF does a Chief of Staff actually do? Our day by day schedule","by":"Top Of The Ops"}],"reads":[{"t":"8 ways the chief of staff role fails (and how to fix it)","u":"https://top-of-the-ops.com/episode-7-8-ways-chief-of-staff-role-fails","n":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms."},{"t":"The expectation gap: why good chiefs of staff fail","u":"https://www.primeexecutiveoffice.com/insights/ms7qyiwihbpy9mfvdim8nvmfdh9j2a","n":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms."}],"ex":"Private synthetic pre-mortem, not a diagnosis of actual coworkers or instruction to resign, hire or take over work. Pick two risks, early signals, alternative explanations, impact, owner, permitted countermeasure and review date. Top of the Ops episode 7 names eight overlapping practitioner patterns: unable to let go of execution; role drifts into EA work; weak credibility with broader team; yes-person; timing/stage mismatch; exclusion from needed context; only fixing recurring fires; invisible work. Not an exhaustive validated taxonomy, prevalence ranking or proof CoS always causes failure. Its publisher transcript contains differing stage views: one host favors Series A, another explicitly says seed can fit depending on company. Do not score funding stage alone as a mandatory rule or copy gender/low-ego stereotypes. Assigned day-by-day-schedule video is not established as this episode 7; this transcript does not establish what the separately assigned video says.\nAdministrative and execution work are valuable, not inferior by title. The risk is persistent mismatch with agreed mandate/resources, not booking one meeting or helping in a crisis. Temporary support needs owner, capacity tradeoff, end/review and accepted handoff. Stable maintenance can move to a capable long-term owner with actual capacity/authority, not be abandoned or dumped. Diagnose administrative load, functional gap, missing rights, resource constraints or unclear expectations before prescribing CoS/EA/COO/process. Prime's expectation gap concerns executive assumptions, CoS understanding AND broader organization expectations. A generic job description isn't enough: agree purpose, rights/nonrights, outputs, channels, exceptions and review. Quarterly review is a source suggestion, not a bar to earlier changes.\nCandor means truthful challenge, evidence and alternatives, not public disagreement on every choice, harassment or automatic obedience. A safe-space pact doesn't waive ethical/reporting/confidentiality duties or guarantee no consequences. Neither loyalty nor principal access grants power to replace the CEO. Use legitimate risk/conflict/governance routes when needed. Exclusion can preserve personnel/privacy/conflict/privilege boundaries. Clarify needed decision context through authorized channels, not forced attendance in every room or secret extraction from colleagues. No automatic unrestricted access after four to six months.\nToy 40 work hours: 18 reactive, 12 planned execution, 6 strategy, 4 administration. Strategy share 6/40=15%, not proof ideal/failed allocation. Transfer 4 maintenance hours with owner acceptance; if principal review adds 1 hour, net principal capacity returned 3, not automatic organizational/cash saving. Comparable incident counts 8 then 5 =>37.5% reduction, not 3 percentage points or causal proof. Exposure, severity, time window and underreporting matter. Worklog shows contribution, collaborators, evidence and limits, not fabricated wins or all company growth attributed to one person. Keep private details within authorized audience. No real interviews, access changes, handoffs, resignation, hires or messages.","quiz":[{"q":"One host favors Series A, another says seed can fit. Conclusion?","o":["Series B cannot have meaningful CoS scope.","Series A guarantees success.","Seed is prohibited regardless of coordination needs.","Assess actual mandate, maturity, resources and needs; stage alone is not decisive."],"a":3},{"q":"Booking one meeting proves role failure?","o":["Compare title alone with a peer CoS instead of actual workload and expectations.","Treat any logistics task as sufficient evidence the strategic mandate was abandoned.","No; compare persistent workload and expectations with agreed scope and capacity.","Ignore cumulative task drift because each individual request takes little time."],"a":2},{"q":"Stable maintenance can move elsewhere. Before transfer?","o":["Keep forever to remain indispensable.","Send a checklist and assume acceptance.","Stop because CoS work must always be novel.","Agree receiving owner, capability, capacity, authority and reviewed handoff."],"a":3},{"q":"Principal/CoS agree privately, team still unsure of rights. Enough?","o":["Keep rights private to preserve flexibility and ask teams to infer them as work arises.","No; communicate relevant boundaries with affected owners and check understanding.","Let the principal introduction substitute for clarifying decisions versus coordination.","Use the original job description without checking how affected teams interpret it."],"a":1},{"q":"Safe-space pact makes confidentiality absolute?","o":["No; actual duties and safeguards remain.","Yes; it overrides governance and safety.","Yes; promise immunity to every speaker.","No; candid private disagreement can never help."],"a":0},{"q":"Excluded from a personnel-sensitive meeting. First response?","o":["Assume missing one sensitive meeting proves broad mandate failure.","Request the full private transcript rather than identifying the minimum context needed.","Respect legitimate limits; clarify context needed for assigned work.","Seek informal copies from colleagues before clarifying the access boundary."],"a":2},{"q":"40 work hours, 6 strategy. Strategy share?","o":["40%, using the total as numerator.","6%, treating hours as percent.","15%, with context needed to judge fit.","85%, returning nonstrategy share."],"a":2},{"q":"Transfer 4 hours, add 1 principal review hour. Net principal capacity?","o":["1 hour, reporting only review.","4 hours, ignoring review.","5 hours, adding review burden as benefit.","3 hours, not automatic company-wide/cash saving."],"a":3},{"q":"Incident counts 8 then 5. Relative reduction?","o":["3%, treating count difference as percent.","37.5%, not causal proof or exposure-adjusted rate.","62.5%, reporting remaining proportion.","60%, using new count as denominator."],"a":1},{"q":"Invisible work concern. Appropriate response?","o":["Claim all company gains as personal impact.","Share permitted contribution/status evidence, credit collaborators and state limits.","Publish private principal praise without audience permission.","Treat activity count as verified outcome value."],"a":1}]},{"t":"Money, levels and what comes next","kick":"A dated global salary survey is not a current local offer. Compare source limits, scope and actual compensation terms.","mins":55,"obj":["Reconcile dated salary evidence and sampling limits.","Separate guaranteed cash, conditional bonuses and contingent equity.","Treat career pathways and levels as examples, not guaranteed progression."],"vids":[{"id":"mYAHbud2YME","t":"How to MAKE MONEY (and Have Influence) as a Chief of Staff","by":"Arlan Hamilton"}],"reads":[{"t":"Chief of Staff Salary Report 2025","u":"https://www.chiefofstaff.network/blog/chief-of-staff-salary-report-2025","n":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms."},{"t":"Chief of Staff Pathways Report 2023","u":"https://www.chiefofstaff.network/blog/chief-of-staff-pathways-report-2023","n":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms."},{"t":"Founder's Office: the India angle","u":"https://foundersoffice.xyz/","n":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms."},{"t":"How to become a startup chief of staff","u":"https://www.1752.vc/learn/how-to-become-a-startup-chief-of-staff","n":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms."}],"ex":"Private compensation-evidence exercise, not current local salary advice, an application or a negotiation. Table fields: source/capture/publication date, currency/geography, sample/method/selection, scope/stage/level, base/bonus/equity/benefits/vesting, mean/median/range and contradictions. Salary Report 2025 live page states nearly 500 respondents, 100 cities, 26 countries, 80% US and base salary. It explicitly prioritizes charts over text: chart average $165,574 (~165.6 k), text $167,954; headline share over $200 k is 23%. Practice comparison of conflicting figures rather than unqualified numeric recall. Keep dated source figures and disagreement, not universal pay, total compensation or current Indian market. Nearly 500 isn't exactly 500; don't infer exact counts. Six self-selected levels on this page aren't universal L 1-L 5 company bands or causal progression. AI-first $189 k versus novice $149 k is association, not proof training causes a $40 k raise; source itself says no causal inference.\n1752 guide repeats $167,954/23.8% and says sample/region not disclosed, conflicting with current assigned page's explicit sample/region/chart priority. Preserve source differences rather than average them. Its illustrative option percentage times fundraising valuation divided by four vesting years is paper arithmetic, not cash salary or realizable option value. Strike, cliff/vesting, dilution, common/preferred rights, liquidity, taxes and failure risk matter. Treat BLS/Carta/a16z references as leads to dated underlying sources, not as current local-offer data. Pathways 2023 uses about 400 public alumni records with title/inflation/missingness caveats. Size alone doesn't establish its claimed 95% population confidence or representative sampling. Selected graduates suggest possible exits, not individual promotion/tenure/COO/VC/founder guarantees or MBA requirement.\nHypothetical local jobs, NOT actual offers/listings: A annual base 12 lakh plus guaranteed cash 2 lakh; B base 13 lakh plus conditional target bonus 3 lakh. Guarantee-only A 14/B 13 lakh, target scenario B 16 not guaranteed. Equity/benefits/tax/commute/hours/risk unpriced; no all-in ranking or CTC=takehome claim. On stipulated synthetic 52-week basis A 45 h/week and B 55 h/week, guaranteed cash per hour A 1400000/(45*52)=598.29; B 1300000/(55*52)=454.55. These aren't actual hours, takehome, statutory wages/overtime or pay promises. Toy posted range 10-18 lakh isn't employer median or accepted individual pay.\nReal exercise needs two currently open comparable local roles from direct employer sources: observed URL/date, location/remote scope, actual mandate/rights/principal, stage, pay definition and open status. Unknown pay stays unknown; no FX-converted US recommendation or funding-round salary proxy. Assigned foundersoffice.xyz is an assorted social/job feed, not standardized Indian compensation evidence or proof a repost remains open. The local-offer rows are fictional examples, not a completed market survey.\nNegotiate with truthful relevant evidence, actual scope/constraints, alternatives and equity terms, not an asserted universal strongest asset, invented achievement or private client work. Titles may overlap or differ; no title grants authority or a guaranteed exit. No contacts, accounts, report purchases, applications, commitments or negotiation sends.","quiz":[{"q":"Page says chart 165,574/text 167,954 and prioritizes charts. Response?","o":["Prefer the secondary citation without checking which source changed.","Average the two figures and omit the disagreement from the table.","Record discrepancy, date, definition and stated chart priority.","Use the text figure while labeling it chart-derived for consistency."],"a":2},{"q":"80% US sample, nearly 500 respondents. Indian pay advice?","o":["Convert mean and call it Indian market median.","Not by FX alone; gather comparable local scope/pay evidence.","Match titles to erase stage/scope differences.","Assume all 26 countries equally represented."],"a":1},{"q":"AI-first group earns 40 k more. Training causes 40 k raise?","o":["Yes; mean difference identifies causal return.","Not established; association may reflect confounding and selection.","Yes; self-chosen levels control all other factors.","No; even association cannot be described."],"a":1},{"q":"~400 public graduates proves representative 95% confidence?","o":["Assume public alumni histories reflect all incumbents and people who left silently.","Treat population size and record count as sufficient without examining selection.","No; selection, coverage, missingness and sampling design matter.","Infer individual promotion odds directly from visible graduate profiles."],"a":2},{"q":"A base 12+guaranteed 2; B base 13+conditional 3 lakh. Guarantee-only?","o":["A 14/B 13 lakh; B 16 is conditional target scenario.","A 12/B 16, dropping guaranteed A cash and guaranteeing B target.","A 14/B 16, guaranteeing all bonuses.","A 16/B 13, swapping bonus amounts."],"a":0},{"q":"A 14 lakh/(45 h*52 weeks). Synthetic hourly cash?","o":["About 31111.11, dividing by 45 and omitting weeks.","About 538.46, using 50 hours.","About 26923.08, using 52 weeks alone.","About 598.29, not actual takehome/statutory wage."],"a":3},{"q":"B 13 lakh/(55 h*52 weeks). Synthetic hourly cash?","o":["About 454.55, before omitted costs/tax/fit.","About 559.44, using conditional 16 lakh as guaranteed.","About 23636.36, omitting 52 weeks.","About 25000, dividing only by 52."],"a":0},{"q":"Posted 10-18 lakh range proves actual accepted salary?","o":["No; check pay definition/status and actual offer.","Yes;14 lakh midpoint is proven employer median.","Yes; every candidate has an approved 18 lakh offer.","No; posted ranges never provide useful evidence."],"a":0},{"q":"Equity percentage*round valuation/4 equals annual salary?","o":["Yes; preferred round price makes common options cash-equivalent.","No; paper arithmetic omits strike/vesting/dilution/rights/liquidity/tax/risk.","Yes; labeling illustrative removes valuation risk.","No; equity terms never matter."],"a":1},{"q":"Founders office/CoS title determines next career role?","o":["Use the largest salary example as proof every role grants similar scope.","Use funding stage and title as a complete substitute for actual mandate.","Treat the most successful graduate profile as a standard predicted exit.","No; compare mandate, rights, context and truthful evidence; no guaranteed exit."],"a":3}]}];
const OSM_MODULES=[{"t":"What an organization actually is","kick":"Compare formal roles with actual decisions, communication and work before prescribing a new structure.","mins":"1.5 hrs","obj":["Explain historical strategy-structure arguments without forcing every reorg.","Compare reporting, explicit rights and actual communication/work flows.","Apply Conway and agile frameworks within source and authority limits."],"vids":[{"id":"jyaS1gy1XkM","t":"Monoliths vs Microservices is Missing the Point - Manuel Pais and Matthew Skelton","by":"IT Revolution"}],"reads":[{"t":"Strategy and structure redux","u":"https://www.london.edu/think/strategy-and-structure-redux","n":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms."},{"t":"The five trademarks of agile organizations","u":"https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/people-and-organization/our-insights/the-five-trademarks-of-agile-organizations","n":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms."},{"t":"MIT OCW 15.668 People and Organizations","u":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-668-people-and-organizations-fall-2010/","n":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms."},{"t":"How Do Committees Invent? (original paper)","u":"https://www.melconway.com/Home/Committees_Paper.html","n":"Melvin Conway's 1968 essay. Focus on the introduction, organization-design stages and conclusion, and test its assumptions rather than treating the argument as a universal law."}],"ex":"Synthetic organizational diagnosis, not permission to reorganize a company or disclose staff. Use a fictional/public example: formal reporting chart AND separate actual work/decision/communication map. For three decisions list decider, contributors, required approvals, constraints, evidence, escalation and ambiguity. Charts show formal reporting, not all rights/incentives/informal networks/process/culture/technical dependencies/behavior. Separate charter, observed practice and hypotheses. Three teams involved may mean legitimate consultation and approvals, not automatically bad ambiguity. One accountable decider can coexist with required expertise/control; don't infer authority from diagram/seniority/proximity.\nLBS 2008 describes Chandler's historical US firms/divisionalization and strategy-before-structure thesis, but explicitly discusses criticism/interdependence. Not proof every strategy change forces a reorg or divisional structure always wins. Test capability/decision/incentive/information mismatch against process/rights/coordination changes or no change. Slowing/escalation is a possible signal, not universally first symptom or single causal diagnosis. Existing structure may fit new strategy. Reorg disruption/cost/authority matters.\nConway 1968 author-hosted primary paper states system-design organizations are constrained to produce designs copying communication structures. Focus on the author's introduction, organization-design stages and conclusion. Evaluate the assumptions behind each design, rather than treating a diagram as a universal rule. Actual communication isn't necessarily formal org chart. It concerns design/interface constraints and flexibility, not product copying competitors/customers or guaranteed performance after team changes. Boundaries/coordination influence alternatives; organization may need flexibility when design concept changes. Adding staff isn't simply additive productivity. Assigned Pais/Skelton talk isn't verified by this prose.\nMcKinsey 2018 model combines stable backbone with dynamic teams, purpose, rapid learning/decision cycles, people and enabling technology. Clear rights/accountability accompany autonomy. Consultancy framework, not universal law, guaranteed transformation, required microservices, elimination of hierarchy or unrestricted personnel/finance sharing. Its 70% probability language is not a measured permission threshold or same as Amazon wished-for-information heuristic. The MIT 2010 course page describes historical and human organizational context plus negotiation, teamwork and leadership exercises; it is not a universal definition of organizations.\nToy five people fully connected undirected pairs=5*4/2=10; ten people=45,4.5 times links for 2 times people. Potential pairs aren't actual meetings, communication hours, required always-on links or proof productivity falls. Sequential local work 2 days+required review 3 days=5 days; review reduced to 1 day with work unchanged gives 3 days,40% cycle reduction on stipulated comparable basis. No causal claim from reporting line changes alone. Cannot remove a required legal/safety/control review simply to improve speed. Check baseline, downtime, parallel work, quality and escalation load before bounded authorized changes with guardrails. No staff names/private chart export, survey, meeting, owner reassignment, migration or real organizational action.","quiz":[{"q":"Every strategy change forces reporting line reorganization?","o":["Treat slower decisions as sufficient proof a reporting reorg is the only answer.","Redraw reporting lines whenever strategy wording changes without diagnosing constraints.","Select a divisional chart first, then reinterpret the strategy to fit it.","No; test actual capability/decision/coordination fit and alternatives."],"a":3},{"q":"Formal org chart establishes all actual rights/information flows?","o":["Yes; reporting implies every approval right below it.","No; combine reporting, explicit charters and observed work/informal networks.","Yes; communication only follows command lines.","No; formal reporting never provides useful evidence."],"a":1},{"q":"Three teams contribute. Ambiguous by definition?","o":["Use meeting attendance to infer rights without checking the charter.","Assign one decision owner but omit required consultation and approval roles.","No; check decider, consultation, approvals and timing.","Centralize every disagreement at the CEO without mapping the specific decision."],"a":2},{"q":"Conway thesis concerns designs reflecting?","o":["Customer buying journey regardless of designers' communication.","Design organization communication structure, not merely formal chart.","Competitor architecture independent of team interaction.","Vendor reference model regardless of coordination."],"a":1},{"q":"Team structure change alone guarantees software performance?","o":["No; interfaces, technical constraints, implementation and evidence matter.","Change team names and reporting lines without changing actual interfaces or dependencies.","Adopt microservices because the team map was decentralized, without testing fit.","Treat more teams as an automatic reduction in coordination cost."],"a":0},{"q":"McKinsey agile model has no stable backbone/accountable owners?","o":["No; stable elements and rights coexist with adaptable teams.","Remove all stable roles so every assignment can change without documented boundaries.","Use common purpose as a substitute for specific decision rights and approval limits.","Keep all decisions at the top while describing local teams as fully autonomous."],"a":0},{"q":"MIT course page proves full lecture/learning review?","o":["Yes; free availability implies inspection.","Yes; overview verifies every simulation/outcome.","No; overview establishes topics; specific lectures need review.","No; overview cannot establish even stated focus."],"a":2},{"q":"5 people vs 10 people undirected potential pairs?","o":["25 and 100; includes self links/order.","5 and 10; linear growth.","20 and 90; double counts undirected pairs.","10 and 45;4.5 times links for 2 times people."],"a":3},{"q":"Work 2 days+review 3 days then review 1 day, work unchanged. Total change?","o":["3 to 1 day,66.67%, measuring only review.","5 to 3 days,2% reduction.","5 to 1 day,80% reduction, omitting work.","5 to 3 days,40% reduction."],"a":3},{"q":"Slow decision proves one cause and permits removing required review?","o":["No; investigate causes/authority and preserve required safeguards.","Yes; any escalation proves reporting structure alone wrong.","Yes; speed metric overrides required controls.","No; slow decisions can never be improved."],"a":0}]},{"t":"Power: where it actually comes from","kick":"Study power claims critically. Influence does not replace truth, consent or legitimate authority.","mins":"1.5 hrs","obj":["Distinguish observed influence, author claims and formal authority.","Evaluate responsible collaboration without deception or coercion.","Use synthetic network measures without treating centrality as permission or competence."],"vids":[{"id":"etVCBDRXUH8","t":"The paths to power: How to grow your influence and advance your career | Jeffrey Pfeffer","by":"Lenny's Podcast"},{"id":"AozJ4AkgAMw","t":"Jeffrey Pfeffer: Why Cultivating Power is the Secret to Success","by":"Stanford Graduate School of Business"}],"reads":[{"t":"Jeffrey Pfeffer: Power (and How to Get More of It)","u":"https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/jeffrey-pfeffer-power-how-get-more-it","n":"Stanford 2015 video chapter prompts; use them to guide your viewing and the written exercise."},{"t":"If You Want to Change the World, You Need Power","u":"https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/if-you-want-change-world-you-need-power-part-1","n":"Stanford's 2020 publisher transcript. Treat the speaker's views as arguments to assess, not proven causal rules or permission to deceive."}],"ex":"Private synthetic influence map, not a dossier on actual colleagues or permission to pressure anyone. Fictional five roles: who affects a decision, resource/relationship/expertise they bring, formal rights, uncertainty, legitimate collaboration and limits. Distinguish observed influence from assumed intent and formal authority. Performance can matter without being the only determinant of advancement; don't claim a universal ranking, that work quality is irrelevant, or all promotion contests are corrupt. Formal role, knowledge, relationships and control of needed resources are possible mechanisms to investigate, not proof the same factor dominates every organization. Network position or willingness to act never grants rights to approve spending, disclose information, impersonate a leader, retaliate or override owners.\nThe Stanford 2015 Pfeffer page offers video chapter prompts, not a full written explanation. Stanford 2020 Part 1 publisher transcript discusses ambition, confidence, persistence, energy, empathy, authenticity and accepting an unfair world. Source statements that emotions are contagious, confidence persuades, persistence wears down opposition and strategic misrepresentation/lying keeps investors/employees are attributed claims, not independently verified universal causal laws or recommended permission to deceive. Critically separate describing tactics from endorsing them. Responsible confidence can say what is known, uncertain, being checked and the next step, without fabricating a fix or hiding material risk. A worthwhile goal doesn't excuse deception, threats, bribery, discrimination, manipulation, unwanted repeated contact or harms to others. Sincere relevant recognition and appropriate help requests differ from false praise and manufactured dependency; success isn't guaranteed and refusal matters.\nBuild one legitimate collaboration proposal: shared problem, value to both sides, specific bounded ask, evidence, actual decision owner, right to decline, approved audience and next checkpoint. A bridge between disconnected groups can facilitate useful context, but need-to-know limits remain; do not hoard information or block alternatives to make yourself indispensable. Privacy-aware synthetic graph: groups A/B connected by one liaison; add an approved alternate route for continuity, not automatic open access to private data. Structural brokerage describes graph position, not consent or a deterministic promotion prediction.\nToy five hypothetical people, undirected observed ties AB, AC, BC, CD, DE. Degree C=3 of 4 possible=75%; D=2/4=50%; C degree isn't 75% approval power, authority or proof superior competence. Remove CD: ABC and DE become disconnected on stated graph. Add authorized AE: liaison loss no longer disconnects groups on that simple map. This topology is not actual resilience evidence, private relationship surveillance or a reason to disrupt ties. Record observation window/missing links/definition, don't infer absent friendship from no message record. No contacts, endorsements, organization changes, private messages, reporting decisions or real network collection.","quiz":[{"q":"Pfeffer transcript describes strategic misrepresentation. Responsible use?","o":["Label all claims uncertain even when reliable facts are available.","Treat the tactic as acceptable if an investor asks for a confident forecast.","Adopt it only in internal updates, assuming external reporting is the sole truth duty.","Identify it as source advice and critique it; preserve truth, uncertainty and legitimate safeguards."],"a":3},{"q":"Confident update with an unresolved problem?","o":["State known facts, uncertainty, check owner and next step without a fabricated fix.","Share the likely solution as confirmed because an experienced colleague proposed it.","Report only good news until engineering supplies a firm resolution date.","Send all raw logs to every stakeholder rather than a relevant scoped update."],"a":0},{"q":"High network centrality establishes formal approval rights?","o":["Yes; withholding access creates legitimate authority.","Yes; if enough colleagues rely on the liaison.","No; verify explicit rights and required approval independently.","No; formal authority can never coexist with relationships."],"a":2},{"q":"A help request is declined. Good next step?","o":["Repeat until the person gives in because persistence always works.","Respect the refusal, consider appropriate alternatives and agreed escalation if needed.","Use false praise and concealed pressure to overcome resistance.","Treat refusal as proof the person opposes the mission."],"a":1},{"q":"Performance and promotion: sound conclusion?","o":["Conclude one promotion dispute establishes organization-wide corruption.","Rank candidates by output alone, treating contextual factors as noise.","Rank candidates by relationship count alone as a proxy for organizational value.","Output matters, but investigate other context; neither automatic merit rewards nor universal corruption is proven."],"a":3},{"q":"C neighbors A, B, D in a five-person undirected toy graph. Normalized degree?","o":["3/4=75%, not approval probability or authority.","3/5=60%, using self as possible neighbor.","4/5=80%, adding self to observed ties.","2/4=50%, omitting one stated neighbor."],"a":0},{"q":"D neighbors C, E, same five-person graph. Degree share?","o":["3/4=75%, counting C ties as D ties.","2/5=40%, including self in possible total.","2/4=50%.","1/4=25%, omitting E."],"a":2},{"q":"Ties AB, AC, BC, CD, DE. Remove CD only. Result?","o":["No change because a node with high degree cannot be disconnected.","All five still connected through an unstated AE tie.","Only isolated C with ABDE connected.","Components ABC and DE; this simple graph loses its only cross-group link."],"a":3},{"q":"Add AE with proper authority, then CD disappears. Connectivity?","o":["A becomes isolated because it has too many ties.","DE becomes disconnected despite AE.","All five remain connected on stated graph; not proof real-world data access or resilience.","Everyone has identical centrality and rights."],"a":2},{"q":"Useful collaboration proposal includes?","o":["Shared problem, bounded ask, evidence, rights, audience and genuine ability to decline.","Only personal advancement and a demand for loyalty.","A private resource promise outside your authority.","A plan to block alternate routes to become indispensable."],"a":0}]},{"t":"Corporate politics: reading the real org chart","kick":"Informal influence matters, but hidden motives need evidence. Keep interpretive models separate from facts and diagnoses.","mins":"1.5 hrs","obj":["Separate informal influence from actual decision rights.","Read the Gervais lens as interpretation, not clinical or empirical employee classification.","Compare competing hypotheses and legitimate action boundaries."],"vids":[{"id":"NU3sCpurdrI","t":"Acting With Power and Using Your Influence with Deborah Gruenfeld","by":"Pfeffer on Power"}],"reads":[{"t":"The Gervais Principle: book landing page","u":"https://books.venkateshrao.com/gervais-principle/","n":"Book description only, not complete six-part series."},{"t":"The Gervais Principle IV: Wonderful Human Beings","u":"https://ribbonfarm.com/2010/10/14/the-gervais-principle-iv-wonderful-human-beings/","n":"Part IV of an interpretive series using fiction to discuss organizations. The categories are a lens, not a validated diagnosis of people at work."},{"t":"Notes on the Gervais Principle","u":"https://www.nateliason.com/notes/gervais-principle-venkatesh-rao","n":"Secondary notes on the Gervais Principle. Treat the categories as an interpretive lens, not a validated personality taxonomy or evidence about a real colleague."},{"t":"Power, relationships and responsibility: Gruenfeld interview","u":"https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/power-what-it-means-have-it-how-reclaim-it","n":"Stanford 2020 edited interview, not full book or assigned episode."}],"ex":"Synthetic case analysis, not a personality diagnosis or hidden-motive finding about real coworkers. Pick a fictional/public decision and separate official reason, observed behavior, plausible incentive/coalition explanation, competing operational/legal/resource explanations, supporting and disconfirming evidence, source limits and what would change your view. An informal network may shape decisions, but formal meetings can genuinely discover, deliberate, refuse or decide. Not every outcome is predetermined by a shadow organization. Official language can inform, coordinate, comply and legitimize; don't assert its usual or only function is cover for a power bargain.\nRao's Gervais Principle is an interpretive series using The Office and provocative labels Sociopaths, Clueless, Losers. These are author categories, not clinical diagnoses, validated employee types, measured population shares or moral rankings. Author landing page returns a description, not complete series text. Selected Part IV prose examines group belonging/status illegibility and fictional scenes; it tells readers earlier parts provide context. Nat Eliason notes are secondary highlights, not full author work or independent empirical replication. Don't call a coworker a sociopath, dismiss sincere belief as incompetence, copy gender/intelligence stereotypes, infer villainy from disagreement or apply fictional rules as established law. Study what an interpretation predicts and why alternative accounts fit, not just remember derogatory vocabulary.\nA budget choice benefiting one function can reflect resource need, legitimate expertise, bargaining, conflict of interest or several factors. Benefit alone doesn't prove motive, secret agreement or wrongdoing. Ask what evidence would distinguish hypotheses without unauthorized private-message access or gossip. Reorg can fail if actual incentives/rights/information/resources don't change, but informal power isn't always the cause and reorg isn't always failure. Stakeholder map names roles/constraints/decision rights, not targets to manipulate. Consider legitimate purpose, harm, required controls and authority before personal stake/winnability; never neglect safety/whistleblowing duties simply because an issue is hard to win. Use proper escalation or qualified advice for credible concerns; don't publish allegations as facts or retaliate.\nSupplemental Stanford Gruenfeld 2020 interview describes power as relational/contextual and responsibility toward others, not merely dominance/posture/status. Acting responsibly can mean speaking firmly or stepping back/listening; confidence alone does not create expertise or rights. Edited interview isn't full book, the assigned Pfeffer-on-Power episode or current political-role fact source. No need to copy historical political examples or promise one body language trick wins a meeting.\nToy hypothesis exercise stipulates prior H probability 20%, P(evidence|H)=60%, P(evidence|not H)=30%. Joint weights.2*.6=.12 and.8*.3=.24; posterior.12/(.12+.24)=1/3≈33.33%, not 60% or proof guilt. Invented assumptions for arithmetic only, not measured workplace probabilities, a real accusation or legal evidential threshold. New evidence quality/selection/dependence matters; don't label a motive true by a synthetic score. Document unknowns and preserve actual rightful decision process. No spying, actual interviews, allegations, clinical labels, messages, complaints or organizational actions.","quiz":[{"q":"Informal network influences a decision. Formal meeting is necessarily a rubber stamp?","o":["Treat informal agreement as final approval before the authorized forum.","Treat the meeting as ratification unless someone explicitly objects.","No; influence and genuine collective deliberation/decision can coexist.","Ignore prior coalition work because only the formal meeting can matter."],"a":2},{"q":"Gervais terms should be applied to coworkers as clinical categories?","o":["Yes; job level establishes psychological diagnosis.","Yes; one disagreement identifies the Sociopath type.","No; they are provocative interpretive author categories, not diagnoses or validated employee types.","No; fictional interpretation cannot raise any useful question."],"a":2},{"q":"Secondary highlights establish full six-part author-series review?","o":["Yes; author landing page title implies all chapters were read.","Yes; notes verify every part and example.","No; label source coverage and seek original context for claims that depend on it.","No; highlights cannot establish their own quoted topics."],"a":2},{"q":"Function gains budget. That alone proves hidden self-serving motive?","o":["No; compare alternative explanations and discriminating evidence.","Treat the beneficiary as the motive owner absent another identified cause.","Accept the official reason without checking supporting evidence.","Infer a secret agreement from budget gain and one social connection."],"a":0},{"q":"Choosing issue priorities means only personal stake and win probability?","o":["No; all disagreements should be escalated publicly at once.","Yes; safety issues can be ignored if unwinnable.","Yes; personal advantage overrides disclosure/conflict requirements.","No; legitimate purpose, downside, duties and authority also matter."],"a":3},{"q":"A reorg did not improve behavior. Conclusion?","o":["Attribute it to informal influence before inspecting implementation.","Investigate incentives, rights, information, resources and execution; no single cause proven.","Repeat the chart change with more detailed job titles as the first fix.","Compare headcount totals alone, assuming process and rights were unchanged."],"a":1},{"q":"Gruenfeld supplemental interview: power travels identically across contexts?","o":["Yes; CEO authority automatically governs every outside relationship.","No; relational context and responsibilities shape it.","Yes; feeling powerful alone grants a mandate.","No; responsible action never affects influence."],"a":1},{"q":"Responsible use of presence means always dominate?","o":["No; an actor mindset requires dishonesty.","Yes; take all airtime to establish power.","Yes; posture substitutes for source evidence and permission.","No; role may call for clear voice or listening/stepping back, without fake expertise."],"a":3},{"q":"Toy prior H.2, likelihood E|H.6/E|not H.3. PosteriorH|E?","o":["1/3≈33.33%, under stated invented assumptions.","60%, confusing likelihood with posterior.","20%, ignoring evidence.","12%, returning only joint H-and-E weight."],"a":0},{"q":"Toy posterior 33.33% proves colleague motive?","o":["No; invented inputs and a model are not evidence of actual intent or wrongdoing.","Yes; any score over prior proves motive.","Yes; arithmetic accuracy makes assumptions real observations.","No; new evidence should never change a hypothesis."],"a":0}]},{"t":"The manager's job: output, leverage and the operating rhythm","kick":"Managerial leverage depends on actual effects, quality and costs, not a calendar label or meeting count.","mins":"2 hrs","obj":["Use output/leverage as context-dependent responsibility lenses without double counting.","Test training and allocation scenarios on a consistent cost/time basis.","Distinguish manager/team research and choose cadence by actual purpose."],"vids":[{"id":"xnjByFtiN7Q","t":"Introduction to High Output Management, Andy Grove, and the Basics of Production","by":"Abi Tyas Tunggal"}],"reads":[{"t":"Andy Grove on Management (Harrison Metal library)","u":"https://www.harrisonmetal.com/library/andy-grove-on-management/video","n":"Summary or description; use the written exercise for the worked case."},{"t":"Top takeaways from High Output Management","u":"https://medium.com/@iantien/top-takeaways-from-andy-grove-s-high-output-management-2e0ecfb1ea63","n":"Ian Tien's 2016 chapter takeaways are a secondary summary, not Andy Grove's original book. Compare the ideas with the explicit fictional management case."},{"t":"Google re:Work: the research behind great managers","u":"https://rework.withgoogle.com/intl/en/guides/following-the-data-the-research-behind-great-managers","n":"Google research overview; distinguish company-reported findings from the underlying studies and from a causal guarantee."}],"ex":"Synthetic management audit, no calendar changes or employee assessment performed. Map a hypothetical week in 30 minute blocks with actual/unknown work, primary purpose, output, affected people, owner/permission and quality/downside. Labels high/low/negative leverage are hypotheses to test, not fixed judgments from meeting type or audience size. Support/recovery/necessary controls can be valuable; doubling high-leverage share is not a universal target and cannot create hours or excuse neglect. Understand constraints, tradeoffs and employee workload before changing cadence or delegating.\nGrove framework in Ian Tien 2016 secondary chapter notes views manager output through organization and neighboring organizations influenced, not only personal deliverables. It is a responsibility lens, not an additive accounting rule giving full credit for all shared output to every manager. Define team boundaries/units/overlap and actual contribution/uncertainty. Leverage can be negative through interruption, delayed decisions or meddling; touching more people doesn't mean helping. Useful training, information gathering, timely decisions and supervised delegation can help under context, not guaranteed return. Production-process analogy highlights inputs/quality/variability/bottlenecks, not people as interchangeable machines or metrics alone proving causality.\nThe Harrison Metal page offers a recap; the full recording and Grove's book are separate resources. Tien notes are not the original book, guaranteed authority, current price or proof every managerial claim is universal. Don't copy blanket manager-fault for every resignation, only-training-or-motivation explanations for all performance, or dual reporting/functional-team superiority as always true. Task-relevant capability, tools, incentives, constraints, accessibility and context may matter. Choose supervision to actual task/sensitivity/rights, not general seniority. Coaching isn't secretly taking over, and delegation doesn't grant spending/representation permission. Clarify review/exception/closure.\nGrove-style 1:1 can focus on employee issues, information, learning and support; not exclusively one speaker or only decisions. Staff/operating/mission-oriented forums have different jobs. A useful information/learning review may produce no decision; diagnose purpose before shrinking/canceling or escalating everything. Informal observation may add context but isn't always more true or never present in reports. No covert monitoring, intimidation or secret recordings. Handle personnel/feedback confidentiality and appropriate audience.\nGoogle re:Work August 2025 body article distinguishes Oxygen manager behavior/retention/results associations from Aristotle team dynamics.10,000 data points not 10,000 unique managers. Ten behaviors are Google-derived, not all environments or randomized proof one training causes specific revenue/retention gain. Its 3 responsibilities deliver results/develop people/build community are a later framework, not original 8 behavior list. Keep correlation/measurement/selection/context limits and don't swap studies. AI prompts don't authorize uploading confidential company/personnel information; use synthetic sanitized examples and actual organizational policy.\nToy training: 12 manager prep hours+10 trainees * 2 hours=32 person-hours input. Stipulated 1% equivalent productive gain over each of 10 people's 2000 annual hours=200 equivalent hours gross, minus 32=168 modeled net on same annual basis. Not observed output, actual cash saving or guaranteed causal lift; output quality, adoption, decay, rework and other costs must be tested. Counting only manager 12 hours would overstate organizational net to 188. Toy 40 hour week with 8 high-leverage hours=20%; 16/40=40% would require shifting 8 existing hours, not adding 8 while keeping denominator 40. Alternative total 48 with 16=>33.33%, not 40%. A request to improve share doesn't approve canceling controls or increasing work hours. No hiring, assessment, training rollout, meetings, messages, private uploads or commitments.","quiz":[{"q":"Manager output lens permits every manager to claim all shared output additively?","o":["Exclude neighboring teams even when a traced contribution affects their work.","Credit all influenced-team output then add direct-team output without checking overlap.","Use meeting count as the manager-output denominator for all work.","No; define boundaries, units, overlap and contribution; avoid double counting."],"a":3},{"q":"Training always highest measured return regardless of context?","o":["No; test bottleneck, adoption, quality, costs and actual effects.","Choose training first because a large audience implies higher return.","Count the gross modeled benefit without trainee time or adoption costs.","Compare training cost against a different time horizon without adjustment."],"a":0},{"q":"Ten trainees * 2000 hours*1% stipulated annual gain. Gross equivalent hours?","o":["2000, using 10% instead of 1%.","20, omitting trainees.","200, not observed cash or automatically realized gain.","32, reporting training input."],"a":2},{"q":"Manager prep 12 hours+10 trainees * 2 hours. Total input?","o":["24, doubling only manager hours.","12, ignoring trainee time.","20, ignoring manager prep.","32 person-hours."],"a":3},{"q":"Gross 200 equivalent hours minus input 32, same annual basis. Modeled net?","o":["180, subtracting only trainee 20.","188, subtracting only manager 12.","168, subject to stipulated effects and omitted costs.","232, adding costs as benefit."],"a":2},{"q":"40 hour week/8 high-leverage hours. Share?","o":["8%, using hours as percent.","20%.","40%, using total hours as numerator.","80%, returning complement."],"a":1},{"q":"Add 8 hours, now 16 high out of 48 total. Share?","o":["40%, retaining old 40 denominator.","33.33%, not 40%; denominator changed.","50%, using an unstated32hour denominator.","20%, ignoring changed numerator."],"a":1},{"q":"Operating review yields no decision. Must cancel?","o":["Cancel it based on decision count without checking its information purpose.","No; check purpose, information/learning value, owners and unresolved decision needs first.","Keep it because attendance is high, without testing usefulness.","Combine it with the decision forum without checking distinct audiences and needs."],"a":1},{"q":"Oxygen findings prove one manager course causes universal retention gains?","o":["Treat Google associations as a forecast of the same gain in another company.","No; Google-derived observations and frameworks need context; do not turn association into causal guarantee.","Count datapoints as unique managers without checking the sampling unit.","Use Aristotle team results as direct verification of the Oxygen manager program."],"a":1},{"q":"Use manager AI prompts with confidential employee records?","o":["Not without actual data/usage authority and protections; use synthetic examples here.","Yes; source prompts authorize all sensitive uploads.","Yes; remove first names and assume every detail anonymous.","No; private generic planning can never use any tool."],"a":0}]},{"t":"One-on-ones, feedback and delegation that actually work","kick":"Coach with care, clear observations and task-specific support, not rigid scripts.","mins":"1.5 hrs","obj":["Use GROW as an adaptive two-way coaching aid, with accurate source labels.","Distinguish caring direct feedback from fixed personality labels and guaranteed outcomes.","Specify delegated outcomes, rights, constraints and review rather than assume unlimited autonomy."],"vids":[{"id":"O9hDTLo5rLA","t":"How to lead with radical candor | Kim Scott | TEDxPortland","by":"TEDx Talks"}],"reads":[{"t":"Google re:Work: Coach using the GROW model","u":"https://rework.withgoogle.com/intl/en/guides/coach-using-the-grow-model","n":"Use this reference to study Google re:Work: Coach using the GROW model."},{"t":"Google re:Work Guides index","u":"https://rework.withgoogle.com/intl/en/guides/","n":"Use this reference to study Google re:Work Guides index."},{"t":"Radical Candor in 6 minutes","u":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YLBDkz0TwLM","n":"Use this reference to study Radical Candor in 6 minutes."},{"t":"Radical Candor approach","u":"https://www.radicalcandor.com/our-approach","n":"Primary written framework; behaviors, not personality types."},{"t":"Public praise/private criticism exceptions","u":"https://www.radicalcandor.com/blog/public-praise-private-criticism","n":"Kim Scott 2022 primary advice, rule of thumb not law."},{"t":"Radical Candor explained","u":"https://www.radicalcandor.com/blog/what-is-radical-candor","n":"Primary 2022 written framework, not full assigned TEDxvideo."}],"ex":"Private synthetic practice, not instruction to hold a real employee conversation. Choose fictional report Maya, new to quarterly forecasting but experienced in sales. Role capability is task-specific. Scenario: she wants help understanding forecast uncertainty; manager also needs one policy update. Draft a 30 minute 1:1 agenda with employee priorities, coaching, manager-critical item and agreed next steps. Employee-led does not mean manager unprepared or barred from necessary safety/performance discussion. Regular cadence and active listening can help, but no source establishes weekly as universally optimal or status updates as the measured most common failure. Some status/context may be useful; avoid substituting a manager monologue for coaching. Adapt accessibility, privacy, workload, employee needs and actual organizational requirements. No forced personal disclosures or promises of absolute confidentiality the role cannot keep.\nGoogle GROW March 2026 written guide says Goal, Reality, Options, Will in its diagram description. Way-forward is a common label variant, but do not present it as the current page's exact W. It is one framework, not a mandatory rigid script or psychological treatment. Employee wants coaching and chooses issues; coach examines own assumptions, facilitates two-way discussion, knows when to stop advising and lets employee choose next steps. Teaching expertise and facilitating questions are both on the continuum; do not force questions when concrete instruction or an urgent protective action is needed. Worked sequence: Goal=produce forecast interval and explain assumptions; Reality=two recent large deals slipped, current worksheet assumes every verbal promise closes; Options=base/upside/downside, evidence threshold, colleague review; Will=employee-selected draft by stated checkpoint with named support. Clarify whose agreement is still missing rather than inventing consent.\nRadical Candor primary pages pair care personally with challenge directly. Ruinous Empathy means withholding/softening useful feedback, Obnoxious Aggression challenging without care, Manipulative Insincerity neither. These describe behaviors, not personality labels or permanent boxes for people. Caring does not guarantee improvement; directness is not permission to humiliate or assume your interpretation is truth. Feedback example: At the forecast review, the worksheet marked three verbal promises as signed, so the signed-commitment count was overstated on those inputs. What context am I missing? Could you separate signed from tentative and propose a review rule? Verify actual facts and quantify only supported impact. Humble observation, inquiry, response and follow-through matter. Don't convert one mistake into a character judgment.\nPublic praise/private criticism is a source rule of thumb, not law: recipient preference can make public praise unwelcome; self-reported group learning and leaders inviting criticism of themselves are source exceptions. Preserve privacy and do not coerce public self-criticism. Praise must be specific and sincere, not padding a difficult message or claiming an unmeasured 85% gain from a source illustration. A compliment sandwich is not categorically illegal or proven always ineffective; keep praise sincere and the concern clear without hiding it. Ask for feedback and listen without retaliation. Quotes/framework anecdotes are not representative prevalence studies.\nDelegation draft: outcome, context, quality evidence, real rights, constraints/resources, review point, exception triggers and actual acceptance. Maya's general seniority does not establish forecast readiness; inspect task understanding and sensitivity. Autonomy over method can coexist with bounded approval gates; high skill/willingness does not confer spend/disclosure rights or justify no follow-up. For a familiar low-risk task, outcome-level checks may fit; a new sensitive task may require nearer checkpoints or teaching. Skill/will is a heuristic, not fixed diagnosis or a validated universal supervision matrix.\nToy budget 30 minutes: 8 employee priorities+10 coaching+5 required update+7 closure=30. Adding a 6 minute urgent issue requires reprioritizing or separately agreeing time, not claiming same 30 with 36 scheduled. Reusable synthetic agenda and delegation brief only; no meetings, contacts, employee ratings, policy changes or private-data collection.","quiz":[{"q":"Google GROW current page labels W as?","o":["Wisdom, a requirement for the manager to provide every answer.","Workload, the number of tasks the manager assigns.","Warning, the formal consequence after feedback.","Will, committing to next action; Way-forward is a variant, not the exact page label."],"a":3},{"q":"Maya is experienced in sales but new to forecasting. Oversight?","o":["Use daily approval for every method choice regardless of task risk.","Delegate without review because general seniority proves readiness.","Assess forecast-specific capability, risk and rights, then agree support and checkpoints.","Transfer budget authority because she accepts the outcome."],"a":2},{"q":"Employee-led 1:1 means?","o":["Manager sends all status items and uses the meeting to read them aloud.","Manager contributes only if employee requests every topic.","Employee must disclose personal issues before work concerns.","Employee priorities and active listening matter; manager still prepares and raises necessary items."],"a":3},{"q":"GROW coaching when employee needs technical instruction?","o":["End coaching permanently because a knowledge gap exists.","Keep asking open questions despite lacking required safety instruction.","Treat the employee as unwilling because they request an example.","Adapt between teaching and facilitating rather than forcing only questions."],"a":3},{"q":"Radical Candor quadrants classify?","o":["Behaviors in context, not fixed personality types.","Permanent employee identities after one exchange.","Four clinical diagnoses that determine reporting lines.","A scoring system that automatically selects promotion candidates."],"a":0},{"q":"Feedback about three tentative deals entered as signed?","o":["State she intended to deceive because the cash estimate was high.","Tell Maya she is careless and cannot be trusted with numbers.","Praise unrelated work and omit the forecast concern.","Describe specific worksheet behavior and supported impact, invite context, agree correction."],"a":3},{"q":"Employee dislikes public recognition. Praise?","o":["Use specific sincere recognition suited to their preference and appropriate audience.","Praise publicly because the rule has no exceptions.","Withhold all praise because only public praise matters.","Require public thanks as proof they accept coaching."],"a":0},{"q":"Delegating an outcome also grants any needed spending?","o":["Yes;if the delegate is high-skill and accepts the delivery goal.","No; confirm actual approval limits and necessary decision rights separately.","Yes;if the manager approved the project in principle.","No;every low-risk method choice must return to the manager."],"a":1},{"q":"8+10+5+7 minutes in a 30 minute 1:1. Unallocated time?","o":["0 minutes; all 30 are allocated.","7 minutes, ignoring closure.","5 minutes, ignoring required update.","10 minutes, ignoring coaching."],"a":0},{"q":"Add urgent 6 minutes while preserving all original 30. Total?","o":["24 minutes by subtracting rather than adding.","30 minutes because urgent items do not count.","36 minutes; reprioritize or agree a different slot/duration.","32 minutes by counting only part of the new issue."],"a":2}]},{"t":"Hard conversations: performance, conflict and accountability","kick":"Prepare evidence, listen to context and make follow-through accurate and fair.","mins":"1.5 hrs","obj":["Separate observations, impact, interpretations and intent in a hard conversation.","Investigate individual and system hypotheses without premature blame.","Record disputed facts and actual agreements accurately with appropriate privacy and process."],"vids":[{"id":"84kPR_NDjI8","t":"What Makes a GREAT Manager? (it's not what you think)","by":"Jeff Su"}],"reads":[{"t":"Developing great managers at Google (re:Work guide)","u":"https://rework.withgoogle.com/intl/en/guides/developing-great-managers-at-google","n":"Use this reference to study Developing great managers at Google (re:Work guide)."},{"t":"Julie Zhuo: how to get better at giving and receiving feedback (video)","u":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=W1yzMRBGGKU","n":"Use this reference to study Julie Zhuo: how to get better at giving and receiving feedback (video)."},{"t":"CCL SBI feedback model","u":"https://www.ccl.org/articles/leading-effectively-articles/sbi-feedback-model-a-quick-win-to-improve-talent-conversations-development/","n":"CCL 2025 written advice; underlying causal evidence not independently checked."},{"t":"CCL difficult conversations","u":"https://www.ccl.org/articles/leading-effectively-articles/5-steps-for-tackling-tough-conversations/","n":"CCL 2025 advice drawing on Difficult Conversations; not full book or legal protocol."}],"ex":"Private synthetic rehearsal, not an actual complaint, performance assessment or disciplinary action. Fictional case: three client handoffs lacked required acceptance fields; recipient says the upstream template omitted those fields and priorities changed. Prepare facts/source/uncertainties, impact, perspective questions, own contribution, purpose, proposed next step and support. Verify agreed expectations and comparable opportunities before concluding a performance gap. Observable dates/numbers/behaviors can be checked; interpretation, hearsay and motive remain labeled, not rewritten as fact. If records conflict, investigate before making a definitive allegation. Do not invent three facts merely because an exercise asks for three.\nCCL SBI framework: Situation, specific observable Behavior, Impact. Add inquiry about intent/context for a two-way conversation; lack of harmful intent does not erase supported impact, and impact does not prove intent. Example: At last fictional handoff, required acceptance field was empty; downstream reviewer could not confirm readiness without a clarification round. What happened from your side? Check whether the requirement actually existed, tool state and reviewer reason. Not: You never care about quality. A genuine shared purpose or neutral third-story description can open discussion, but no universal safest opener or guaranteed cooperation. CCL difficult-conversation article distinguishes what happened, feelings, identity; checking own contribution and exploring both stories precede joint options. Learning stance does not mean treating a substantiated harmful act as harmless, refusing required escalation or compelling emotional disclosure.\nFor disagreement, paraphrase each account, locate common facts and differing interpretations, ask what evidence could settle it, then agree a bounded next step if authorized. Don't demand instant admission or use a script to silence response. Repeated conflict with different people raises a system hypothesis (roles, incentives, workflow, resources), not proof systems are sole cause or individuals innocent/guilty. Check selection and exposure, history, expectations, workload and evidence. Managers may contribute to unclear direction. Bound conclusions and keep concerns private to appropriate participants; no public personality labels or retaliation. Safety, harassment, discrimination or formal employment decisions may need appropriate qualified channels and actual applicable rules, not a generic coaching template alone. No legal advice or universally lawful discipline process is established here.\nDraft follow-through record: confirmed facts, disputed items, the employee perspective, proposed/agreed actions, owners, resources, observable success criteria, review date and what approvals/process would be needed if issues continue. Distinguish proposal from actual agreement and an acknowledgement of receipt from consent or admission. No manager can unilaterally write mutual agreement and call it signed. Documentation can support shared memory, fairness, organizational records and formal requirements; do not falsely say it never has legal/HR uses or is merely a legal case against someone. Minimize personnel details and honor policy/access limits. Don't promise automatic salary freezes, termination or other consequences without rights, process, context and applicable advice. Accountability includes manager support and follow-up, not only employee obligations.\nGoogle developing-managers October 2024 guide names Deliver Results, Develop People, Build Community and describes Google-specific annual goals, quarterly manager feedback, annual team feedback and year-end review. Useful example, not universal statutory cadence or causal proof for another employer. It recommends relevant feedback/coaching but does not independently verify every Julie Zhuo claim. The written conflict exercise below stands on its own; use the recording as a separate discussion source. Don't score a paraphrase as Zhuo advice without the supporting passage. Use the written conversation case as the basis of the exercise, with the recording as a separate discussion source. CCL does state a 4: 1 positive/challenging interaction ratio in advice prose; underlying evidence not checked and no fixed-ratio guarantee or obligation to manufacture four compliments is taught.\nToy comparable-opportunity check: period A 8 complete of 10 eligible handoffs=80%; period B 12 complete of 15 eligible=80%. Four more completions is 50% count growth, not improved completion rate; incomplete 2->3 also 50% count growth with unchanged 20% rate. Cancelled or ineligible handoffs need consistent disclosed rules, not selective removal. Counts/rates do not establish cause, quality equivalence or who is at fault. Write a synthetic conversation and review plan only; no real meeting, warning, employee record, discipline, HR disclosure or contact.","quiz":[{"q":"Which statement is an observable behavior rather than intent inference?","o":["The blank field proves the employee wanted a reviewer to fail.","The acceptance field was blank in the recorded handoff.","The late correction proves general disregard for quality.","A peer reported missing fields,so the event is confirmed without checking the record."],"a":1},{"q":"SBI feedback becomes a two-way conversation by?","o":["Asking about context/intent and listening, while checking supported impact.","Requiring immediate agreement before showing evidence.","Treating your interpretation as definitive because you are manager.","Replacing observations with peer personality ratings."],"a":0},{"q":"Employee says the template lacked the required field. Next?","o":["Record an admission because a response confirms the event happened.","Check template/version/expectations and both accounts before concluding a performance failure.","Dismiss the explanation as avoidance without inspecting it.","Declare all individual responsibility removed without verification."],"a":1},{"q":"Shared purpose is universally the safest opener?","o":["No; use a suitable truthful opener and learning stance; context and risk still matter.","Yes; the phrase guarantees the person will cooperate.","Yes; shared purpose replaces facts and listening.","No; all conversations must start with a formal written warning."],"a":0},{"q":"Same conflict appears with different employees. Conclusion?","o":["Investigate system and individual/context factors; recurrence alone proves no single cause.","System design is proven the only cause.","Hiring failure is proven because several people struggled.","Escalation alone establishes the manager contribution was zero."],"a":0},{"q":"Manager notes say we agree but employee explicitly disputed the account. Record?","o":["Treat receipt of the note as admission of every detail.","Keep we agree because the manager wrote the minutes.","Separate confirmed/disputed facts and proposed/agreed actions accurately.","Delete the dispute to keep the record concise."],"a":2},{"q":"Documentation after a performance conversation can be for?","o":["Only protecting the manager; employee perspective is irrelevant.","Only building a legal case against the employee.","Shared memory, fairness, tracking and applicable organizational/formal needs, with privacy controls.","Only informal memory; it can never have formal HR uses."],"a":2},{"q":"Google quarterly manager-feedback cadence is?","o":["A statutory minimum for every employer.","A company-specific example, not universal legal or causal requirement.","Proof that annual employee feedback alone causes retention gains.","A mandate to skip ongoing conversations between reviews."],"a":1},{"q":"A 8/10 complete, B 12/15 complete. Rate change?","o":["40 percentage points, because completion count rose 4.","50 percentage points, because count rose 8 to 12.","Both 80%; 0 percentage-point change despite more completed handoffs.","20 percentage points, because denominator rose 5."],"a":2},{"q":"Completed count 8 to 12. Relative count growth?","o":["33.33%, using final 12 rather than baseline 8.","4%, using absolute increase as percent.","50%, not proof the completion rate improved.","80%, returning the completion rate instead."],"a":2}]},{"t":"Influence without authority","kick":"Persuade with real evidence and genuine common interests, without manufacturing authority or consensus.","mins":"1.5 hrs","obj":["Distinguish historical six from expanded seven influence principles.","Use truthful, voluntary influence without bypassing decision rights.","Treat capacity metaphors and interest metrics as bounded models, not proof of authority or outcomes."],"vids":[{"id":"w19IBxDu2Y4","t":"How to Operate with Keith Rabois (How to Start a Startup 2014: Lecture 14)","by":"Y Combinator"}],"reads":[{"t":"Tapping our powers of persuasion (APA Monitor)","u":"https://www.apa.org/monitor/2011/02/persuasion","n":"Use this reference to study Tapping our powers of persuasion (APA Monitor)."},{"t":"Barrels and ammunition (Rabois notes)","u":"https://thinkingpark.com/dmq/q/rabois-barrels-ammunition","n":"Use this reference to study Barrels and ammunition (Rabois notes)."},{"t":"Cialdini expanded seven principles","u":"https://cialdini.com/principles","n":"Current primary framework, not underlying experimental replication."},{"t":"Cialdini and Eberhardt interview","u":"https://www.psychologicalscience.org/observer/universal-principles-of-influence","n":"APS 2022 edited conversation; seven principles and genuine/noncounterfeit ethical influence."},{"t":"Lecture 14 publisher reading index","u":"https://startupclass.samaltman.com/courses/lec14/","n":"Use this reference to study Lecture 14 publisher reading index."}],"ex":"Synthetic cross-functional proposal, not approval to contact stakeholders. Fictional task: launch a customer-support handoff checklist, subject to actual owner/customer/data/operations approvals. Map decision owner, required reviewers, affected roles, benefits, costs, constraints, evidence, uncertainties and one bounded ask for each. A person benefiting does not automatically support the plan; resources, other priorities and risks matter. Pre-meeting conversations can discover disagreement and improve the proposal, not secretly pre-decide a formal forum or exclude rightful dissenters. Meeting may genuinely debate, refuse or decide. Record conditional interest vs actual commitment and whose permission remains missing. Don't announce consensus from friendly replies or private calendars.\nHistorical APA Monitor February 2011 archived interview describes six influence principles: reciprocity, commitment/consistency, social proof, authority, liking, scarcity. Current Cialdini primary page and APS March/April 2022 edited conversation describe seven, adding Unity(shared identity)distinct from merely liking/similarity. Teach historical six vs expanded seven; not a timeless exhaustive six or an eighth Contrast principle. Primary site calls contrast an amplifier, not eighth principle. This is an author framework and explanatory lens, not guaranteed compliance, proof every study replicates or all 7 always apply. APA says no one strategy optimal in all circumstances. Underlying individual experiments and current effect estimates aren't independently verified here.\nEthical adaptation: reciprocity=genuine useful help, not undisclosed gift obligations/bribes; consistency=voluntary relevant commitments that people can revise as evidence changes, not trapped public promises; social proof=true comparable peer behavior with source/window/denominator, not invented testimonials or majority-as-logical-proof; authority=real relevant expertise with conflicts/limits, not costume, title laundering or formal approval substitution; liking=honest rapport, not fabricated similarities; scarcity=real disclosed constraint, not fake deadlines; unity=genuine broadly framed shared identity, not impersonation, exclusion or pressure to prove loyalty. An explanation of why influence works is not permission to manipulate. Consent, rights, policies, fairness and ability to decline remain important. Cialdini APS says point to principles actually inherent in the situation rather than fabricate/counterfeit them. Long-term relationship risk from dishonesty is a source argument, not proof one manipulative act permanently destroys all trust in every case.\nThinkingpark notes attribute barrels/ammunition to Rabois 2014: people able to carry initiatives from conception to ship and bring others along; roles/culture matter. This metaphor is not a clinical employee type, a moral hierarchy or proof a fixed number of leaders mathematically caps all work. Specialists and execution roles matter. Do not call actual coworkers ammunition or grant leadership purely from tenure/loudness. The publisher page is a reading index rather than the lecture transcript. Use management-as-editing as an explicitly proposed analogy: clarify outcome, remove needless complexity, ask for evidence, resource legitimate work and leave bounded ownership with the team. It does not mean rewrite every draft yourself, cut headcount, hide disagreement or remove required review. Good prioritization checks bottlenecks/dependencies, not just a label on a person.\nWorked ask: I propose a two-week synthetic pilot on anonymized fictional handoffs to test missing-field reduction; operations reviews workload, privacy owner checks data minimization, support lead owns quality. Please review whether this fits your constraints; no launch or cost until the actual owners approve. Proposed measures: eligible handoffs, adoption, completion, quality incidents, review time and stop/review threshold. Not a message sent or approved actual plan. Authentic uncertainty is compatible with persuasive clarity.\nToy survey: not scientific population estimate.45 of 60 respondents express interest=75% of respondents, not 75% of all employees or formal approval. If 30 of 60 actually adopt later, adoption 50% of same respondent cohort, 15 fewer than stated interest or 25 percentage points lower, not evidence persuasion caused the difference. Missing answers and selection matter. Toy capacity: five initiative owners*one simultaneous initiative each=5 only under stipulated exclusive-capacity assumptions; two half-time owners do not automatically add two full slots or remove shared engineering bottleneck. No headcount decisions, coalition messages, gifts, signups, commitments, permission changes or real survey.","quiz":[{"q":"APA 2011 lists six; Cialdini current/APS 2022 list seven. Interpretation?","o":["The historical interview must have claimed Unity and was misread.","Historical six versus expanded seven including Unity; do not erase date/context.","Current framework has six because Unity is identical to liking.","Both establish contrast as an eighth universal principle."],"a":1},{"q":"Scarcity in a cross-functional proposal can ethically mean?","o":["State a genuine resource/date constraint and relevant consequences without inventing urgency.","Create an artificial deadline and present it as external policy.","Claim other teams approved to make delay seem costly.","Hide available capacity so the audience fears missing out."],"a":0},{"q":"Social proof from comparable peers establishes?","o":["Anonymous large numbers are always stronger than credible comparable evidence.","Majority behavior proves the decision logically correct.","Peer interest automatically supplies every formal approval.","Information about those peers under stated source/limits, not proof of correctness or actual approval."],"a":3},{"q":"A voluntary small commitment becomes inappropriate when?","o":["It gives the recipient time to inspect relevant evidence.","It names a clear bounded next step and actual owner.","It records uncertainty and allows a person to decline.","Used to block revising a decision after new evidence or to imply unapproved larger consent."],"a":3},{"q":"Pre-meeting stakeholder conversations should?","o":["Guarantee the meeting only ratifies a hidden decision.","Surface constraints/disagreement and inform a proposal without bypassing rightful decision forums.","Replace formal approval with a collection of friendly replies.","Exclude critical reviewers until supporters form a majority."],"a":1},{"q":"A relevant recommendation from an expert is?","o":["Proof every part of an unrelated plan is valid.","Formal authorization to spend if their credentials are strong.","Evidence to weigh within expertise/conflict limits, not automatic organizational signoff.","Permission to quote them as endorsing details they never reviewed."],"a":2},{"q":"Barrels/ammunition notes should be treated as?","o":["Proof specialists cannot contribute to end-to-end delivery.","Permanent talent identities assignable from job title.","Attributed role/context metaphor, not validated employee diagnoses or a universal capacity law.","A formula proving any five managers can launch exactly five products."],"a":2},{"q":"Management-as-editing in this exercise means?","o":["Clarify, reduce needless complexity, resource and review while preserving bounded team ownership.","Personally rewrite every output to eliminate delegation risk.","Remove required dissenting reviews because they slow a draft.","Treat simplification as authorization to cut personnel."],"a":0},{"q":"45 of 60 respondents interested. Defensible share?","o":["75% formal approval regardless of respondents rights.","45% of all employees without a population denominator.","60% because 60 responded.","75% of respondents, not all employees or approval."],"a":3},{"q":"Interest 45/60, later adoption 30/60. Difference?","o":["15 percentage points, using count gap as a rate gap.","25 percentage points; not proof persuasion caused a change.","50 percentage points, using adoption rate as decline.","33.33 percentage points, using relative count decline as point change."],"a":1}]},{"t":"Teams that think: psychological safety and group norms","kick":"Make room for evidence and dissent while keeping standards, privacy and real consent.","mins":"1.5 hrs","obj":["Distinguish psychological safety from comfort, agreement and absence of accountability.","Scope Google team findings to observational context and actual measures.","Design inclusive norms and interpret silence/survey data without unsupported diagnoses."],"vids":[{"id":"LhoLuui9gX8","t":"Building a psychologically safe workplace | Amy Edmondson | TEDxHGSE","by":"TEDx Talks"}],"reads":[{"t":"Google re:Work: understanding team effectiveness","u":"https://rework.withgoogle.com/intl/en/guides/understand-team-effectiveness","n":"Use this reference to study Google re:Work: understanding team effectiveness."},{"t":"Amy Edmondson's TED speaker page","u":"https://www.ted.com/speakers/amy_edmondson","n":"Use this reference to study Amy Edmondson's TED speaker page."},{"t":"Edmondson on safety and accountability","u":"https://www.hbs.edu/recruiting/guides-and-stories/leading-in-tough-times","n":"HBS 2022 Q&A, not book or causal experiment."},{"t":"1999 study citation","u":"https://www.hbs.edu/faculty/Pages/item.aspx?num=2959","n":"Summary or description; use the written exercise for the worked case."}],"ex":"Synthetic team-norm exercise, not a real survey or instruction to call out quiet colleagues. Psychological safety is a shared belief that a team is safe for interpersonal risk-taking, such as asking for help, admitting a mistake or respectfully challenging an idea. It is not guaranteed employment, absence of standards, freedom from evidence-based feedback or permission for misconduct. HBS Edmondson 2022 Q&A treats safety and accountability as separate dimensions: low fear plus motivation/standards can support learning. High safety alone is not a measured guarantee of performance. A leader declaring safety or admitting one mistake cannot prove trust has been created, or that this is always the fastest intervention.\nGoogle current official guide is /understand-team-effectiveness; use the current publisher route linked here rather than the retired /understanding-team-effectiveness route. Article labels original publication 2015 and editor note describes Aristotle 2016/Oxygen 2011; preserve source-era qualifiers, not invent a publication correction. It reports 180 teams (115 engineering project teams, 65 sales pods), ranging 3-50 members, median 9, and 35+statistical models/hundreds of variables. Outcome measures combine executive, leader, member evaluations and sales quota performance; subjectivity and context remain. Ordered five dynamics are psychological safety, dependability, structure/clarity, meaning, impact. This is Google-specific observational/modeling evidence, not a randomized causal proof that the five universally explain every team. Nonsignificant factors at Google do not establish irrelevance elsewhere; individual skill, colocation, team size and other factors may matter in another setting. Oxygen managers and Aristotle teams are distinct studies. The HBS 1999 citation page identifies the study; consult the full paper for its methods and results.\nWorked fictional meeting: manager endorses proposal immediately, two experts stop contributing, a later private note raises a flaw. Separate observations from hypotheses: fear, processing time, language/accessibility, agreement, unclear decision rights or lack of knowledge may explain silence. Do not diagnose a safety problem, consent or incompetence from silence alone. Invite relevant dissent without demanding personal vulnerability: What risks or assumptions should we test? Offer time to think, written/private input where appropriate and a genuine right to pass. Ask for expertise rather than public role labels or humiliating a quiet person. Clarify how input affects decisions and who actually decides. No agreement from non-response.\nProposed norms: leader states uncertainty and invites correction; critique ideas with evidence, not people; acknowledge input and act on relevant concerns; separate error reporting from blame while retaining appropriate accountability; explain decisions and follow through. Norms come from repeated behavior, peer interactions, incentives and formal processes, not only a poster or one leader act. Immediate containment/escalation may be needed for harmful incidents; learning review does not replace controls or individual/context inquiry. Share lessons with appropriate audience and minimization, not every sensitive error broadly. Don't promise anonymity in a tiny team or collect identifiable survey responses without actual authority/privacy plan.\nToy measurement: 10 members, 8 respond, 6 favorable to a clearly defined item.6/8=75% respondents favorable; 6/10=60% of whole cohort known favorable. If 2 nonrespondents could be either favorable/unfavorable, full-cohort favorable fraction bounds 60%-80%, not a verified safety score, causal outcome or probability one person feels safe. Nonresponse and small sample limitations remain. Airtime distribution and counts of ideas/errors may prompt inquiry, not diagnose psychological safety; more reports can reflect detection or opportunity rather than more underlying mistakes. Write one synthetic scenario, norm, observable follow-through and alternative explanation; no real meetings, personnel judgments, recordings, surveys or policy rollout.","quiz":[{"q":"Psychological safety alongside accountability means?","o":["High standards alone prove people feel safe speaking.","Safety requires removing feedback about errors.","Low interpersonal fear can coexist with clear standards and fair responsibility.","A leader promise eliminates all interpersonal risk."],"a":2},{"q":"Google Aristotle results justify which conclusion?","o":["All statistically nonsignificant Google variables are irrelevant elsewhere.","The study randomized every team to psychological-safety training.","The five dynamics exhaust all possible causes of every team outcome.","Five dynamics mattered in the studied Google teams; test context and avoid universal causal claims."],"a":3},{"q":"A meeting falls silent after the manager speaks. Interpretation?","o":["Conclude the team needs no written-input channel because nobody asked.","Treat the absence of objections as enough to record full agreement.","Infer fear before testing processing-time or expertise explanations.","Several hypotheses remain; invite input without treating silence as consent or proof fear."],"a":3},{"q":"Appropriate invitation to a quiet expert?","o":["Invite relevant risks or assumptions, allow processing and suitable alternative channels without forced disclosure.","Demand they explain their silence to prove commitment.","Ask for a personal failure story before accepting technical input.","Record silence as approval and move ahead."],"a":0},{"q":"Leader admits one mistake. Safety now verified?","o":["No; look at repeated behavior, experience and response to real concerns.","Yes; fallibility is always the fastest sufficient intervention.","Yes; if the leader also declares the room safe.","No; leaders should never acknowledge uncertainty."],"a":0},{"q":"Sharing a mistake lesson should consider?","o":["Containment, context, accountability and appropriate audience/privacy, not indiscriminate disclosure.","Always publish every name and personnel detail to all staff.","Treat all mistakes as exempt from responsibility.","Hide all errors until annual performance reviews."],"a":0},{"q":"Nonsignificant colocation association at Google means?","o":["Statistical nonsignificance establishes zero true effect.","Remote work always causes equivalent results in every role.","Colocation is universally harmful.","Not demonstrated there under those measures; not proof location never matters elsewhere."],"a":3},{"q":"10 members, 8 respond, 6 favorable. Respondent share?","o":["60%, using whole cohort denominator.","75%.","80%, returning response rate.","6%, using count as percent."],"a":1},{"q":"Same data. Known whole-cohort favorable share?","o":["75%, using respondents as whole cohort.","60%, with 2 unknown responses.","80%, counting both missing as observed favorable.","100%, ignoring unfavorable responses."],"a":1},{"q":"With 2 unknown members, the full-cohort favorable fraction could be?","o":["60%-80%, not a verified safety probability.","75%-100%, using wrong lower bound.","0%-75%, discarding six known favorable.","Exactly 75%, assuming nonrespondents match respondents."],"a":0}]},{"t":"Culture as the operating system","kick":"Read culture documents as stated operating choices, then test context and lived-practice evidence.","mins":"2 hrs","obj":["Compare Netflix, Valve and Bridgewater stated mechanisms with source-era and evidence limits.","Separate autonomy/transparency from absence of rules, coordination or privacy rights.","Investigate stated/lived value gaps without unsupported universal culture-change claims."],"vids":[{"id":"zCO0lNfs4oM","t":"Reed Hastings, Netflix: Stanford GSB 2014 Entrepreneurial Company of the Year","by":"Stanford Graduate School of Business"}],"reads":[{"t":"Netflix Culture Memo","u":"https://jobs.netflix.com/culture","n":"Use this reference to study Netflix Culture Memo."},{"t":"Valve's Handbook for New Employees (PDF)","u":"https://cdn.akamai.steamstatic.com/apps/valve/Valve_NewEmployeeHandbook.pdf","n":"Use this reference to study Valve's Handbook for New Employees (PDF)."},{"t":"Fast Company: how the 2024 Netflix memo differs","u":"https://www.fastcompany.com/91146182/netflix-revamped-2009-memo-on-culture","n":"Use this reference to study Fast Company: how the 2024 Netflix memo differs."},{"t":"Bridgewater idea meritocracy","u":"https://www.bridgewater.com/culture/bridgewaters-idea-meritocracy","n":"Bridgewater's 2023 description of its own culture. Separate company-described practices from independent evidence of their effects."},{"t":"Bridgewater FAQ","u":"https://www.bridgewater.com/working-at-bridgewater/faqs","n":"Current company self-description; not independent employee-experience research."}],"ex":"Private synthetic culture comparison, not an assessment of actual coworkers or authority to change rewards, hiring or employment. Compare stated principles, operating mechanisms, decision rights, expected behaviors, exceptions, tradeoffs and evidence of lived practice. A culture memo is a company-authored account of intent/claimed practice, not proof everyone acts that way or a randomized explanation of business success. Culture can be examined through repeated choices, stories, peer behavior, leadership, incentives, processes and context; it is not exhausted by one quote about nobody watching. A stated/lived mismatch can erode trust, but is not empirically always worse than having no value. No source proves promotions/firings are universally the fastest culture-change lever; those actions have separate rights, fairness and applicable-process requirements. Do not prescribe termination as an experiment.\nNetflix current official memo lists Dream Team, People Over Process, Uncomfortably Exciting, Great and Always Better. It describes performance, collaboration, candor, context not control, informed captains, farming for dissent and disagree then commit. Keeper test has both would I fight to keep and would I hire again questions; memo says whole record, ongoing discussion, short-term bumps, decency and respect. Not a one-mistake automatic firing formula or advice to copy into an unrelated organization. Context not control is not hands-off: managers coach and may intervene for unethical/material harm, crisis or new employee context gaps. Significant decisions have an informed captain; seeking opinions is not mandatory unanimous committee decision or permission to ignore rightful constraints. The memo explicitly retains processes and rules against harassment, marginalization, leaks and insider trading. Expense slogan does not authorize any reader's spend; actual employer authority/policy still governs. Current source includes a Q 1-2024 audience estimate, not verified current subscribers, so do not repeat it as current scale. The Fast Company June 26, 2024 introduction is a historical overview, not a substitute for comparing the original and revised culture documents.\nValve 2012 handbook PDF describes a flat, managerless philosophy with founder/president and self-directed projects. It explicitly allows temporary project structure and leads as information resources; flat does not mean no coordination, influence, role clarity or rules. Selected sections Welcome to Flatland, Team leads/Structure happens, Your Most Important Role/Hiring, and What Is Valve Not Good At read. Stated downsides include new-person orientation, mentoring, information dissemination, new disciplines, longer-horizon forecasts and excluding people preferring traditional structure. It says hiring quality and self-funding/founder commitment support its model; these are source explanations, not independently measured universal causal laws or verification of current Valve practices. Do not translate it into hidden hierarchy is proven the only problem, all companies need no managers, or senior specialists are less valuable. Historical employment/hiring statements do not establish current openings or lawful policy elsewhere.\nBridgewater 2023 IdeaMeritocracy primary document plus currentFAQ fill the original missing third-company-source gap. Its account separates honest belief from objective truth: share views with humility and curiosity to test accuracy, not because the loudest debater is right. Leaders carry inclusion/accountability duties; transparency aims at accurate useful information, not drowning others in noise. It explicitly treats culture as evolving and judgment-dependent. Company claims about truth/learning/outcomes are not independent verification of realized information quality or quantification of emotional/attrition costs. Don't treat radical transparency as permission to record, expose personnel data, breach confidentiality or coerce every private thought. No current individual employment recommendation or universal fit judgment follows. Keep the three companies distinct, including different source eras.\nWorked fictional values-evidence table: stated We surface risks early; observation dates/categories for 10 comparable decisions; 6 included documented pre-decision risk review, 4 did not. This is 60% observed review coverage, not 60% good culture or evidence those 4 people lack integrity. Records may be incomplete; review quality, urgency, eligible denominator, outcomes, voices excluded and counterexamples before interpreting. Proposed small reversible test: clarify risk-review owner and evidence field for synthetic decisions, measure burden/quality/missed risks with explicit review and actual approval before real rollout. Mechanisms may reinforce or conflict with values; copy underlying problem/constraint thinking, not slogans or assume cultures mostly fail when copied. No personnel records, promotions, rewards, transparency tools, real surveys or policy changes.","quiz":[{"q":"A company culture memo establishes?","o":["Use the memo as evidence of actual employee behavior if the company confirms the document is still current.","Its stated principles/mechanisms and claims, not independently verified universal lived practice.","Copy its mechanisms into the new team after matching job titles, without checking local rights or constraints.","Attribute business outcomes mainly to the memo because its stated mechanisms fit the company's success story."],"a":1},{"q":"Netflix keeper test should be read with?","o":["A requirement to replace anyone with less tenure.","An automatic exit after any failed experiment.","Whole-record/short-term-bump/context and respectful ongoing-feedback qualifications.","A guarantee all managers apply the test without bias."],"a":2},{"q":"Context not control at Netflix means no manager intervention?","o":["Yes; new employees must always learn entirely alone.","Yes; ethical harm must be ignored if employee owns the decision.","No; memo describes coaching and intervention for defined serious/context cases.","No; managers must approve every routine method choice."],"a":2},{"q":"Flat Valve 2012 handbook means?","o":["Read flatness as no roles or coordination, with each person independently deciding priorities throughout every project.","No traditional management hierarchy in its account, but temporary structure/leads and coordination remain.","Use the handbook to support removing managers locally once team size matches, without a role-and-rights review.","Treat the 2012 handbook as a current external assessment of how employees experience every Valve team."],"a":1},{"q":"Which is an explicit selected Valve downside?","o":["Mentoring/onboarding/information-sharing limitations, among others.","Proof every project is secretly managed by one unlisted boss.","A measured universal 50% productivity loss from flatness.","A claim traditional hierarchies cannot ever work."],"a":0},{"q":"Bridgewater honest belief and objective truth are?","o":["Accept a sincere belief as objective truth when its owner has more organizational experience than the challengers.","Different; views should be tested with humility/evidence, not accepted because strongly expressed.","Treat public agreement by most participants as sufficient evidence that a strongly held view is factually correct.","Keep belief and truth separate by declining to compare either with observable results or contrary evidence."],"a":1},{"q":"Radical transparency authorizes sharing confidential personnel details?","o":["Yes; recording everything is inherent universal permission.","Yes; if the information may improve debate.","No; organizational norms do not replace actual rights/privacy limits.","No; even relevant authorized information must always be hidden."],"a":2},{"q":"Culture-change claim promotions/firings always fastest?","o":["True regardless of fair process or consequences.","Proven universally by reading three company memos.","Not established; investigate mechanisms, context, evidence and legitimate authority before action.","False because incentives can never affect behavior."],"a":2},{"q":"6 of 10 comparable decisions have documented risk review. Coverage?","o":["40%, returning the missing-review share.","60%, not a measured culture-quality score.","6%, using count as percent.","100%, assuming undocumented reviews occurred."],"a":1},{"q":"Four decisions lack a recorded review. Conclusion?","o":["The stated value is proven worse than having none.","Four owners are proven dishonest.","Check eligibility, missing records, quality and context before inferring motives or real practice.","The other six decisions are guaranteed high quality."],"a":2}]},{"t":"Hiring, bar-raising and letting people go","kick":"Use role evidence and clear decision rights, not vibes, percentile confusion or rushed exits.","mins":"1.5 hrs","obj":["Distinguish peer-percentile language from mean and formal review roles from actual authority.","Build role-relevant structured questions, rubrics and evidence checks.","Treat hiring/exit decisions as consequential and authority/process-dependent."],"vids":[{"id":"HxLlgBATjpI","t":"Liz Wiseman: Diminisher vs. Multiplier","by":"Stanford eCorner"}],"reads":[{"t":"What is an Amazon bar raiser?","u":"https://www.aboutamazon.com/news/workplace/amazon-bar-raiser","n":"Use this reference to study What is an Amazon bar raiser?."},{"t":"Amazon's 16 Leadership Principles, explained","u":"https://www.aboutamazon.com/news/workplace/what-do-each-of-amazons-leadership-principles-really-mean","n":"Use this reference to study Amazon's 16 Leadership Principles, explained."},{"t":"Google structured interviewing","u":"https://rework.withgoogle.com/intl/en/guides/a-guide-to-structured-interviewing-for-better-hiring-practices","n":"March 2026 written guide read; underlying research/claimed savings not independently checked."}],"ex":"Private synthetic interview design, not a real vacancy, candidate decision, reference request or exit plan. Start with actual role outcomes, essential capabilities, evidence and rights. Choose job-related prompts/work samples, consistent conditions and scoring anchors, trained evaluators, privacy/accessibility considerations and a legitimate decision process. A proposed reviewer has no veto merely because a worksheet calls them bar-raiser. Hiring can be delegated/shared within actual authority; the decisions you cannot delegate is not a universal governance rule. Avoid unsupported most common hiring-mistake rankings and pedigree/first-minute affinity as substitutes for evidence.\nAmazon official bar-raiser article describes trained third-party perspective, interview/debrief coaching and final decision with hiring manager. It states new hire skills/abilities better than 50% of would-be peers in similar roles, not necessarily above arithmetic team mean. Its three responsibilities are interviewer, decision driver, teacher/mentor. Long-term/company perspective is the intended role, not proof bias is eliminated or independence from every incentive/conflict. Assigned page does not itself establish a universal stand-alone veto rule, mandatory training duration or current headcount; 3600 historical figure not current census. Keep source descriptions and outcome evidence separate. Bar-raiser training/rights aren't created by appointing an uninformed outsider in an exercise.\nAmazon 2023 LeadershipPrinciples interview describes 16 principles as its framework, role-specific examples and tradeoffs. It does not require one candidate to score high on every principle. STAR=Situation, Task, Action, Results; interviewee suggests adding Improvement as STARI. Explain personal contribution and team context, source/result basis, learning and limits. Fluent story format is not independent verification or proof true attribution. Ask neutral follow-ups and seek relevant evidence; do not reward fabricated specificity or require disclosure of a former employer's secrets. Use Wiseman's written definitions rather than attributing an unsupported exact phrase to the recording.\nAdded Google March 2026 structured-interview guide: relevant vetted questions, detailed candidate-answer feedback, standardized anchored rubrics, interviewer training/calibration. It explicitly includes both behavioral and job-related hypothetical questions. Historical behavior and well-designed work samples can be useful, but hypothetical does not mean useless puzzle, and no single method is strongest for every hiring decision. Reject irrelevant brainteasers/vague culture fit; test actual role requirements. Google's reported 40 minutes saved/35% rejected-candidate satisfaction claim lacks independently checked design/scale here, not a guaranteed local result. Same job fair comparison does not mean denying reasonable accommodations or checking protected/private attributes. No legal compliance claim is established by a template.\nWorked fictional operations-analyst loop: capability 1 reconcile inconsistent metrics; prompt with synthetic table and missing denominator; strong evidence identifies mismatch and asks for source/time basis. Capability 2 tradeoff reasoning; hypothetical capacity conflict with cost/quality constraints; strong evidence names options, uncertainty and decision owner. Capability 3 communication; behavioral example with neutral probes for situation, individual action, result and learning; strong evidence separates team from personal contribution and does not expose confidential clients. Set anchors before reviewing candidates, document actual observations and uncertainty, then independently score/calibrate rather than adjust criteria to favorite. Different decision rules may require essential-skill minimums, not a compensating average. Work samples must be scoped fairly and not covert unpaid real production work; actual terms require owner/process review.\nToy peer scores[1, 2, 3, 4,100]: median 3, mean 22. Candidate 4 exceeds 3 of 5 peer scores=60% but is below arithmetic mean 22. This illustrates the difference only; real human capabilities aren't one validated scalar or a requirement to force a percentile on small samples. A mean can move for many reasons; raising a hiring standard does not mathematically ensure every future hire/outcome improves. Toy predeclared weights 50/30/20%, scores 4/3/2 out of 5 give 3.3/5=66%, not 330% or guaranteed hire. Check scale meaning, missing data, essential thresholds, review disagreement and fairness. Numeric toy is not a hiring algorithm or observed validation.\nAn exit is consequential, not simply move earlier than feels comfortable. Before any real decision, confirm facts, authority, applicable employment process/contract, notice/terms, non-discrimination, privacy and qualified HR/legal input where needed. This research does not verify jurisdiction-specific rules or authorize any firing/payment/access removal. Dignity may include clear truthful communication, appropriate privacy, support and accurate documentation without unapproved promises; speed does not override process or evidence. Review system/hiring/onboarding contributions without assuming every departure proves bad hiring or manager fault. No actual interviews, candidate data, rating, rejection, offer, warning, exit, reference contact or compensation change.","quiz":[{"q":"Amazon better than 50% of similar-role peers necessarily means above arithmetic mean?","o":["No; it means better than only one specific teammate.","Yes; median and mean always coincide.","Yes; job-related scores cannot be skewed.","No; percentile-style comparison and arithmetic mean can differ."],"a":3},{"q":"Assigned bar-raiser page establishes?","o":["Bias is eliminated whenever one reviewer comes from another team.","Any outsider appointed in a worksheet now has veto authority.","Hiring manager decisions can never be delegated or shared.","Amazon-described trained review/shared decision responsibilities, not a universal automatically granted veto."],"a":3},{"q":"Structured interviews should use?","o":["Relevant questions with detailed notes but no shared anchors or calibration.","The same scoring labels but different role criteria chosen after first impressions.","Relevant planned questions, consistent anchors, evidence and trained/calibrated reviewers.","A common script with vague culture-fit scores substituted for actual capability evidence."],"a":2},{"q":"Job-related hypothetical questions are?","o":["Always weaker than any story about prior work.","A valid structured format for novel-scenario reasoning, not inherently useless puzzles.","Proof the candidate actually performed that work previously.","A permission to demand confidential prior-employer data."],"a":1},{"q":"Candidate gives fluent STAR story. What follows?","o":["Probe actions, basis, context and learning; format alone does not verify facts or contribution.","Hire automatically because structured narration proves competence.","Credit all team outcomes solely to the speaker.","Reject because only unstructured spontaneity can reveal skill."],"a":0},{"q":"Interview criteria should be set?","o":["Only after the most senior interviewer announces a ranking.","After comparing personalities so they fit the favorite.","Before reviewing candidates, with relevant anchors and declared essential thresholds.","From unrelated company slogans without role analysis."],"a":2},{"q":"Peer scores 1, 2, 3, 4,100: arithmetic mean?","o":["22.","3, the median.","4, the candidate score.","20, using only the extreme score divided by 5."],"a":0},{"q":"Candidate 4 compared with those 5 peer scores?","o":["Exceeds none because mean exceeds 4.","Exceeds 4 of 5=80%, counting the tie as strictly higher.","Exceeds all 5 because it is above median.","Exceeds 3 of 5=60% yet is below mean 22."],"a":3},{"q":"Weights 50/30/20%, scores 4/3/2 out of 5. Weighted score?","o":["330%, not dividing weighted score by 5.","3/5=60%, ignoring weights.","9/5=180%, adding scores without weights.","3.3/5=66%, under the toy rule, not automatic hire."],"a":3},{"q":"Necessary employment exit should prioritize?","o":["Evidence, authority, applicable process, privacy and dignity; speed is not blanket permission.","Early action alone, no need to verify facts or rights.","A public warning as a universal precondition.","Immediate access removal and compensation change on a quiz result."],"a":0}]},{"t":"Leading through others: intent, multipliers and energy","kick":"Stop being the bottleneck you complain about","mins":"1.5 hrs","obj":["Practise intent-based leadership: give intent, receive intent, resist giving orders (Marquet)","Spot diminisher habits in yourself and replace them with multiplier habits (Wiseman)","Manage energy and attention as the scarce resources of leadership"],"vids":[{"id":"IzJL8zX3EVk","t":"Turn the Ship Around | L. David Marquet | Talks at Google","by":"Talks at Google"},{"id":"amk-1_wcmKo","t":"How the Best Leaders Make Everyone Smarter | Liz Wiseman & Greg McKeown | Talks at Google","by":"Talks at Google"}],"reads":[{"t":"Intent-Based Leadership - David Marquet","u":"https://davidmarquet.com/"},{"t":"Vistage: Turn the Ship Around introduction","u":"https://pages.vistage.co.uk/turn-the-ship-around"},{"t":"Multipliers - Wiseman Group","u":"https://thewisemangroup.com/books/multipliers/"}],"ex":"Synthetic delegation design, not authority to change reporting lines, assign real work or spend. Marquet's official account describes organizational intent (what the team is trying to achieve) and the decision-maker's intent (how they plan to achieve it). Intent differs from permission: the default is action absent a veto rather than waiting for approval. That language alone does not transfer legal, financial or employer authority. Define the actual scope, constraints, competence, shared purpose, escalation triggers and review before using an intent protocol. Vistage's assigned landing page says teams need to know what/how/why and leaders must provide tools, training and a clear why. Its video outline is not a full audiovisual review. The page's information-to-authority wording should not silently be presented as a verified quotation reversing that direction. Use the underlying decision-rights problem explicitly instead.\nDo not ban direct answers for a week. In a safe, reversible, understood task, invite a proposal and reasoning before offering your answer. A novice may need instruction and practice; an urgent safety issue may need immediate containment and authorized direction. Asking questions is a tool, not always the best response. Giving an answer is not inherently diminishing, and autonomy is not abandonment. Clarify who can decide, who needs consulting or notice, what must stop and what still needs approval. A message saying I intend does not approve a purchase, disclosure or commitment.\nWiseman's own Multipliers page defines learned behaviors: Talent Magnet uses people at their highest contribution; Liberator creates an intense environment for thinking; Challenger stretches opportunities; Debate Maker supports rigorous debate; Investor gives ownership and invests in success. Diminisher/Multiplier are framework descriptions, not clinical labels or fixed employee identities. Accidental diminishing can arise from well-intended rescuing or always supplying an answer. Examine behavior and its effect without declaring a person defective. A leader may provide a scaffold, resources, feedback and checkpoints while preserving ownership. Debate needs actual decision rights and a close, not endless consensus or performative agreement.\nThe vendor reports 150 executives in 35 countries and 1.97 times capability. This is an attributed publisher claim, not independently validated causal evidence, a promise to double output or a universal staffing formula. Reported energy is not measured production capacity. Do not convert a metaphor about multiplying intelligence into a physical productivity law. Track relevant outcomes, quality, rework, burden and context before claiming improvement.\nToy decision log: 12 eligible routine decisions; 8 acted within a pre-authorized scope without asking permission, 4 required approval under their scope. Eight of twelve = 66.7% descriptive in-scope independent action coverage, not a leadership score. If 2 of those 8 later needed rework, rework among independent decisions is 25%; whole-log rework from that subset is 16.7%, with other decisions' rework unknown. Do not claim the intervention caused either rate. Toy time model: 4 people save 30 minutes each per week = 2 gross team hours. Coaching/setup uses 45 leader minutes plus 15 minutes from each of the four people = 1.75 person-hours. Net first-week person-time = 0.25 hours, not 2 hours cash savings. Counts are synthetic and time may not be redeployable. Write one fictional intent, boundary, resource, stop condition and checkpoint. No actual team experiment, personnel judgment or authority grant.","quiz":[{"q":"What distinguishes intent from permission in Marquet's account?","o":["Intent removes all existing financial and safety limits.","Intent requires an approval reply before each routine step.","Intent defaults to action absent a veto within an established scope.","Intent means informing nobody until after outcomes are known."],"a":2},{"q":"A novice is about to make an unsafe change. Best response?","o":["Contain the risk and give appropriate instruction within actual authority.","Continue only open questions because direct answers diminish people.","Allow failure first so the novice can discover every constraint.","Avoid involvement because ownership excludes all intervention."],"a":0},{"q":"What makes a delegation test more defensible?","o":["A reversible task and a deadline, with competence and escalation left implicit.","A reversible task with clear rights, competence, resources and stop conditions.","Clear outcomes and resources, but decision rights inferred from the intent phrase.","A competent owner and a checkpoint, without specifying exposure or stop conditions."],"a":1},{"q":"A leader repeatedly rescues work before others can think. Interpretation?","o":["An accidental diminishing hypothesis to test through behavior and effects.","A fixed personality diagnosis established by one observation.","Proof every rescue should be forbidden even during serious incidents.","Evidence the team must be unable to learn independently."],"a":0},{"q":"Wiseman's Investor discipline most closely supports?","o":["Ownership transferred with all feedback and support withdrawn.","Ownership of results with investment in people's success.","Detailed method approval for every step irrespective of competence.","Credit assigned to the leader so the team remains accountable."],"a":1},{"q":"How should the 1.97-times capability claim be used?","o":["Attribute it to the publisher and avoid promising a causal local output doubling.","Use it as a guaranteed reduction in required headcount.","Convert it into exactly 97% more revenue for any team.","Treat it as independently measured capacity in this organization."],"a":0},{"q":"Rigorous debate in an intent-based task needs?","o":["Evidence, relevant voices, clear decision rights and a way to close.","Unanimity before every routine action regardless of scope.","A leader withholding all expertise so ownership stays pure.","Agreement inferred from anyone who does not object aloud."],"a":0},{"q":"8 of 12 eligible decisions used in-scope independent action. Coverage?","o":["150%, reversing eligible and independent counts.","75%, treating four approval cases as a different total.","33.3%, reporting the approval share instead.","66.7%, a descriptive rate rather than a validated leadership score."],"a":3},{"q":"2 of those 8 independent decisions needed rework. Subset rate?","o":["25% of independent decisions, with other cases' rework unknown.","16.7% of independent decisions, using the whole-log denominator.","20%, adding rework cases to the independent denominator.","75%, reporting the no-rework share as rework."],"a":0},{"q":"Gross saving 2 team hours; setup/coaching 1.75 person-hours. Net?","o":["1.25 person-hours, excluding participant setup time.","2 person-hours, because coaching is not part of the work.","0.25 person-hours in the first week, not verified cash savings.","3.75 person-hours, adding the costs to the savings."],"a":2}]},{"t":"The laws of organizational life","kick":"Peter, Parkinson, Goodhart, Dunbar and the fence","mins":"1.5 hrs","obj":["Use the Peter Principle, Parkinson's Law and Goodhart's Law as testable hypotheses about organizational problems, not diagnoses of motives or universal causal laws","Assess coordination costs using actual workflow and relationship evidence; treat Dunbar's number as a context-dependent heuristic, not a universal headcount limit","Respect Chesterton's fence: understand why a rule exists before removing it"],"vids":[{"id":"IbFr5DAyZBM","t":"Peter Principle: When People Get Promoted Into Maximum Incompetence","by":"Sprouts"}],"reads":[{"t":"Promotions and the Peter Principle - abstract","u":"https://www.nber.org/papers/w24343"},{"t":"Goodhart original and later restatement","u":"https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/mem2/papers/LHCE/goodhart.html"},{"t":"Dunbar number reanalysis - university report","u":"https://www.su.se/english/news/articles/2021-05-05-new-study-deconstructs-dunbars-number---yes-you-can-have-more-than-150-friends"},{"t":"Chesterton fence - reproduced passage","u":"https://www.chesterton.org/taking-a-fence-down/"},{"t":"Munger revised essay - selected sections","u":"https://fs.blog/great-talks/psychology-human-misjudgment/"},{"t":"Parkinson dictum - Britannica","u":"https://kids.britannica.com/students/article/Parkinsons-law/332542"},{"t":"Five trademarks of agile organizations","u":"https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/people-and-organization/our-insights/the-five-trademarks-of-agile-organizations"}],"ex":"Synthetic organizational diagnosis, not a judgment about real colleagues or permission to reorganize. Treat Peter, Parkinson, Goodhart, Dunbar and Chesterton as distinct lenses with different evidence, not deterministic laws explaining every workplace. State an observation, competing explanations, relevant source scope, a reversible test and evidence that would change your view. A memorable name does not identify a root cause or establish a remedy.\nPeter Principle: strong performance in an individual role need not predict managing others. The NBER 2018 working-paper abstract reports sales-worker microdata at 214 firms, evidence consistent with prioritizing current performance at the expense of other observable predictors of managerial performance. It also notes a possible incentive benefit that could justify mismatch costs. This is not proof all managers are incompetent, everyone must eventually fail, or sales stars must never be promoted. A paper abstract alone does not establish the strength or applicability of the method. Define the next role's outcomes and assess relevant capability rather than presume rank, tenure or current output establishes readiness. Offer expert and management paths where appropriate, without assuming authority to implement them.\nParkinson's dictum says work expands to fill available time. Britannica states the wording and 1955 Economist origin. The original Economist page provides an introduction; distinguish it from the full essay. Use bounded timeboxes as a hypothesis about scope and delay, not proof any task can be completed safely by halving its deadline. Waiting for inputs, genuine complexity, queues, quality requirements and capacity can explain delay. Preserve safety and quality and examine actual work before compressing time.\nGoodhart's original control-pressure formulation and Strathern's later measure-as-target restatement are distinguished on Cambridge's scholarly discussion page. The practical risk is proxy distortion under pressure, not that every metric instantly becomes worthless or that measuring without incentives is always harmless. Pair the operational measure with quality/outcome checks, audit eligibility and exclusions, and inspect gaming and case mix. Do not use the page's quantum analogy as empirical proof of social behavior.\nDunbar's roughly 150 stable-relationship proposition is not a universal legal or biological team-size cap. Stockholm University's 2021 account of a reanalysis says estimates from updated primate data/methods had wide uncertainty and did not support a precise human limit in that manner; it also acknowledges prior empirical support and different estimates. This is a university report of a study, not a review of every underlying paper or proof human attention has no limits. Choose coordination structure from work dependencies, communication load and observed outcomes, not automatically split organization number 151.\nChesterton's fence asks reformers to understand why a rule or institution exists before removing it. Chesterton Society reproduces a passage from The Thing (1929). Understanding a purpose is not a command to retain every rule forever. Check present risks, beneficiaries, costs, changed context and a replacement safeguard; legitimate urgent containment may be needed before a long historical study. Actual change still requires authority.\nMunger's assigned revised 2005 essay explicitly says it was revised from memory without research, acknowledges his limited psychology training, lists 25 tendencies and discusses combinations. Focus on the introduction, the list of tendencies and the incentive discussion; treat the historical illustrations as attributed examples. It warns against one-hammer explanations even while emphasizing incentives. Treat his examples as attributed illustrations, not independently verified experiments, a validated comprehensive taxonomy or proof most dysfunction has a single incentive root cause. McKinsey's five agile trademarks combine shared purpose, empowered teams, rapid learning/decision cycles, people and enabling technology with stability; not a universal claim that removing hierarchy fixes all dysfunction.\nToy service metric: before, 90 of 100 eligible cases meet the recorded response-time target and 80 resolve correctly; after, 95 of 100 meet target and 70 resolve correctly. Target compliance rises from 90% to 95% (+5 percentage points); correct resolution falls 80% to 70% (-10 points). Relative compliance increase is 5/90=5.56%, not 5.56 points. This divergence warrants investigation of incentives, case mix, exclusions and measurement; it does not prove deliberate gaming or causality. Propose a fictional balanced measure and one disconfirming test; do not read personnel files, alter compensation, or split real teams.","quiz":[{"q":"What does the cited Peter Principle study establish here?","o":["A finding that current high performers should never lead teams.","A randomized proof all management promotions reduce performance.","Evidence consistent with promotion mismatch in sales-worker data, not a universal incompetence law.","An estimate that every employee reaches the same competence ceiling."],"a":2},{"q":"Assessing a strong salesperson for manager should focus on?","o":["Tenure alone as a proxy for all managerial requirements.","Current revenue rank as sufficient proof of coaching ability.","Evidence for the new role's outcomes alongside current-role performance.","Avoiding sales performers entirely because success prevents leadership."],"a":2},{"q":"A task misses a timebox. Parkinson's lens implies?","o":["Remove quality checks because available time causes all work.","Halve every future deadline until the team stops reporting delay.","Treat any delay as proof the employee created unnecessary work.","Investigate scope, dependencies and capacity rather than assume a shorter deadline is safe."],"a":3},{"q":"Goodhart risk is best described as?","o":["Control pressure can distort a proxy's relationship to the desired outcome.","Any measurement is guaranteed useless before anyone sees it.","A metric and outcome become identical once a bonus is added.","Changing a target eliminates the need to examine behavior."],"a":0},{"q":"An organization reaches 151 people. Dunbar means?","o":["Hierarchy becomes ineffective at precisely the 151 st person.","A biological limit requires immediate division into two companies.","Every employee has exactly 150 available work relationships.","No automatic split; study dependencies and coordination evidence in context."],"a":3},{"q":"Chesterton's fence supports which reform?","o":["Understand the safeguard's purpose, then assess current risks and a justified replacement.","Preserve every inherited rule permanently after finding its origin.","Delete any rule whose purpose is not immediately obvious.","Wait for complete history even when urgent safety containment is needed."],"a":0},{"q":"Munger's incentive discussion is best used as?","o":["A validated finding that incentives explain all organizational behavior.","An attributed diagnostic lens alongside other tendencies and competing explanations.","A controlled experiment proving his anecdotes generalize to every firm.","A complete substitute for observing actual decisions and constraints."],"a":1},{"q":"90/100 target compliance becomes 95/100. Change?","o":["An increase of 5 percentage points.","An increase of 5.56 percentage points.","An increase of 95 percentage points.","A decrease of 5 percentage points."],"a":0},{"q":"Correct resolutions fall from 80/100 to 70/100. Change?","o":["An increase of 12.5 percentage points.","A decrease of 10% relative to the initial 80.","A decrease of 10 percentage points.","No change because the response-time target improved."],"a":2},{"q":"Faster recorded responses but fewer correct resolutions proves?","o":["A reason to drop response-time measurement before testing complementary checks.","Evidence of deliberate gaming before checking eligibility or case mix.","Enough evidence to attribute the change to the target without a comparison.","A divergence worth investigating, not yet deliberate gaming or causality."],"a":3}]}];

const SCOM_MODULES=[{"t":"Operations is the strategy you actually execute","kick":"Where plans meet physics","mins":"1.5 hrs","obj":["Define operations as the transformation system that turns inputs into outputs, in factories and services alike","Explain how operational choices (cost, quality, speed, flexibility) become competitive strategy","Distinguish operational effectiveness from strategy - and why you need both"],"vids":[{"id":"q0U5yquI-fY","t":"An Introduction to Operations Management with Christian Terwiesch","by":"Coursera"}],"reads":[{"t":"MIT Operations Strategy course scope","u":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-769-operations-strategy-fall-2010/"},{"t":"MIT Operations Management lecture notes","u":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-760a-operations-management-spring-2002/pages/lecture-notes/"},{"t":"MIT lecture 1 selected pages","u":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-760a-operations-management-spring-2002/resources/lecture1_feb6/"},{"t":"MIT lecture 3 - operations choices and capacity","u":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-760a-operations-management-spring-2002/resources/lecture3_feb11/"},{"t":"Harvard: Operational Effectiveness vs Strategy","u":"https://www.isc.hbs.edu/strategy/business-strategy/Pages/operational-effectiveness-vs-strategy.aspx"}],"ex":"Synthetic operations-strategy design, not a claim about a real founder's motives or an instruction to change a live operation. Operations choices help deliver a customer promise through product development, process design/management and supply-chain choices. MIT's 2010 Operations Strategy description covers technology, facilities, vertical integration and human resources, with cost, quality and innovation as means of competing. The 2002 Operations Management lecture 1 presents the three foundational components; lecture 3 connects Burger King's product/process choices, inventories, peak demand, capacity and marketplace competition. These are historical teaching materials, not current Burger King performance or universal recipes.\nKeep operational effectiveness and strategy distinct. Harvard's Institute for Strategy and Competitiveness describes both as important: doing similar activities better can improve performance, but rivals may copy best practices; strategy concerns doing things differently to support a distinctive position. The module title is a prompt to examine the choices actually embodied in operations, not proof that efficiency alone is strategy or that an operating metric reveals founder intent. No observed delay, cost or process proves a motive without additional evidence. Distinguish stated promise, observed capability, constraints and hypotheses.\nStart with a fictional customer segment and promise. Example: predictable next-day delivery for a narrow standardized product range. Map a coherent activity system: limited assortment, compatible inventory policy, replenishment discipline, realistic order cutoff, reliable carrier handoff and an exception route. Define who owns each decision, evidence and escalation. Examine cost, quality, dependability, speed and flexibility with explicit definitions and customer relevance. Do not force every business to select exactly two priorities or assume all dimensions are incompatible. Some changes reduce waste across several dimensions; genuine tradeoffs require choosing, such as holding more inventory for availability at an explicit carrying/obsolescence cost. Separate minimum requirements from differentiating priorities and investigate their joint feasibility.\nA 1-5 scorecard needs anchors and observations: for example, define on-time as delivery by the promised date, denominator all eligible orders in a stated window, and disclose exclusions. Rating 5 could mean at least 98% on time over that window; rating 3 could mean 90% to below 95%. Other ranges must be fully specified before use, and a score is a chosen ordinal summary, not an interval-scale fact or inferred customer preference. Do not average arbitrary scores into a validated strategy grade. Compare segment/period like for like and investigate cancellations, missing timestamps, expedited orders and changed promise dates. A synthetic sponsor review is not real approval.\nToy sequential process on the same item/unit basis: stage A capacity 120 units/hour, stage B 80, stage C 100. Assume one visit at each stage, no parallel alternative, adequate input/staffing, steady conditions and no losses/changeovers. Nominal process capacity = min(120, 80, 100)=80 units/hour at B. Demand 90/hour exceeds nominal capacity by 10/hour, or 80 units during an 8-hour period starting with no backlog if conditions persist. Demand is not achieved throughput; the accumulating queue means no steady-state claim for that case. Raising A to 150 alone does not raise process capacity. Raising B to 110 moves the nominal bottleneck to C at 100, enough for the 90 demand under the toy assumptions; variability, downtime and quality can still reduce real delivery performance. Do not blindly target 100% utilization.\nToy delivery evidence: 92 on-time orders of 100 eligible =92%, with eight late. If another period has 45 of 50 on time =90%, pooled rate is 137/150=91.33%, not the unweighted 91% average of period percentages. Period mix and denominators matter; neither figure by itself verifies the customer promise or a strategy advantage.\nSource caution: MIT lecture 3 lists broiler capacity 480 patties/hour and peak patty demand 147/hour, but calls 147/200=74% broiler utilization while 200 is stated assembly capacity. Do not score that inconsistent label as a verified utilization formula or silently substitute the numerator's units. The coherent toy process above is independently specified, not a correction to the case without its full data. Use lecture 1 pages 1-12 and lecture 3 for the written definitions. Historical cases and figures are illustrations, not current operations data. Write a fictional promise, linked decisions, one genuine tradeoff, a defined measure, a capacity model and a counterexample. No procurement, vendor contact, process rollout, staffing change, spending or current-company verdict.","quiz":[{"q":"Operations strategy is best examined through?","o":["A list of efficiency metrics independent of customer needs.","Linked operational choices that support a customer promise and competitive position.","The founder's presumed personality inferred from one queue.","A requirement to rank exactly two priorities above all others."],"a":1},{"q":"Operational effectiveness versus strategy means?","o":["Any efficiency improvement creates a permanent uncopyable advantage.","Both matter, but improving similar activities is not itself a distinctive strategic position.","Strategy excludes process choices because only marketing defines value.","Operational improvement is unnecessary once a position is chosen."],"a":1},{"q":"A delay proves the founder prefers low cost over service?","o":["Yes; every operational outcome directly reveals personal intent.","No; capacity, variability, dependencies and other causes must be investigated.","Yes; if a customer complains before the promise date.","No; operating evidence can never inform strategy analysis."],"a":1},{"q":"A defensible 1-5 operations score needs?","o":["Shared anchors with weights chosen after inspecting which result looks strongest.","A manager rating with a fixed scale name but no operational anchors.","Defined anchors, eligible denominator, time window and observations before interpretation.","A peer benchmark with its different denominator and promise definitions left unchanged."],"a":2},{"q":"Sequential capacities 120, 80, 100 units/hour, one visit each. Capacity?","o":["300 units/hour, the sum of stage capacities.","100 units/hour, the average of the three capacities.","120 units/hour, the first stage's capacity.","80 units/hour, with stage B the nominal bottleneck."],"a":3},{"q":"Demand 90/hour, capacity 80/hour for 8 hours, no initial backlog. Added backlog?","o":["10 units, ignoring the eight-hour period.","720 units, treating all demand as backlog.","640 units, treating all capacity as backlog.","80 units under the stated constant no-loss assumptions."],"a":3},{"q":"Raise stage A 120 to 150 while B 80, C 100 stay. New capacity?","o":["110 units/hour, the new average.","80 units/hour; the bottleneck is unchanged.","150 units/hour, the largest stage.","100 units/hour, because C was second slowest."],"a":1},{"q":"Instead raise B 80 to 110 with A 120, C 100. New nominal capacity?","o":["120 units/hour, because A becomes fully used.","110 units/hour, because the repaired stage sets every limit.","100 units/hour, with C now the bottleneck.","330 units/hour, summing all stages."],"a":2},{"q":"92/100 and 45/50 orders on time. Pooled rate?","o":["92%, the rate of the larger period alone.","91%, the unweighted average of the two rates.","137/150=91.33%, rounded to two decimals.","90%, the rate of the smaller period alone."],"a":2},{"q":"When does holding more inventory present a genuine tradeoff?","o":["When the purchase price is ignored so service can be rated independently.","Whenever stock rises, because every performance dimension must worsen.","Only when exactly two objectives are selected for the whole company.","When added availability is weighed against explicit carrying and obsolescence costs."],"a":3}]},{"t":"Process thinking: flow, bottlenecks and Little's Law","kick":"Three numbers that run every operation","mins":"2 hrs","obj":["Measure any process with throughput, flow time and work-in-process (WIP)","Apply Little's Law (WIP = throughput x flow time) to real systems","Find the bottleneck and compute process capacity"],"vids":[{"id":"pfC6hivsUH0","t":"Process Analysis Example using Little's law: A Dr. Office with two types of flow units (patients)","by":"Jan Albert Van Mieghem"},{"id":"Mow-Bk3YSCg","t":"Lean Six Sigma (Module 4 Lecture 3 - Little's Law)","by":"Infinity MFG"}],"reads":[{"t":"MIT 15.760 Class 8: Basic Concepts in Queueing (PDF)","u":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-760a-operations-management-spring-2002/e6c66bef8fc37c9f4928ea8f816895ab_lecture8_feb22.pdf","n":"MIT's lecture notes on queues, variability and waiting. Free PDF."},{"t":"MIT 15.772 J D-Lab Supply Chains - process analysis problem set","u":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-772j-d-lab-supply-chains-fall-2014/f17bfc0d70c0a4009931352e72ad7716_MIT15_772JF14_ProblemSet2.pdf","n":"Process-analysis problem questions, not a supplied worked-solution key. Derive answers with the given assumptions."},{"t":"MIT Urban Operations Research: Little formula and boundaries","u":"https://web.mit.edu/urban_or_book/www/book/chapter4/4.4.html"}],"ex":"Synthetic process analysis, not authority to time coworkers, photograph private work or change a real queue. Choose a consistent flow unit, system entry/exit boundaries, observation period and denominator before calculating. Throughput R is the actual completed flow rate for that boundary, not demand or nominal machine capacity. Average WIP I is the time-average number of units inside that same boundary; average flow time T includes waiting and processing between those entry/exit events. Little's Law I=R*T relates compatible long-run averages when the required finite limits exist and flows are accounted for. It does not require exponential arrivals, first-come-first-served service or every individual's time to equal the mean. Practical stable-flow use needs attention to growing backlog, start/end effects, abandoned/rejected units, rework visits and consistent class membership. Finite-window ratios from a snapshot may not equal long-run averages.\nQueue-only Iq=R*Tq excludes units currently being served and service time. Whole-system I=R*T includes both. MIT's urban operations text explicitly separates these boundaries and says finite-capacity systems use the rate actually entering, not people turned away. Do not put all applicants in WIP while dividing only by approved applicants, or include repeat visits in one measure but unique customers in another. Class-specific flows can be analyzed separately, then combined with appropriate weights.\nToy clinic: average 60 patients within arrival-to-departure boundary and 40 completed patients/hour, with compatible stable averages and all exits counted. T=60/40=1.5 hours=90 minutes, not a promise every patient waits 90 minutes. If 20 of those patients on average are waiting and all 40/hour enter service, queue wait=20/40=.5 hours=30 minutes; the remaining average 60 minutes is time elsewhere inside the chosen system, not automatically one worker's service time. Cutting WIP from 60 to 40 at an actually maintained 40/hour would imply mean time 1 hour. The equation alone does not establish a causal intervention or guarantee throughput will remain unchanged; removing necessary buffers can cause starvation and losses.\nThe assigned MIT 2002 lecture 8 distinguishes arrival/service rates, server count, capacity, utilization and congestion. Its nonlinear waiting formulas have distribution/model assumptions, not a universal exponential-growth law. Its seasonal fish examples show storage and processing capacity tradeoffs; lowering inventory can sacrifice output. Read the fourteen-page note for definitions and assumptions; use the explicit fictional figures in this exercise. The MIT 2014 three-page problem set is questions, not worked solutions. It requires product-equivalent units, setups, class-specific routing and supplied time conventions; do not label the file itself an answer key.\nWorked derivation from that problem set, not an official supplied solution: 1000 applications/month, 30-day working month. Initial-review WIP 200 =>.2 month=6 days overall. Assume each class has that same 6-day initial-review mean for this worked derivation; the supplied averages do not independently identify class-specific initial waits. Class A 25% has 250/month, WIP 25=>.1 month=3 additional days; total 9 days. Class B 25% has 250/month, WIP 150=>.6 month=18 additional days; total 24 days. Class C 50% exits after initial review=>6 days. Weighted time=.25*9+.25*24+.5*6=11.25 days. Total WIP 375/all 1000 monthly arrivals=.375 month=11.25 days. Old WIP 500=>15 days. Approval proportions do not change these all-application denominators. Shorter mean alone does not establish adoption: review error, fairness, workload, cost, tail times and actual authority before a real change.\nFor a simple serial same-unit process with one visit per stage, no losses/changeovers, adequate supply and steady conditions, minimum effective stage capacity limits nominal process capacity. Actual throughput may be lower due to demand, blocking, starvation, variability or quality. The longest-looking task or largest visible queue is a candidate, not proof: batching, scheduling and shared resources can create queues. A nonbottleneck change may not raise nominal capacity but can improve reliability, quality or cost and reduce bottleneck starvation. Do not claim it is always worthless or that improving only the bottleneck guarantees a free gain. Document a fictional flow map, compatible measurements, bottleneck hypothesis, assumptions and one safe test with a cost/risk estimate. No real observation, photography, staffing or process rollout.","quiz":[{"q":"Little's Law requires which pairing?","o":["All applicants in WIP divided only by approved applicants per hour.","A WIP snapshot paired with nominal capacity from a different shift.","Compatible average inventory, actual flow rate and time across the same boundary.","Queue-only inventory paired with arrival-to-departure system time."],"a":2},{"q":"Does Little's Law require exponential arrival times?","o":["Yes; every lawful flow process must have exponential arrivals.","No; that assumption belongs to particular queue models, not the general average relation.","Only when all service is first-come-first-served.","Only if average flow time is shorter than service time."],"a":1},{"q":"60 average patients inside, 40 completed/hour. Mean system time?","o":["0.67 hours, dividing flow rate by inventory.","1.5 hours, or 90 minutes, under the compatible-average assumptions.","60 minutes, reading the patient count as time.","40 minutes, reading the hourly rate as time."],"a":1},{"q":"Queue-only WIP 20, service-entry rate 40/hour. Mean queue wait?","o":["20 hours, using queue count without a rate.","1.5 hours, using whole-system inventory 60.","2 hours, reversing rate and queue WIP.","0.5 hours, excluding service time."],"a":3},{"q":"Reduce WIP 60 to 40 while actual flow stays 40/hour. What follows?","o":["Mean time would become 1 hour; the equation alone does not ensure a safe causal change.","Throughput must stay unchanged regardless of buffer removal.","Mean time must rise to 2 hours to conserve output.","All patients now spend exactly 1 hour inside."],"a":0},{"q":"A visibly large queue identifies the bottleneck?","o":["It is sufficient proof without considering batching or scheduling.","It suggests a candidate requiring capacity, routing and variability evidence.","Only if its waiting area is physically larger than other areas.","No, because queue observations cannot inform capacity analysis."],"a":1},{"q":"MIT problem set: 200 initial-review WIP, 1000/month, 30-day month. Time?","o":["30 working days, using the supplied month length directly.","0.2 working days, leaving month units unconverted.","6 working days for initial review.","15 working days, using old WIP 500 instead."],"a":2},{"q":"Class B 250/month, WIP 150, plus initial-review 6 days. Total time?","o":["6 working days, excluding specialist review.","18 working days, excluding initial review.","10.5 working days, using all 1000 as class flow.","24 working days."],"a":3},{"q":"Class mix A 25% at 9 days, B 25% at 24, C 50% at 6. Overall mean?","o":["13 working days, the unweighted class mean.","11.25 working days.","39 working days, the sum of class times.","15 working days, the old process mean."],"a":1},{"q":"A nonbottleneck reliability improvement can be worthwhile when?","o":["It reduces starvation, rework, risk or cost even if nominal serial capacity is unchanged.","Only when it increases its own displayed utilization.","Only when it changes the identity of the slowest nominal stage.","Never, because every nonbottleneck benefit is a throughput mirage."],"a":0}]},{"t":"The Goal: Theory of Constraints","kick":"Goldratt's five focusing steps","mins":"1.5 hrs","obj":["State the five focusing steps: identify, exploit, subordinate, elevate, repeat","Explain drum-buffer-rope scheduling in plain terms","Compute the effect of a binding constraint under explicit demand, routing and capacity assumptions; distinguish lost constraint capacity from realized system output"],"vids":[{"id":"wTZcDXlAB2U","t":"Theory of Constraints in production - 5 min. summary","by":"Marris Consulting"}],"reads":[{"t":"Dr. Eli Goldratt keynote: What is TOC?","u":"https://learningcenter.tocico.org/courses/eli-goldratt-keynote-what-is-toc","n":"Summary or description; use the written exercise for the worked case. No registration needed for this conceptual synopsis."},{"t":"Implementing TOC as a holistic approach","u":"https://www.toc.tv/player/64","n":"Summary or description; use the written exercise for the worked case."},{"t":"Marris: five focusing steps publisher description","u":"https://www.marris-consulting.com/en/videos/theory-of-constraints-in-production/production-management-with-the-theory-of-constraints"},{"t":"TOCPA: throughput definition","u":"https://tocpractice.org/throughput-t/"},{"t":"TOCPA: totally variable cost","u":"https://tocpractice.org/10506/"},{"t":"Lean Production: TOC mechanisms and measures","u":"https://www.leanproduction.com/theory-of-constraints/"}],"ex":"Synthetic constraint-management case, not an order to change shifts, skip maintenance, assign overtime or buy equipment. First define the system, goal, flow unit, boundary and actual constraint with evidence. TOC can concern physical capacity, material, market or policy limits, not only the slowest machine. A simple single-constraint production example is useful but not proof every network has one permanently fixed bottleneck. Product mix, routing, shared resources, variability and demand can change the binding limit. Production capacity is not automatically saleable output.\nFive focusing steps: identify the current constraint; exploit it using existing resources; subordinate other activities to support its effective operation; consider elevation if it remains binding; then repeat and avoid allowing old assumptions to become the next constraint. Exploit means improve useful goal output, not exploit workers or run unsafe equipment continuously. Protect suitable maintenance, breaks, training and quality. Existing-resource work still consumes time and can have opportunity cost. Elevation can involve changed capacity/resources, not necessarily a purchase; investments need actual approval, full economics and a verified remaining constraint. If an earlier step removes the constraint, reassess rather than mechanically complete irrelevant spending.\nClassic Drum-Buffer-Rope describes a constraint-based schedule (drum), protective time/buffer against disruption (buffer), and controlled upstream release linked to the schedule/buffer (rope). It does not require all machines to produce at 100% utilization or forbid protective nonconstraint capacity. A buffer is not unlimited stock; too little can starve the constraint, too much ties up resources and adds delay. Define scope and distinguish classic DBR from simplified variants rather than claim one mechanical recipe for every service or supply chain. Subordination supports the system goal, not arbitrary obedience or neglect of downstream customer commitments.\nAssigned TOCICO Goldratt keynote page gives a presentation synopsis and TOC assumptions, not a full transcript or independently proven universal claims. Assigned TOC.tv page is a 36-minute program description, not the full recording. Check the publisher's current access terms; this exercise does not require registration. Marris publisher page explicitly lists identify/exploit/subordinate/elevate/repeat and synchronization; LeanProduction's selected explainer supplies the focusing-step table, accounting measures, DBR and kinds of constraints. Its fastest/guaranteed-benefits headlines and numerical industry claims are not independently validated or promised outcomes. TOCICO certification overview is curriculum scope only, not proof course access or a purchased credential.\nDistinguish process throughput (units/time) from TOC accounting throughput for a commercial organization: money generated through sales less totally variable costs (TVC), with units/time or period explicit. TOCPA defines non-profit throughput in goal units rather than forcing profit as every organization's goal. TVC depends on whether a cost actually changes with the additional sale, not merely its accounting label. Regular fixed salaries are not deducted as per-unit TVC in the stated toy; truly unit-linked commissions/materials are. Total throughput less operating expense gives the toy period operating result; this is not a replacement for statutory reporting, tax rules or a cash-flow forecast. Produced-but-unsold inventory is not automatically earned throughput. Cost allocation or machine-hour absorption is not incremental cash saving.\nToy product mix, same period and one binding resource: product A sells for 100 currency units, TVC 40, uses 10 constraint minutes; B sells for 90, TVC 50, uses 5 minutes. Throughput contribution is 60 per A and 40 per B, or 6 versus 8 currency units/constraint-minute. With 60 constraint minutes, ample inputs/nonconstraint capacity, no changeovers/losses, and demand for at least 12 B, all B yields 12 units and 480 contribution versus 6 A and 360. This ranking is valid for the stated single-constraint/divisible-time scenario, not a universal product-pricing rule. If B demand is capped at 4, produce 4 B consuming 20 minutes and use remaining 40 for 4 A: contribution 160+240=400. Include costs, contracts, quality and other constraints before a real mix decision.\nToy bottleneck downtime: 30 lost minutes at 6 saleable units/hour could forgo 3 units. At 40 contribution/unit, the upper stated opportunity is 120 currency units, only if demand, inputs, quality and no later recovery support those sales. It is not necessarily 120 lost cash or profit; lost capacity may be recovered later. A nonbottleneck hour can improve reliability, cost, safety or bottleneck feeding even without raising nominal capacity. Write an evidence table, one reversible exploit proposal, buffer/release logic, full cost/risk basis and what would show the constraint moved. No real scheduling, maintenance, staffing, sales, procurement or commitments.","quiz":[{"q":"Five focusing steps in order?","o":["Identify, elevate, exploit, subordinate, declare completion.","Identify, exploit, subordinate, elevate if needed, repeat.","Exploit, subordinate, identify, elevate, repeat.","Identify, subordinate, elevate, exploit, stop after expansion."],"a":1},{"q":"Exploit a constraint responsibly means?","o":["Maximize constraint units while postponing checks on saleable demand.","Increase scheduled runtime while treating planned maintenance as a later issue.","Use existing staff time but omit its opportunity cost from the proposal.","Improve useful output with existing resources while preserving safety, quality and necessary support."],"a":3},{"q":"Subordination is best represented by?","o":["Maximizing each upstream machine's output into any available space.","Aligning release and support so the constraint is neither starved nor blocked.","Making every resource use an identical utilization target.","Slowing downstream service regardless of shipping commitments."],"a":1},{"q":"In classic DBR, the rope controls?","o":["Upstream release linked to the protected schedule/buffer.","Every employee's individual performance ranking.","The selling price of every finished product.","An unlimited reserve of work before each station."],"a":0},{"q":"After the constraint moves during exploitation?","o":["Keep the old schedule because constraints are permanent roles.","Complete the planned elevation purchase before checking again.","Reassess the new limit instead of buying capacity for the old one automatically.","Declare the whole system unconstrained and end monitoring."],"a":2},{"q":"A price 100, TVC 40, 10 constraint minutes. Contribution/minute?","o":["4 currency units per constraint minute, using TVC.","10 currency units per constraint minute, using revenue.","60 currency units per constraint minute, ignoring time.","6 currency units per constraint minute."],"a":3},{"q":"B price 90, TVC 50, 5 minutes. Contribution/minute?","o":["10 currency units per constraint minute, using TVC.","18 currency units per constraint minute, using revenue.","8 currency units per constraint minute.","40 currency units per constraint minute, ignoring time."],"a":2},{"q":"60 constraint minutes, ample B demand, no other limits. All B yields?","o":["12 units and 1080 currency contribution, using sales revenue.","6 units and 240 currency contribution.","12 units and 480 currency contribution.","10 units and 400 currency contribution, using 6 minutes per B."],"a":2},{"q":"Same case, but B demand capped 4. Best stated mix?","o":["6 A, contribution 360 while leaving B's higher return unused.","12 B, contribution 480 despite the demand cap.","4 B and 4 A, contribution 400.","4 B and 6 A, contribution 520 despite exceeding time capacity."],"a":2},{"q":"30 lost constraint minutes at 6 units/hour, 40 contribution/unit. Interpretation?","o":["120 guaranteed lost profit regardless of later recovery.","Up to 120 foregone contribution under saleability/no-recovery assumptions, not automatic cash loss.","240 foregone contribution, using a full hour.","3 currency units lost, confusing units with contribution."],"a":1}]},{"t":"Toyota Production System and Lean","kick":"The machine that changed the world","mins":"2 hrs","obj":["Explain the two pillars: just-in-time and jidoka (built-in quality)","Name the seven wastes and spot them in any process","Distinguish real lean (respect for people + kaizen) from cost-cutting theatre"],"vids":[{"id":"F5vtCRFRAK0","t":"How Toyota Changed The Way We Make Things","by":"Bloomberg Originals"},{"id":"HIEufX83EEY","t":"The Toyota Production System as Taught by Toyota - Episode 04","by":"Toyota Motor Corporation"}],"reads":[{"t":"Toyota's official TPS pages","u":"https://global.toyota/en/company/vision-and-philosophy/production-system/","n":"The source itself: JIT, jidoka, kaizen, heijunka in Toyota's own words. Free."},{"t":"Toyota Production System - Toyota Europe explainer","u":"https://www.toyota-europe.com/about-us/toyota-vision-and-philosophy/toyota-production-system","n":"A clean readable summary of the two pillars. Free."},{"t":"75 years of Toyota: the TPS story","u":"https://www.toyota-global.com/company/history_of_toyota/75years/data/automotive_business/production/system/change.html","n":"How the system was actually built, decade by decade. Free."},{"t":"LEI: seven wastes","u":"https://www.lean.org/lexicon-terms/seven-wastes/"},{"t":"LEI: respect for people - Womack 2007 essay","u":"https://www.lean.org/the-lean-post/articles/respect-for-people/"}],"ex":"Synthetic lean problem-solving, not permission to photograph a workplace, stop equipment, remove stock or change staffing. Toyota's current global source names two TPS pillars: jidoka (automation with a human touch, detect/stop abnormalities and build in quality) and Just-in-Time (make what is needed, when needed, in the amount needed through synchronized processes). Toyota describes work made easier for people, daily incremental kaizen, and elimination of muda (waste), mura (unevenness) and muri (unreasonable burden). These are company principles and mechanism descriptions, not independently measured proof that every Toyota operation achieves them or a guarantee a copied tool delivers the same outcome.\nJidoka includes machines detecting an abnormality and stopping, or an operator signaling/stopping the line. Andon makes the problem visible for response. Do not translate Toyota's operating design into a universal legal right to stop any employer's equipment or a rule that every minor signal always triggers an identical full-factory shutdown. Actual safety/stop/escalation procedures and roles govern real work. Contain the defect, seek qualified help, understand cause and verify the countermeasure; do not bypass a safeguard to meet output. Automation alone is not jidoka: abnormality detection, response and human learning matter. The global source emphasizes understanding/improving manual work before building that logic into machines.\nJIT is not zero stock by decree. Toyota explicitly describes minimum parts stocks in advance and preceding-process stores, withdrawal by the next process and replenishment before the next pickup. Reliability, synchronization, quality and replenishment lead times matter. A kanban is an authorization/replenishment signal within a designed system, not a magic card that removes uncertainty. Reducing stock without addressing instability can create shortages; inventory can be necessary protection even while excess conceals problems or ties up resources. The historical 75-year page describes decades of trial, error, expansion and adaptation to long local procurement times, not one universal rollout sequence or current supply-network performance.\nLEI's seven-waste taxonomy lists overproduction, waiting, conveyance/transport, unnecessary processing, excess inventory, unnecessary or straining motion, and correction/rework/scrap. Distinguish items moved from people moving; producing ahead of need differs from stored excess though they can be linked. Not every non-customer-value activity is immediately removable waste: necessary regulatory, safety, quality and enabling work may remain until its underlying need is safely addressed. A category label is not permission to cut training or inspection. LEI's entry awkwardly announces two work categories but then lists three; use the actual distinctions without scoring a category count from that wording. The seven named wastes are a lens, not proof training is always useful or every process must exhibit exactly one example of each. Record absent/unknown categories rather than invent evidence.\nRespect for people is not mere politeness, unrestricted autonomy or demanding more effort. LEI's 2007 Womack essay describes mutual problem-solving dialogue, worker knowledge, evidence, causes, alternatives and how success will be measured. Its distribution-center turnover/productivity comparisons are attributed observation/rough estimate, not an independently controlled causal study or promised local gain. Challenge must not become humiliation, unreasonable burden or denial of necessary resources. Kaizen includes learning and standardizing a tested improvement so the same avoidable problem need not be solved repeatedly. A small local speed gain is not success if it shifts rework or risk downstream. Tool-only adoption may disappoint, but no source proves a universal failure rate or that every cost reduction is theatre.\nToy observation log: 10 fictional orders, one record per order, 6 minutes average processing and 24 minutes average waiting across a defined boundary. Mean total time 30 minutes; processing-time share 6/30=20%, waiting share 80%. Processing is not automatically all customer-value time; this is not a validated lean score. Countermeasure removes 8 waiting minutes without changing processing: mean 22 minutes, an 8/30=26.67% reduction, not 36.36% (which uses the new denominator). Check quality, load, service mix and externalized waiting before attributing a real effect.\nToy quality: 100 units enter a process, 90 pass first time; 8 of 10 initial failures are recovered by rework and 2 scrapped. First-pass yield 90%, final good yield 98%, not 98% first-pass yield. Rework time, cost and safety matter even if recovered units ship. Define whether each unit is counted once and whether counts are comparable before comparing periods. Write one fictional waste observation, alternative cause, worker burden/risk check, countermeasure, measure and review condition. No real photography, employee monitoring, line stops, stock reduction, staffing change or policy rollout.","quiz":[{"q":"Toyota's two TPS pillars are?","o":["Jidoka and Just-in-Time.","Kaizen and kanban, the improvement method and signal alone.","Muda and mura, two conditions to address.","Automation and maximum machine utilization."],"a":0},{"q":"Jidoka is more than installing automation because it needs?","o":["Detection of abnormalities, response/stopping and built-in quality with human learning.","Continuous running with defects sorted at the shipping dock.","A machine purchase replacing the need to understand work.","Output targets that defer every stoppage until shift end."],"a":0},{"q":"Toyota's JIT description permits?","o":["Stock reduction assessed only by carrying cost,without shortage risk.","Minimal stocks with replenishment timing left to local informal judgment.","A pull signal without checking whether lead time and reliability support it.","Minimum replenishment stocks and synchronized withdrawal/replacement."],"a":3},{"q":"Transport versus motion waste is distinguished by?","o":["Unnecessary movement of items versus unnecessary or straining movement of people.","Transport meaning only international freight and motion meaning only robots.","Transport being always needed while people's movement is always waste.","A difference solely in whether the movement uses electricity."],"a":0},{"q":"An inspection does not directly transform the product. Best response?","o":["Classify every minute as customer value without examining purpose.","Remove it immediately because correction is in a waste taxonomy.","Keep it forever because any inspection guarantees customer quality.","Assess its necessary safeguard role and safely remove the underlying defect need before cutting it."],"a":3},{"q":"Respect for people in the selected LEI essay emphasizes?","o":["Targets without resources so people must discover improvements alone.","Autonomy without discussion because challenge signals distrust.","Mutual evidence-based problem solving that values workers' knowledge and support.","Praise replacing inquiry into causes and working conditions."],"a":2},{"q":"6 processing minutes and 24 waiting minutes. Processing share?","o":["20% of the 30-minute mean total, not automatically value-added share.","25%, dividing processing by waiting only.","80%, reporting waiting share instead.","6%, treating minutes as a percentage."],"a":0},{"q":"Remove 8 waiting minutes, processing unchanged. New total and reduction?","o":["22 minutes and 26.67% relative reduction from 30.","22 minutes and 36.36% reduction, using the new denominator.","16 minutes and 46.67% reduction, subtracting processing too.","30 minutes and 8% reduction, ignoring the time change."],"a":0},{"q":"100 entered, 90 pass first time, 8 recovered, 2 scrapped. First-pass yield?","o":["92%, subtracting recovered units from all inputs.","98%, including recovered units as first-pass successes.","90%.","80%, counting each initial defect twice."],"a":2},{"q":"Same data. Final good yield?","o":["8%, reporting the recovered share only.","90%, ignoring 8 successfully recovered units.","100%, assuming every initial failure is recovered.","98%, while rework burden remains separate."],"a":3}]},{"t":"Quality: variation, Deming and the checklist","kick":"Quality is a system property, not an inspection result","mins":"1.5 hrs","obj":["Explain common-cause vs special-cause variation and why confusing them wastes fortunes","Summarise Deming's 14 points and 'drive out fear'","Design checklists and poka-yoke that make errors hard to commit"],"vids":[{"id":"ckBfbvOXDvU","t":"Red Bead Experiment with Dr. W. Edwards Deming","by":"The Deming Institute"},{"id":"tsF-8u-V4j4","t":"W. Edwards Deming: The 14 Points","by":"The Deming Institute"}],"reads":[{"t":"Deming's 14 Points for Management","u":"https://deming.org/explore/fourteen-points/","n":"The Deming Institute's official list with commentary. Free."},{"t":"Deming discussing the 14 points (video)","u":"https://deming.org/deming-library-video-with-dr-deming-discussing-the-14-points/","n":"Deming himself, from the Deming Institute library. Free."},{"t":"NIST: control charts","u":"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc31.htm"},{"t":"NIST: process capability","u":"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section1/pmc16.htm"},{"t":"WHO: historical checklist study release","u":"https://www.who.int/news/item/11-12-2010-checklist-helps-reduce-surgical-complications-deaths"},{"t":"WHO: checklist implementation overview","u":"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241598590"},{"t":"LEI: error-proofing","u":"https://www.lean.org/lexicon-terms/error-proofing/"}],"ex":"Synthetic quality analysis, not a diagnosis of a real worker or authority to remove inspections. Deming's Institute presents his 14 points as a connected philosophy, not a menu of isolated tricks. The condensed list covers constancy of purpose, new philosophy, quality built into the process, total supplier cost rather than price alone, continual system improvement, training, leadership, driving out fear, cross-department collaboration, removing exhortations/quotas that ignore the system, pride in workmanship, education and shared transformation. Read the actual 14 points including 11 a/b and 12 a/b, not just a slogan. Single-supplier advocacy and abolishing merit ratings are his stated recommendations in that text, not universal mandates authorizing procurement or HR changes. Understand continuity, risk, context and actual rights before implementation.\nThe assigned 2014 Institute article contains selected quotations/commentary around video clips, not the full recording or book. Deming's 94% system/6% special statement is explicitly an estimate from his experience, not a measured universal allocation for your defects. System responsibility does not prove individuals never contribute or remove accountability. Drive out fear supports effective participation and reporting; no source here establishes that any fear inevitably causes hiding or that one reassuring speech creates trustworthy data. Fixing an immediate defect/containing harm is necessary but differs from improving the process that produced it.\nCommon-cause variation refers to the ongoing system of variation in an appropriately stable process; special/assignable causes indicate a changed condition worthy of investigation. A special cause need not be a single event, and a repeated defect alone does not identify common cause. Nor does a point outside a chart prove malice or its root cause. Deming-style tampering means reacting to ordinary noise as though each fluctuation needs a local setting change; it is a process-control concept here, not an accusation of data falsification. Look at time-ordered comparable data, measurement reliability, process changes and a suitable chart before classifying. Contain unsafe output regardless of whether a chart has detected a signal.\nNIST distinguishes control limits derived for process monitoring from specification limits describing requirements. A stable process can consistently miss requirements. Points inside limits do not guarantee control if a specified nonrandom-pattern rule signals. A point outside suggests investigation with a false-alarm risk, not certainty. Select the chart, subgroups, baseline and signal rules before inspecting favorable patterns; sample size, distribution, autocorrelation and changing denominators matter. Do not apply a normal three-sigma tail interpretation indiscriminately to skewed counts.\nPoka-yoke is design that prevents or detects a mistake, such as a keyed connector or a verified required-input check. LEI gives physical-orientation and part-detection examples. A warning or checklist can help detection but is not automatically an impossible-to-bypass interlock or proof zero errors. Test the specific failure mode, false alarms, workarounds, accessibility and safe exceptions. Inspection remains useful where necessary; building quality in reduces dependence rather than instructing readers to delete mandated checks.\nWHO's historical checklist release reports before/after groups in eight cities, 3733 and 3955 patients, and lower recorded complications/deaths after introduction. It is not a randomized proof every checklist works, not a full reading of Gawande's book and not a forecast for software or another clinic. The WHO 2009 manual overview lists staff engagement, leadership, training, multidisciplinary involvement, coaching, feedback and local adaptation. A checklist supports skilled teamwork, it does not replace expertise or justify medical practice changes by an unqualified reader. The release's displayed 2010 date and 2009 journal-reference wording are retained as source-era ambiguity, not silently made into a new trial date.\nToy capability with a stable normal process and known parameters: LSL 8, USL 20, mean 16, sigma 2. Cp=(20-8)/(6*2)=1; Cpk=min((20-16)/6,(16-8)/6)=2/3. The spread could fit if centered, but the mean is shifted, so Cp alone overstates the nearest-limit margin. These are conditional formula values, not a certified capability or field-data conclusion. Toy defects: 12 defects across 100 units with 5 defined opportunities each gives 12/500*1000000=24000 DPMO; it does not establish 12 defective units or defect probability without the distribution of defects among units. Avoid an unqualified few-per-million target: opportunity definitions, distribution, centering and convention must be specified. Write a fictional failure mode, measurement plan, prevention/detection design and validation condition. No real personnel data, control-limit reset, inspection removal, medical rollout or external disclosure.","quiz":[{"q":"Deming's 94% system/6% special statement is?","o":["A universal probability that any observed worker error is innocent.","An explicitly experience-based estimate, not a measured cause allocation for every process.","A requirement to classify exactly 94 of every 100 defects as common cause.","A controlled experiment estimating this organization's defect causes."],"a":1},{"q":"Recurring defects alone establish common cause?","o":["Yes; if no employee reports a change in the process.","Yes; special causes are defined as events that happen only once.","No; inspect time-ordered evidence, measurement and possible changed conditions.","No; repetition proves intentional misconduct instead."],"a":2},{"q":"All points inside control limits establish control?","o":["Not necessarily; predefined nonrandom-pattern rules may still signal.","Yes; the limits are also the customer's acceptance requirements.","Yes; false alarms are impossible once a center line exists.","No; every within-limit fluctuation requires an immediate adjustment."],"a":0},{"q":"Control limits versus specification limits?","o":["Control limits establish every unit is fit for the customer.","Both are interchangeable versions of a customer tolerance.","Specifications should move whenever process variation increases.","Monitoring behavior versus requirements; a stable process may still fail specifications."],"a":3},{"q":"A point outside a valid control-chart limit means?","o":["The requirement limits should be widened immediately.","The operator intentionally changed the process.","The nearest worker caused the signal.","Investigate an assignable-cause hypothesis while recognizing false-alarm risk."],"a":3},{"q":"Which conclusion best distinguishes a checklist from an error-preventing interlock?","o":["Completion signatures establish prevention strength without a bypass test.","A checklist may support detection/memory but needs testing and can still be bypassed.","A device called an interlock can be accepted without a safe-exception review.","A required-field check establishes correctness once blank submissions are blocked."],"a":1},{"q":"What limits the WHO checklist inference here?","o":["Training and local adaptation are unnecessary if the form is copied.","The release randomized every hospital and proves all checklist designs equivalent.","The effect must apply unchanged to any software team.","Before/after evidence and implementation context do not prove universal effects of any checklist."],"a":3},{"q":"LSL 8, USL 20, sigma 2. Cp under the stated stable-normal assumptions?","o":["0.5, doubling the specification width denominator.","2.0, using 3 sigma rather than 6 sigma in the denominator.","1.0.","6.0, dividing the width only by sigma."],"a":2},{"q":"Same process, mean 16. Cpk?","o":["2/3, limited by the upper specification margin.","4/3, using only the farther lower margin.","1.0, copying Cp without centering adjustment.","2.0, dividing the near margin only by sigma."],"a":0},{"q":"12 defects, 100 units, 5 opportunities/unit. DPMO?","o":["120000, using units instead of opportunities.","24000, with defective-unit count still unknown.","12000, using 1000 opportunities without support.","12, using the defect count as the normalized rate."],"a":1}]},{"t":"Inventory, queues and the mathematics of waiting","kick":"Why the warehouse and the waiting room obey the same math","mins":"1.5 hrs","obj":["Explain the motives for holding inventory: cycle stock, safety stock, and the cost of each","Describe the EOQ logic: ordering cost versus holding cost trade-off","Relate utilization and variability to waiting under stated queue assumptions; explain the M/M/1 stability requirement and distinguish its limiting behavior from deterministic schedules"],"vids":[{"id":"PBaAA28IhHk","t":"What's that \"EOQ solution\" for sourcing, batching and inventory management? (4 min)","by":"Jan Albert Van Mieghem"},{"id":"aHnbTWmcnU0","t":"Safety Stock Calculation Explained - Step-by-Step Tutorial","by":"SupplyChainXcel"}],"reads":[{"t":"MIT 15.760 queueing lecture (PDF)","u":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-760a-operations-management-spring-2002/e6c66bef8fc37c9f4928ea8f816895ab_lecture8_feb22.pdf","n":"The queueing math behind every waiting line. Free PDF."},{"t":"MIT 15.772 J D-Lab Supply Chains","u":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-772j-d-lab-supply-chains-fall-2014/","n":"Supply chains designed for the real world: inventory and distribution with constraints. Free."},{"t":"MIT D-Lab lecture 13: deterministic inventory/EOQ","u":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-772j-d-lab-supply-chains-fall-2014/b0b41cc09a83ffa4cf1335ac65f5f9ab_MIT15_772JF14_Lec13.pdf"}],"ex":"Synthetic inventory/queue analysis, not a real reorder or instruction to change groceries, ad spend, medicines or staffing. Define a physical item, flow unit, time basis, service objective, lead time and costs first. Classical inventory models do not fit every stream of spending: ad spend is not automatically a storable unit with an inventory holding cost. Cycle stock arises from batching under the stated replenishment pattern; safety stock protects against uncertainty in demand, lead time or yield. MIT's inventory lecture also distinguishes anticipation, pipeline and decoupling stocks. Half a batch is average cycle stock only in the simple constant-depletion/instantaneous-replenishment pattern without added safety stock, not a law of every warehouse.\nMIT 2014 D-Lab lecture 13 develops the classical EOQ model beyond the course description. Constant known demand, instantaneous replenishment in its baseline, all demand met, fixed cost K per order, holding cost h per unit per year, and purchase cost independent of batch size yield annual relevant cost KD/Q+hQ/2. Q*=sqrt(2 KD/h); orders/year=D/Q; cycle length=Q/D years. Purchase spend cD is constant with Q under that model, not absent from cash needs. Lead-time extensions, quantity discounts, capacity, perishability, minimum order sizes, uncertainty and shortage/service constraints may require another policy. The model does not prove your observed batch is irrational; inputs and constraints may differ. MIT's slide 32 extension writes a reorder expression with questionable units; do not score that wording as a verified formula. This exercise uses explicit demand-during-lead-time arithmetic instead.\nToy D 1200 units/year, K 50 currency/order, h 12 currency/unit/year gives Q*=sqrt(10000)=100 units. Twelve orders/year; cycle 1/12 year; average cycle stock 50 units. Annual fixed ordering 600 and holding 600, total 1200 relevant currency/year. If Q 200, ordering 300 and holding 1200, total 1500, 25% above that modeled minimum. These are cost-model results, not an approved purchase, price quote, guaranteed cash saving or full landed-cost budget. Separate purchase cost, stock investment and recurring holding cost. Simple deterministic reorder illustration: 10 units/day, 5 days replenishment lead time, no uncertainty, no shortages or pipeline anomalies =>50 units of lead-time demand. Real reorder control must define inventory position (including on-order stock and commitments), review timing and service conditions, not blindly trigger from shelf count alone.\nQueueing: utilization is offered load relative to effective service capacity under a defined model, not a command to keep everyone busy. In an infinite-buffer M/M/1 model with Poisson arrivals, independent exponential service, a single server and lambda<mu, the stationary mean system time is 1/(mu-lambda); queue-only wait excludes service. MIT 2002 lecture 8 supplies distribution-conditioned nonlinear congestion formulas. This does not establish that every real system at 100% utilization has infinite actual waiting. Finite buffers may reject arrivals, finite-horizon cases may not reach stationarity, and perfectly synchronized deterministic systems can behave differently. At/beyond capacity the stated stochastic stationary model loses its finite stable mean; it is not a field-data forecast for every shift.\nToy M/M/1: lambda 8/hour, mu 10/hour, rho 0.8. Mean system time 0.5 hour=30 minutes; mean service 0.1 hour=6 minutes; mean queue wait 0.4 hour=24 minutes; mean system WIP 4. With lambda 9 andmu 10, rho 0.9, system time 1 hour=60 minutes, not an observed promise. Changes in variability, capacity, appointments, priorities or pooling have different costs/risks; no source proves variability reduction is always the cheapest remedy. Do not demand faster work or add overtime from a model alone.\nRisk-pooling toy, two independent same-period demands each standard deviation 10 units, with equal lead times and an identical fixed safety multiplier: separate summed standard deviations 20; pooled standard deviation sqrt(100+100)=14.14. This can reduce the corresponding modeled aggregate buffer, but not eliminate forecasting, stockouts or transport/delivery constraints. If demands are perfectly positively correlated, pooled standard deviation 20, so this variance benefit disappears. Correlation, lead times, service definition and distribution affect real results; physical centralization need not be the only pooling method. ABC by annual usage value is a useful prioritization convention, not a reason to ignore inexpensive safety-critical parts. Stockout costs can include lost contribution, expediting, backorders and customer effects, but their size and future-demand impact must be estimated, not universally asserted greater than margin. Write a toy calculation, assumption table, sensitivity and one reason the model may not fit. No ordering, spending, staffing or real stock policy.","quiz":[{"q":"Average cycle stock equalsQ/2 when?","o":["The stated constant-depletion, instantaneous-replenishment cycle applies without added safety stock.","Any warehouse has a fixed order quantity regardless of demand pattern.","All on-order pipeline stock is included inQ.","Demand is uncertain enough that stockouts reset inventory unpredictably."],"a":0},{"q":"Classical EOQ trades off?","o":["Service quality against annual sales revenue only.","Purchase price against demand without any batch-dependent costs.","Total cash balance against marketing spending.","Fixed ordering cost per period against holding cost per period under explicit assumptions."],"a":3},{"q":"D 1200/year, K 50/order, h 12/unit/year. EOQ?","o":["10000 units, omitting the square root.","100 units.","200 units, doubling the final quantity.","10 units, taking an extra square root."],"a":1},{"q":"AtQ 100 in that model, annual ordering and holding costs?","o":["600 and 600 currency/year, total 1200 relevant cost.","600 and 1200, total 1800, using full batch as average stock.","50 and 600, total 650, using cost of one order only.","1200 and 600, total 1800, doubling the order rate."],"a":0},{"q":"AtQ 200, total relevant cost 1500 instead of 1200. Increase?","o":["No difference, because purchase spend is unchanged.","20%, using 1500 as the reference denominator.","300%, treating currency difference as a percent.","25% relative to 1200."],"a":3},{"q":"10 units/day, 5 day deterministic lead time. Lead-time demand?","o":["0.5 units, dividing lead time by demand rate.","2 units, dividing demand rate by lead time.","50 units under the stated no-uncertainty conditions.","15 units, adding a rate and a time."],"a":2},{"q":"M/M/1 lambda 8/hour, mu 10/hour. Mean system time?","o":["48 minutes, using utilization as time.","24 minutes, the queue-only wait.","6 minutes, the mean service time only.","30 minutes under the stationary model assumptions."],"a":3},{"q":"Same model. Mean queue-only waiting time?","o":["30 minutes, including service in the queue boundary.","24 minutes, after subtracting 6 minutes mean service.","6 minutes, reporting service alone.","36 minutes, adding service to the system time."],"a":1},{"q":"Does 100% utilization always prove infinite real waiting?","o":["No; variability never matters when staffing is fixed.","Yes, including every finite deterministic schedule.","Yes, even when excess arrivals are rejected from a finite buffer.","No; that conclusion needs a particular stationary stochastic model and boundary."],"a":3},{"q":"Two independent demands with SD 10 each. Pooled SD?","o":["sqrt 200=14.14 units; perfect positive correlation would instead give 20.","20 units under independence, adding SDs directly.","10 units, because combining locations erases one demand stream.","0 units, because independent spikes fully cancel."],"a":0}]},{"t":"Supply chain strategy: efficient versus responsive","kick":"Fisher's question: what is your product, really?","mins":"1.5 hrs","obj":["Classify products as functional or innovative and match the supply chain type to the product","Explain why mismatch (efficient chain for innovative product) destroys value","Analyse Zara's speed-as-strategy model"],"vids":[{"id":"1iwFEDlBVT0","t":"How to Source Products? Strategic Sourcing explained (Case studies of Walmart, Ikea and Zara)","by":"Marketing 91"}],"reads":[{"t":"What Is the Right Supply Chain for Your Product? (Fisher, HBR)","u":"https://hbr.org/1997/03/what-is-the-right-supply-chain-for-your-product","n":"Summary or description; use the written exercise for the worked case."},{"t":"Rapid-fire fulfillment (HBR)","u":"https://hbr.org/2004/11/rapid-fire-fulfillment","n":"Historical 2004 summary only retrieved, not full review. Authors Ferdows, Lewis and Machuca."},{"t":"Historical Zara textbook section 3.2(accessible replacement)","u":"https://biz.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Management/Book%3A_Information_Systems__A_Managers_Guide_to_Harnessing_Technology/03%3A_Zara-_Fast_Fashion_from_Savvy_Systems/3.02%3A_Dont_Guess_Gather_Data","n":"Historical case with 2000-2009 references, not current operating statistics; assigned Gallaugher PDF inaccessible."},{"t":"Fisher fit framework: secondary discussion and extensions","u":"https://scmresearch.org/2017/09/13/fishers-supply-chain-product-matchmismatch-framework/"},{"t":"Inditex 2025 Our Drivers company account","u":"https://annualreport.inditex.com/anrpxxvui/en/our-drivers"}],"ex":"Synthetic supply-chain fit comparison, not a current verdict on a brand or authorization to contact suppliers. Fisher's 1997 framework distinguishes functional products with more predictable demand from innovative products with more uncertain demand and shorter commercial lives. It recommends physically efficient chains for the former and market-responsive chains for the latter. Product classification is a decision lens, not a label fixed by luxury status, technology, retailer name or one stockout. A basic item can face volatile launches/promotions; an innovative line can contain predictable replenishment segments. Analyze forecast errors, life cycle, variety, lead time, margin and service exposure by product/segment, not only company averages.\nPhysical costs include production, transport and holding; market-mediation costs concern mismatch, including missed contribution, markdowns and obsolescence. Either class can matter, and their relative sizes must be estimated rather than presume mismatch always dwarfs physical cost. An efficient chain is not inherently slow or irresponsible; responsiveness is not free or always superior. The secondary 2017 SCM Research discussion summarizes Fisher and points to both resource costs and extensions adding supply-side risk; the page's claims should be distinguished from the underlying empirical studies. No deterministic claim that any mismatch destroys value follows from that page or a two-by-two diagram. A hybrid strategy by item/time/customer may fit better than a forced whole-firm binary.\nThe assigned HBR 1997 and 2004 pages provide summaries or introductions; full articles have separate access terms. Correct author name for 2004 Rapid-Fire Fulfillment is Jose A. D. Machuca, alongside Kasra Ferdows and Michael A. Lewis, not Machura. Use the accessible textbook reading below rather than relying on the unavailable assigned Zara PDF. Accessible textbook section 3.2 on LibreTexts and a related 3.1 introduction on a teaching Pressbooks site provide a historical case largely citing 2000-2009 sources. Do not relabel those descriptions as current 2026 cycle times, sourcing shares, advertising ratios, margins or employee pay. The University of Minnesota chapter page is only an index, not the full text. Replace the blocked PDF reading with the observed accessible historical section and keep the assigned-source-depth gap explicit.\nThe historical Zara case describes customer/store/POS feedback, small limited runs, nearby production for responsiveness, mixed internal/supplier production, distribution coordination and selected technology embedded in people/process/data. It also describes outsourcing more predictable staples, so it is not evidence all Zara production is nearshore or in-house. Lower mismatch and option value may justify some higher unit costs in that account, but one decisive cause or universally optimal geography is not established. Historical gross-margin figures are not net profit or proof transport/environment/labor risks vanish. Current Inditex 2025 Our Drivers corporate page reports evolving store/online technologies and customer experience; it does not validate the old case's detailed lead-time/sourcing economics. No current brand performance, generic low-cost-country ethics verdict or independent sustainability audit is made here.\nPostponement delays irreversible product differentiation until better demand information is available, where a common platform, modular design or final-stage flexibility makes it feasible. It is not merely delaying shipments or launch. Examine extra process/capacity/lead-time costs, component commonality, shelf life and service commitments. Speed can reduce exposure to forecast errors but cannot guarantee demand knowledge or eliminate all markdowns and stockouts. Track stock availability, forecast error, full-price sell-through, returns, lead-time distributions and cost on consistent definitions; one observed markdown alone does not identify the whole supply chain or causal strategy.\nToy equal-probability demand 100 or 200 units, one season, sale price 40, cost 20, unsold salvage 10, no unmet-demand penalty beyond missed sales, no other costs. A specified fixed plan buys 150: low-demand sales 4000+salvage 500-cost 3000=1500 profit; high-demand sales 6000-cost 3000=3000; expected 2250. A specified responsive plan observes demand perfectly before commitment, can supply exactly needed units on time at cost 24, and has no leftover: profit 1600 or 3200, expected 2400. Difference 150 in this comparison is conditional model profit, not proven value of speed or a real cash forecast. The baseline was not optimized: fixed 200 would give 1000 or 4000, expected 2500, exceeding 2400. Do not select a favored responsive plan against a weak baseline and announce universal superiority. Demand information may be imperfect and capacity expensive in reality. Write a product-level evidence table, at least two plausible strategies, comparable economics and a sensitivity to information/cost/service assumptions. No real supplier contact, sourcing commitment, brand ranking, stock order or spend.","quiz":[{"q":"Fisher's product classification should use?","o":["The retailer's name as one permanent classification for every item.","Luxury branding as sufficient proof of innovative demand.","Demand predictability, life cycle, variety and economics at product/segment level.","A single markdown as proof of a firm-wide supply-chain mismatch."],"a":2},{"q":"Physically efficient versus responsive chains imply?","o":["Select efficiency using unit cost but omit mismatch and service exposure.","Select responsiveness using mismatch savings but omit its added capacity and transport costs.","A fit question with cost/service constraints, not universal superiority of either.","Use one whole-firm classification before checking differences by product and demand phase."],"a":2},{"q":"Market-mediation cost includes?","o":["Supply-demand mismatch such as missed contribution, markdowns and obsolescence.","Only advertising-agency fees paid during a product launch.","Every transport bill regardless of mismatch.","Only the accounting purchase value of items sold at full price."],"a":0},{"q":"What supports the historical Zara mechanism in the accessible case?","o":["A controlled proof that one software purchase caused every margin advantage.","A finding that all products were locally made without external suppliers.","Linked feedback, limited runs, production/logistics choices and technology within an operating system.","A current 2026 audit of exact sourcing shares and replenishment times."],"a":2},{"q":"Can the old case's 15-day figure be presented as current 2026 Zara lead time?","o":["Yes; an accessible textbook URL makes all its statistics current.","No; historical textbook claims need current matching evidence before that use.","Yes; if a current corporate page mentions technology in general.","No; historical cases can never teach mechanisms."],"a":1},{"q":"Postponement means?","o":["Delay irreversible differentiation where feasible while managing lead-time/capacity costs.","Delay every customer shipment until all demand uncertainty disappears.","Use one finished SKU regardless of customer needs.","Move all production nearby without changing when differentiation occurs."],"a":0},{"q":"Fixed 150 plan, low demand 100, sale 40, cost 20, salvage 10. Profit?","o":["2500, omitting the cost of 50 unsold units.","1000, omitting salvage revenue.","1500 currency units.","4000, reporting sales revenue as profit."],"a":2},{"q":"Same fixed 150 plan, high demand 200. Profit?","o":["6000, using sales revenue as profit.","4000, charging only 100 units of purchase cost.","3000 currency units, with 50 units of demand unmet.","5000, assuming 200 sales despite only 150 available."],"a":2},{"q":"Responsive exact-demand plan at cost 24, sale 40, demands 100/200 equally likely. Expected profit?","o":["2250, the specified fixed 150 plan expectation.","2400 currency units under perfect-information/on-time-supply assumptions.","3000, using 20 cost instead of 24.","6000, using expected revenue without purchase cost."],"a":1},{"q":"Fixed 200 plan earns 1000/4000 equally likely. Implication?","o":["Its 2500 expected profit exceeds responsive 2400 here, so baseline optimization matters.","Responsive 2400 is still necessarily superior because it avoids leftovers.","The fixed 150 plan was proven optimal by its smaller batch.","A costlier responsive chain is never worth considering in any other scenario."],"a":0}]},{"t":"The bullwhip effect and supply chain coordination","kick":"Why small ripples become tidal waves upstream","mins":"1.5 hrs","obj":["Distinguish downstream consumption from upstream order variability and examine four bullwhip mechanisms","Match information, policy and logistics changes to a cause while testing costs and incentives","Use a legitimate facilitated game if available, or explicitly labeled synthetic data without claiming a played run"],"vids":[{"id":"sZnxWVKhObo","t":"Why Everything Is On Sale: The Bullwhip Effect | WSJ","by":"The Wall Street Journal"},{"id":"3GXePK2T65M","t":"Bullwhip Effect Explained with a Simple Example","by":"Simple Understanding"}],"reads":[{"t":"MIT Sloan Beer Game Online","u":"https://mitsloan.mit.edu/teaching-resources-library/mit-sloan-beer-game-online","n":"MIT description of a facilitated multiplayer simulation; independent free solo access not established. External access route described; do not register or pay just to complete this lesson."},{"t":"TUM Beer Game (free simulation)","u":"https://beergame.mgt.tum.de/","n":"TUM teaching simulation page says browser participants need a lecturer join code. Its classroom rules differ from other versions; check the lecturer's access instructions before attempting a session."},{"t":"HBS: Web-Based Beer Game exercise","u":"https://www.hbs.edu/faculty/Pages/item.aspx?num=27602","n":"Summary or description; use the written exercise for the worked case. HBS abstract describes an instructor-configurable 90-minute exercise."},{"t":"Lee, Padmanabhan and Whang 1997: The Bullwhip Effect in Supply Chains","u":"https://sloanreview.mit.edu/wp-content/uploads/1997/04/633ecdb037.pdf","n":"Historical company examples illustrate the mechanisms; they are not current company policy."}],"ex":"Synthetic coordination study, not a real order, game registration or instruction to share commercial data. The bullwhip effect concerns amplification of order variability relative to downstream demand, not simply higher mean demand or every upward movement in orders. Compare like items, time buckets, units and periods, account for promotions, lead times, lost sales and lifecycle changes, and distinguish customer consumption from replenishment orders. A variance ratio is one descriptive measure, not causal identification. If the demand variance is zero, an orders-to-demand variance ratio is undefined, not a finite measured multiplier.\nRead Lee, Padmanabhan and Whang's 1997 Sloan Management Review article from the publisher PDF. Its four operational mechanisms are demand forecast updating, order batching, price fluctuations, and rationing/shortage gaming. The companion Management Science abstract was also read, not the full mathematical paper. Forecast updating can amplify orders when each stage treats its immediate customer's replenishments as end demand and adjusts both forecasts and safety stock. This can occur under locally rational policies, not only careless people or an arbitrary extra padding rule. Delays and feedback misunderstandings can also matter. The article's Beer Game narrative is not a theorem guaranteeing amplification under every policy, demand sequence and information configuration.\nBatching can create intermittent upstream orders despite smoother consumption. Lowering order-processing cost, mixed-SKU loads, consolidated delivery and staggered order cycles can support smaller product-specific batches while retaining transport economics. More frequent tiny dedicated trucks can instead raise cost and emissions. Forward buying moves purchases across time because of promotions, not necessarily new final consumption. Stable pricing targets this mechanism, but changes margins and commercial incentives; do not present a historical P&G example as a current company policy or proven universal remedy.\nIf shortage allocations depend on requested quantities, buyers may inflate/duplicate orders, then cancel. Historical sales-based allocation can reduce that incentive but raises questions for new customers, real growth and data accuracy. A supplier's genuine capacity shortage cannot be removed simply by publishing stock figures. Contracts, order commitments, information quality, trust and allocation design affect outcomes. No authority to change contracts or allocations follows from this lesson.\nSharing timely downstream consumption data can reduce repeated signal distortion, but the original article explicitly notes that differences in forecasting methods and buying practices can still create fluctuations even with the same source data. There is no source-grounded single most powerful fix in every chain. VMI gives an agreed supplier replenishment responsibility and relevant sales/inventory visibility; it is not ownership of the customer's retail prices or guaranteed inventory elimination. Define service levels, decision rights, data permissions, exceptions and incentives before an actual agreement. Visibility is not the same as coordination or permission to disclose.\nAssigned access check: MIT's January 2022 page describes facilitated multiplayer, groups of four or more and an external Forio route; it does not establish independent free solo access. TUM's teaching page says browser play requires a join code from a lecturer, four roles and 40 rounds, with its own delay/cost settings. HBS's abstract describes a 90-minute instructor-configurable exercise, not a guaranteed 45-minute solo session. This written exercise does not require joining a game. Check current registration, price and classroom access terms if you choose to play. Read these descriptions, not full instructor materials or the assigned videos. If a legitimate facilitated game is available, record the actual rules, order/shipment delays, inventory, backlog, pipeline and weekly decisions. Do not invent a played result. Otherwise use the following no-login calculation and label it synthetic.\nToy four-week end demand is [8, 12, 8, 12] units and orders are [0, 20, 0, 20]. Both have mean 10 and total 40. Population variance is 4 for demand and 100 for orders, giving ratio 25; using the sample convention consistently also gives ratio 25. This is descriptive amplification in this short example, not proof of batching as the sole cause or evidence of the stockout rate. With constant consumption [10, 10, 10, 10], the ratio denominator is zero. In a separate fixed-rule toy, the book inventory position equals on-hand 20 plus on-order 30 minus backlog 10 = 40; with an order-up-to target of 60, order 20. This formula assumes those are all relevant commitments, no other allocations or pipeline and no capacity/minimum-order constraint. A real policy needs the review horizon, service objective and known in-transit orders; repeatedly ignoring pipeline can create overordering. Submit a causal hypothesis, one matched mitigation, its tradeoff and evidence that could disprove it. No supplier contact, real order or private data sharing.","quiz":[{"q":"A four-week toy has demand [8, 12, 8, 12] and orders [0, 20, 0, 20]. Using population variances, what is the order/demand variance ratio?","o":["1, because equal totals imply equal variability.","5, because the standard-deviation ratio is the variance ratio.","25, although both series have the same mean and total.","0.04, because the demand variance is divided by the order variance."],"a":2},{"q":"Demand is [10, 10, 10, 10] and orders vary. What can this window establish about the order/demand variance ratio?","o":["It is one because mean demand has not changed.","It is zero because every denominator variance becomes the final ratio.","It is undefined because demand variance is zero; describe the series separately.","It equals order variance because a zero denominator is replaced by one."],"a":2},{"q":"All stages receive the same accurate POS data, but use different buying and forecasting policies. Which conclusion follows from the original article?","o":["Order fluctuations may remain, so inspect policies and incentives as well as data.","The common input makes each stage's replenishment sequence identical.","The common input removes shortage-allocation incentives without contract changes.","The common input makes shipment lead times irrelevant to safety-stock decisions."],"a":0},{"q":"A promotion induces a buyer to purchase next month's requirements early, while consumption changes little. Which mechanism is most directly illustrated?","o":["Independent service failures reduce actual delivered quantities.","Rationing based on inflated requests shifts shortage allocations.","Longer production lead time changes physical replenishment delay.","Price-driven forward buying shifts orders across periods."],"a":3},{"q":"A retailer waits to accumulate a full load of one SKU. Which proposed mitigation preserves the possibility of transport consolidation?","o":["Send each unit in a dedicated truck regardless of distance and loading cost.","Combine several SKUs or nearby customers while reducing each SKU's order batch.","Increase the minimum batch of that SKU to reduce its ordering frequency.","Synchronize every customer's large order on the same date."],"a":1},{"q":"Scarce units are allocated in proportion to requested orders. Buyers inflate requests and later cancel. Which response targets that incentive?","o":["Publish total factory capacity and assume the physical shortage has ended.","Count every duplicate request as independent final consumption.","Increase forecast smoothing while leaving the allocation rule and incentives unchanged.","Consider verified historical use and explicit growth exceptions rather than raw requests alone."],"a":3},{"q":"What is established by the currently read TUM game page?","o":["MIT and TUM use identical cost rules and shipment delays.","A free solo session has been completed and its results can be reported.","Every participant can create an anonymous classroom without authorization.","Browser play requires a lecturer join code; an independent solo session is not established."],"a":3},{"q":"What does an agreed VMI arrangement change most directly?","o":["It removes the need to define ownership of stock and operational exceptions.","It transfers retail price-setting authority whenever a supplier sees POS data.","It delegates defined replenishment decisions using relevant downstream data and service rules.","It makes shared-data access sufficient to guarantee zero shortages."],"a":2},{"q":"A stated order-up-to toy has on-hand 20, on-order 30, backlog 10 and target 60, with no other commitments or constraints. How much is ordered?","o":["20, from inventory position 20+30-10=40.","50, from target 60 minus on-hand 20 plus backlog 10 while ignoring pipeline.","10, from adding on-hand 20 to on-order 30 without subtracting backlog 10.","30, from subtracting pipeline 30 while ignoring both on-hand and backlog."],"a":0},{"q":"A game run shows higher upstream order variability. What is the strongest justified interpretation?","o":["It proves players were irrational because rational decisions cannot amplify orders.","It proves delays alone guarantee the pattern under every possible ordering policy.","It demonstrates this run's pattern; identify rules and competing mechanisms before assigning causes.","It proves the same mitigation minimizes both cost and variance in every chain."],"a":2}]},{"t":"Logistics, networks and the box that changed the world","kick":"Containers, cross-docks and the last mile","mins":"1.5 hrs","obj":["Explain containerization as a system of compatible handling and multimodal connections, without a universal causal cost claim","Compare network locations, cross-docking and transport modes using total costs and service constraints","Analyze last-mile and reverse-flow unit economics with explicit successful-delivery and recovery denominators"],"vids":[{"id":"FN3VFgG922A","t":"Meet the enormous boats that carry your stuff","by":"Vox"}],"reads":[{"t":"Levinson: The Now-Ubiquitous Shipping Container Was an Idea Before Its Time","u":"https://www.smithsonianmag.com/innovation/shipping-container-idea-before-time-180963730/","n":"2017 essay by the author of The Box. It is a historical account, not the full book or a current freight-cost estimate."},{"t":"Zara historical systems textbook: Don't Guess, Gather Data","u":"https://biz.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Management/Book%3A_Information_Systems__A_Managers_Guide_to_Harnessing_Technology/03%3A_Zara-_Fast_Fashion_from_Savvy_Systems/3.02%3A_Dont_Guess_Gather_Data","n":"Historical textbook section, not current routing or market-share data. Test the proposed operating mechanism rather than assuming a historical network is unchanged."},{"t":"MIT CTL via OCW: D-Lab Supply Chains readings","u":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-772j-d-lab-supply-chains-fall-2014/","n":"Summary or description; use the written exercise for the worked case."},{"t":"Cross-docking: State of the art","u":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0305048312000060","n":"Summary or description; use the written exercise for the worked case."},{"t":"ITF 2024: The Final Frontier of Urban Logistics","u":"https://www.itf-oecd.org/sites/default/files/docs/final-frontier-urban-logistics.pdf","n":"Selected executive-summary/recommendation and opening definition passages read, not complete simulations, figures or legal advice."}],"ex":"Synthetic logistics design, not a freight booking, live quotation or current company logistics audit. Separate the factory gate, loading, inland movement, port handling, ocean/air leg, destination handling, customs, storage and final delivery. Mark where ownership, custody, information and physical units change. A map based on a product label is a hypothesis until shipment/production evidence identifies the actual route. The longest distance need not be the highest cost leg, and a cheaper leg does not prove a cheaper whole network.\nThe assigned SuperSummary page currently returns a plot summary and says a full study guide is not yet available, contrary to the previous generic paid-study-guide note. This is a secondary summary, not a reading of Levinson's complete book. Replace the lead reading with the observed 2017 Smithsonian essay written by Marc Levinson, author of The Box. Its historical account describes handling/transshipment costs, compatible corner fittings, cranes, ships, ports, inland connections, regulation, labor and commercial adoption. The value came from a system that could move compatible loaded boxes across modes with fewer separate cargo handling operations, not simply faster ships. Stacking and space utilization also matter. Do not turn an individual historical handling-cost comparison into a universal tenfold reduction in door-to-door freight cost, or claim containers alone caused globalization. Worker displacement and required capital are part of the history. Standardization does not imply that every cargo type or modern oversized container fits every route.\nThe UNCTAD 2004 paper's selected logistics-integration and multimodal passages treat transportation, inventory, production and service as connected tradeoffs. They support a systems lens, not current freight prices or a complete reading of its full report. MIT D-Lab's assigned course page gives a design/operations syllabus, not a complete network-design proof. Use the accessible historical textbook section rather than the unavailable assigned Zara PDF; it is not current claims that all stock goes through Spain or that airfreight always has a proven margin advantage. No current brand logistics verdict is scored.\nCross-docking transfers goods from inbound to outbound flows with little or no storage, often sorting and consolidating by destination. It does not mean an X-shaped dock or the elimination of all inspection, staging or material handling. The 2012 Omega review's abstract and introduction explain the distinction from put-away/storage/picking, the need to synchronize inbound and outbound operations, and that exact dwell-time cutoffs vary. The full review and underlying studies were not independently read. DHL's 2023 operational article also describes coordination demands and fragile/specialized-goods limitations; its benefit claims are operator commentary, not a measured guarantee for your network. Little buffer can make a late inbound load miss an outbound departure. Compare a mixed design instead of assuming every SKU must cross-dock.\nCentralization can pool inventory, consolidate inbound transport and reduce some facility costs, while changing outbound distance, service, congestion and concentration risk. It does not guarantee lower total inventory or facility cost, or universally increase every customer's distance. Site locations, capacity, demand correlation, product rules and service promises decide the result. A decentralized network can improve some delivery times but duplicate stock/cost. Do not optimize only the warehouse rent and ignore transport or service.\nToy annual central design has facility 300, transport 500 and inventory 200 =1000 currency units. Decentralized design has 450+350+280=1080. On only these modeled costs, central is 80 cheaper. The example does not price lateness, disruption, emissions, implementation or differences in service; the lower total is not an approved location decision. A decentralized option can still be chosen if a stated service constraint makes central infeasible.\nMode selection compares door-to-door time, variability, capacity, handling, inventory financing, damage, cold chain and service constraints, not a permanent air-versus-sea ranking. Toy one shipment has 10000 units, value 50 each, annual inventory-capital rate 20%, air saves 15 days and adds 0.80 freight per unit. On a simple 365-day interest basis, time-related capital saving is 10000*50*0.20*15/365 =4109.59, less than the 8000 air premium. On these inputs alone, air costs 3890.41 more. If independently estimated additional avoided lost contribution is 5000 without double counting the capital effect, combined modeled benefit 9109.59 exceeds premium by 1109.59. That sensitivity is not proof of demand, a cash saving already realized or a guaranteed Zara result. Customs, schedules and damage could reverse it.\nITF's 2024 last-metres report executive summary and opening definitions describe the final movement to recipients, failed attempts, curb access, building access and time at doors. Its modeling is not a universal last-mile cost share. Last-mile costs vary with density, delivery windows, parcel mix, successful stops, access and network design; the final leg does not always eat all profit. A parcel locker can reduce delivery attempts but transfer travel and accessibility burdens to recipients. Smaller vehicles/micro-depots can add transshipment and facility costs; drones/robots do not remove the need for secure handoff. No current legality or deployment permission is inferred from an older policy report.\nToy route driver cost 30/hour for 2 hours plus vehicle cost 20 gives 80. At 40 successful deliveries it is 2 per delivery; at 32 successful deliveries with the same route cost it is 2.50. This excludes repeated attempts, claims, overhead and recipient travel, so it is not a live unit-economics quote. Separate attempts, parcels, stops and successful deliveries.\nReverse logistics includes returns, recovery, repair, recycling and disposal, not always tiny unpredictable parcels. Flows may be consolidated or planned; quality/condition uncertainty and disposition decisions add work. Toy recovered resale value 25 minus reverse freight 8, inspection 4 and refurbishment 5 leaves 8 incremental recovery value before other effects. Original sale refunds, original purchase cost and alternative disposal value are not included; do not call 8 total transaction profit. Submit a route map with known versus inferred edges, compare two network designs on the same horizon, and identify missing cost/service evidence. No actual carrier contact, route change, purchase or shipping claim.","quiz":[{"q":"What does Levinson's observed historical essay support about containerization?","o":["Faster ship propulsion alone established the whole door-to-door cost reduction.","Compatible boxes plus handling, equipment and network changes helped reduce transfer friction.","One handling comparison fixes the cost saving on every future international shipment.","Container compatibility eliminated the need for ports, labor agreements and inland links."],"a":1},{"q":"A receiving team sorts goods directly into outbound loads with brief staging. Which description is most accurate?","o":["It can be cross-docking; little storage does not require zero handling or zero waiting.","It cannot be cross-docking unless every dock building is X-shaped.","It is ordinary long-term warehousing whenever any package waits briefly.","It establishes that inbound timing can be ignored once destinations are labelled."],"a":0},{"q":"A late inbound truck causes missed outbound departures in a cross-dock. Which design issue is most directly exposed?","o":["Tight coupling and limited buffers make synchronized schedules and exceptions important.","The loss establishes that removing every inspection will restore synchronization.","The loss proves cross-docking cannot consolidate shipments from different suppliers.","The loss establishes that conventional storage can never serve these customers."],"a":0},{"q":"Toy annual costs are central 300+500+200 and decentralized 450+350+280. What follows on these cost categories alone?","o":["Central totals 1000, which is 80 less; unpriced service and risk still matter.","Decentralized totals 1080, which is 80 less because outbound transport is lower.","Central saves 200 because only the facility-cost difference belongs in the comparison.","Both total 1000 because inventory must be excluded whenever it is already owned."],"a":0},{"q":"A central option is cheaper but misses an explicit mandatory delivery-time requirement. How should that affect this comparison?","o":["Treat lower rent as evidence that the mandatory delivery time is achievable.","Ignore the service requirement because the transport line is included in the cost table.","Treat the service requirement as a feasibility constraint rather than silently choosing the cheaper option.","Subtract the unmet service requirement from inventory units to balance the table."],"a":2},{"q":"Air adds 8000 freight and saves 15 days of capital on 10000 units valued 50 at 20% annual, 365-day basis. Ignoring other benefits, what is the net cost effect?","o":["Air saves about 4109.59 when the 8000 freight premium is omitted from the comparison.","Air saves about 3890.41 because every reduction in transit time is net profit.","Air saves 8000 because the freight premium is an avoided inventory cost.","Air costs about 3890.41 more after about 4109.59 capital saving."],"a":3},{"q":"In that same air toy, an additional non-overlapping avoided contribution loss is independently estimated at 5000. What changes?","o":["Capital saving becomes 9109.59 because contribution loss is part of the interest formula.","Modeled total benefit becomes 9109.59, exceeding 8000 premium by 1109.59, conditional on that estimate.","Net benefit becomes 5000 because the 8000 freight premium is now a sunk cost.","The estimate proves every future air shipment will earn 1109.59 in cash."],"a":1},{"q":"A route costs 80 and achieves 32 successful deliveries rather than 40, with unchanged route cost. What is the modeled cost per successful delivery?","o":["2.50 instead of 2.00, before omitted retry and overhead effects.","2.00 in both cases because total route cost has not changed.","0.40 because deliveries are divided by route cost.","3.20 because all route costs vary directly with successful-delivery count."],"a":0},{"q":"What does a locker solution require in a last-metres comparison?","o":["Treat a locker as evidence that building access and mobility needs no longer matter.","Count failed-attempt savings but assume recipient travel is automatically zero.","Count operator benefits and recipient travel, access and secure-handoff costs or constraints.","Compare only locker acquisition price because recurring collection behavior cannot affect service."],"a":2},{"q":"Toy returned goods recover 25 resale value with reverse freight 8, inspection 4 and refurbishment 5. What does the resulting 8 represent?","o":["A universal recovery margin that can be used for every return condition.","Complete profit on the original sale after all refunds and acquisition costs.","A guaranteed cash saving equal to the original retail price.","Incremental recovery value before refunds, original cost and other omitted alternatives."],"a":3}]},{"t":"Global sourcing, risk and resilience","kick":"Efficient until it isn't","mins":"1.5 hrs","obj":["Separate quotation, landed-cost boundaries and wider risk-adjusted economic costs","Map critical and shared supply nodes and compare scenario-dependent recovery and survival","Interpret historical resilience and chip-shortage evidence without universal, current-law or current-inventory claims"],"vids":[{"id":"LR3obYidqMs","t":"Placing a premium on resilience: How businesses can thrive through volatility","by":"McKinsey & Company"},{"id":"hiizMrhSjT0","t":"Supply Chain Resilience Strategies Designed for the Age of Disruption","by":"Gartner"}],"reads":[{"t":"McKinsey Global Institute: Risk, resilience and rebalancing in global value chains","u":"https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/operations/our-insights/risk-resilience-and-rebalancing-in-global-value-chains","n":"Read the historical risk framework and evaluate its assumptions. Linked PDFs or studies may have separate access terms."},{"t":"McKinsey: A practical approach to supply-chain risk management (article; official PDF download on the page)","u":"https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/operations/our-insights/a-practical-approach-to-supply-chain-risk-management","n":"Read the historical risk framework and evaluate its assumptions. Linked PDFs or studies may have separate access terms."},{"t":"McKinsey: Future-proofing the supply chain","u":"https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/operations/our-insights/future-proofing-the-supply-chain","n":"Read the historical risk framework and evaluate its assumptions. Linked PDFs or studies may have separate access terms."},{"t":"MIT: Companies use research to identify supply-chain risks","u":"https://news.mit.edu/2022/companies-use-mit-research-identify-respond-supply-chain-risks-0615","n":"June 2022 institutional account; TTR/TTS and hidden/common risks, not full REI method replication."},{"t":"US Commerce 2022 semiconductor supply-chain RFI results","u":"https://www.commerce.gov/news/blog/2022/01/results-semiconductor-supply-chain-request-information","n":"Historical January 2022 primary main text read. Inventory statistic is selected difficult-to-acquire products/responding buyers 2019 vs 2021, not current global inventory."}],"ex":"Synthetic sourcing/risk assessment, not supplier outreach, procurement approval or instruction to buy inventory. Separate a supplier quotation, landed cost and wider total economic cost. Define the accounting boundary explicitly: factory price, freight, duties, insurance and handling may sit in a landed-cost schedule, while quality losses, qualification, financing, disruption and disposal can belong in a wider decision model depending on accounting practice. Do not assume every jurisdiction or company defines landed cost identically. Toy quotation 10 plus freight 2, duty 1, insurance 0.50 and handling 0.50 totals 14 per unit. An additional modeled expected quality loss 1 gives 15 relevant economic cost, not a claim about tax capitalization or current customs law.\nRisk is not just low price, foreign location or the number of suppliers. Map critical products, components, nodes, capacity, routes and common subtiers; a low-spend part can stop a high-value product. A single source can create dependence but does not logically maximize every risk in every chain. Two suppliers can share one fab, power grid, port, raw-material plant or cyber system. Geographic proximity/reshoring alone does not establish resilience. Assess substitution, qualification time, correlated failures, capacity availability, concentration and the cash/complexity costs of alternatives. Do not disclose private BOMs, supplier information or personal data to a mapping tool without permission.\nRead the main text of McKinsey's 2019 practical risk article. It distinguishes known and unknown risks and gives four steps for known risks: identify/document, build a framework including impact/likelihood/preparedness, monitor, and institute governance/review. Mapping is a useful starting activity, not a proof all risks are knowable or an excuse to postpone urgent containment. Risk registers need owners, decisions, review dates and honest unknowns. Ordinal scores are prioritization devices, not calibrated probabilities. A risk-aware reporting culture and defensive layers complement dashboards; no framework guarantees prevention.\nThe August 2020 McKinsey Global Institute web report is historical analysis; distinguish the published summary from the underlying model and studies. Its 3.7-year disruption average refers to expert reports from four industries about material disruptions lasting a month or longer, not the arrival schedule for a particular firm in 2026. Financial scenarios use hypothetical companies based on public-company data and a 100-day shutdown. The web prose says almost 45% of a year's profits across a decade, while one exhibit alternative text says 42% of a year's EBITDA. Preserve those scope/wording differences rather than ask learners to force one precise headline percentage. A decade's expected cumulative losses relative to one year's earnings are not an annual 42% loss, a measured current margin or an inevitable realized loss. Historical potential trade-relocation estimates were scenarios, explicitly not forecasts of actual movement. No new operative tariff, export rule or current-law claim is scored here.\nThe assigned June 2022 future-proofing article adds resilience, agility and sustainability to cost/capital, quality and service. Its examples and survey percentages are historical descriptions, not current performance verification or causal proof. The article and its linked studies may have different access terms. Do not substitute reader access claims for teaching depth. Use the written references for this exercise; the recording is a separate study resource.\nMIT's June 2022 account of Simchi-Levi's Risk Exposure Index defines time-to-recover(TTR) as time for a node to return to full function and time-to-survive(TTS) as how long the chain can match supply to demand after disruption. Both depend on a specified scenario, inventories, pipeline, demand, substitutions and available capacity. They are not the same as an insurance payout date or time to first partial restart. A gap TTR>TTS flags exposure under that model; multiplying a gap by demand is only valid under additional simple assumptions. The institutional article reports implementations, not an independent replication or complete REI methodology audit.\nToy critical part has TTR 20 days and TTS 12 days, constant demand 100 units/day, no substitutes/replenishment during disruption, no ramp-up and immediate full restoration on day 20. Uncovered interval is 8 days or 800 units of modeled unmet supply. An extra 400 usable units at the same time/location could extend survival by 4 days to 16, leaving 4 days/400 units exposed. This is not a forecast that all unmet units become lost sales, or authorization to buy 400 parts. Recovery distributions, backlogs, demand changes and expiry can change the result.\nDual sourcing is not justified by comparing total disruption loss with premium while pretending the alternative removes all loss. Use incremental expected avoided loss after residual/correlated risk, qualification/coordination cost, capacity and service constraints. Toy one-year baseline disruptive-event probability 0.10 and loss 100000 gives expected loss 10000. A qualified alternative reduces modeled probability to 0.03 with the same loss severity and costs 6000 additional for the same year; residual expected loss 3000, avoided loss 7000 and net modeled benefit 1000. If common-subtier exposure means residual probability 0.08 instead, residual loss 8000, avoided loss 2000 and net benefit minus 4000. These event probabilities are assumptions, not observed industry rates; a risk-averse organization or mandatory service/safety condition may choose differently than a risk-neutral expectation calculation. Do not sum correlated scenario losses without defining overlap.\nThe chip-shortage case is historical, not a current 2026 inventory audit. Read US Commerce's January 25, 2022 RFI account: it describes semiconductor-intensive demand growth, pandemic demand shifts and production disruptions including fires, weather/energy and shutdowns, alongside wafer-fab, material and assembly/test/packaging constraints. Acute bottlenecks included legacy logic, analog and optoelectronics, not only a few cutting-edge fabs. Chips and qualification paths are not freely interchangeable. Its 40 days in 2019 to less than 5 in 2021 inventory statistic concerns difficult-to-acquire products highlighted by responding buyers; it is not the median of every chip worldwide. The article's policy advocacy is historical, not a current law or instruction to endorse a policy. Full raw confidential RFI responses and the underlying statistical studies and figures require separate evaluation.\nLean/JIT does not mean zero inventory or an assumption that shocks never occur. Reliably replenishing normal flow and designing targeted buffers/alternatives can coexist; insurance, spare capacity, product redesign, postponement, supplier development and recovery planning have different failure modes. Inventory can expire, consume cash and fail to help when distribution itself is shut. A common component can enable substitution but also become a common point of failure. Write a small risk register for a public, non-sensitive product example, separate evidence from estimates, compare two mitigations on the same horizon and show a correlated-failure sensitivity. No purchasing, contracts, supplier commitment, staffing, account creation or change to real safety stock.","quiz":[{"q":"A defined cost schedule has factory 10, freight 2, duty 1, insurance 0.50 and handling 0.50. A separate expected quality-loss allowance is 1. What is the correct comparison?","o":["Both figures are 15 because every quality-loss allowance is legally part of landed cost.","The stated landed schedule totals 14; adding the separate allowance gives 15 on the wider decision basis.","The relevant total is 10 because all costs outside the factory price are sunk.","The landed schedule totals 13 because insurance and handling cannot be included."],"a":1},{"q":"Two qualified suppliers use the same critical sub-tier plant. What does the supplier count establish?","o":["It makes mapping beyond tier-one irrelevant because contracts differ.","It proves the chance of simultaneous failure is the product of independent probabilities.","It proves the second supplier reduces residual disruption loss to zero.","It does not establish independent resilience; the common node may still stop both."],"a":3},{"q":"How should the 2020 MGI 3.7-year statement be taught?","o":["As a 2026 measured probability that every single supplier fails each year.","As a guaranteed maximum time until every firm suffers its next shutdown.","As a historical expert-reported cross-industry average with defined disruption duration, not a firm-specific clock.","As the expected recovery time of any interrupted semiconductor factory."],"a":2},{"q":"A report models decade-long cumulative disruption loss relative to one year's earnings. Which interpretation preserves that denominator?","o":["Treat the modeled expectation as a realized loss guaranteed for every company.","Apply that percentage to each year's revenue because earnings and revenue share a denominator.","Keep the decade horizon and one-year earnings reference; do not relabel it as an annual loss rate.","Treat exhibit and prose percentage wording as identical without checking metric definitions."],"a":2},{"q":"A toy has TTR 20 days, TTS 12 days, constant 100 units/day, no substitutes and immediate full restoration. What is the modeled uncovered supply?","o":["8 days or 800 units, not automatically 800 permanently lost sales.","12 days or 1200 units, because survival time is the uncovered interval.","20 days or 2000 units, because all inventory ceases to exist after disruption.","32 days or 3200 units, because recovery and survival durations are added."],"a":0},{"q":"In that same toy, 400 extra usable units are available immediately with no other changes. What follows?","o":["TTR falls to 16 days because inventory repairs the interrupted node.","TTS extends to 16 days and modeled exposure falls to 4 days/400 units.","TTS extends to 20 days because any buffer eliminates the full exposure.","Exposure rises to 1200 units because added inventory is counted as additional demand."],"a":1},{"q":"A one-year toy has baseline probability 0.10, loss 100000; alternative probability 0.03, same severity and 6000 premium. What is incremental modeled net benefit?","o":["1000, from 7000 avoided expected loss minus 6000 premium.","4000, from 10000 baseline expected loss minus 6000 while ignoring residual risk.","7000, because the premium does not belong in the incremental comparison.","97000, because alternative probability is subtracted directly from loss."],"a":0},{"q":"If shared exposure instead leaves alternative probability 0.08 with the same 100000 loss and 6000 premium, what happens to that risk-neutral toy?","o":["Net benefit becomes 14000 by adding baseline and residual loss before premium.","Net benefit stays 1000 because two suppliers guarantee the earlier residual probability.","Net benefit becomes 2000 because the qualification premium is a separate horizon.","Net benefit becomes minus 4000 because only 2000 of expected loss is avoided."],"a":3},{"q":"Which statement preserves the scope of US Commerce's January 2022 chip-shortage evidence?","o":["It identified only advanced-node fabs as the sole root cause for every automotive shortage.","It described multiple demand/supply constraints, including legacy/analog bottlenecks, and selected responding buyers' inventories.","It measured the 2026 global inventory of every chip as less than five days.","It established that any available chip could replace any missing chip without qualification."],"a":1},{"q":"A risk register assigns ordinal impact, likelihood and preparedness scores. Which follow-up is justified?","o":["Multiply any two ordinal labels and report the result as a measured annual failure probability.","Use scores to prioritize with owners/reviews, and separately validate probabilities, common causes and model limits.","Treat a completed map as evidence that unknown risks can no longer occur.","Choose solely by annual supplier spend because low-cost components cannot stop production."],"a":1}]},{"t":"Operations in India: dabbawalas, jugaad and scale","kick":"World-class ops at Indian prices","mins":"1.5 hrs","obj":["Analyze historical dabbawala service-design descriptions without asserting a current certified error rate","Distinguish boxes, transactions, defects and opportunities, and test an invented accessible handoff code","Separate historical GST logistics reporting from current-law and firm-specific network decisions"],"vids":[{"id":"N25inoCea24","t":"TEDxSSN - Dr. Pawan Agrawal - Mumbai Dabbawalas","by":"TEDx Talks"},{"id":"dX-0el2wuEU","t":"The World's Best Delivery Service? Lunch in Mumbai","by":"Great Big Story"}],"reads":[{"t":"HBS case: The Dabbawala System - On-Time Delivery, Every Time","u":"https://www.hbs.edu/faculty/Pages/item.aspx?num=38410","n":"HBS case abstract. Its Six-Sigma-equivalent description is attributed to the case, not an independently established error rate; the full case and teaching note have separate access terms."},{"t":"Mumbai's Models of Service Excellence: author-article description","u":"https://store.hbr.org/product/mumbai-s-models-of-service-excellence/R1211K?sku=R1211K-PDF-ENG","n":"2012 product description covering cross-training, buffers, coding and organization. The full article is a separate publication."},{"t":"BBC: The network that feeds Mumbai","u":"https://www.bbc.com/future/article/20170114-the-125-year-old-network-that-keeps-mumbai-going","n":"Historical BBC feature. Its prices, volumes, partnerships and error-rate descriptions are not current 2026 operations data."},{"t":"PIB July 3, 2017: check-post implementation report","u":"https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=1494418&lang=2&reg=48","n":"Historical July 2017 report: twenty-two check posts abolished and action on eight others in progress. This is not current transport or GST law; consult current operative sources for a real route."}],"ex":"Synthetic service-design and measurement study, not a current dabbawala operating audit, delivery booking or authority to change a work process. The assigned HBS case abstract calls the system Six-Sigma-equivalent or better, but an abstract is not the underlying measurement record or a certificate. Its teaching-note abstract describes approximately 130000 lunchboxes and more than 260000 transactions per day, whereas the BBC's historical feature describes roughly 200000 customers. Different dates, definitions and sources must not be merged into a current precise volume or error rate. The phrase one mistake in six million is not established here as an independently measured rate. The HBS abstract is an overview; the full case and teaching note are separate publications. Do not falsely retract the existence of the abstract's claim; attribute it and keep validation separate.\nRead the HBR store's 2012 author-article product description and the BBC's historical feature. The former describes a combined system of organization, management, process and culture: small autonomous units, train-linked rhythm, simple codes, cross-training and extra workers as buffer capacity. The latter describes local collection areas, repeated sorting, alphanumeric markings, standby workers and time buffers. These accounts support hypotheses about coordination and service design, not a causal experiment proving any one feature creates the reported accuracy. The article's figures, prices, staffing, partnerships and no-cell-phone descriptions are historical, not verified 2026 facts. Low digital intensity is not literally no technology: trains, bicycles, containers, clocks and the coding system are technologies. Low formal literacy is not zero training or zero expertise. Do not claim a zero-literacy barrier or present all workers as interchangeable/unskilled.\nUse a published code example only if its markings and meaning are actually legible and sourced; a description alone does not make the full code diagram legible or establish how it works. Instead design an explicitly invented three-field routing label: collection zone, transfer route and destination stop. Give it a written legend, redundant text/shape cues rather than color alone, a unique item identifier and a clear exception path for an unreadable or duplicate label. An invented label is not the authentic Mumbai code. Test interpretation with people unfamiliar with the label before assuming it works, include language/accessibility needs, and use fictitious locations instead of personal addresses. Standardized handoffs can prevent specific ambiguous-transfer errors, but the statement most errors occur at interfaces needs data for the process in question. Skills, infrastructure, buffers and recovery remain important.\nMeasurement needs the unit, opportunity definition, failure criteria, time window, sampling and completeness. Distinguish a lunchbox, an outward/return transaction, a handoff, a defect and a defective box. A late delivery and a misroute can both affect one box. A denominator of deliveries cannot be silently substituted for opportunities. Toy only: 100000 boxes each have five predefined inspected opportunities, giving 500000 opportunities; ten recorded defects give 20 defects per million opportunities. If those defects occur in eight distinct boxes, defective-box rate is 8/100000*1000000 =80 per million boxes. Neither is an estimate of the real dabbawala network, and neither alone establishes a sigma-level certification or a comparison with another service's differently defined errors. Zero observed errors in a small sample does not prove a zero failure probability or an extreme one-in-millions rate. Ask what failures were not detected or recorded.\nToy fixed departure is 12:30, handoff sorting takes ten minutes and a train arrives at 12:15, leaving five minutes of slack before departure. If the train arrives at 12:23, sorting finishes at 12:33, so this load misses that departure by three minutes unless a feasible, authorized alternative changes the scenario. Do not pretend slack prevents every disruption or solve it by instructing unsafe rushing. Test standby capacity, alternative connections, earlier cutoffs and communication against service and cost. No real dispatch change is authorized.\nJugaad can describe improvisation under constraints; it is not a formal quality standard or a universal good/bad operating model. Treat a local workaround as a hypothesis: state the failure mode, safety/compliance needs, test conditions and criteria for keeping, redesigning or retiring it. A successful low-risk prototype does not prove suitability for regulated or safety-critical production. No ad hoc food-safety, medical, labor or transport exception is granted by this lesson.\nFor India logistics context, the July 3, 2017 PIB release reports 22 states/territories had abolished check posts and lists eight where abolition was in process. The July 26 historical transport release describes reduced tax-related border friction and projected warehousing/freight benefits. A government communication is evidence of what was reported/projected then, not a full operative legal analysis, measured causal effect or proof that all border, police, safety, customs and documentation checks disappeared. Its proposed e-way-bill threshold, historical rates and forecast cost savings are not taught as current law. The phrase centralized warehouses became rational for the first time is too strong: pre-existing service, transport, geography and capital tradeoffs still matter, and removing a tax-driven warehouse incentive does not prove one optimal national warehouse. Compare feasible networks on total costs and service as in D 260, not a one-factor tax story. No current GST compliance instruction is scored.\nIndia e-commerce is not one uniform last-mile market. Address quality, payment mode, returns, density, accessibility, weather and service promises are dimensions to measure for a chosen segment, not inherent flaws of every Indian customer or proof all networks need cash on delivery. Historical startup anecdotes are not current coverage, performance or partnership verification. The assigned MIT Zara handle did not return readable content in this check; its title is tangential to this India-service module and does not establish a dabbawala measurement. Replace that reading with the observed relevant HBR/BBC material, keep the original-source gap explicit and do not claim the thesis was read. Submit an invented routing label, a handoff/exception table, the two toy rate calculations and one evidence gap. No real user addresses, delivery order, worker evaluation or current-law verdict.","quiz":[{"q":"The HBS abstract calls the dabbawala system Six-Sigma-equivalent but provides no underlying error log. What can this review establish?","o":["The wording proves that every possible handoff has the same measured failure probability.","The claim alone supplies the exact present-day numerator and denominator.","The abstract makes that claim; independent rate/certification validation still needs measurement evidence.","The wording proves the network uses a particular formal certification process."],"a":2},{"q":"A toy has 100000 boxes, five predefined inspected opportunities each and ten defects. What is its DPMO?","o":["100, using ten defects over 100000 boxes and labeling it opportunity rate.","20, using ten defects over 500000 opportunities.","2, using five million opportunities not present in the scenario.","50, using 200000 opportunities instead of the stated 500000."],"a":1},{"q":"Those ten defects affected eight distinct boxes among 100000. What is the defective-box rate per million boxes?","o":["80, which differs from the 20 DPMO opportunity measure.","20, because box and opportunity denominators are identical.","100, because every defect must affect a different box.","8, because the observed defective count already is per million."],"a":0},{"q":"A small sample has no recorded delivery errors. Which conclusion is justified?","o":["The sample proves a one-in-six-million rate regardless of its size.","The population failure probability is exactly zero because the observed count is zero.","No errors were recorded in that sample; detection and sample size limit stronger claims.","The sample proves no errors were possible outside the recorded period."],"a":2},{"q":"Which interpretation fits the read historical dabbawala descriptions?","o":["Low formal schooling means the service can be replicated without training or local knowledge.","Simple codes, trained routines, transport infrastructure and buffers work together as a design hypothesis.","The absence of advanced IT makes trains, clocks and coding irrelevant to reliability.","A reported accurate service establishes that extra workers and time buffers are unnecessary."],"a":1},{"q":"An invented route label uses color alone for destination. Which next step best tests handoff reliability?","o":["Remove the item identifier so duplicate labels cannot be detected.","Declare it authentic dabbawala coding because it uses colored markings.","Treat successful interpretation by its designer as validation for every worker.","Add redundant legible cues and an exception route, then test with unfamiliar users and accessibility needs."],"a":3},{"q":"A 12:23 arrival needs ten minutes of sorting before a fixed 12:30 departure. Under these toy rules, what happens?","o":["Sorting finishes at 12:28 because ten minutes of sorting count only during slack.","Sorting finishes at 12:30 because the earlier five-minute slack is permanent.","Sorting finishes at 12:33, missing that departure by three minutes.","The departure moves automatically to 12:33 because an upstream arrival was late."],"a":2},{"q":"What does the July 3, 2017 PIB communication establish within this review?","o":["It proves the later projected cost savings were already measured causal outcomes.","It proves all Indian border, safety and customs checks ceased permanently on that date.","It proves every warehouse became legally required to consolidate into one national site.","It reported 22 had abolished check posts and eight were in process at that time, not all current transport-check rules."],"a":3},{"q":"A tax-related reason for several warehouses weakens. Which network conclusion is justified?","o":["Re-evaluate feasible locations using service, transport, stock and transition costs rather than presuming one winner.","Choose one central warehouse before checking delivery times because tax reform fixes every cost tradeoff.","Keep the old network because a changed tax incentive cannot affect physical operations.","Treat the historical national freight forecast as the exact savings for this particular firm."],"a":0},{"q":"A low-cost workaround succeeded in one low-risk pilot. What follows for using it at scale?","o":["The successful pilot is a formal quality certification for any regulated process.","Check failure modes, safety constraints and repeatable performance before deciding whether to standardize it.","The workaround must remain permanent because redesign would contradict frugality.","The workaround can bypass compliance whenever the acquisition price is lower."],"a":1}]},{"t":"Service operations and the software-age ops stack","kick":"Queues you cannot see, from hospitals to SRE","mins":"1.5 hrs","obj":["Compare service-capacity and waiting models without inferring universal outcomes from average utilization","Specify SLIs, SLOs and measurement windows, and calculate request/time error budgets on separate bases","Distinguish toil, engineering and overhead, and analyze coordinated incidents with reviewed private-data-safe follow-up"],"vids":[{"id":"uTEL8Ff1Zvk","t":"What's the Difference Between DevOps and SRE? (class SRE implements DevOps)","by":"Google Cloud Tech"},{"id":"tEylFyxbDLE","t":"SLIs, SLOs, SLAs, oh my! (class SRE implements DevOps)","by":"Google Cloud Tech"}],"reads":[{"t":"Google SRE: Embracing Risk","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/embracing-risk/","n":"Published Google risk-management practices. Choose a reliability target based on service impact and cost; the chapter does not state the current SLA of a named live service."},{"t":"Google SRE: Service Level Objectives","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/","n":"Distinguish the indicator, objective and agreement, and define the measurement window and eligible events. A book example is not a current product entitlement."},{"t":"Google SRE: Eliminating Toil","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/eliminating-toil/","n":"Study toil as operational work that is manual, repetitive and scales with service growth. The described 50% goal is not a universal daily split or current Google staffing measurement."},{"t":"Google SRE: Managing Incidents","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/managing-incidents/","n":"Compare incident roles, communication, mitigation and recovery using the historical case. The exercise does not authorize action on a real live service."},{"t":"Google SRE: Postmortem Culture","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/","n":"Record contributing causes, protect sensitive information and assign concrete actions. A blameless learning culture is not proof that every proposed benefit or fix will occur."},{"t":"Google SRE workbook: Example Error Budget Policy","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/error-budget-policy/","n":"Published 2018 example policy, including exceptions, causes and escalation. Adapt it to the service and authority; it is not a universal or current live policy."}],"ex":"Synthetic service/operations analysis, not a change to a production service, medical triage, real SLA, release policy or staffing plan. The assigned SRE book table of contents, resources hub and Part III introduction are navigation/overview, not a full reading of the book or every practice. Read the actual chapters Embracing Risk, Service Level Objectives, Eliminating Toil, Managing Incidents and Postmortem Culture, plus the workbook's Example Error Budget Policy. These are published Google practices/examples, not verified current configuration of any named live service or a universal rule every organization must adopt. Assigned the linked videos, other chapters and figures are separate optional study.\nDefine the flow unit, arrival/service processes, available capacity, queue discipline, observation period and objective before calculating utilization. Service capacity can perish with time, but it is too broad to say services cannot inventory anything: appointment slots, digital artifacts, supplies and backlogs are different forms of stored resources/work. Idle capacity can absorb variability but is not literally interchangeable with finished-goods stock. You cannot infer an entire queue model from one person's wait or a single average occupancy number. Waiting, unmet demand, abandonment, cancellations, priority and finite buffers require explicit modeling.\nToy single-server M/M/1 has stationary Poisson arrivals 19/hour, independent exponential service with rate 20/hour, an unlimited queue, no abandonment and utilization 0.95. It gives mean system time 1/(20-19)=1 hour, service time 1/20 hour=3 minutes and mean queue wait 57 minutes. This is not a prediction that every hospital at 95% occupancy must have these waits or crises. A deterministic comparison with arrivals evenly spaced 60/19 minutes apart and fixed three-minute services can have zero waiting when aligned initially, despite the same average utilization. Real hospital beds, staff, admissions, acuity and discharge constraints are not interchangeable single-server requests. No clinical recommendation follows. Appointment schedules may smooth arrivals, but bunching, no-shows, walk-ins and service-time variation can defeat that intention. Priority/triage changes who waits and receives resources, not automatically total demand or a clinical outcome; use an invented non-medical service example and do not prescribe real patient triage.\nAn SLI is a carefully defined quantitative measure of service; an SLO is its target/range; an SLA is an agreement with consequences. Do not label every missed internal target a contractual penalty. Choose metrics from user needs, not only what is easy to collect. Specify the population, valid events, measurement point, threshold, window, aggregation and exclusions before counting. Average latency can hide long tails or subgroup failures, and server success can miss client failures. Correctness, privacy and data durability matter in addition to HTTP success. Published historical Google product targets/examples are not current account entitlements or live SLAs.\nAn error budget is the allowable unreliability implied by the chosen SLO over its defined window and measurement basis, not money, permission for negligent harm or a literal stockpile that carries into the next window. It supports an agreed risk/release decision loop. For a request-based toy with one million eligible requests in a fixed four-week window and 99.9% success SLO, allowable failures are 1000. If 600 counted failures occur, 60% is consumed and 400 failures remain for that population/window. If total eligible traffic changes, the final count allowance changes under this ratio definition; specify a fixed expected population if forecasting. One request is one counted event in this toy; retries, duplicates, exclusions and affected subgroups need a real specification. Do not convert a request-failure budget to downtime minutes without assumptions about traffic and outage shape.\nSeparate time-based toy: a 30-day window is 43200 minutes; a 99.9% time-availability SLO permits 43.2 minutes of unavailability on that explicitly defined basis. That is not the same metric as the one-million-request toy, and is not proof a 43.2-minute security/privacy failure is acceptable. An SLO reflects an authorized service decision and does not override safety, security, law or another obligation.\nThe workbook's published 2018 Example Error Budget Policy halts changes/releases, except P0 issues or security fixes, after the preceding four-week budget is exceeded, until back within SLO. It also distinguishes circumstances requiring reliability work from those where feature work may continue, and has an escalation route for disputed calculations/actions. This is one example policy, not blanket authority to freeze all production or a claim about the user's current policy. Embracing Risk describes alternatives such as slowing or rolling back releases rather than a single universal on/off rule. Do not hide miscategorizations to manufacture budget, quietly relax the SLO to erase a miss, or treat every outage type as equivalent user harm. Changes need the real service owner's agreed policy and permissions.\nEliminating Toil defines production-service work that tends to be manual, repetitive, automatable, tactical, without enduring value and scales with service size. Manual execution of an otherwise automated script can still consume toil time; enjoyable routine work can still be toil. Novel engineering with lasting benefit is different, and general administration/meetings can be overhead rather than toil. The chapter's advertised goal is below 50% toil with at least half engineering over a suitable multi-quarter/year horizon; it acknowledges spikes and variation. This is not a universal law, measured current Google average or a guarantee every person must split each day exactly in half. Removing essential response work merely to meet a number is not improvement.\nToy six-person week is 240 person-hours: 72 toil, 120 engineering and 48 overhead. Toil is 30%, engineering 50%, overhead 20%. A projection eliminating 24 repeated toil hours would leave 48 toil hours or 20% of the unchanged total, with 24 hours potentially available for other work. It does not prove a headcount or cash saving, and it is not actual saved time until the change works and recurring load is measured. Reclassifying overhead as engineering is not an engineering improvement.\nManaging Incidents separates incident command, coordinated operational changes, communication and planning, with a living incident record and explicit acknowledged handoffs. Contain impact/restore service while preserving evidence; avoid uncoordinated freelance fixes or invented ETAs. An incident-role description is not permission for an unassigned reader to change a system. Postmortem Culture advocates non-punitive analysis of contributing conditions and learning, not denying actions, individual responsibility or the need for follow-up owners. Record impact, timeline, evidence, mitigation and specific preventive actions; review and verify them. A reassuring tone alone does not establish psychological safety or causally eliminate repeat incidents. Keep end-user identifying data out of shared learning documents and restrict other sensitive evidence. No source authorizes publishing a private incident log or using a postmortem to evade another duty.\nSubmit a fictitious service specification with one user-relevant SLI/SLO, the request-budget and time-budget calculations on separate bases, the utilization-model counterexample and a redacted imaginary incident timeline/action table. Identify one actual evidence gap before recommending appointments, capacity or error-budget policy. No real user data, contract, service disruption, deployment, release freeze, staffing or clinical action.","quiz":[{"q":"An internal target says 99.9% of eligible requests succeed over four weeks. Which distinction is correct?","o":["The target is a contractual penalty even if no such agreement exists.","The measured fraction is an SLA because any operational measurement implies a contract.","The target is an SLO, the measured success fraction is an SLI, and an SLA additionally involves agreement/consequences.","An SLI is the target and an SLO is only the raw number of requests."],"a":2},{"q":"A fixed-window toy has one million eligible requests, 99.9% SLO and 600 counted failures. How much budget remains?","o":["The allowance is exceeded by 500 failures, by treating 0.1% as 0.01% in the allowance.","600 failures remain, because consumed failures are also the unused allowance.","998400 failures remain, by subtracting failures from successful-request target.","400 failures remain, after consuming 60% of the 1000-failure allowance."],"a":3},{"q":"What would justify converting that request-based budget directly into minutes of downtime?","o":["Additional assumptions about traffic over time and the pattern of affected requests, not the success target alone.","Only the fact that the request population has more than one event.","Only rounding the request count to the nearest full calendar month.","Only the existence of a release policy, regardless of how availability is measured."],"a":0},{"q":"For a separate 30-day time-based 99.9% availability toy, what is the unavailability allowance?","o":["1000 minutes, by copying the request-count allowance from another metric.","4.32 minutes, from applying a 99.99% allowance to the stated target.","432 minutes, from treating 0.1% as one percent.","43.2 minutes, from 30*24*60*0.001 on this defined time basis."],"a":3},{"q":"Which statement preserves the scope of the workbook Example Error Budget Policy?","o":["It automatically authorizes a reader to freeze another team's releases without an agreed policy.","It requires every organization to stop all security repairs whenever a budget is exceeded.","Its four-week exceedance rule retains P0/security-fix exceptions and is an example, not every service's rule.","It defines every network outage as a reason to abandon all feature work permanently."],"a":2},{"q":"Which task most closely fits the chapter's toil definition?","o":["A novel capacity model whose lasting results guide the next architecture.","A one-time redesign that permanently removes a recurring failure mode.","A general HR meeting not directly tied to operating the production service.","Repeated manual production-service resets with no lasting change, increasing with service load."],"a":3},{"q":"A 240-person-hour toy week has 72 toil, 120 engineering, 48 overhead. If 24 toil hours are truly eliminated and total remains 240, what follows?","o":["Engineering was 80% because all overhead is automatically lasting technical work.","Toil falls from 30% to 20%, freeing 24 potential hours, not automatically a cash/headcount saving.","Toil falls to 10% because saved hours are subtracted twice from the baseline.","A 24-hour saving proves one employee can be removed without service or workload evidence."],"a":1},{"q":"At utilization 0.95, which queue conclusion is justified by the stated single-server examples?","o":["The same utilization requires 57 minutes mean wait under every arrival/service distribution.","M/M/1 with rates 19/20 per hour has 57 minutes mean queue wait; aligned deterministic arrivals/service can have zero wait.","The same utilization proves a hospital's clinical triage rules are inefficient.","The same utilization makes service time and queue time identical in both examples."],"a":1},{"q":"A blameless postmortem is being prepared. Which approach fits the read chapter?","o":["Record actions and contributing conditions, assign follow-up owners, and remove end-user identifying data from shared learning.","Remove the incident timeline because identifying actions is inherently punishment.","Avoid assigning preventive work because ownership contradicts blamelessness.","Publish private customer identifiers because broad learning always overrides data restrictions."],"a":0},{"q":"An ongoing incident crosses shifts while several engineers are making changes. What is the strongest next process step?","o":["Coordinate operational changes, update the live state, and explicitly hand over command with acknowledgment.","Let every expert independently deploy fixes so coordination cannot delay them.","Assume the next shift has command because they received an unread status document.","Replace the incident record with a confident ETA even when recovery timing is unknown."],"a":0}]}];
